/10/01/sports/baseball/rays-continue-their-run-with-rout-of-rangers.html', 'snippet': 'Nothing is slowing down Tampa Bay. Behind a rookie pitcher, the Rays blasted the Rangers to take a lead in the division series.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ARLINGTON, Tex. — The aura surrounding the adrenaline-fueled Tampa Bay Rays shifted to wonderment Friday. Now the question is when they might come crashing back to baseball reality instead of making more history.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/01/sports/01rays-1/01rays-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/01/sports/01rays-1/01rays-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/01/sports/01rays-1/01rays-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/01/sports/01rays-1/01rays-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/01/sports/01rays-1/01rays-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rays Continue Their Run With Rout of Rangers', 'kicker': 'Rays 9, Rangers 0', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rays Avoid Letdown by Starting With Rout of Rangers in Texas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tampa Bay Rays', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Texas Rangers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-01T00:34:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Spousta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spousta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a015621c-5b9d-568d-ab26-c14c9143a385', 'word_count': 902, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a015621c-5b9d-568d-ab26-c14c9143a385'}, {'abstract': 'In one of those hard-to-explain scheduling quirks, the Brewers will play one of their rare playoff games directly against the Packers’ game on Sunday.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/09/30/brewers-biggest-opponent-may-be-packers/', 'snippet': 'In one of those hard-to-explain scheduling quirks, the Brewers will play one of their rare playoff games directly against the Packers’ game on Sunday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MILWAUKEE — Naturally the question offended Milwaukee Brewers infielder Craig Counsell, who grew up a Brewers fan in Whitefish Bay, Wis., but knows what kind of pull the Green Bay Packers exert on his fellow Wisconsinites.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brewers’ Biggest Opponent May Be Packers', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Counsell, Craig', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodgers, Aaron', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Bay Packers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Milwaukee Brewers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Wisconsin-Madison', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Milwaukee (Wis)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-01T00:34:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pat Borzi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pat', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borzi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6564a300-6252-5517-b2c4-8227a71a542e', 'word_count': 391, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6564a300-6252-5517-b2c4-8227a71a542e'}, {'abstract': 'The playwright Sarah Ruhl used a fresh, “literal” translation of Anton Chekhov’s 110-year-old drama “Three Sisters,” now at the University Theater at Yale.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/02/nyregion/sarah-ruhls-take-on-chekhovs-three-sisters-is-at-yale-review.html', 'snippet': 'The playwright Sarah Ruhl used a fresh, “literal” translation of Anton Chekhov’s 110-year-old drama “Three Sisters,” now at the University Theater at Yale.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sarah Ruhl has a taste for and a way with the classics. In 2003 she rewrote the Orpheus myth as “Eurydice,” in which the heroine falls from a high-rise and arrives in hell by elevator. Last fall, Classic Stage Company in New York presented her pitch-perfect 2003 re-creation of Virginia Woolf’s novel “Orlando.” Now she has tackled “Three Sisters,” Anton Chekhov’s 110-year-old drama about young women in the provinces with big-city dreams. Just as soon as they move back to Moscow, they say frequently, their dreary lives will become beautiful.', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/02/nyregion/02ARTSCT-2_SPAN/02ARTSCT-2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 's