L.', 'lastname': 'GOLDSMITH', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/ecd1c314-dc38-547e-aa44-95b2cd9d3bdd', 'word_count': 1006, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ecd1c314-dc38-547e-aa44-95b2cd9d3bdd'}, {'abstract': 'Richard Dresser’s latest play, “The Last Days of Mickey and Jean,” is having its New Jersey premiere at the Bickford Theater in Morris Township.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/02/nyregion/the-last-days-of-mickey-and-jean-review.html', 'snippet': 'Richard Dresser’s latest play, “The Last Days of Mickey and Jean,” is having its New Jersey premiere at the Bickford Theater in Morris Township.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Richard Dresser tends to write compact comedies that glint with a dark edge. Among his best-known works, “Rounding Third” (2002) studies two Little League coaches with clashing attitudes toward competition; “Gun-Shy” (1997) looks at divorced mates suffering through rebound romances; and “Below the Belt” (1995) presents a satirical saga of three corporate minions antagonizing one another at a remote industrial outpost.', 'print_section': 'NJ', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/02/nyregion/02ARTSNJ-1/02ARTSNJ-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 663, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/02/nyregion/02ARTSNJ-1/02ARTSNJ-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/02/nyregion/02ARTSNJ-1/02ARTSNJ-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Romance Is Fraying, but the Banter Goes On', 'kicker': 'Arts | New Jersey', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Romance Is Fraying, but the Banter Goes On', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dresser, Richard', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Playwrights Theater of New Jersey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-01T00:44:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Sommers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sommers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/55c4e368-9fcd-5370-b299-f7abc1960f6b', 'word_count': 642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55c4e368-9fcd-5370-b299-f7abc1960f6b'}, {'abstract': 'The Massachusetts senator who tried to lead the way in freeing the slaves, and was denounced for it.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/09/30/ahead-on-emancipation/', 'snippet': 'The Massachusetts senator who tried to lead the way in freeing the slaves, and was denounced for it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No politician was more determined than Senator Charles Sumner of Massachusetts to ensure that secession and war resulted in emancipation. He interpreted every event through the lens of freedom. A quick end to the conflict, he feared, would leave slavery intact. Although he did not wish for it, he saw the benefit of the humiliating Union defeat at Bull Run in July 1861. He informed the abolitionist Wendell Phillips, “The battle & defeat have done much for the slave. I told the Predt that our defeat was the worst event & the best event in our history; the worst, as it was the greatest present calamity & shame, — the best, as it made the extinction of Slavery inevitable.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ahead on Emancipation', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-01T00:45:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Louis P. Masur', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Louis', 'middlename': 'P.', 'lastname': 'Masur', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ad94aec-cbfc-54af-bd48-7e8fc65809a0', 'word_count': 1547, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ad94aec-cbfc-54af-bd48-7e8fc65809a0'}, {'abstract': 'Teachers in Acapulco have agreed to go back to work on Monday after more than a month on strike in protest over crime.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/01/world/americas/mexico-teachers-end-strike.html', 'snippet': 'Teachers in Acapulco have agreed to go back to work on Monday after more than a month on strike in protest over crime.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Teachers in Acapulco have agreed to go back to work on Monday after more than a month on strike in protest over crime. Thousands of the city’s teachers have stayed home after receiving extortion threats demanding half their salary. Leaders of the teachers signed an agreement with the state government late Thursday that lays out increased security measures around schools. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mexico: Teachers End Strike', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | THE AMERICAS', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mexico: Teachers End Strike', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strikes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ACAPULCO (ME