inals', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-01T01:33:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Kepner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kepner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bde6ea3-1820-5cf6-b540-fcf94385c6f2', 'word_count': 1076, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bde6ea3-1820-5cf6-b540-fcf94385c6f2'}, {'abstract': 'Exactly 65 years ago, the Greatest Generation put the Nazis on trial. And Harold Burson reported on it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/01/opinion/nocera-the-nuremberg-scripts.html', 'snippet': 'Exactly 65 years ago, the Greatest Generation put the Nazis on trial. And Harold Burson reported on it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In November 1945, six months after Germany’s surrender to the Allies, a 24-year-old Army combat engineer named Harold Burson was handed a new assignment: to cover the upcoming Nuremberg trial for the American Forces Network. For the next five months, Burson was one of two soldiers who reported on the trial and produced a daily “script,” which was read over the air by the network’s announcers.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbLarge-v4.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Nuremberg Scripts', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Nuremberg Scripts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuremberg War Crimes Tribunal (1945-49)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burson, Harold', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-01T01:37:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea64e671-556d-5109-82c5-039a72c78014', 'word_count': 818, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea64e671-556d-5109-82c5-039a72c78014'}, {'abstract': 'For us, the fourth annual Food and Drink issue is like one of those dinner parties where you decide to make something you’ve never tried. An experiment in real time, with immediate consequences for your trusting guests.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/09/30/this-sunday-hong-kong-fish-balls-baby/', 'snippet': 'For us, the fourth annual Food and Drink issue is like one of those dinner parties where you decide to make something you’ve never tried. An experiment in real time, with immediate consequences for your trusting guests.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fourth annual Food and Drink issue is like one of those dinner parties where you decide to make something you’ve never tried before. An experiment in real time, with inescapable consequences for your trusting guests. In place of the long-form narrative journalism that is our stock in trade, we concocted a completely bite-size issue made up of concise answers to a wide-ranging series of questions. Everything from “How can you tell if food is genetically engineered?” to “How many kinds of Pringles are there?” (Anyone for Hong Kong Fish Balls? Yes, that’s a flavor.) The longest answer is about 600 words, or less than one-tenth the size of a typical cover story, and there are more than 50 of them. We did this because it enabled us to cover a lot of ground, and because we thought it would be fun just to change up the menu.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Sunday: Hong Kong Fish Balls, Baby', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hesser, Amanda', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hetherington, Tim', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pollan, Michael', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sifton, Sam', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-01T01:38:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article'