 'Mr. Davenport was a physicist who worked on a complex system used for tracking and shooting down enemy planes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lee Davenport, a physicist who developed a radar device that helped bring Allied victories on major World War II battlefronts in Europe and the Pacific, died Friday in Greenwich, Conn. He was 95. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/01/us/DAVENPORT-obit/DAVENPORT-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 960, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/01/us/DAVENPORT-obit/DAVENPORT-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/01/us/DAVENPORT-obit/DAVENPORT-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lee Davenport Dies at 95; Developed Battlefront Radar', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lee Davenport, 95; Developed Battlefront Radar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radar', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-01T03:01:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Goldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd0ef217-1915-561f-87d9-0f931c301d60', 'word_count': 774, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd0ef217-1915-561f-87d9-0f931c301d60'}, {'abstract': 'Stay Thirsty, Uncle Mo and Havre de Grace will headline the action Saturday at Belmont Park.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/01/sports/on-horse-racing-no-front-runner-but-three-who-dazzle.html', 'snippet': 'Stay Thirsty, Uncle Mo and Havre de Grace will headline the action Saturday at Belmont Park.', 'lead_paragraph': 'They may not be the best or even necessarily the fastest horses in the land, but they certainly have provided the most drama and entertainment through an unpredictable but satisfying 2011 thoroughbred racing season. We’re talking about two boys and a girl — Stay Thirsty, Uncle Mo and Havre de Grace — all of whom are in town Saturday at Belmont Park for what is one of the sport’s best afternoons outside the Breeders’ Cup World Champions next month at Churchill Downs.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Front-Runner This Season, but Three Who Dazzle', 'kicker': 'On Horse Racing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Front-Runner This Season, but Three Who Dazzle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Belmont Stakes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kentucky Derby', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Breeders' Cup (Horse Race)", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-01T03:03:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Drape', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drape', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a657138d-7bc3-5d4b-a166-60f5e84dfde9', 'word_count': 789, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a657138d-7bc3-5d4b-a166-60f5e84dfde9'}, {'abstract': 'The director of Unesco called Friday for President Teodoro Obiang Nguema Mbasogo, of Equatorial Guinea, to withdraw the controversial prize he has endowed for the organization.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/01/world/africa/unesco-director-asks-dictator-to-withdraw-science-prize.html', 'snippet': 'The director of Unesco called Friday for President Teodoro Obiang Nguema Mbasogo, of Equatorial Guinea, to withdraw the controversial prize he has endowed for the organization.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The director of Unesco called Friday for a West African dictator to withdraw the controversial prize he has endowed for the organization. The prize in the life sciences, financed by and named for President Teodoro Obiang Nguema Mbasogo of Equatorial Guinea, would damage the “prestige” and “credibility” of Unesco, its director general, Irina Bokova, said at a meeting of the group’s executive board in Paris. Human rights activists, scientists and political leaders have denounced the prize as a public-relations stunt by Mr. Obiang. The Unesco board is expected to meet again next week on the issue.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/01/world/01briefs-Bokova/01briefs-Bokova-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/01/world/01briefs-Bokova/01briefs-Bokova-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Unesco Director Asks Dictator to Withdraw Science Prize', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | UNITED NATIONS', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unesco Director Asks Dictator To Withdraw Science Prize', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obiang Nguema Mbasogo, Teodoro', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bokova, Irina', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Contests and Prizes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Equatorial Guinea', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-01T03:08:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Sayare', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sayare', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/156ae3ed-b3f8-505a-8bf9-37c7624857ca', 'word_count': 99, 'uri': 'nyt://article/156ae3ed-b3f8-505a-8bf9-37c7624857ca'}, {'abstract': 'A group of about a dozen protesters broke into the newsroom of The Guardian newspaper on Friday, demanding further coverage of Kurdish conflicts with the Turkish government.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/01/world/europe/britain-kurdish-protesters-besiege-newspaper.html', 'snippet': 'A group of about a dozen protesters broke into the newsroom of The Guardian newspaper on Friday, demanding further coverage of Kurdish conflicts with the Turkish government.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A group of about a dozen Kurdish separatist protesters broke into the newsroom of The Guardian newspaper on Friday and staged a brief demonstration, demanding further coverage of Kurdish conflicts with the Turkish government, according to reports from within the newspaper. A video of the incident showed the protesters gathered on a stairway, chanting and handing out literature to newspaper staff members, many of whom appeared bemused. The protests were low-key and peaceful, said one reporter, Alexandra Topping. The invasion of The Guardian comes amid a season of Kurdish protests against a campaign of Turkish airstrikes in northern Iraq in pursuit of violent Kurdish separatists. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Britain: Kurdish Protesters Besiege Newspaper', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Britain: Kurdish Protesters Besiege Newspaper', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kurds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Guardian (British Newspaper)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-01T03:21:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ravi Somaiya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ravi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Somaiya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ba257f0-b0bb-5d3e-b68e-422942cfd624', 'word_count': 105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ba257f0-b0bb-5d3e-b68e-422942cfd624'}, {'abstract': 'Namibian government representatives and leaders of the Herero and Nama tribes attended a ceremony on Friday to take possession of 20 skulls brought to Germany for racial research a century ago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/01/world/europe/germany-returns-20-skulls-to-africa.html', 'snippet': 'Namibian government representatives and leaders of the Herero and Nama tribes attended a ceremony on Friday to take possession of 20 skulls brought to Germany for racial research a century ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Namibian government representatives and leaders of the Herero and Nama tribes attended a ceremony at Charité Hospital in Berlin on Friday to take possession of 20 skulls brought to Germany for racial research a century ago.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Germany Returns 20 Skulls to Africa', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Germany Returns 20 Skulls to Africa', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Namibia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Skull (Body Part)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Herero', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-01T03:22:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Victor Homola', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Victor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Homola', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7dbfc924-1855-52f7-919b-b4d36ed8475a', 'word_count': 212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7dbfc924-1855-52f7-919b-b4d36ed8475a'}, {'abstract': 'Weekend games in college football include Nebraska at Wisconsin and Alabama at Florida.', 'web_url': 'https://thequad.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/09/30/games-to-watch-on-saturday-4/', 'snippet': 'Weekend games in college football include Nebraska at Wisconsin and Alabama at Florida.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NO. 8 NEBRASKA AT NO. 7 WISCONSIN8 p.m. Eastern, ABC', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], '