lide-TR4X-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A.L.D.S. Game 1: Yankees 9, Tigers 3', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-02T05:52:03+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/4522e4d2-5132-5d90-a0ba-2c3aec1ff863', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/4522e4d2-5132-5d90-a0ba-2c3aec1ff863'}, {'abstract': 'Pete Gent was able to express in words what many players feel about pro football, both good and bad.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/02/remembering-pete-gent-who-wrote-north-dallas-forty/', 'snippet': 'Pete Gent was able to express in words what many players feel about pro football, both good and bad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pete Gent was able to express in words what many players feel about pro football, both good and bad. While his writing left its mark on the game, it’s equally clear that the game left its mark on him. Gent died Friday from a pulmonary illness at his home in Bangor, Mich. He was 69. A few thoughts:In the early 1970s, as pro football was becoming the nation’s most popular spectator sport, a group of ex-players exposed, through both their words and their actions, the game’s seamy underside. A novel by Pete Gent, “North Dallas Forty,” based on his five years with the Cowboys, graphically depicted the pain and anguish the public couldn’t feel or see on their television screens. Yet, despite the broken bones and sleepless nights, most of the players wished it could go on forever. Many, like Gent himself, remembered their football days as some of the best times of their lives.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Remembering Pete Gent, Who Wrote ‘North Dallas Forty’', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dallas Cowboys', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-02T09:38:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Barall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54b2fffa-224f-599c-9efc-a9326e4c90cc', 'word_count': 1278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54b2fffa-224f-599c-9efc-a9326e4c90cc'}, {'abstract': 'Some would argue that London’s cycling credentials are on the rise, and so it’s fitting that the annual Bicycle Film Festival makes its return to London, Oct. 5 to 9, in various locations around the city.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/02/london-film-festival-rolls-in-on-two-wheels/', 'snippet': 'Some would argue that London’s cycling credentials are on the rise, and so it’s fitting that the annual Bicycle Film Festival makes its return to London, Oct. 5 to 9, in various locations around the city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'London did not make USA Today’s “10 Great Places for City Cycling” list this July. But the city’s did earn a top-20 mention in a more European-focused Danish report on the best bicycle-friendly cities. Combined with local efforts to promote cycling in anticipation of the upcoming 2012 Olympics, some would argue that London’s cycling credentials are on the rise.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'London Film Festival Rolls in on Two Wheels', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Festivals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-02T10:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Moskowitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moskowitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c20004d7-5864-5e35-a143-a8488b3f3c24', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c20004d7-5864-5e35-a143-a8488b3f3c24'}, {'abstract': 'Besides a mountain of statistics, Baseball-Reference.com artfully captures a player’s history with cool circular icons that represent uniform icons.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/02/the-joy-of-needless-details/', 'snippet': 'Besides a mountain of statistics, Baseball-Reference.com artfully captures a player’s history with cool circular icons that represent uniform icons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you have ever visited Baseball-Reference.com, you know that it is one of the greatest inventions of human history. Not only does the site contain every baseball statistic you could ever want, sorted in every imaginable way, but it also has cool circular icons that represent every team a player ever played for, and his uniform number on that team.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Joy of Needless Details', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Statistics', 'rank': 2, '