'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weilerstein, Alisa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bargemusic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-02T21:27:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/af1d0dbb-649b-550f-825d-7495ed0315ae', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af1d0dbb-649b-550f-825d-7495ed0315ae'}, {'abstract': '“Courageous” is about a Georgia police officer whose job exposes him to social decay and spiritual desolation wrought by fatherless homes, strengthening his devotion to conservative family values.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/movies/courageous-directed-by-alex-kendrick-review.html', 'snippet': '“Courageous” is about a Georgia police officer whose job exposes him to social decay and spiritual desolation wrought by fatherless homes, strengthening his devotion to conservative family values.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fourth and latest feature from the “moviemaking ministry” of Sherwood Pictures, a media arm of the Sherwood Baptist Church in Albany, Ga., “Courageous” contains enough parables, “are you there, God?” monologues and tearful affirmations of faith-based fatherhood to furnish a dozen megachurch services. To sweeten the sermon there is also some light gunplay.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fathers Who Tend to Their Flocks', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Courageous'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sherwood Pictures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-02T21:27:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Brunick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brunick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f94d0a17-5e60-5c46-99be-d1d230b1967a', 'word_count': 319, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f94d0a17-5e60-5c46-99be-d1d230b1967a'}, {'abstract': '“Sahib Biwi Aur Gangster” is a riff on a 1962 Hindi classic, with amorous rapture, political machinations, bloodlettings and (of course) musical sequences.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/movies/sahib-biwi-aur-gangster-directed-by-tigmanshu-dhulia-review.html', 'snippet': '“Sahib Biwi Aur Gangster” is a riff on a 1962 Hindi classic, with amorous rapture, political machinations, bloodlettings and (of course) musical sequences.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For all its flashy sunglasses and gunplay, there’s a folk-tale essence in Tigmanshu Dhulia’s “Sahib Biwi Aur Gangster,” a riff on “Sahib Bibi Aur Ghulam,” a 1962 Hindi classic. That film concerned the chaste bond between a married noblewoman and a servant; Mr. Dhulia’s take offers Sahib (Jimmy Shergill), a rural mob godfather of declining fortunes, and Biwi (Mahie Gill), his neglected, hot-tempered wife. The gangster (Randeep Hooda), hired as a chauffeur, is secretly on a mission to set up Sahib for a hit. There’s nothing chaste about his designs: He covets Biwi and Sahib’s status as well.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Amorous Triangle, Spiced Up by Gunplay', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Sahib Biwi Aur Gangster'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-02T21:28:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f096e083-4ee5-5343-afb8-27834ea2fb4b', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f096e083-4ee5-5343-afb8-27834ea2fb4b'}, {'abstract': 'Among the actors and the books they will read: Naomi Watts, “Summer,” by Edith Wharton; Dustin Hoffman, “Being There,” by Jerzy Kosinski; and Samuel L. Jackson, “A Rage in Harlem,” by Chester Himes.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/02/stars-will-read-amazon-units-new-audio-book-series/', 'snippet': 'Among the actors and the books they will read: Naomi Watts, “Summer,” by Edith Wharton; Dustin Hoffman, “Being There,” by Jerzy Kosinski; and Samuel L. Jackson, “A Rage in Harlem,” by Chester Himes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Would you listen to a recording of “The End of the Affair” that lasted hours? What if Colin Firth were reading it?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stars Will Read Amazon Unit’s New Audio Book Series', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Audiobooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Celebrities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-02T21:30:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2529f62b-1bdc-5237-937c-d2fae668c442', 'word_count': 300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2529f62b-1bdc-5237-937c-d2fae668c442'}, {'abstract': 'Rohinton Mistry has been named the 2012 laureate of the $50,000 Neustadt International Prize for Literature, according to World Literature Today.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/books/neustadt-prize-awarded.html', 'snippet': 'Rohinton Mistry has been named the 2012 laureate of the $50,000 Neustadt International Prize for Literature, according to World Literature Today.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rohinton Mistry has been named the 2012 laureate of the $50,000 Neustadt International Prize for Literature, according to World Literature Today, a magazine of the University of Oklahoma. Mr. Mistry previously won the Los Angeles Times Book Prize for fiction and was a finalist in 1996 for the Man Booker Prize for “A Fine Balance,” which was also selected by Oprah Winfrey’s book club in 2001. The Neustadt Prize is given every two years and is sponsored by the University of Oklahoma, World Literature Today and the Neustadt family. An international jury considers the work of poets, playwrights and novelists for the honor.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Neustadt Prize Awarded', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Neustadt Prize Awarded', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mistry, Rohinton', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-02T21:30:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/95f43785-18bd-5a9b-8e1b-3fc39e7dba1e', 'word_count': 105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95f43785-18bd-5a9b-8e1b-3fc39e7dba1e'}, {'abstract': 'Three women and six teenage girls were removed from the Paper Mill Playhouse in Millburn, N.J., after patrons complained about disruptive behavior during a performance of “Newsies the Musical.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/theater/feisty-performance-at-paper-mill-playhouse.html', 'snippet': 'Three women and six teenage girls were removed from the Paper Mill Playhouse in Millburn, N.J., after patrons complained about disruptive behavior during a performance of “Newsies the Musical.”', 'lead_paragraph': '“Newsies the Musical” may feature rambunctious youths loudly proclaiming their rebellion onstage, but the same description could have been used for a few audience members on Thursday at the Paper Mill Playhouse in Millburn, N.J. Three women and six teenage girls were removed from the theater after several patrons complained about an altercation and disruptive behavior during the performance, according to The Star-Ledger of Newark. Asked to leave by theater management during the intermission, the women reportedly refused. A uniformed officer then escorted them out. Based on the 1992 film “Newsies” starring Christian Bale and Bill Pullman and produced by Disney Theatrical Productions, the musical opened last week to glowing reviews under the direction of Jeff Calhoun with choreography by Christopher Gattelli and a book by Harvey Fierstein. The musical has recently drawn interest from theater owners in New York, with the intention of mounting a Broadway production.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Feisty Performance at Paper Mill Playhouse', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Feisty Performance At Paper Mill Playhouse', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Paper Mill Playhouse', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-02T21:32:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/deab426d-2740-549e-9041-a57304255620', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/deab426d-2740-549e-9041-a57304255620'}, {'abstract': 'Humberto Leon and Carol Lim presented their first Kenzo collection Sunday evening with a show that was every bit as fun as promised by the two founders of Opening Ceremony.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/02/crazy-fun-at-kenzo/', 'snippet': 'Humberto Leon and Carol Lim presented their first Kenzo collection Sunday evening with a show that was every bit as fun as promised by the two founders of Opening Ceremony.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — Humberto Leon and Carol Lim presented their first Kenzo collection on Sunday with a show that was every bit as fun as promised by the two founders of Opening Ceremony.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Crazy Fun at Kenzo', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-02T21:32:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9057d4c-f4a0-5099-944d-19f6fe04706a', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9057d4c-f4a0-5099-944d-19f6fe04706a'}, {'abstract': 'Members of the Philadelphia chapter of the International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees ended a daylong strike on Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/arts/theater-strike-in-philadelphia.html', 'snippet': 'Members of the Philadelphia chapter of the International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees ended a daylong strike on Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Members of the Philadelphia chapter of the International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees ended a daylong strike on Saturday, announcing that a one-week cooling-off period would go into effect to allow for renewed negotiations between the chapter and the Kimmel Center for the Performing Arts, according to The Associated Press. The strike began after the current chapter contract, which covers about 1,000 employees from the Kimmel Center, the Merriam Theater and the Philadelphia Academy of Music, expired on Friday. The Kimmel Center canceled all Saturday events, including a musical performance by Audra McDonald. Talks between the chapter and the Kimmel Center broke down last week after the sides failed to agree on wages, benefits, working conditions and the chapter’s jurisdiction.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Theater Strike in Philadelphia', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Theater Strike In Philadelphia', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strikes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Philadelphia (Pa)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-02T21:32:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/72669175-81b8-5919-be43-dd207f34006e', 'word_count': 120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72669175-81b8-5919-be43-dd207f34006e'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Simon was among 178 artists, scientists and institutional leaders inducted into the American Academy of Arts and Sciences on Saturday at Harvard, The Associated Press reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/arts/music/another-honor-for-paul-simon.html', 'snippet': 'Paul Simon was among 178 artists, scientists and institutional leaders inducted into the American Academy of Arts and Sciences on Saturday at Harvard, The Associated Press reported.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paul Simon, above, was among 178 artists, scientists and institutional leaders inducted into the American Academy of Arts and Sciences on Saturday at Harvard, The Associated Press reported. Founded in 1780 by John Adams, James Bowdoin, John Hancock and others, the academy recognizes achievement in public affairs, science, education and culture. The ceremony included a performance by Mr. Simon of “American Tune,” from his 1973 album “There Goes Rhymin’ Simon.” Other inductees from the academy’s 231st class included the novelists Oscar Hijuelos and Luisa Valenzuela, the landscape architect Michael Van Valkenburgh and the Shakespeare scholar James Shapiro.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Another Honor for Paul Simon', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Another Honor For Paul Simon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Simon, Paul', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-02T21:33:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/3325c117-142e-5ea4-aee1-4ad362a9ceb7', 'word_count': 98, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3325c117-142e-5ea4-aee1-4ad362a9ceb7'}, {'abstract': 'Among the artists to appear at the annual series of concerts were Rihanna, Elton John, Metallica, Red Hot Chili Peppers and Shakira.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/arts/music/rock-in-rio-music-festival-ends.html', 'snippet': 'Among the artists to appear at the annual series of concerts were Rihanna, Elton John, Metallica, Red Hot Chili Peppers and Shakira.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Chris Martin, above, the lead vocalist for the group Coldplay, practiced his spray painting on a stage wall during his band’s performance at the Rock in Rio Music Festival in Rio de Janeiro on Saturday. Among the artists to appear at the annual series of concerts were Rihanna, Elton John, Metallica, Red Hot Chili Peppers and Shakira. The festival, which opened on Sept. 23, ended on Sunday.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rock in Rio Music Festival Ends', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rock in Rio Music Festival Ends', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rio de Janeiro (Brazil)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-02T21:33:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e33ef508-4ba2-5b85-9985-66b13a831b99', 'word_count': 70, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e33ef508-4ba2-5b85-9985-66b13a831b99'}, {'abstract': 'Account assignments, executive appointments and miscellaneous news from advertising agencies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/business/media/news-from-the-advertising-industry.html', 'snippet': 'Account assignments, executive appointments and miscellaneous news from advertising agencies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Accounts', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News From the Advertising Industry', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'News From the Advertising Industry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-02T21:37:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/32bb472f-ef56-5a20-b7dc-a4f5bd801122', 'word_count': 696, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32bb472f-ef56-5a20-b7dc-a4f5bd801122'}, {'abstract': 'Twenty-six current and former ballerinas who had worked with the choreographer Jerome Robbins took the stage at New York City Ballet to accept an award named for him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/arts/dance/city-ballet-in-west-side-story-suite-at-koch-theater-review.html', 'snippet': 'Twenty-six current and former ballerinas who had worked with the choreographer Jerome Robbins took the stage at New York City Ballet to accept an award named for him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert Fairchild is a natural Riff, the charismatic head of the Jets, who, in the Jerome Robbins ballet “West Side Story Suite,” as in the classic musical, meets a senselessly violent end. It was almost a surprise, at Friday night’s New York City Ballet performance, to realize he was making his debut in the role; after that, it was just a delight. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/03/arts/NYCB/NYCB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 896, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/03/arts/NYCB/NYCB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/03/arts/NYCB/NYCB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ballet Divas Steal Stage From Riff, if Only Briefly', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ballet Divas Steal Stage From Riff, If Only Briefly', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Ballet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robbins, Jerome', 'rank': 3, 