None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab8abb8f-7781-524d-b265-b8efb18e5be4', 'word_count': 1286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab8abb8f-7781-524d-b265-b8efb18e5be4'}, {'abstract': 'The Brewers took advantage of some mental mistakes by the Diamondbacks to break open a close game and take a 2-0 lead in their National League division series.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/sports/baseball/brewers-flex-their-muscles-and-push-past-the-diamondbacks-again.html', 'snippet': 'The Brewers took advantage of some mental mistakes by the Diamondbacks to break open a close game and take a 2-0 lead in their National League division series.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MILWAUKEE — Prince Fielder started it, naturally, since every gesture and celebration by the Milwaukee Brewers seems to emanate from him. It is called “beast mode.” Whenever a Brewer gets a hit or makes a nice play, Fielder, the bearlike first baseman, flexes his arms forward as if trying to hug a sequoia, and the player flashes it back. Bloop or blast, it does not matter. A grunt tops it off.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/03/sports/YBREWERS/YBREWERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/03/sports/YBREWERS/YBREWERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/03/sports/YBREWERS/YBREWERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Brewers Flex Their Muscles and Push Past the Diamondbacks Again', 'kicker': 'Brewers 9, Diamondbacks 4', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brewers Flex Their Muscles and Push Past the Diamondbacks Again', 'na