_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be63d595-a3cc-5e31-a52c-1233f018a101', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be63d595-a3cc-5e31-a52c-1233f018a101'}, {'abstract': 'Hanging over both chambers is the specter of yet another serious fiscal fight this week, with longer-term battles over trade, regulations and how best to stimulate the economy also on the horizon.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/back-from-break-congress-has-a-full-plate/', 'snippet': 'Hanging over both chambers is the specter of yet another serious fiscal fight this week, with longer-term battles over trade, regulations and how best to stimulate the economy also on the horizon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Congress returns on Monday after a week off, but it was the sort of recess during which it rains every day, and no one gets to play on the swings. Hanging over both chambers is the specter of yet another serious fiscal fight this week, with longer-term battles over trade, regulations and how best to stimulate the economy also on the horizon. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Back From Break, Congress Has a Full Plate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-03T11:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Steinhauer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinhauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8830c21-8a93-5628-bc11-4012be4616c3', 'word_count': 638, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8830c21-8a93-5628-bc11-4012be4616c3'}, {'abstract': 'In this week’s Complaint Box: Do ill-mannered children belong in restaurants? Must their uncontrolled screams and strewn debris be tolerated by other patrons?', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/complaint-box-children-in-restaurants/', 'snippet': 'In this week’s Complaint Box: Do ill-mannered children belong in restaurants? Must their uncontrolled screams and strewn debris be tolerated by other patrons?', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a weekend excursion to Baltimore in July, I chanced upon an unusual scene in a hotel restaurant. After a toddler and his parents had finished eating and left their table, a two-person cleanup crew immediately appeared, attempting to reverse the devastation that the child had wrought on and beneath the table. Apparently the toddler had shredded every last piece of bread on the table and tossed it to the floor, along with various other breakfast items. Yet nothing in the parents’ expressions or words would have led one to conclude that they were aware of the mess their child had created.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Complaint Box | Children in Restaurants', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-03T11:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sharon Mast', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sharon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mast', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported',