'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7dbc0598-c95e-5c6e-a712-e873798ae7cd', 'word_count': 926, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7dbc0598-c95e-5c6e-a712-e873798ae7cd'}, {'abstract': 'This latest video by Inez and Vinoodh highlights Pierre Balmain’s women’s collection, featuring Abbey Lee and her band Our Mountain.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/new-from-paris-pierre-balmain/', 'snippet': 'This latest video by Inez and Vinoodh highlights Pierre Balmain’s women’s collection, featuring Abbey Lee and her band Our Mountain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Balmain’s new diffusion line, Pierre Balmain, made its debut in Paris on Sunday and can best be described as a greatest-hits collection, but at one third of the price. All of the Balmain pieces that we’ve come to love — black motocross jeans, zebra-print button-down shirts and shrunken leather jackets — came styled in exactly the right model-off-duty way. The men’s wear, too, was very rocker-looking, with leather bombers and silky button-down shirts neatly tucked into skinny jeans.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New From Paris | Pierre Balmain', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-03T18:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sara Moonves', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moonves', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca99d213-ebd4-5cca-aceb-075f907de93b', 'word_count': 118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca99d213-ebd4-5cca-aceb-075f907de93b'}, {'abstract': 'What to send to school with a child who has better things to do than eat lunch? Sweet bites with a nutritional boost.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/health/nutrition/03recipehealth.html', 'snippet': 'What to send to school with a child who has better things to do than eat lunch? Sweet bites with a nutritional boost.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My son, an eighth grader who has grown six inches in the last year, will not eat lunch at school. It’s not about the sandwiches I’ve tried to pack, or the school lunches (which I wouldn’t blame him for not eating). Nor is it a question of appetite: He’s always hungry. During his 30-minute lunch period he wants to play basketball. If he goes to the cafeteria he doesn’t have time to get a game in.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/04/science/03recipehealth/03recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/04/science/03recipehealth/03recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/04/science/03recipehealth/03recipehealth-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/04/science/03recipehealth/03recipehealth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1339, 'width': 1999, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/04/science/03recipehealth/03recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/04/science/03recipehealth/03recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/04/science/03recipehealth/03recipehealth-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Substantial Pastries for Busy Teenagers', 'kicker': 'Recipes for Health', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bakeries and Baked Products', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-03T18:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Fitness & Nutrition', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Rose Shulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Shulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'recipe', '_id': 'nyt://article/14ca2b3c-91fa-5e01-9c14-3fc65b2308c7', 'word_count': 534, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14ca2b3c-91fa-5e01-9c14-3fc65b2308c7'}, {'abstract': 'If you read the Food and Drink issue, you already know what rattlesnake tastes like: ‘‘sinewy, half-starved tilapia,’’ according to Maud Newton.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/another-helping-of-rattlesnake/', 'snippet': 'If you read the Food and Drink issue, you already know what rattlesnake tastes like: ‘‘sinewy, half-starved tilapia,’’ according to Maud Newton.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you read Sunday’s Food and Drink issue, you already know what rattlesnake tastes like: ‘‘sinewy, half-starved tilapia,’’ according to Maud Newton. Check out Maud’s follow-up post on her blog to find out what raw rattlesnake looks like (‘‘a garden hose made of filleted haddock’’) and what might be needed to cut it safely (‘‘hedge clippers’’), among other details.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Another Helping of Rattlesnake?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-03T18:03:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Staff', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Staff'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/20a86f0d-dee2-5f12-807e-dc0f3f035bcd', 'word_count': 59, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20a86f0d-dee2-5f12-807e-dc0f3f035bcd'}, {'abstract': 'The challenge was to describe a blood pressure measuring device that is long-lasting, portable and easy to use. Here are some of the best responses.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/health/04small.html', 'snippet': 'The challenge was to describe a blood pressure measuring device that is long-lasting, portable and easy to use. Here are some of the best responses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week’s special issue, Small Fixes, dealt with low-cost health innovations that are saving lives in the developing world. Online, at nytimes.com/smallfixes, readers were invited to come up with a small fix of their own: a device for detecting high blood pressure, which, left untreated, can raise the risk of coronary artery disease, stroke, kidney damage and heart attack.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Small Fix for Hypertension: Readers Weigh In', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Small Fix for Hypertension: Readers Weigh In', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blood Pressure', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'na