cf31d'}, {'abstract': 'A young Tibetan monk from Kirti Monastery set himself on fire Monday in a remote western town to protest Chinese policies, according to a Tibet advocacy group based in London.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/world/asia/fourth-tibetan-monk-self-immolates-in-anti-china-protest.html', 'snippet': 'A young Tibetan monk from Kirti Monastery set himself on fire Monday in a remote western town to protest Chinese policies, according to a Tibet advocacy group based in London.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — A young Tibetan monk set himself on fire Monday in a remote western town to protest Chinese policies, the fourth monk from Kirti Monastery to self-immolate this year, according to a Tibet advocacy group based in London.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fourth Tibetan Monk Self-Immolates in Anti-China Protest', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: 4th Tibetan Monk Burns Himself', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tibet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Buddhism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suicides and Suicide Attempts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-03T18:16:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51149a4c-13d1-527b-b5b6-8a6a38d85b7c', 'word_count': 474, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51149a4c-13d1-527b-b5b6-8a6a38d85b7c'}, {'abstract': 'John Solomon, a Newsweek reporter who interviewed Mr. Strauss-Kahn’s accuser, will write a book for St. Martin’s Press.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/book-on-strauss-kahn-incident-planned/', 'snippet': 'John Solomon, a Newsweek reporter who interviewed Mr. Strauss-Kahn’s accuser, will write a book for St. Martin’s Press.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dominique Strauss-Kahn has left New York for Paris, but the story of his sexual encounter with a hotel chambermaid, and the criminal case that developed and then was dropped, is just reaching book publishers. St. Martin’s Press, part of Macmillan, said on Monday it had acquired a book by John Solomon, the Newsweek reporter who interviewed Nafissatou Diallo, the housekeeper who accused Mr. Strauss-Kahn of sexual assault in May. Mr. Solomon’s book “will report the inside story of how the DSK case was transformed from a slam-dunk, five-day indictment against Strauss-Kahn to a flat-out dismissal that turned the tables on his very accuser,” a statement from the publisher said. The charges against Mr. Strauss-Kahn — who called the encounter “an error” and a “moral failure” but not a criminal act — were dismissed in August after prosecutors said the accuser lacked credibility. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Book on Strauss-Kahn Incident Planned', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diallo, Nafissatou', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Solomon, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "St Martin's Press", 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-03T18:17:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d68b5d98-397d-5272-b03d-4224dd5299cc', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d68b5d98-397d-5272-b03d-4224dd5299cc'}, {'abstract': 'Off the Rails: The latest numbers from the transit system show just how packed the L train has become.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/soon-l-will-mean-less-crowded-subway-officials-say/', 'snippet': 'Off the Rails: The latest numbers from the transit system show just how packed the L train has become.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Want to find a seat on the L train? Head to the back three cars. Avoid peak hours entirely. And if it’s between 4 p.m. and 6 p.m. on a Saturday, forget it. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Soon, L Will Mean Less Crowded, Subway Officials Say', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Look at L Train Crowding, With Some Good News, Too', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Transportation Authority', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subways', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Transportation Authority', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Transit Authority', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-03T18:21:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a177484-7b63-54e7-91ef-0a208ea4c4c8', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a177484-7b63-54e7-91ef-0a208ea4c4c8'}, {'abstract': 'At 57, the author has noticed a few changes since his first cross-country bicycle journey, 18 years ago. On this trip, he’s in less of a hurry, with a greater propensity for stopping to take pictures and enjoying the scenery.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/riding-slowly-through-the-heartland/', 'snippet': 'At 57, the author has noticed a few changes since his first cross-country bicycle journey, 18 years ago. On this trip, he’s in less of a hurry, with a greater propensity for stopping to take pictures and enjoying the scenery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WOOSTER, Ohio — Eighteen years ago, when I made my first cross-country bicycle journey, the ride from the Golden Gate Bridge to the George Washington Bridge took 75 days. Sunday was day 75 of my second transcontinental trip, and the distance I have yet to travel from Wooster, Ohio, to Manhattan — 470 miles by car, probably another 70 or 80 on a bicyclist’s more meandering path — is one way to measure how I’ve aged. (Another is that my knees are stiff and sore; every morning I feel\xa0 little like the Tin Man from “The Wizard of Oz,” pleading squeakily for his oil can.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Riding (Slowly) Through the Heartland', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-03T18:25:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Weber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be3ee83b-26a0-580a-8160-86f373953eff', 'word_count': 721, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be3ee83b-26a0-580a-8160-86f373953eff'}, {'abstract': 'Can New York replicate the spark that ignited Silicon Valley?', 'web_url': 'https://keller.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/silicon-valley-ii/', 'snippet': 'Can New York replicate the spark that ignited Silicon Valley?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The underlying assumption of Mayor Michael Bloomberg’s plan to subsidize a top-flight applied science campus in New York is that New York can replicate the spark that ignited Silicon Valley. Can it?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Silicon Valley II?', 'kicker': 'Bill Keller', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-03T18:25:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Keller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65f81012-d287-56a4-8d5e-9c85ca50b32f', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65f81012-d287-56a4-8d5e-9c85ca50b32f'}, {'abstract': 'In readers’ comments, passions often obscure the nuances of illegal immigration.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/addressing-some-misperceptions-about-mexican-immigration/', 'snippet': 'In readers’ comments, passions often obscure the nuances of illegal immigration.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reading the comments attached to my article today – looking at how today’s illegal immigrants are more likely to be people who have roots and families in the United States – I was struck by something that advocates for and against expanded immigration told me while I was reporting: “This is a fact-free zone.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Addressing Some Misperceptions About Mexican Immigration', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-03T18:29:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Damien Cave', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Damien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cave', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64be9d87-6286-5b8d-a7f9-abaf25440406', 'word_count': 933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64be9d87-6286-5b8d-a7f9-abaf25440406'}, {'abstract': 'Having assisted in the death of many newspapers, technology will save those that adapt.', 'web_url': 'https://keller.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/disrupters-and-adapters-continued-will-the-internet-save-newspapers/', 'snippet': 'Having assisted in the death of many newspapers, technology will save those that adapt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The two visions of higher education’s future I described in my column this week – Stanford Professor Sebastian Thrun’s mission of a virtual university and Stanford President John Hennessy’s devotion to a flesh-and-blood campus – intrigued me because of the larger context. So much of the debate about the impact of new technology tends to be polarizing. The utopians versus the skeptics, the idealists versus the realists, those who throw themselves headlong into the great mosh pit of the new, and those who cherish the familiar and time-honored. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Disrupters and Adapters, Continued: Will the Internet Save Newspapers?', 'kicker': 'Bill Keller', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hennessy, John L', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thrun, Sebastian', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stanford University', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-03T18:30:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Keller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29f8284d-b72e-5700-a8c0-604423b3e341', 'word_count': 1026, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29f8284d-b72e-5700-a8c0-604423b3e341'}, {'abstract': 'The bright glazed skin was meant to repel moisture but over the decades leaks have come, and in many cases the repairs are proving to be very expensive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/nyregion/white-brick-buildings-begin-to-show-their-age.html', 'snippet': 'The bright glazed skin was meant to repel moisture but over the decades leaks have come, and in many cases the repairs are proving to be very expensive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the decades, white brick buildings — those wedding-cake-shape stalwarts of postwar living — have fallen out of favor and in again among residents, architects and preservationists. Now some of them are falling apart.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/04/nyregion/APPRAISAL2/APPRAISAL2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/04/nyregion/APPRAISAL2/APPRAISAL2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/04/nyregion/APPRAISAL2/APPRAISAL2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Covered in White Brick, and Showing Their Age', 'kicker': 'The Appraisal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Covered in White Brick, And Showing Their Age', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brick and Tile', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-03T18:31:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Diane Cardwell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Diane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cardwell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5aea034-4d77-5d13-b41d-3dee318b064f', 'word_count': 961, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5aea034-4d77-5d13-b41d-3dee318b064f'}, {'abstract': 'A breast cancer patient endures an uncomfortable and embarrassing security pat-down after an airport imaging machine detected tissue expanders that were part of her reconstructive surgery.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/airport-pat-down-for-breast-cancer-patient/', 'snippet': 'A breast cancer patient endures an uncomfortable and embarrassing security pat-down after an airport imaging machine detected tissue expanders that were part of her reconstructive surgery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tightened security at airports has created new problems for medical patients, who may be subjected to embarrassing public pat-downs after imaging machines detect devices or implants related to their health.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Airport Pat-Down for Breast Cancer Patient', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Airport Security', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Breast Cancer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transportation Security Administration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-03T18:39:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c78ea75-1847-5bd0-a904-3a6aba4fc58b', 'word_count': 784, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c78ea75-1847-5bd0-a904-3a6aba4fc58b'}, {'abstract': 'Even during the baseball playoffs, talk radio favors football.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/a-tale-of-two-cities-talking/', 'snippet': 'Even during the baseball playoffs, talk radio favors football.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On sports talk radio, the N.F.L. is king, even during the baseball playoffs, and even in baseball playoff cities. That may not be quite as true in New York: with two baseball teams, one great and one awful, there is a steady supply of simultaneous gloating and grousing, and the Yankees are perfectly capable of keeping the spotlight away from the Giants and Jets. But drive 90 miles south, or head out to the Midwest, and pro football’s dominance becomes clear: who cares if we won the World Series yesterday, I am calling to demand that we give the backup quarterback a shot!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tale of Two Cities Talking', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cataldi, Angelo', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Didinger, Ray', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Griffin, Robert', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moore, Matt', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reid, Andy', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodgers, Aaron', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Verlander, Justin', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vick, Michael', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Russell', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baylor University', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Detroit Lions', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Detroit Red Wings', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations