ld’s forests starts to look precarious, scientists do not really have the capability they need to monitor the problems.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A year ago, I was sitting in a room overlooking the Pacific Ocean with a man named Ralph Keeling. The son of Charles David Keeling, who had discovered the trend of rising carbon dioxide in the atmosphere in the 1950s, Ralph Keeling had become a famous climate scientist in his own right. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dying Forests: How Bad Is It, Really?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forest and Brush Fires', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insects', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snow and Snowstorms', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Forest Service', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scripps Institution of Oceanography', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Geological Survey', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'British Columbia (Canada)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Western States (US)', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-03T19:42:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Gillis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'midd