in evolution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/science/04slime.html', 'snippet': 'In a study of slime molds, scientists are learning more about how they cooperate, which ties into some of the deepest questions in evolution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most of the aliens that come out of Hollywood don’t really look alien at all. They may have pizza-size eyes or roachlike antennae, but their oddities are draped on a familiar humanoid frame.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/04/science/04SLIM_SPAN/04SLIM_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 328, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/04/science/04SLIM_SPAN/04SLIM_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 328}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/04/science/04SLIM_SPAN/04SLIM1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 328, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/04/science/04SLIM_SPAN/04SLIM_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/04/science/04SLIM_SPAN/04SLIM_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Can Answers to Evolution Be Found in Slime?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Can Answers to Evolution Be Found in Slime?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slime Mold', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evolution', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-