, 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Financial District (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T00:54:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Ross Sorkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'Ross', 'lastname': 'Sorkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e58071cb-e392-5154-aa57-9c6bf7cb7fad', 'word_count': 964, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e58071cb-e392-5154-aa57-9c6bf7cb7fad'}, {'abstract': 'Despite all the hoopla surrounding the Apple announcement, more people buy smartphones running Google’s Android operating system than buy Apple phones.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/technology/apple-iphone-faces-divided-loyalties.html', 'snippet': 'Despite all the hoopla surrounding the Apple announcement, more people buy smartphones running Google’s Android operating system than buy Apple phones.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — As soon as Apple unveils its highly anticipated new version of the iPhone on Tuesday, millions of people are likely to start plotting how to be among the first to buy it. But millions more may be considering a competitor — an Android phone.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/04/business/APPLE/APPLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/04/business/APPLE/APPLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/04/business/APPLE/APPLE-th