ttps://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/sports/baseball/confident-burnett-puts-uneven-year-behind-him.html', 'snippet': 'The suspension of Game 1 upset the Yankees’ pitching plans and compelled them to turn to the erratic A. J. Burnett.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — This may not bring much comfort to the segment of Yankees fandom that covers its eyes whenever A. J. Burnett walks to the mound. On Monday, before a 5-4 loss in Game 3 put the Yankees on the brink of elimination, Burnett summarized his task as the starter for Game 4: ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/04/sports/SubYBurnett/SubYBurnett-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 737, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/04/sports/SubYBurnett/SubYBurnett-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/04/sports/SubYBurnett/SubYBurnett-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Confident Burnett Puts Uneven Year Behind Him', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Running Out of Options: Confident Burnett Puts Uneven Year Behind Him', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burnett, A J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Detroit Tigers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sabathia, C C', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T00:58:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pat Borzi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pat', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borzi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/efe79eb4-d176-5d3e-848e-7476948e1adf', 'word_count': 696, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efe79eb4-d176-5d3e-848e-7476948e1adf'}, {'abstract': 'You’re better off not meeting your personal piñatas — you’d run the risk of being pleasantly surprised.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/03/when-harry-should-avoid-meeting-sally/', 'snippet': 'You’re better off not meeting your personal piñatas — you’d run the risk of being pleasantly surprised.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A philosopher I know told me that once, when he was explaining his views on a contested matter to a group of his peers, he was met with this objection: “But that sounds like Stanley Fish.” I would bet that no one else in the room had ever met me and that few if any had ever read me. It is just that for many in his corner of the discipline, “Stanley Fish” is a placeholder for ideas you don’t want to be associated with. Saying of someone that he sounds like Stanley Fish is a quick and easy way of refuting him. You might say that it takes the place of an argument or, perhaps, that it is an argument all by itself and one that can be conveniently made in shorthand. (It’s not quite ad hominem; it’s ad hominem backed up by reasons that don’t even have to be given.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Harry Should Avoid Meeting Sally', 'kicker': 'Stanley Fish', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T01:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stanley Fish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stanley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1db6ef1-71d6-5d17-b60f-c5d517a5a855', 'word_count': 911, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1db6ef1-71d6-5d17-b60f-c5d517a5a855'}, {'abstract': 'Investigators from the Government Accountability Office said Medicare officials had been slow to recognize and act on the evidence of abuse, which is to be presented at a Senate hearing on Tuesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/health/policy/04medicare.html', 'snippet': 'Investigators from the Government Accountability Office said Medicare officials had been slow to recognize and act on the evidence of abuse, which is to be presented at a Senate hearing on Tuesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Medicare is subsidizing drug abuse by thousands of beneficiaries who shop around for doctors and fill prescriptions for huge quantities of painkillers and other narcotics far exceeding what any patient could safely use, Congressional investigators say in a new report.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Report on Medicare Cites Prescription Drug Abuse', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Report on Medicare Cites Prescription Drug Abuse', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Government Accountability Office', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronic Health Records', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 