jor': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T12:32:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ab2fe1f-1532-52dc-8e63-f904ca0951d8', 'word_count': 225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ab2fe1f-1532-52dc-8e63-f904ca0951d8'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: Political ploys in Russia could almost serve as an instruction manual for the leadership at either end of New York’s City Hall. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/like-putin-like-bloomberg/', 'snippet': 'The Day: Political ploys in Russia could almost serve as an instruction manual for the leadership at either end of New York’s City Hall. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Political ploys under way in Russia could almost serve as an instruction manual for the leadership at either end of New York’s City Hall. You have to slap your forehead in wonder that the New Yorkers didn’t think up comparable high jinks for themselves.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Like Putin, Like Bloomberg', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quinn, Christine C', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T12:44:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fac4ca10-2200-53d6-af76-1a3c4fe51b16', 'word_count': 1034, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fac4ca10-2200-53d6-af76-1a3c4fe51b16'}, {'abstract': 'Why militant atheists love biblical literalism.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/why-atheists-need-fundamentalists/', 'snippet': 'Why militant atheists love biblical literalism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve written before about the interesting symbiosis that exists between militant atheism and religious fundamentalism — the way the Richard Dawkinses of the world are always eager to insist that a cartoonish figure like Pat Robertson represents the truest form of Christian faith, and that any believer who disagrees with Robertson’s pronouncements on Haiti or Hurricane Katrina obviously doesn’t understand his own religion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Atheists Need Fundamentalists', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T12:51:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86cac02d-5dc8-5fe6-9ad7-285aef94cccf', 'word_count': 1244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86cac02d-5dc8-5fe6-9ad7-285aef94cccf'}, {'abstract': 'Hoping to increase the number of visitors to New York City during the usually slow period after the December holidays, the city’s tourism agency announced a partnership with the city of Seoul and Korean Air.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/promoting-more-visits-between-korea-and-new-york/', 'snippet': 'Hoping to increase the number of visitors to New York City during the usually slow period after the December holidays, the city’s tourism agency announced a partnership with the city of Seoul and Korean Air.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Hoping to increase the number of visitors to New York City during the usually slow period after the December holidays, the city’s tourism agency announced a partnership with the city of Seoul and Korean Air on Tuesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Promoting More Visits Between Korea and New York', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Korean Air Lines', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T12:58:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGeehan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGeehan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30a77730-2423-52b4-bd03-7199684ee736', 'word_count': 394, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30a77730-2423-52b4-bd03-7199684ee736'}, {'abstract': 'Thakoon’s recent spring collection was inspired by his trip to the country’s northern mountain villages.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/thailand-in-technicolor/', 'snippet': 'Thakoon’s recent spring collection was inspired by his trip to the country’s northern mountain villages.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Officially, the designer Thakoon Panichgul (above left), of Thakoon, is an adviser to Thailand’s ministry of commerce. But this summer he put business aside and spent two weeks looking for inspiration in the country’s northern mountain villages near Chiang Mai. He found it in the bright, hand-woven and embroidered silk and cotton ikats of the Karen and Lua tribes — a vibrant palette similar to the saturated red, turquoise, mustard and pink yarns in his recent spring collection. “There is something so special about these communities of grandmothers,” Panichgul says. “It’s really a dying art. Each tribe’s ikat pattern is based on memory.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thailand in Technicolor', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T13:00:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc7db40a-b221-5df0-b9bd-a3b96a335dbc', 'word_count': 103, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc7db40a-b221-5df0-b9bd-a3b96a335dbc'}, {'abstract': 'One reason that the United States should embrace such research, the report suggests, is the threat of unilateral action by another country.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/geoengineering-time-to-wake-up-panel-warns/', 'snippet': 'One reason that the United States should embrace such research, the report suggests, is the threat of unilateral action by another country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With political action on curbing greenhouse gases stalled in Congress, Cornelia Dean reports that a panel is recommending that the government begin researching the possibility of directly manipulating the Earth’s climate to lower the temperature. The Bipartisan Policy Center, which created the panel, plans a webcast discussion on the idea at 10 E.D.T. on Tuesday. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Geoengineering? Time to Wake Up, Panel Warns', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T13:10:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb924dda-17a4-5a18-9690-372e54a88bf8', 'word_count': 56, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb924dda-17a4-5a18-9690-372e54a88bf8'}, {'abstract': 'The study also finds different “reproductive messages” in various genres of music.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/this-just-in-study-shows-songs-about-sex-are-hot-sellers/', 'snippet': 'The study also finds different “reproductive messages” in various genres of music.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Psychologists sometimes have a way of proving what you always suspected was true. A recent study of popular song lyrics done at the State University of New York in Albany shows not only that messages about sexual relationships dominate the songs at the top of the charts, but also that songs about sex sell better than other songs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Just In: Study Shows Songs About Sex Are Hot Sellers', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State University of New York', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALBANY (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T13:21:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f2ce448-01c9-57b5-b808-401599bf6c90', 'word_count': 447, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f2ce448-01c9-57b5-b808-401599bf6c90'}, {'abstract': 'Using the net wisely in a health crisis, new iPhone faces stiff competition, odometer tampering rising on off-lease cars and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/tuesday-reading-using-the-net-wisely-in-a-health-crisis/', 'snippet': 'Using the net wisely in a health crisis, new iPhone faces stiff competition, odometer tampering rising on off-lease cars and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tuesday Reading: Using the Net Wisely in a Health Crisis', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T13:30:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/38ffc76b-8192-5d46-a275-8f2e80267fe2', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38ffc76b-8192-5d46-a275-8f2e80267fe2'}, {'abstract': 'A hopeful report on European banks prompted a late rally after the Standard & Poor’s 500-stock index officially traded in bear-market territory much of the day.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/business/daily-stock-market-activity.html', 'snippet': 'A hopeful report on European banks prompted a late rally after the Standard & Poor’s 500-stock index officially traded in bear-market territory much of the day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A late frenzy on Wall Street led stocks to a sharp increase on Tuesday, even as fears over a major banking crisis in Europe mounted along with expectations that Greece could soon default on its debt. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stocks Rally After Report Europe Might Recapitalize Banks', 'kicker': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Anticipating a Brutal Fallout: A Chance of Bailouts Ignites Final-Hour U.S. Stock Rally', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T13:41:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Brustein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brustein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/650a3dc6-db85-506d-bfe7-8dbb15fe1dc6', 'word_count': 829, 'uri': 'nyt://article/650a3dc6-db85-506d-bfe7-8dbb15fe1dc6'}, {'abstract': 'A press conference may settle the question of Gov. Chris Christie’s political future.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/christie-to-make-1-p-m-announcement/', 'snippet': 'A press conference may settle the question of Gov. Chris Christie’s political future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chris Christie, the Republican governor of New Jersey, has scheduled a 1 p.m. news conference for Tuesday afternoon in Trenton to announce his decision about a possible presidential run.\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Christie to Make 1 P.M. Announcement', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christie, Christopher J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Trenton (NJ)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T13:45:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1064f75b-34c5-5703-91af-8a5e52d34bc2', 'word_count': 92, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1064f75b-34c5-5703-91af-8a5e52d34bc2'}, {'abstract': 'Delta Airlines recently announced that it would probably drop or reduce air service to 24 smaller cities, but Hibbing officials say the flights are important to them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/us/from-hibbing-minn-a-plea-for-planes-to-keep-flying.html', 'snippet': 'Delta Airlines recently announced that it would probably drop or reduce air service to 24 smaller cities, but Hibbing officials say the flights are important to them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Delta Airlines recently announced that it would probably drop or reduce commercial air service to 24 smaller cities in the United States because the flights are often half-full — or even less — and lose millions of dollars a year. Delta, under an agreement with the federal government, is the only carrier to serve the cities, which rely on the flights to stay connected to the rest of the country. Without the service, they fear isolation, and in some cases, economic calamity. Butte, Mont.; Muscle Shoals, Ala.; Waterloo, Iowa; Pierre, S.D.; Greenville and Tupelo, Miss.; Sault Sainte Marie, Mich.; and Hibbing, Minn., are among the cities where the flights are threatened. Residents of Hibbing, population 16,000, and the heart of Minnesota’s Iron Range — where iron-ore mining is the predominant industry — are particularly concerned. The city’s mayor, Rick Cannata, and Patty Shafer, a city councilor, discuss:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Hibbing, Minn., a Plea for Planes to Keep Flying', 'kicker': 'Asked and Answered', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Hibbing, Minn., a Plea for Planes to Keep Flying', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Delta Air Lines Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Minnesota', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T13:48:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Timothy Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ed2c557-4760-5081-81fc-1f01487eca6c', 'word_count': 776, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ed2c557-4760-5081-81fc-1f01487eca6c'}, {'abstract': 'A spate of infections, leaving some patients blind, has caused some physicians to choose the more expensive Lucentis for eye disease in the elderly.', 'web_url': 'https://prescriptions.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/doctors-grow-wary-of-avastin-for-eye-treatment/', 'snippet': 'A spate of infections, leaving some patients blind, has caused some physicians to choose the more expensive Lucentis for eye disease in the elderly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dr. Robert Feig, a retina specialist in Brooklyn and the Bronx, says he wants to save patients and Medicare money by treating eye diseases with a drug that costs $50 a dose, rather than one that costs $2,000 a dose.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Doctors Grow Wary of Avastin for Eye Treatment', 'kicker': 'Prescriptions', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Avastin (Drug)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eyes and Eyesight', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Macular Degeneration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Genentech Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Veterans Affairs Department', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T13:51:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Pollack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pollack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3f24ffa-de1b-5c74-b580-602a27c1fd69', 'word_count': 970, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3f24ffa-de1b-5c74-b580-602a27c1fd69'}, {'abstract': 'In his 18 years at Pinckney Community High School, Jim Darga, the principal, said, the homecoming queen had always been crowned at halftime of the school’s football game. Never before, though, had she had to be summoned from the team’s locker room.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/queen-of-the-football-field/', 'snippet': 'In his 18 years at Pinckney Community High School, Jim Darga, the principal, said, the homecoming queen had always been crowned at halftime of the school’s football game. Never before, though, had she had to be summoned from the team’s locker room...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Four decades ago the federal education law called Title IX opened the door for girls to participate in high school and college athletics. But even the creators of that landmark legislation probably didn’t imagine the homecoming queen kicking the winning field goal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Queen of the Football Field', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T13:59:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf2703d9-f0ab-56bd-9965-529e3899ff43', 'word_count': 201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf2703d9-f0ab-56bd-9965-529e3899ff43'}, {'abstract': 'T’s Online Editor Jane Herman talks to the sound illustrator Michel Gaubert.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/morning-t-michel-gaubert/', 'snippet': 'T’s Online Editor Jane Herman talks to the sound illustrator Michel Gaubert.', 'lead_paragraph': 'T’s Online Editor Jane Herman talks to the sound illustrator Michel Gaubert about soundtracking fashion shows, finding music to match the height of a heel and why he’s always wearing sunglasses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning T | Michel Gaubert', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trade Shows and Fairs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lagerfeld, Karl', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chanel SA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T14:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By T Magazine', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dc82d2a-ac69-5e18-bb7c-0c98af4887f2', 'word_count': 41, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dc82d2a-ac69-5e18-bb7c-0c98af4887f2'}, {'abstract': 'Why did the controlling family accept a lower offer? And why did the board not question that decision?', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/a-strange-lesson-from-renaissance-learning/', 'snippet': 'Why did the controlling family accept a lower offer? And why did the board not question that decision?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Something weird is happening in Wisconsin. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Strange Lesson From Renaissance Learning', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Equity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Renaissance Learning Incorporated', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T14:29:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Davidoff Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Davidoff', 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/550ae7fb-6678-53b4-b5fe-7cba06f28924', 'word_count': 1378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/550ae7fb-6678-53b4-b5fe-7cba06f28924'}, {'abstract': 'My sister, 82, and I, 65, are visiting Edinburgh in late October to visit her granddaughter who is studying at the university. We have visited before and have seen the major sights. Can you give suggestions for off-the-beaten-track treasures and treks? We both love history and share the maiden name of Duncan — so anything that links us to the “homeland” is a lure. We recognize the weather will probably be challenging, but we are intrepid!Sandy Holmes, Shaker Heights, Ohio', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/qa-an-autumn-trip-to-edinburgh/', 'snippet': 'My sister, 82, and I, 65, are visiting Edinburgh in late October to visit her granddaughter who is studying at the university. We have visited before and have seen the major sights. Can you give suggestions for off-the-beaten-track treasures and t...', 'lead_paragraph': ' My sister, 82, and I, 65, are visiting Edinburgh in late October to visit her granddaughter who is studying at the university. We have visited before and have seen the major sights. Can you give suggestions for off-the-beaten-track treasures and treks? We both love history and share the maiden name of Duncan — so anything that links us to the “homeland” is a lure. We recognize the weather will probably be challenging, but we are intrepid!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: An Autumn Trip to Edinburgh', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Folk Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Edinburgh (Scotland)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scotland', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T14:31:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ccebb001-1eff-5dbd-ad2c-77203e7066db', 'word_count': 626, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ccebb001-1eff-5dbd-ad2c-77203e7066db'}, {'abstract': 'On Oct. 4, 1957, the Soviet Union put the first artificial satellite, Sputnik I, into the Earth’s orbit, signifying the birth of the Space Age.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/october-4-1957-soviet-union-launches-sputnik-satellite/', 'snippet': 'On Oct. 4, 1957, the Soviet Union put the first artificial satellite, Sputnik I, into the Earth’s orbit, signifying the birth of the Space Age.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Oct. 4, 1957, the Soviet Union put the first artificial satellite, Sputnik I, into the Earth’s orbit, signifying the birth of the Space Age. The spherical satellite, which was about the size of a beach ball and weighed 184 pounds, was able to orbit the Earth in 98 minutes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oct. 4, 1957 | Soviet Union Launches Sputnik Satellite', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T14:38:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2a7c65a-58bf-5ad5-99b4-a5e008112dd9', 'word_count': 440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2a7c65a-58bf-5ad5-99b4-a5e008112dd9'}, {'abstract': 'Panic reaches the Yankees faithful as they reach the brink in the divisional playoff series against the Tigers, while the Rays make their home on the brink (again) against the Rangers.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/leading-off-panic-in-yankeeland/', 'snippet': 'Panic reaches the Yankees faithful as they reach the brink in the divisional playoff series against the Tigers, while the Rays make their home on the brink (again) against the Rangers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For those outside the greater New York and Boston rooting areas, the past week has been an extended festival of great amusement. First, we got to watch an overflow of Red Sox panic, which is unlike any other kind of panic in its gloom and reverberating echoes of Bill Buckner, and now we get full-on Yankees panic. Just a week after they seemed to rule the universe as the Red Sox suffered through their epic collapse, the Yankees are down, two games to one, to the plucky Tigers and facing an elimination game pitched by — and here’s where the real panic comes in — A.J. Burnett.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Leading Off: Panic in Yankeeland', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burnett, A J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Girardi, Joe', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons',