'headline': {'main': 'The Neon Lights Are Bright on Proskauer', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bettman, Gary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stern, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Times Square and 42nd Street (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T17:25:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/12151fa3-85ed-51dd-9fb3-bb86c3c243be', 'word_count': 589, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12151fa3-85ed-51dd-9fb3-bb86c3c243be'}, {'abstract': 'Some of the most reputable academics who favor limited immigration argue that legislation to require all employers to verify the status of prospective employers is vital if the goal is to limit the economic impact of illegal immigration. But an approach that seems simple in concept would be anything but simple in its consequences.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/another-immigration-misconception/', 'snippet': 'Some of the most reputable academics who favor limited immigration argue that legislation to require all employers to verify the status of prospective employers is vital if the goal is to limit the economic impact of illegal immigration. But an ap...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yesterday I addressed three misconceptions swirling around immigration and the latest story in our series, Immigration Upended, but one question seems to keep arriving in my inbox and Twitter feed: Why not just go after employers? If businesses can be regulated and punished into keeping their payrolls free of illegal immigrants,