 {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'St Louis (Mo)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T19:29:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Keh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9b5b9e7-c782-5b03-a7af-683306258bb4', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9b5b9e7-c782-5b03-a7af-683306258bb4'}, {'abstract': 'If Bank of America were to put Countrywide Financial in bankruptcy, bondholders would still be guaranteed to be paid.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/how-bondholders-would-fare-in-a-countrywide-bankruptcy/', 'snippet': 'If Bank of America were to put Countrywide Financial in bankruptcy, bondholders would still be guaranteed to be paid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the past weeks, it has become quite common to speculate that Bank of America might solve its “Countrywide problem” by putting the mortgage unit into bankruptcy to escape some liabilities and litigation losses. Bloomberg News has reported on the idea, and so has Dealbook’s Deal Professor.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Bondholders Would Fare in a Countrywide Bankruptcy', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of America Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Countrywide Financial Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T19:29:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen J. Lubben', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Lubben', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0a7bb2f-1b28-5a18-95e8-3c3977143e40', 'word_count': 387, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0a7bb2f-1b28-5a18-95e8-3c3977143e40'}, {'abstract': 'Four other people were injured, at least one critically, when the chopper tried to land at the East 34th Street Heliport, then pulled back up and spun into the river, dropping 40 feet to the muddy bottom.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/helicopter-crashes-in-east-river/', 'snippet': 'Four other people were injured, at least one critically, when the chopper tried to land at the East 34th Street Heliport, then pulled back up and spun into the river, dropping 40 feet to the muddy bottom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A helicopter crashed into the East River off 34th Street at 3:22 p.m. on Tuesday, killing a woman passenger and injuring three other passengers, at least one of them critically, the authorities said. The pilot escaped unharmed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Live Blog: Woman Dies in Helicopter Crash in East River', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Airplane Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Helicopters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rescues', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East River (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T19:34:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16426d20-1653-5de1-885a-8f020c8cf52b', 'word_count': 1439, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16426d20-1653-5de1-885a-8f020c8cf52b'}, {'abstract': 'An understandable decision adds to an institutional failure.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/exit-christie/', 'snippet': 'An understandable decision adds to an institutional failure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though I urged the New Jersey governor to consider running for president and liked his chances if he did, I can understand why Chris Christie has decided against a bid. The hour is late, the hurdles are significant, and if his candidacy crashed and burned Christie would have probably thrown away his still-young political career — and with it, his ability to fundamentally reform a long-misgoverned state. As Reihan Salam put it the other day, “New Jersey needs its governor,” and a Republican Party that believes in the importance of federalism shouldn’t necessarily be in the business of promoting its most successful state officials to the national level before their statehouse work has come to fruition. (See Palin, Sarah, for a cautionary tale on that front.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Exit Christie', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T19:37:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39023b5c-9773-5b5a-b4a7-7d2c5507dd34', 'word_count': 368, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39023b5c-9773-5b5a-b4a7-7d2c5507dd34'}, {'abstract': 'Afghanistan officials signaled on Tuesday they had little interest, for now, in healing a rift with Pakistan, their eastern neighbor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/world/asia/afghanistan-curries-favor-with-india-and-denigrates-pakistan.html', 'snippet': 'Afghanistan officials signaled on Tuesday they had little interest, for now, in healing a rift with Pakistan, their eastern neighbor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — Fuming over what they have called the Pakistani role in exporting terrorism across the border, Afghan officials signaled on Tuesday that they had little interest, for now, in healing a rift with Pakistan, their eastern neighbor.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/05/world/5afghanistan-image2/5afghanistan-image2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 716, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/05/world/5afghanistan-image2/5afghanistan-image2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/05/world/5afghanistan-image2/5afghanistan-image2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Afghanistan Favors India and Denigrates Pakistan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Afghanistan’s Ties to India Grow Stronger, Its Rift With Pakistan Widens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T19:45:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Healy and Alissa J. Rubin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Alissa', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Rubin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4cfa3b8-0f14-5dc0-be85-77494e8eb532', 'word_count': 679, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4cfa3b8-0f14-5dc0-be85-77494e8eb532'}, {'abstract': 'The successful, and lucrative, rerelease of “The Lion King” in 3-D has convinced Disney to go the same route with three other favorites: “Finding Nemo,” “Monsters Inc.” and “The Little Mermaid.”', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/more-disney-characters-to-resurface-in-3-d/', 'snippet': 'The successful, and lucrative, rerelease of “The Lion King” in 3-D has convinced Disney to go the same route with three other favorites: “Finding Nemo,” “Monsters Inc.” and “The Little Mermaid.”', 'lead_paragraph': ' Nemo, Sulley and Ariel are being converted into 3-D and dispatched to multiplexes as Disney seeks to squeeze new profits from its old animated films.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Disney Characters to Resurface in 3-D', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Three-Dimensional Devices and Effects', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Walt Disney Company', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T19:48:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0786bc0-6a49-5027-a859-59e40c5cc1ce', 'word_count': 144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0786bc0-6a49-5027-a859-59e40c5cc1ce'}, {'abstract': 'Don Kelly, the only active major league player who has played all nine positions, played one to perfection in Game 3 against the Yankees.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/sports/baseball/kelly-the-tigers-mr-fix-it-shows-his-versatility.html', 'snippet': 'Don Kelly, the only active major league player who has played all nine positions, played one to perfection in Game 3 against the Yankees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — The Detroit Tigers’ Don Kelly is prepared for almost anything, and everything, that could happen during a game.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/05/sports/baseball/05kelly/05kelly-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/05/sports/baseball/05kelly/05kelly-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/05/sports/baseball/05kelly/05kelly-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 813, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/05/sports/baseball/05kelly/05kelly-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/05/sports/baseball/05kelly/05kelly-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kelly, the Tigers’ Mr. Fix-It, Shows his Versatility', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kelly, the Tigers’ Mr. Fix-It, Shows his Versatility', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Detroit Tigers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leyland, Jim', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T19:52:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne C. Gerstner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Gerstner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39309b2d-e12f-5926-b78f-f57749a2c8b6', 'word_count': 789, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39309b2d-e12f-5926-b78f-f57749a2c8b6'}, {'abstract': 'The Vikings’ proposal for building a $1.1 billion N.F.L. stadium on a Superfund site in Arden Hills, Minn., with the public paying about 60 percent of the cost, has faced stiff opposition.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/sports/football/fiscal-worries-cloud-vikings-push-for-publicly-financed-stadium.html', 'snippet': 'The Vikings’ proposal for building a $1.1 billion N.F.L. stadium on a Superfund site in Arden Hills, Minn., with the public paying about 60 percent of the cost, has faced stiff opposition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ARDEN HILLS, Minn. — For decades, N.F.L. teams, including those in Arizona, Ohio and Texas, have persuaded taxpayers to help them cover most of the costs of their new stadiums. The Minnesota Vikings, whose roof on their current home, the Metrodome, collapsed spectacularly last winter, are no different. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/05/sports/football/05stadium-pic2/05stadium-pic2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fiscal and Political Shifts Cloud Stadium Push', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fiscal and Political Shifts Cloud Stadium Push', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Minnesota Vikings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Minneapolis (Minn)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stadiums and Arenas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T19:54:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bc74a63-8143-53a7-8bd3-107709728d39', 'word_count': 1415, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bc74a63-8143-53a7-8bd3-107709728d39'}, {'abstract': 'While the initial curiosity behind a Charlie Sheen-less “Two and a Half Men” seems to be fading, the CBS comedy was still able to reach 17.3 million viewers on Monday night, narrowly beating ABC’s “Dancing With the Stars” (16.5 million) as the most-watched program.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/no-sheen-no-problem-for-two-and-a-half-men/', 'snippet': 'While the initial curiosity behind a Charlie Sheen-less “Two and a Half Men” seems to be fading, the CBS comedy was still able to reach 17.3 million viewers on Monday night, narrowly beating ABC’s “Dancing With the Stars” (16.5 million) as the mos...', 'lead_paragraph': 'While the initial curiosity behind a Charlie Sheen-less “Two and a Half Men” seems to be fading, the CBS comedy was still able to reach 17.3 million viewers on Monday night, narrowly beating ABC’s “Dancing With the Stars” (16.5 million) as the most-watched program. On Fox, “Terra Nova” retained 100 percent of its premiere 18-to-49-year-old audience, a fantastic sign for the expensive sci-fi program. Meanwhile, the fall season had its first network casualty on Tuesday when NBC announced that it had canceled “The Playboy Club” after its third episode fell to just 3.4 million viewers. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Sheen, No Problem for ‘Two and a Half Men’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheen, Charlie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC INC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T19:57:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/61383b92-de5c-5ae6-ad1f-9c95437bc89c', 'word_count': 96, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61383b92-de5c-5ae6-ad1f-9c95437bc89c'}, {'abstract': 'The photographer turned graffiti artist discusses the opening of his first two-man show in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/the-lowdown-curtis-kulig/', 'snippet': 'The photographer turned graffiti artist discusses the opening of his first two-man show in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is not a place on earth that Curtis Kulig doesn’t have friends. In the darkest little dive bars in Tokyo, there is evidence that he has been there. In alleyways in Los Angeles that no one ventures down and high up above the rooftops in New York, his mark winks at you familiarly — his mark being the omnipresent two word cursive tag “Love Me” that litters metropolitan downtowns the world over in spray paint and endless stickers. A photographer turned graffiti artist, Kulig is SoHo’s most unexpected nice guy. Known as equally for his sweet demeanor as for his public cry for affection (which he’s had to tone down lately), Kulig has successfully gone legit with commercial commissions like billboards for the makeup brand Bobbi Brown. This month, Kulig is trying his hand at fine art, with the opening of his first two-man show in New York, with the Los Angeles graffiti artist Skullphone.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Lowdown | Curtis Kulig', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T19:59:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By IO Tillett Wright', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Io', 'middlename': 'Tillett', 'lastname': 'Wright', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67d30246-ba96-5ea8-aa0e-075e764cee76', 'word_count': 573, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67d30246-ba96-5ea8-aa0e-075e764cee76'}, {'abstract': 'In the baseball playoffs, memorable characters include Nyjer Morgan, Matt Treanor, Yorvit Torrealba, and a tribute to the Cardinals that seems somewhat creepy.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/04/backup-catchers-a-scary-fountain-and-the-saga-of-tony-plush/', 'snippet': 'In the baseball playoffs, memorable characters include Nyjer Morgan, Matt Treanor, Yorvit Torrealba, and a tribute to the Cardinals that seems somewhat creepy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'All through the baseball playoffs, Mike Tanier will provide the Bats blog with daily updates on less-than-important developments from around the league. He vows to keep Phillies homerism to a minimum.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Backup Catchers, a Scary Fountain, and the Story of Tony Plush', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Andrus, Elvis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Corman, Roger', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Counsell, Craig', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hamilton, Josh', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morgan, Nyjer', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Portis, Clinton', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pujols, Albert', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Torrealba, Yorvit', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Washington, Ron', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Milwaukee Brewers', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MTV Networks', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Philadelphia Phillies', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'St Louis Cardinals', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'St Louis Rams', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tampa Bay Rays', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Texas Rangers', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'USA Today', 'rank': 18, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'St Louis (Mo)', 'rank': 19, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T19:59:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Tanier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd7b7cc1-2be4-5eee-b509-df331c54c4a3', 'word_count': 991, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd7b7cc1-2be4-5eee-b509-df331c54c4a3'}, {'abstract': 'Cassandra Wilson’s two-night engagement at the Rose Theater was a sort of career retrospective.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/arts/music/cassandra-wilson-at-the-rose-theater-review.html', 'snippet': 'Cassandra Wilson’s two-night engagement at the Rose Theater was a sort of career retrospective.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During one inspired stretch of her casually luxurious concert at the Rose Theater on Friday night, Cassandra Wilson sang material from her oldest and most recent Blue Note albums, released 17 years apart. A few of these were original songs, full of spirit talk and churning, viscous grooves. One was an adaptation of Charley Patton’s “Pony Blues,” soulful and self-assured. There was no demarcation between old and new, no sense of shifting gears. Ms. Wilson presented the songs as points in a continuum, scenes in a story yet unfinished, and maybe unfinishable. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/05/arts/05cassandra/05cassandra-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 342, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/05/arts/05cassandra/05cas