in, Earl Ray', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Underwood, Cecil H', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'West Virginia', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-04T22:09:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6aafe34e-b573-5ddd-bbfc-4e892b09647a', 'word_count': 769, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6aafe34e-b573-5ddd-bbfc-4e892b09647a'}, {'abstract': 'Caorunn gin from Scotland offers herbal complexity, thanks to native botanicals like heather, rowan berry and dandelion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/dining/a-bonnie-glass-of-highlands-gin.html', 'snippet': 'Caorunn gin from Scotland offers herbal complexity, thanks to native botanicals like heather, rowan berry and dandelion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gins from Scotland have distinctive personality. Hendrick’s, with its clean whisper of cucumber, is a case in point. Now there is Caorunn (pronounced ka-ROON), which offers uncommon herbal complexity, thanks to native botanicals like heather, rowan berry and dandelion, along with the more typical herbs and spices used for gin. A touch of sweetness lingers. The spirit is made in a malt distillery in the Highlands, using some equipment for treating the botanicals that dates back nearly 100 years. This gin seems to have a particular affinity for grapefruit, and a mere splash of the juice is all it needs on the rocks, though a long drink with grapefruit juice and a dash of maraschino liqueur is another winner.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia':