rticle', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ded3ec55-551e-5521-8c99-f205498bd419', 'word_count': 345, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ded3ec55-551e-5521-8c99-f205498bd419'}, {'abstract': 'Two advice classics — Dale Carnegie’s “How to Win Friends and Influence People” and “Emily Post’s Etiquette” — have been updated for the era of Facebook and Google Plus.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/books/books-of-the-times-classic-advice-please-leave-well-enough-alone.html', 'snippet': 'Two advice classics — Dale Carnegie’s “How to Win Friends and Influence People” and “Emily Post’s Etiquette” — have been updated for the era of Facebook and Google Plus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dale Carnegie’s “How to Win Friends and Influence People,” which turns 75 this year, has sold more than 30 million copies and continues to be a best seller. The book, a paean to integrity, good humor and warmth in the name of amicable capitalism, is as wholesome as a Norman Rockwell painting. It exists alongside Dr. Spock’s child-rearing guide, Strunk and White’s volume on literary style and Fannie Farmer’s cookbook as a classic expression of the American impulse toward self-improvement and reinvention. Testimonials to its effectiveness abound. It’s said that the only diploma that hangs in Warren Buffett’s office is his certificate from Dale Carnegie Training. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/05/books/BOOKS/BOOKS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 711, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/05/books/BOOKS/BOOKS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/05/books/BOOKS/BOOKS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Classic Advice: Please, Leave Well Enough Alone', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Classic Advice: Please, Leave Well Enough Alone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Post, Emily', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carnegie, Dale', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T05:14:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dwight Garner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dwight', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3a92ba5-f0b9-5328-970b-9fe2961c6844', 'word_count': 1203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3a92ba5-f0b9-5328-970b-9fe2961c6844'}, {'abstract': 'Instead of using the international break to work on the problems that have left them at the bottom of the Premier League, Blackburn Rovers flew to India for a commercial tour set up by its owners, Venky’s.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/blackburn-is-in-india-the-question-is-why/', 'snippet': 'Instead of using the international break to work on the problems that have left them at the bottom of the Premier League, Blackburn Rovers flew to India for a commercial tour set up by its owners, Venky’s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Under ordinary circumstances Steve