 they never imagined how long they’d be held captive – or who would ultimately save them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Taken by Pirates, Illustrated', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Somalia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hostages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Piracy at Sea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T09:48:00+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/b01c7242-2288-51e5-816d-8ae8b9514c4d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/b01c7242-2288-51e5-816d-8ae8b9514c4d'}, {'abstract': 'Search-engine map sites can do more for trip planning than just calculating directions.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/mapping-extras-worth-knowing-about/', 'snippet': 'Search-engine map sites can do more for trip planning than just calculating directions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Search-engine map sites, like those from Bing, Google and Yahoo, can do more for trip planning than just calculating directions. All three have a setting that shows you the current traffic conditions along the planned route to avoid jams.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tip of the Week: Mapping Extras Worth Knowing About', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Maps', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Search Engines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Maps', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yahoo! Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T09:53:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7250f12c-fb57-5087-9c90-a3f0ae220a80', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7250f12c-fb57-5087-9c90-a3f0ae220a80'}, {'abstract': '“Gráfika: 30 Artists of Young Spain,” an exhibition of urban art by artists born in the 1970s and ’80s, runs from Oct. 7 to Jan. 15 at the Instituto Cervantes in Madrid.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/young-artists-the-focus-at-madrid-show/', 'snippet': '“Gráfika: 30 Artists of Young Spain,” an exhibition of urban art by artists born in the 1970s and ’80s, runs from Oct. 7 to Jan. 15 at the Instituto Cervantes in Madrid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Drive along Spain’s highways long enough and you might pass the silhouette of a black bull that stands nearly 46 feet high. Originally an advertising campaign for the Osborne sherry company, the bull — there are actually a number of them across Spain — has since become a national symbol. In 2006, after almost 50 years since the first “Osborne bull” went up, the Madrid-based artist SpY, who has often been referred to as the Spanish Banksy, protested bullfighting by splattering one of the bulls with a huge splash of red paint.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Young Artists the Focus at Madrid Show', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Instituto Cervantes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Madrid (Spain)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T10:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Grace Duggan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Grace', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Duggan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/275590de-571d-5ecb-b7ff-6ef1e8a8ada7', 'word_count': 275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/275590de-571d-5ecb-b7ff-6ef1e8a8ada7'}, {'abstract': 'To raise funds, the Lane Museum in Nashville offered the public an opportunity to drive its cars in a daylong rally.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/hitting-the-tennessee-roads-in-rolling-museum-pieces/', 'snippet': 'To raise funds, the Lane Museum in Nashville offered the public an opportunity to drive its cars in a daylong rally.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NASHVILLE — The Lane Motor Museum is not sitting on its collection.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hitting the Tennessee Roads in Rolling Museum Pieces', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Citroen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fiat SpA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nashville (Tenn)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T10:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles McEwen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McEwen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b7b7454-3aaa-5523-b83b-d6b82d6fa27e', 'word_count': 637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b7b7454-3aaa-5523-b83b-d6b82d6fa27e'}, {'abstract': 'Jeff Z. Klein and Christopher Botta of the Times discuss the Islanders’ prospects for the 2011-12 season.', 'web_url': 'https://slapshot.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/2-on-1-do-the-islanders-have-the-defense-and-goaltending-to-make-the-playoffs/', 'snippet': 'Jeff Z. Klein and Christopher Botta of the Times discuss the Islanders’ prospects for the 2011-12 season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the first of three conversations previewing the coming seasons of the tri-state area’s hockey teams, hockey writers Jeff Z. Klein and Christopher Botta discuss the chances of the Islanders qualifying for the Stanley Cup playoffs for the first time since 2007.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '2-on-1: Do the Islanders Have the Defense and Goaltending to Make the Playoffs?', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dipietro, Rick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Montoya, Al', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Okposo, Kyle', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Snow, Garth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Islanders', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T10:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fefedc23-2808-5541-b6d0-c315317450e8', 'word_count': 911, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fefedc23-2808-5541-b6d0-c315317450e8'}, {'abstract': 'The bitter fight over prostate screening – and why it might be better not to know.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/magazine/can-cancer-ever-be-ignored.html', 'snippet': 'The bitter fight over prostate screening – and why it might be better not to know.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As chief medical and scientific officer of the American Cancer Society,\xa0Otis Webb Brawley — who is also a professor of oncology and epidemiology at Emory University — is the public face of the cancer establishment. He operates in a world of similarly high-achieving, multiple-credentialed, respectable professionals, where insults tend to be delivered, stiletto-style, in scientific language that lay people aren’t meant to understand. So it can be more than a little jarring to hear, for example, James Mohler, chairman of the urology department and associate director of the Roswell Park Cancer Institute in Buffalo, say of his friend: “I have known Otis for over 20 years. He doesn’t come off as being ignorant or stupid, but when it comes to prostate-cancer screening, he must not be as intelligent as he seems.” Or Skip Lockwood, the head of Zero, a prostate-cancer patient advocacy group, charge that Brawley is more concerned about saving men’s sex lives than about saving the men themselves.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09prostate2/mag-09Prostate-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09prostate2/09prostate2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09prostate2/09prostate2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwid