donated hundreds of thousands of dollars from sales to charities helping cancer patients. The founder, Lee Rhodes, a cancer survivor, says the idea originated with a glass vessel her then husband made for her, which she says gave her a soothing, peaceful feeling.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Glassybaby Is Trying to Win Over New Yorkers', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T11:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Weed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/08ac8ac0-fb8e-53bc-a73d-2d8f84815d31', 'word_count': 882, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08ac8ac0-fb8e-53bc-a73d-2d8f84815d31'}, {'abstract': 'Paddling a kayak, feeding a pit fire or biking around on their own, the author’s sons exult in a rustic independence.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/for-3-boys-an-excellent-adventure/', 'snippet': 'Paddling a kayak, feeding a pit fire or biking around on their own, the author’s sons exult in a rustic independence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As some of you may know, my husband and I are in the midst of a yearlong adventure off the grid with our children in the Maine woods. I recently returned from playing hooky for a few days to attend the Peace Corps 50th-anniversary celebrations in Washington along with 5,000 other returned volunteers — just a fraction of the nearly quarter-million who have served our country since J.F.K. challenged college students on a freezing campaign night in Michigan to devote their skills to helping people overseas.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'mu