glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Irene (2011)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vermont', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T15:41:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michelle Higgins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michelle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Higgins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52f9b083-cd05-59e3-803b-06b9821ae2e5', 'word_count': 1125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52f9b083-cd05-59e3-803b-06b9821ae2e5'}, {'abstract': 'The Federal Reserve’s long-awaited report said that big banks had taken significant steps in deferring a greater portion of compensation and shifting more pay in the form of stock.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/business/fed-sees-progress-in-bank-compensation-plans.html', 'snippet': 'The Federal Reserve’s long-awaited report said that big banks had taken significant steps in deferring a greater portion of compensation and shifting more pay in the form of stock.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amid the uproar over bailouts and bonuses in the fall of 2009, the chief executives of the nation’s largest banks walked through the iron gates of the Federal Reserve Bank of New York and received a stern warning that their pay practices needed to be overhauled. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fed Reports Modest Progress in Bank Compensation Plans', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fed Reports Modest Progress in Bank Compensation Plans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executive Compensation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T15:42:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Dash', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dash', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f308d7b8-9c6a-5038-ab0a-ce13ba6a024c', 'word_count': 955, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f308d7b8-9c6a-5038-ab0a-ce13ba6a024c'}, {'abstract': 'A new comedy series, “Harve Karbo,” about a Los Angeles private investigator, would be the television project from Joel and Ethan Coen, the Academy Award-winning filmmakers of “Fargo” and “No Country for Old Men.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/coen-brothers-producing-comedy-pilot-for-fox/', 'snippet': 'A new comedy series, “Harve Karbo,” about a Los Angeles private investigator, would be the television project from Joel and Ethan Coen, the Academy Award-winning filmmakers of “Fargo” and “No Country for Old Men.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than 13 years and who knows how many White Russians after “The Big Lebowski,” the sibling filmmakers Joel and Ethan Coen are turning their attention to another brother shamus – not an Irish monk, but a fellow private investigator – for what could be their first television project. The Coens, the Academy Award-winning writer-directors of “No Country for Old Men” and “Fargo,” are collaborating with the “Cedar Rapids” screenwriter Phil Johnston on a television series called “Harve Karbo,” about another hapless aspiring Sam Spade. According to The Hollywood Reporter, the oddly specific series will chronicle “a touchy Los Angeles private investigator — and his deadbeat friends in El Segundo — whose cases frequently force him to cross paths with a who’s who of Hollywood,” and if that sounds at least a little Lebowski-esque, we can abide. The series, which the Coens will produce for Imagine Television and for which Mr. Johnston will write the script, has so far received a pilot commitment from Fox, which means there are still, you know, a lotta ins, a lotta outs, a lotta what-have-yous, before “Harve Karbo” appears on your screen.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Coen Brothers Producing Comedy Pilot for Fox', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Detectives (Private)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coen, Ethan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coen, Joel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T15:44:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81da95c3-667a-55cd-83d3-ea68f102d006', 'word_count': 188, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81da95c3-667a-55cd-83d3-ea68f102d006'}, {'abstract': 'Riskalyze aims to help you evaluate your risk tolerance for investments.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/new-site-seeks-to-analyze-investors-appetite-for-risk/', 'snippet': 'Riskalyze aims to help you evaluate your risk tolerance for investments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Would you rather be guaranteed that you’ll lose $489 on a $1,000 investment? Or, would you prefer a 50-50 chance of either losing all of it, or gaining $1,500?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Site Seeks to Analyze Investors’ Appetite for Risk', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T15:51:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/518ca7a4-2e29-5f5a-b3aa-327af34c55a1', 'word_count': 997, 'uri': 'nyt://article/518ca7a4-2e29-5f5a-b3aa-327af34c55a1'}, {'abstract': 'Secretary of Defense Leon E. Panetta said the example of the Libya war showed the need to cooperate on defense spending in order not to “hollow out” the NATO alliance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/world/panetta-exhorts-european-nato-members-to-share-defense-spending.html', 'snippet': 'Secretary of Defense Leon E. Panetta said the example of the Libya war showed the need to cooperate on defense spending in order not to “hollow out” the NATO alliance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BRUSSELS — With the Pentagon facing severe budget cuts, Secretary of Defense Leon E. Panetta asked NATO’s European members on Wednesday to heed the lessons of the Libya war and cooperate on much-needed defense spending in order “not to hollow out this alliance.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/06/world/06nato/06nato-articleLarge.j