r', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'The Threepenny Opera'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Toxic Dispatches From Weimar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Robert', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brecht, Bertolt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Berliner Ensemble', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Academy of Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T16:50:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/235686f3-da48-53de-8e67-aea13516bad7', 'word_count': 1377, 'uri': 'nyt://article/235686f3-da48-53de-8e67-aea13516bad7'}, {'abstract': 'Even a performance of “The Lion King,” where everyone is supportive, can be stressful.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/to-broadway-with-autism/', 'snippet': 'Even a performance of “The Lion King,” where everyone is supportive, can be stressful.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You may have read about the first of its kind “autism friendly” performance of “The Lion King” last weekend. As my colleague Erik Piepenburg described it on the ArtsBeat blog: ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Broadway, With Autism', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Autism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T16:54:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/55041dea-c897-56c5-96f2-61f9f0ca7b1e', 'word_count': 886, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55041dea-c897-56c5-96f2-61f9f0ca7b1e'}, {'abstract': 'The United Nations estimates that 1.6 million to 1.8 million people in developing countries die prematurely each year because of exposure to indoor emissions from inefficient cookstoves. Then there are the black carbon emissions, which hasten global warming.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/a-trek-for-cleaner-cookstoves/', 'snippet': 'The United Nations estimates that 1.6 million to 1.8 million people in developing countries die prematurely each year because of exposure to indoor emissions from inefficient cookstoves. Then there are the black carbon emissions, which hasten glob...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United Nations estimates that nearly half the world’s population, some three billion people in developing countries, cook over open wood fires or primitive stoves. The practice leads to persistent health problems and accounts for nearly 20 percent of global greenhouse gas emissions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Trek for Cleaner Cookstoves', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Clinton Global Initiative', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Global Alliance for Clean Cookstoves', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations Foundation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Diego (Calif)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T16:59:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Witkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Witkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/47ff6f95-c3be-5ece-a581-bc3656f32fd3', 'word_count': 568, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47ff6f95-c3be-5ece-a581-bc3656f32fd3'}, {'abstract': 'Ladbrokes, the British bookmaking chain, has added Bob Dylan’s name to the list of leading contenders for the Nobel Prize in literature, which will be announced Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/new-nobel-contender-is-blowin-in-the-wind/', 'snippet': 'Ladbrokes, the British bookmaking chain, has added Bob Dylan’s name to the list of leading contenders for the Nobel Prize in literature, which will be announced Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Predictions for the Nobel Prize in Literature tend to be all over the place, especially in the final hours before the winner is named. But Bob Dylan? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dylan Tangled Up in Nobel Prize Speculation', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nobel Prizes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dylan, Bob', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T17:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/446cc552-4623-514b-a8b8-17e9bf149c77', 'word_count': 172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/446cc552-4623-514b-a8b8-17e9bf149c77'}, {'abstract': 'Even Philadelphia public radio is chiming in with “Phillies Fever,” Curtis Granderson makes two thrilling catches for the Yankees and the collision between the Rays’ Sean Rodriguez and Rangers catcher Mike Napoli is worth a little analysis.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/going-retro-to-catch-phillies-fever-grandersons-catches-worth-a-replay/', 'snippet': 'Even Philadelphia public radio is chiming in with “Phillies Fever,” Curtis Granderson makes two thrilling catches for the Yankees and the collision between the Rays’ Sean Rodriguez and Rangers catcher Mike Napoli is worth a little analysis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'All through the baseball playoffs, Mike Tanier will provide the Bats blog with daily updates on less-than-important developments from around the league. He vows to keep Phillies homerism to a minimum.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Going Retro to Catch Phillies Fever; Granderson’s Catches Worth a Replay', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T17:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Tanier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/502efb4b-1473-5d06-a330-08db18696c80', 'word_count': 1132, 'uri': 'nyt://article/502efb4b-1473-5d06-a330-08db18696c80'}, {'abstract': 'Amber Heard, the face of Guess, may be Hollywood’s sultriest new star.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/styled-to-a-t-amber-heard/', 'snippet': 'Amber Heard, the face of Guess, may be Hollywood’s sultriest new star.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Trend', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Styled to a T | Amber Heard', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T17:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By T Magazine', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bd2d177-2f63-5d14-82a1-23e328624f7b', 'word_count': 82, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bd2d177-2f63-5d14-82a1-23e328624f7b'}, {'abstract': 'With only hours to go in an Arctic research cruise, scientists are collecting as many bits of information about seafloor geology as they can, even if it’s not yet clear how it will all fit together.', 'web_url': 'https://scientistatwork.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/scooping-up-data-as-the-clock-ticks/', 'snippet': 'With only hours to go in an Arctic research cruise, scientists are collecting as many bits of information about seafloor geology as they can, even if it’s not yet clear how it will all fit together.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bernard Coakley, a marine geologist with the University of Alaska at Fairbanks, writes from the Arctic Ocean, where his team is reconstructing the geologic history of the Chukchi Shelf and the Chukchi Borderland.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scooping Up Data as the Clock Ticks', 'kicker': 'Scientist at Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub