e': {'original': 'Nadia Sussman|Emily B. Hager', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nadia', 'middlename': 'Sussman', 'lastname': 'Emily', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/c0775525-59e8-55f9-9eea-229f2546469c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/c0775525-59e8-55f9-9eea-229f2546469c'}, {'abstract': 'Richard Serra is back in New York City making statements in vast swaths of steel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/arts/design/richard-serras-sculpture-at-gagosian-gallery-review.html', 'snippet': 'Richard Serra is back in New York City making statements in vast swaths of steel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At 71 the indefatigable sculptor Richard Serra is making some of the most intensely visceral work of his career. His latest sculptures in weatherproof steel are big enough to test the boundaries of the immense Gagosian Gallery on 24th Street in Chelsea, though their power has less to do with their sheer magnitude than with the way they carve up the space.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/06/arts/Sierra-1/Sierra-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/06/arts/Sierra-1/Sierra-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/06/arts/Sierra-1/Sierra-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/06/arts/Sierra-1/Sierra-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/06/arts/Sierra-1/Sierra-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Shockingly Orange, Invitingly Meandering, Immensely Imposing', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shockingly Orange, Invitingly Meandering, Immensely Imposing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gagosian Gallery', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Serra, Richard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T21:35:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bda77ff4-0bcd-5d69-8485-0dedbbd09253', 'word_count': 794, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bda77ff4-0bcd-5d69-8485-0dedbbd09253'}, {'abstract': 'Mikhail Baryshnikov has donated a cache of personal recordings, photographs, documents, letters and scrapbooks to the New York Public Library.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/arts/dance/baryshnikov-packs-up-his-memories-in-boxes.html', 'snippet': 'Mikhail Baryshnikov has donated a cache of personal recordings, photographs, documents, letters and scrapbooks to the New York Public Library.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the hundreds of video recordings that Mikhail Baryshnikov has collected over the decades are a handful that show him rehearsing with the titans of dance George Balanchine, Jerome Robbins, Martha Graham and Merce Cunningham. But perhaps the most treasured is a cloudy black-and-white clip of the 16-year-old Baryshnikov at a lesson with his revered teacher Alexander Pushkin at the Vaganova Academy of Russian Ballet in St. Petersburg.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/06/arts/JPMISHA1/JPMISHA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/06/arts/JPMISHA1/JPMISHA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/06/arts/JPMISHA1/JPMISHA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/06/arts/JPMISHA1/JPMISHA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/06/arts/JPMISHA1/JPMISHA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Baryshnikov Packs Up His Memories in Boxes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Baryshnikov Packs Up His Memories in Boxes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baryshnikov, Mikhail', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T21:45:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0aea29a1-432c-5c5e-9852-9feb2f0c02b9', 'word_count': 739, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0aea29a1-432c-5c5e-9852-9feb2f0c02b9'}, {'abstract': '“He wasn’t talking. He was looking from the window of the car all the way. Two adults in the front seat spoke quietly under their breath. He could have listened if he wanted to, but he didn’t.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/05/books/review/excerpt-the-cats-table-by-michael-ondaatje.html', 'snippet': '“He wasn’t talking. He was looking from the window of the car all the way. Two adults in the front seat spoke quietly under their breath. He could have listened if he wanted to, but he didn’t.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'He wasn’t talking. He was looking from the window of the car all the way. Two adults in the front seat spoke quietly under their breath. He could have listened if he wanted to, but he didn’t. For a while, at the section of the road where the river sometimes flooded, he could hear the spray of water at the wheels. They entered the Fort and the car slipped silently past the post office building and the clock tower. At this hour of the night there was barely any traffic in Colombo. They drove out along Reclamation Road, passed St. Anthony’s Church, and after that he saw the last of the food stalls, each lit with a single bulb. Then they entered a vast open space that was the harbour, with only a string of lights in the distance along the pier. He got out and stood by the warmth of the car.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Cat’s Table’', 'kicker': 'Excerpt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T21:52:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Ondaatje', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ondaatje', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'First Chapter', '_id': 'nyt://article/f36d2697-e15f-5f1e-897f-73d31431afd7', 'word_count': 1578, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f36d2697-e15f-5f1e-897f-73d31431afd7'}, {'abstract': 'In “The Cat’s Table,” Michael Ondaatje tells of a boy’s sea voyage that forms the future man.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/books/the-cats-table-by-michael-ondaatje-review.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Cat’s Table,” Michael Ondaatje tells of a boy’s sea voyage that forms the future man.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Cat’s Table” is about an 11-year-old boy, “green as he could be about the world,” who takes an indelible sea voyage in the early 1950s. The boy’s name is Michael, as is the author’s, and both sailed to England from Sri Lanka; but this lovely, shimmering book of Michael Ondaatje’s is no memoir. It is a tender meditation on how a child can be “smuggled away accidentally, with no knowledge of the act, into the future.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/06/arts/JPBOOK/JPBOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 681, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/06/arts/JPBOOK/JPBOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/06/arts/JPBOOK/JPBOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Boy’s Life, Not Quite His Own', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Boy’s Life, Not Quite His Own', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ondaatje, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-05T21:53:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b93ce0b-38fb-51de-9ce8-5081751568ed', 'word_count': 887, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b93ce0b-38fb-51de-9ce8-5081751568ed'}, {'abstract': 'The twin themes resulted in collections bathed in incandescent white and highlighted by wimples, veils, cassocks and other mass-worthy raiment stripped down, and softened, for a modern eye.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/10/05/fashion/06trendspottingslideshow.html', 'snippet': 'The twin themes resulted in collections bathed in incandescent white and highlighted by wimples, veils, cassocks and other mass-worthy raiment stripped down, and softened, for a modern eye.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The twin themes resulted in collections bathed in incandescent white and highlighted by wimples, veils, cassocks and other mass-worthy raiment stripped down, and softened, for a modern eye.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/05/fashion/06trendspottingslideshow-slide-HGJT/06trendspottingslideshow-slide-HGJT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 902, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/05/fashion/06trendspottingslideshow-slide-HGJT/06trendspottingslideshow-slide-HGJT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 902}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/05/fas