ion': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fec484be-8f55-518d-a15b-690a06fe92fd', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fec484be-8f55-518d-a15b-690a06fe92fd'}, {'abstract': 'The contents of the living room at the Eames house in Los Angeles — the home of Charles and Ray Eames — have been transferred to a replica of the room installed at the Los Angeles County Museum of Art', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/garden/the-eames-house-living-room.html', 'snippet': 'The contents of the living room at the Eames house in Los Angeles — the home of Charles and Ray Eames — have been transferred to a replica of the room installed at the Los Angeles County Museum of Art', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Eames House was assembled in Los Angeles in 1949, almost like a kit of parts, combining off-the-shelf steel elements with panels of glass, metal, stucco and other materials, some in primary colors. The husband-and-wife team Charles and Ray Eames — titans of modern design who were becoming known for their innovative furniture, as well as their contributions to architecture, film and industrial and graphic design — created it as a home for themselves, in a eucalyptus grove in Pacific Palisades.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/06/garden/06QNA1/06QNA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/06/garden/06QNA1/06QNA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/06/garden/06QNA1/06QNA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Eames Grandchildren on Charles and Ray’s Living Room', 'kicker': 'Currents | Q&A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In the Living Room With Charles and Ray', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eames, Ray', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eames, Charles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Demetrios, Eames', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T00:35:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Amelar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amelar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fa06103-1e49-59f8-8ae4-079b137a9503', 'word_count': 1073, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fa06103-1e49-59f8-8ae4-079b137a9503'}, {'abstract': 'The painter Pat Passlof lives and works in a former synagogue on the Lower East Side, near the former synagogue where her husband, Milton Resnick, lived and worked until his death.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/garden/milton-resnick-and-pat-passlofs-his-and-hers-synagogues.html', 'snippet': 'The painter Pat Passlof lives and works in a former synagogue on the Lower East Side, near the former synagogue where her husband, Milton Resnick, lived and worked until his death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN Milton Resnick’s living quarters, a spare cube built over a soaring, two-story studio space in a former synagogue on Eldridge Street in Manhattan, the monastic iron bed, as narrow as a child’s cot, is unmade, a tangle of sheets at the headboard. But on a garment rack in a closet, worn tweed jackets hang expectantly. There’s a cane next to the bed, and in the paint-flecked mini-studio, the walls are covered with bright, kinetic paintings, pencil sketches on lined Manila paper and photographs.',