rs, whose season opens Friday in Stockholm against the Los Angeles Kings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the second installment of our season preview conversations, Jeff Z. Klein and Christopher Botta discuss the Rangers, whose season opens Friday in Stockholm against the Los Angeles Kings.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '2-on-1: Are Rangers Stanley Cup Contenders, or Just a Good Hockey Club?', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T10:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42a3b37a-e79f-5e7f-9ca3-b867d006b9cc', 'word_count': 918, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42a3b37a-e79f-5e7f-9ca3-b867d006b9cc'}, {'abstract': 'The former prime minister won’t apologize for bromances with Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi and Rupert Murdoch.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/magazine/talk-tony-blair-unlikely-godfather.html', 'snippet': 'The former prime minister won’t apologize for bromances with Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi and Rupert Murdoch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many have compared the mood of England when you became prime minister in 1997 to the euphoria that greeted Barack Obama’s election. Did you imagine what Obama was thinking on election night?  I knew exactly what he’d be feeling. When you’re fighting to win, your time line is focused on the victory. The moment you know you’ve got the victory is just, literally, like entering into a new, very unfamiliar territory that’s got lots of flashing lights, and it can be very hard to feel comfortable at first. I spent my first few months in office mostly anxious because of the weight of the responsibility and my awareness of my own ignorance.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09talk/mag-09TALK-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09talk/09talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09talk/09talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tony Blair, the Unlikely Godfather', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Godfather', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blair, Tony', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T10:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee8e47a1-2e4d-53f5-bec6-dbd2eb0fdff6', 'word_count': 708, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee8e47a1-2e4d-53f5-bec6-dbd2eb0fdff6'}, {'abstract': 'The Owling Supper Club in Berlin is a restaurant within a home, and, to a degree, a secret.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/berlin-club-gives-new-meaning-to-dining-at-home/', 'snippet': 'The Owling Supper Club in Berlin is a restaurant within a home, and, to a degree, a secret.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a dimly lit room, a table is set for 14 hungry strangers. Flutes of prosecco and small plates of fried green tomatoes appear and relieve the predinner tension as guests — Berliners and travelers alike — sip, sample and chat. The aroma of bone marrow, pork belly and roasted squash floats in from the kitchen to the table, courtesy of Natalie Mouzarkel, the chef. This is the Owling Supper Club — a restaurant within a home, and, to a degree, a secret.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Berlin Club Gives New Meaning to Dining at Home', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T10:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Molly Hannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Molly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/697c5125-3135-570f-92cc-905fde206251', 'word_count': 466, 'uri': 'nyt://article/697c5125-3135-570f-92cc-905fde206251'}, {'abstract': 'The racecourse, now crumbling, was the site of one of the most storied road races in automotive history, the Targa Florio.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/driving-the-remains-of-sicilys-targa-florio-circuit/', 'snippet': 'The racecourse, now crumbling, was the site of one of the most storied road races in automotive history, the Targa Florio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CERDA, Italy — Just three miles north of this Sicilian village, on old Highway 120, there’s an incongruous wide spot in the road where several crumbling buildings sit behind a wire fence intended to keep t