ion will be open to the public for at least one day this year as part of the Garden Conservancy Open Days Program.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2009/04/26/nyregion/26garden-S.JPG', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2009/04/26/nyregion/26garden-S.JPG', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Opening Private Gardens to the Public', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trees and Shrubs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Garden Conservancy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T12:01:59+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'New York', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/919c8944-8a68-5bc5-9525-605c4de2900e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/919c8944-8a68-5bc5-9525-605c4de2900e'}, {'abstract': 'What used to be your Facebook personal profile page will soon be known as Timeline. It will allow you to present a picture of yourself as you were once and are now.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/facebook-gets-a-past/', 'snippet': 'What used to be your Facebook personal profile page will soon be known as Timeline. It will allow you to present a picture of yourself as you were once and are now.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Everyone gets old. Even the clever young men who created Facebook, which is why Facebook is no longer going to be only about who you are now. It will soon allow you to curate, as they say in digi-speak, your past. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Facebook Gets a Past', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuckerberg, Mark E', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T12:17:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Somini Sengupta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Somini', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sengupta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf951e79-3ee2-5bda-9a8b-fb488f48d268', 'word_count': 484, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf951e79-3ee2-5bda-9a8b-fb488f48d268'}, {'abstract': 'Britain’s film certification board gave the go-ahead for Tom Six’s shock-horror movie to be shown to viewers 18 and older after 2 minutes 37 seconds of footage were cut from the film.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/ban-on-human-centipede-2-is-overturned-in-britain/', 'snippet': 'Britain’s film certification board gave the go-ahead for Tom Six’s shock-horror movie to be shown to viewers 18 and older after 2 minutes 37 seconds of footage were cut from the film.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s generally not a good sign when anyone in a horror movie wields a sharp instrument, but by taking some editing scissors to his film “The Human Centipede 2 (Full Sequence),” the director Tom Six has gained the right to show it in Britain, reversing a ban imposed by the British Board of Film Classification.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ban on ‘Human Centipede 2’ Is Lifted in Britain', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Six, Tom', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Board of Film Classification', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T12:27:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cae7e163-84c4-5727-89a9-fe74f4980b9b', 'word_count': 315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cae7e163-84c4-5727-89a9-fe74f4980b9b'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: Chris Christie, who says now is not his time, is a Republican from the Northeast. That fact in itself is a problem. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/christies-regional-handicap/', 'snippet': 'The Day: Chris Christie, who says now is not his time, is a Republican from the Northeast. That fact in itself is a problem. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'lead_paragraph': ' When former Gov. George E. Pataki announced in August, to the consternation of almost no one, that he would not run for president, I wrote about the backwater that New York had become in national politics. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Christie’s Regional Handicap', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christie, Christopher J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Northeastern States (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T12:31:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/78d3415b-060d-5a67-98db-616a218a3650', 'word_count': 1194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78d3415b-060d-5a67-98db-616a218a3650'}, {'abstract': 'Great offenses tend to be greater than great defenses. Advantage, New England.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/week-5-n-f-l-game-probabilities-including-jets-patriots/', 'snippet': 'Great offenses tend to be greater than great defenses. Advantage, New England.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This Sunday features an A.F.C. East showdown when the Jets travel to Foxborough to take on the Patriots. I looked at my write-up for this same matchup from Week 13 last season, and much of what I wrote a year ago could be cut and pasted for this week, complete with the unstoppable force-immovable object cliché.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Week 5 N.F.L. Game Probabilities, Including Jets-Patriots', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Patriots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T13:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/82cfe816-efbf-5ddf-8982-a780cb87666d', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82cfe816-efbf-5ddf-8982-a780cb87666d'}, {'abstract': 'Gareth Pugh lends his gothic-abstract aesthetic to Melissa’s signature plastic shoes.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/eco-meets-exoskeletal/', 'snippet': 'Gareth Pugh lends his gothic-abstract aesthetic to Melissa’s signature plastic shoes.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Basically they asked me, and I said O.K.,” says the designer Gareth Pugh of his easy decision to collaborate with Melissa, the Brazilian shoe company best known for making synthetic vegan footwear with designers like Vivienne Westwood, Jason Wu and Jean Paul Gaultier. In fact, Pugh says that working with Melissa — a brand that took an early interest in his work (it helped sponsor his second fashion show back in 2006) — on his first-ever shoe collection and most mainstream venture yet feels “like a natural move.” The six-piece Melissa + Gareth Pugh spring collection, which recently had its debut in Rio de Janeiro, was perfected via a steady stream of intercontinental e-mails in which Pugh would “tweak this, change that.” “I’m a Virgo,” he explains. “I’m very particular about how I want things to be.” Here, Pugh’s gothic-abstract aesthetic in Melissa’s signature plastic: an exoskeletal sandal that inches over the ankle with a trio of vertebrae-like pieces in combinations of black, white and silver.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Eco Meets Exoskeletal', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T13:00:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Scire', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scire', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a97c41a-ffa1-5e9d-bebe-252d68681cd3', 'word_count': 174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a97c41a-ffa1-5e9d-bebe-252d68681cd3'}, {'abstract': 'There are few situations more horrible than having to tell another human being that he or she is going to die. And it doesn’t get any easier with experience, writes Dr. Danielle Ofri.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/so-doc-how-much-time-i-got/', 'snippet': 'There are few situations more horrible than having to tell another human being that he or she is going to die. And it doesn’t get any easier with experience, writes Dr. Danielle Ofri.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The medical team hung back in the hallway before entering the patient’s room. The intern had just presented the case — a 50-year-old man who’d never seen a doctor until three months before, when he noticed blood in his stool. It turned out he had extensive colon cancer that had already spread to the liver and lungs. The man, Mr. M, was now admitted for his second cycle of chemotherapy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘So, Doc, How Much Time I Got?’', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chemotherapy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T13:04:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Danielle Ofri, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Danielle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ofri', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/485fe8fd-6db1-5a2d-bcbd-abfb4050e0e9', 'word_count': 1020, 'uri': 'nyt://article/485fe8fd-6db1-5a2d-bcbd-abfb4050e0e9'}, {'abstract': 'A European court rules that France’s insider trading laws were sufficiently clear to hold George Soros criminally responsible for buying and selling shares of Société Générale in 1988 with the knowledge the bank might be a takeover target.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/soros-loses-challenge-to-insider-trading-conviction/', 'snippet': 'A European court rules that France’s insider trading laws were sufficiently clear to hold George Soros criminally responsible for buying and selling shares of Société Générale in 1988 with the knowledge the bank might be a takeover target.', 'lead_paragraph': '5:47 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Soros Loses Challenge to Insider Trading Conviction', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Soros, George', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Court of Human Rights', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Societe Generale', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T13:05:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicola Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicola', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_mat