cker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'NATO Is Not Yet Willing to Halt Its Libya Operations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Panetta, Leon E', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T16:25:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Erlanger and Stephen Castle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Erlanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Castle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3129052e-ec5e-52d7-a94d-12fabb6eefc9', 'word_count': 517, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3129052e-ec5e-52d7-a94d-12fabb6eefc9'}, {'abstract': 'Steve Jobs, the innovative force behind Apple Computers, grew up in San Francisco but traced his roots back to Syria.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/steve-jobs-son-of-a-syrian-is-embraced-in-the-arab-world/', 'snippet': 'Steve Jobs, the innovative force behind Apple Computers, grew up in San Francisco but traced his roots back to Syria.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steve Jobs, the innovative force behind Apple, grew up in San Francisco but traced his roots back to Syria. It was a fact that many Syrians celebrated on Thursday even as they mourned his death and lamented how the political crisis gripping the country made it unlikely that a future Steve Jobs would emerge from Syria and stay there.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Steve Jobs, Son of a Syrian, Is Embraced in the Arab World', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T16:26:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. David Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c8bc93c-31db-57b0-a936-1da4c4d677c2', 'word_count': 705, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c8bc93c-31db-57b0-a936-1da4c4d677c2'}, {'abstract': 'The hills in the Appalachians may be shorter than in the West, but they stand up straighter, making your quads, hamstrings and glutes take notice as you ride.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/headed-east-more-hill-than-dale/', 'snippet': 'The hills in the Appalachians may be shorter than in the West, but they stand up straighter, making your quads, hamstrings and glutes take notice as you ride.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WEST HOMESTEAD, Pa. — A long bike ride delivers you from one set of conditions to another. For almost a year — well, O.K., 10 days, but it felt like a year — I was riding in the damp and cold as a crummy weather front arrived over lower Michigan and Ohio and sat there. More than once I played the what-if game with myself: Would I trade a steady rain and a chilling wind for the heat and rolling hills of the Palouse in eastern Washington? How about for the endless prairie of Montana? A cyclist can appreciate all those challenges and enjoy meeting them, including the rain, but after a week or so of being soggy to the bone, the answer to every trade possibility was yes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Headed East, More Hill Than Dale', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pennsylvania', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T16:27:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Weber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45607f1d-01cb-581b-abb4-e9037fc74813', 'word_count': 930, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45607f1d-01cb-581b-abb4-e9037fc74813'}, {'abstract': 'Norbert Wabnig, a cheese expert, answers more readers’ questions on all things cheese.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/your-cheese-questions-answered-part-two/', 'snippet': 'Norbert Wabnig, a cheese expert, answers more readers’ questions on all things cheese.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Norbert Wabnig, the owner of the Cheese Store of Beverly Hills, is answering readers’ questions on all things cheese. Read his first batch of answers here. Post your questions in the comments section here. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Your Cheese Questions, Answered —\xa0Part II', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheese', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T16:28:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fdc84f2-5aac-5bc1-a126-048bbcb9e389', 'word_count': 604, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fdc84f2-5aac-5bc1-a126-048bbcb9e389'}, {'abstract': 'The villagers in this border region are surrounded by a landscape where hundreds of caves are hidden beneath thick vines and towering dipterocarp, kapok and teak trees.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/guess-where-this-is-35/', 'snippet': 'The villagers in this border region are surrounded by a landscape where hundreds of caves are hidden beneath thick vines and towering dipterocarp, kapok and teak trees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s time again for our weekly contest: guess the location of the photograph above.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Guess Where This Is', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T16:30:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ff9a1ff-ed80-5371-b186-237889680994', 'word_count': 78, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ff9a1ff-ed80-5371-b186-237889680994'}, {'abstract': 'Jessie J., a 23-year-old pop singer, won four MOBO awards, which honor the best music of black origin.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/newcomer-jessie-j-sweeps-british-pop-awards/', 'snippet': 'Jessie J., a 23-year-old pop singer, won four MOBO awards, which honor the best music of black origin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Adele may be the biggest-selling singer and songwriter to come out of Britain in years, but a newcomer to the British pop scene, Jessie J, swept the MOBO awards in Glasgow on Wednesday night, Billboard and other wires services reported. The 23-year-old singer, won best album for her debut effort, “Who You Are,” as well as best song for “Do it like a Dude.” She also took home awards for best newcomer and best U.K. act.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Newcomer Jessie J. Sweeps British Pop Awards', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adele (Singer)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jessie J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T16:30:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2045a7c-4350-51ce-9cdf-257459d8da8b', 'word_count': 231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2045a7c-4350-51ce-9cdf-257459d8da8b'}, {'abstract': 'We asked our readers to submit their best memories and tributes to Steve Jobs. Here are their photos.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/10/06/business/steve-jobs-user-photos.html', 'snippet': 'We asked our readers to submit their best memories and tributes to Steve Jobs. Here are their photos.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We asked our readers to submit their best memories and tributes to Steve Jobs. Here are their photos.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reader Memories of Steve Jobs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jobs, Steven P', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T16:33:14+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business Day', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/2714dd8c-bcd0-5015-ba7a-dd7cb8af5eb1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/2714dd8c-bcd0-5015-ba7a-dd7cb8af5eb1'}, {'abstract': 'The Chinese government dismissed the chief dispatcher of the Shanghai Metro’s Line 10, and disciplined 11 other managers and workers in the operation control and power sections.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/world/asia/12-punished-for-shanghai-subway-crash.html', 'snippet': 'The Chinese government dismissed the chief dispatcher of the Shanghai Metro’s Line 10, and disciplined 11 other managers and workers in the operation control and power sections.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SHANGHAI — The authorities here have punished 12 people for negligence over the subway accident last week that injured 290 people, according to Xinhua, the official Chinese new agency. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '12 Punished for Shanghai Subway Crash', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China Punishes 12 in Subway Crash', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroad Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infrastructure (Public Works)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suspensions, Dismissals and Resignations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T16:34:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Barboza', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barboza', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/151418a1-3ef0-59e0-b31b-95fad2d71437', 'word_count': 277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/151418a1-3ef0-59e0-b31b-95fad2d71437'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s podcast focuses on the new iPhone 4S and Siri, its personal assistant software.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/tech-talk-podcast-the-iphone-4s/', 'snippet': 'This week’s podcast focuses on the new iPhone 4S and Siri, its personal assistant software.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week’s Tech Talk podcast focuses on Apple‘s new iPhone, the 4S, announced this week and the release of the new iOS 5 mobile operating system and its iCloud online service. Alan Yacavone, part of The New York Times’s I.T. team, provides his analysis of Apple’s new iPhone 4S hardware, as well as the company’s minor updates to its iPod line. He also joins a round-table discussion concerning the gap between the rampant rumors that swirl around most pending Apple announcements and the actual products that arrive.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tech Talk Podcast: The iPhone 4S', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T16:34:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11b933ba-1a32-5d19-9ad0-7aee649b6748', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11b933ba-1a32-5d19-9ad0-7aee649b6748'}, {'abstract': 'I bought my first Apple computer while stationed in Moscow in 1985. It did everything I could imagine a computer doing, at the time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/opinion/steven-paul-jobs.html', 'snippet': 'I bought my first Apple computer while stationed in Moscow in 1985. It did everything I could imagine a computer doing, at the time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I heard the news that Steve Jobs had died, my mind flashed back to 1985, when I began my love affair with computers. I was stationed in Moscow for The Associated Press and I ordered an Apple IIc — by Telex — from a department store in Helsinki, Finland. They express shipped it to me, a month later, by train.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/06/opinion/apple-appreciation-image/apple-appreciation-image-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 673, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Steven Paul Jobs', 'kicker': 'Editorial | Appreciations', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Steven Paul Jobs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jobs, Steven P', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moscow (Russia)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T16:35:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/52963bbd-1eb9-5414-a658-0afcc692166f', 'word_count': 373, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52963bbd-1eb9-5414-a658-0afcc692166f'}, {'abstract': 'Texas Christian’s decision to join the Big 12 and leave the Big East, which it had yet to join officially, leaves the conference with only six football-playing universities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/sports/ncaafootball/big-east-loses-texas-christian-putting-football-future-in-doubt.html', 'snippet': 'Texas Christian’s decision to join the Big 12 and leave the Big East, which it had yet to join officially, leaves the conference with only six football-playing universities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the uncertainty of realignment continues to overshadow the college football season, Texas Christian delivered a crushing blow to the Big East Conference when news of its imminent departure to the Big 12 emerged Thursday. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Loss of T.C.U. Imperils Big East’s Football Future', 'kicker': 'Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Loss of T.C.U. Leaves Big East’s Football Future in Doubt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football (College)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Big East Conference', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Texas Christian University', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Big 12 Conference', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T16:42:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pete Thamel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pete', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thamel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56e0bb73-842c-5d3c-8464-5479b248e2d6', 'word_count': 961, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56e0bb73-842c-5d3c-8464-5479b248e2d6'}, {'abstract': '“To start with, look at all the books.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/books/the-marriage-plot.html', 'snippet': '“To start with, look at all the books.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To start with, look at all the books. There were her Edith Wharton novels, arranged not by title but date of publication; there was the complete Modern Library set of Henry James, a gift from her father on her twenty-first birthday; there were the dog-eared paperbacks assigned in her college courses, a lot of Dickens, a smidgen of Trollope, along with good helpings of Austen, George Eliot, and the redoubtable Brontë sisters. There were a whole lot of black-and-white New Directions paperbacks, mostly poetry by people like H.D. or Denise Levertov. There were the Colette novels she read on the sly. There was the first edition of Couples, belonging to her mother, which Madeleine had surreptitiously dipped into back in sixth grade and which she was using now to provide textual support in her English honors thesis on the marriage plot. There was, in short, this mid-size but still portable library representing pretty much everything Madeleine had read in college, a collection of texts, seemingly chosen at random, whose focus slowly narrowed, like a personality test, a sophisticated one you couldn’t trick by anticipating the implications of its questions and finally got so lost in that your only recourse was to answer the simple truth. And then you waited for the result, hoping for “Artistic,” or “Passionate,” thinking you could live with “Sensitive,” secretly fearing “Narcissistic” and “Domestic,” but finally being presented with an outcome that cut both ways and made you feel different depending on the day, the hour, or the guy you happened to be dating: “Incurably Romantic.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Marriage Plot’', 'kicker': 'Excerpt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T16:43:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeffrey Eugenides', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eugenides', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'First Chapter', '_id': 'nyt://article/49b12860-6967-5899-9ca3-aad7950032b1', 'word_count': 6213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49b12860-6967-5899-9ca3-aad7950032b1'}, {'abstract': 'Timothy F. Geithner urged Congress to pass the Obama administration’s jobs plan, warning that a failure to act would result in tax increases for families and businesses.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/business/geithner-urges-quick-action-on-jobs-plan.html', 'snippet': 'Timothy F. Geithner urged Congress to pass the Obama administration’s jobs plan, warning that a failure to act would result in tax increases for families and businesses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Treasury Secretary Timothy F. Geithner told Congress on Thursday that legislative action was required to buttress the American economy, particularly as economic conditions in Europe continued to deteriorate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Treasury Secretary Urges Quick Action on Jobs Plan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Treasury Secretary Urges Quick Action on Jobs Plan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Geithner, Timothy F', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T16:45:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Binyamin Appelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Binyamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/daf355ea-260a-5c50-80a8-890e823203bc', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/daf355ea-260a-5c50-80a8-890e823203bc'}, {'abstract': 'The Bank of England’s surprise decision to inject £75 billion into the stagnant British economy is a vindication for Adam Posen.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/in-bank-of-england-decision-a-once-lonely-voice-is-heard-loud-and-clear/', 'snippet': 'The Bank of England’s surprise decision to inject £75 billion into the stagnant British economy is a vindication for Adam Posen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pretty soon they might even call it Posenomics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Bank of England Decision, a Once Lonely Voice Is Heard Loud and Clear', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quantitative Easing', '