, 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T17:59:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63c74043-b68e-5aeb-b160-471c9ecd9387', 'word_count': 135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63c74043-b68e-5aeb-b160-471c9ecd9387'}, {'abstract': 'Another suggested recipe from Times readers for football tailgating parties.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/tailgating-recipes-buffalo-chicken-wraps/', 'snippet': 'Another suggested recipe from Times readers for football tailgating parties.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When The Quad, the Times’s college sports blog, put out a call for readers’ recipes for tailgating parties, we honestly didn’t expect much healthy eating. Of course, we’ve been surprised by some of the submissions. What should not have surprised us was that a recipe from a Southern city had an eye on calorie counting. We wouldn’t expect that from Chicago, Green Bay or New York, where fans are pretty well bundled up for most of the season. This week’s recipe is Buffalo chicken lettuce wraps, the spicy wing dish with a few of the calories lopped off. It’s not too late to submit your own recipe for barbecue or chowder, or whatever makes your pregame feast tasty.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tailgating Recipes: Buffalo Chicken Wraps', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parties (Social)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T18:04:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/950d7fea-4266-56e1-9bea-aec4c6603928', 'word_count': 118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/950d7fea-4266-56e1-9bea-aec4c6603928'}, {'abstract': 'The father of Manchester United striker Wayne Rooney was arrested as part of an investigation into suspicious bets placed on a Scottish League game.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/rooneys-father-arrested-in-betting-investigation/', 'snippet': 'The father of Manchester United striker Wayne Rooney was arrested as part of an investigation into suspicious bets placed on a Scottish League game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The father of Manchester United striker Wayne Rooney was one of nine men arrested on Thursday as part of an investigation into suspicious bets placed on a Scottish league match in December. Rooney’s father, also named Wayne, was arrested at his home in Liverpool. Rooney’s uncle, Richie, was also charged, though the younger Wayne Rooney — who is in Montenegro with England’s national team for a Euro 2012 qualifier — was not implicated, the police said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rooney’s Father Arrested in Betting Investigation', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Officiating (Sports)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rooney, Wayne', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Associated Press', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Liverpool FC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Manchester United', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scottish Premier League', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scotland', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T18:05:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Das', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Das', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc1ee981-d1dd-5333-a52d-9a34241843de', 'word_count': 293, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc1ee981-d1dd-5333-a52d-9a34241843de'}, {'abstract': 'Steve Jobs changed how business journalism is perceived, adding glitz and esteem to a subject that had often been associated with faceless conglomerates or Wall Street excess.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/steve-jobs-he-brought-the-show-to-business/', 'snippet': 'Steve Jobs changed how business journalism is perceived, adding glitz and esteem to a subject that had often been associated with faceless conglomerates or Wall Street excess.', 'lead_paragraph': '4:28 p.m. | Updated Much has been and will be written about Steve Jobs’s outsize footprint in digital culture: the screens we stare at, the music files we listen to, the hardware in our pockets. I’d stipulate to all that, but I found myself thinking about his less obvious influence on business and the journalists who cover it. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Steve Jobs: He Brought the Show to Business', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jobs, Steven P', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wozniak, Stephen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Silicon Valley (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T18:06:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Carr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85ad3e7c-9c6f-5735-be2f-5b3b2231c3df', 'word_count': 1258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85ad3e7c-9c6f-5735-be2f-5b3b2231c3df'}, {'abstract': 'Taliban and Haqqani fighters in one Afghan province declared a cease-fire to ensure that pine-cone pickers were not caught between the two warring sides.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/world/asia/harvesting-cease-fire-offers-respite-in-afghanistan.html', 'snippet': 'Taliban and Haqqani fighters in one Afghan province declared a cease-fire to ensure that pine-cone pickers were not caught between the two warring sides.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OBSERVATION POST TWINS, Afghanistan — When an 82-millimeter mortar round slammed onto a bunker at this mountaintop post last week, its explosion signaled more than the start of another attack on an American position overlooking an arc of hostile Afghan villages. It marked the end of a particular harvest.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/world/07truce/07truce-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 341, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/07/world/07truce/07truce-articleLa