06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e18bbc9-dc70-5bbe-98e7-fa3cc6e703c7', 'word_count': 256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e18bbc9-dc70-5bbe-98e7-fa3cc6e703c7'}, {'abstract': 'If “Seven Minutes in Heaven” only conjures up over-analyzed middle-school disappointments, you’re in luck. (If it conjures up middle-school conquests, you’re in denial.)', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/celebrities-in-the-closet/', 'snippet': 'If “Seven Minutes in Heaven” only conjures up over-analyzed middle-school disappointments, you’re in luck. (If it conjures up middle-school conquests, you’re in denial.)', 'lead_paragraph': 'If “Seven Minutes in Heaven” only conjures up over-analyzed middle-school disappointments, you’re in luck. (If it conjures up middle-school conquests, you’re in denial.) Mike O’Brien, a writer for “Saturday Night Live,” has redefined the sophomoric game for a grown-up, but possibly still sophomoric, audience. The rules are still the same: two people, one closet, roughly seven minutes. (The timing isn’t precise, but it never really was.) There are also a few additions: cameras, which at least at the suburban basement parties I attended were not part of the deal, and celebrities.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Celebrities in the Closet', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Web-Original Programming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clarkson, Patricia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T18:46:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'n