'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6cc1bff1-b26f-540e-8734-97b492ac3620', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6cc1bff1-b26f-540e-8734-97b492ac3620'}, {'abstract': 'Betty Buckley’s two sides, Broadway diva and quasi-jazz experimentalist, meet in her show at Feinstein’s at Loews Regency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/arts/music/betty-buckley-at-feinsteins-review.html', 'snippet': 'Betty Buckley’s two sides, Broadway diva and quasi-jazz experimentalist, meet in her show at Feinstein’s at Loews Regency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the flowery lyrics of “Younger Than Springtime,” that heavily perfumed Rodgers and Hammerstein love song from “South Pacific” sung by Lieutenant Cable to his islander sweetheart, the ethereal beloved is “softer than starlight” and “warmer than winds of June.” At the opening night performance of her new show, “Ah Men! The Boys of Broadway!,” at Feinstein’s at Loews Regency on Tuesday evening Betty Buckley sang the song with conviction, then mused aloud that if only men addressed women that way, “the world would be a better place.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/BUCKLEY/BUCKLEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 710, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Singer Asks, Why Can’t a Man Be More Like a Woman?', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Singer Asks, Why Can’t a Man Be More Like a Woman?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Feinstein's at the Regency", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buckley, Betty', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T19:55:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a78db8a4-53ce-5ce7-b35b-d22a2b6faacf', 'word_count': 439, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a78db8a4-53ce-5ce7-b35b-d22a2b6faacf'}, {'abstract': 'The violinist Frank Peter Zimmermann appeared alongside the New York Philharmonic and its music director, Alan Gilbert, who made his solo debut as a violinist with the orchestra.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/arts/music/alan-glibert-and-frank-peter-zimmermann-on-violin-review.html', 'snippet': 'The violinist Frank Peter Zimmermann appeared alongside the New York Philharmonic and its music director, Alan Gilbert, who made his solo debut as a violinist with the orchestra.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Virtually all conductors were trained as instrumentalists before they took up the baton, but too few keep their technique sufficiently up to date to perform publicly. Alan Gilbert, like Lorin Maazel, his predecessor as music director of the New York Philharmonic, is a violinist, and though he has played in the Philharmonic’s chamber concerts, his appearance alongside the violinist Frank Peter Zimmermann in Bach’s Double Concerto on Wednesday evening at Avery Fisher Hall was his solo debut with the orchestra. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Conductor Replaces Baton With Violin, for an Evening', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Conductor Replaces Baton With Violin, for an Evening', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zimmermann, Frank Peter', 