has been appointed to a second term. Reviews of her first term are mixed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/us/police-review-board-administrator-draws-mixed-reviews.html', 'snippet': 'Ilana Rosenzweig, the head of Chicago’s police review board, has been appointed to a second term. Reviews of her first term are mixed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At her first City Council meeting four years ago, Ilana Rosenzweig, the chief administrator of the agency charged with investigating accusations of police abuse, heard this greeting from an audience member: “Welcome to hell.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/us/07CNCIPRA/07CNCIPRA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 629, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/us/07CNCIPRA/07CNCIPRA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/07/us/07CNCIPRA/07CNCIPRA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Police Review Board Administrator Draws Mixed Reviews', 'kicker': 'Chicago News Cooperative', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chief Administrator Of Police Board Gets Mixed Reviews', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality and Misconduct', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T22:22:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adrienne Lu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adrienne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe8e704c-e996-568e-a911-0027f8505968', 'word_count': 1220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe8e704c-e996-568e-a911-0027f8505968'}, {'abstract': '“The Women on the 6th Floor” involves a bourgeois Parisian businessman who discovers a happy world with a small group of Spanish maids living in his family’s building.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/movies/the-women-on-the-6th-floor-french-comedy-review.html', 'snippet': '“The Women on the 6th Floor” involves a bourgeois Parisian businessman who discovers a happy world with a small group of Spanish maids living in his family’s building.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lighter than a meringue and as insubstantial, the French boulevard comedy “The Women on the 6th Floor” was designed for the gentle laughter it easily earns. Set in 1962 Paris, the story involves a seemingly hopeless, staid bourgeois businessman whose entire existence — family, career, sense of purpose and patrimony — is overturned after he discovers another, happier world with a group of Spanish maids living in his family’s building. As in Henri Murger’s classic “Scènes de la Vie de Bohème” (and in the Puccini opera), redemption is just a garret away.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07WOMEN1/07WOMEN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07WOMEN1/07WOMEN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07WOMEN1/07WOMEN1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 605, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07WOMEN1/07WOMEN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07WOMEN1/07WOMEN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For a Bourgeois Frenchman, Happiness Is a Garret Away', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'The Women on the 6th Floor'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For a Bourgeois Frenchman, Happiness Is a Garret Away', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Luchini, Fabrice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T22:24:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f44203d-13aa-5f9f-b693-01cc59ae816f', 'word_count': 823, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f44203d-13aa-5f9f-b693-01cc59ae816f'}, {'abstract': 'A Twitter response to a Wall Street Journal riff on “settled science” and global warming.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/settled-science-and-co2/', 'snippet': 'A Twitter response to a Wall Street Journal riff on “settled science” and global warming.', 'lead_paragraph': '6:42 p.m. | Updated There’s an amusing Twitter chat under way via the hashtag #WSJscience, all centered on a Wall Street Journal opinion piece by Robert Bryce of the Manhattan Institute that makes a big deal of the utterly boring fact that climate science is not settled — as if it ever was.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Settled Science’ and CO2', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Physics', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bryce, Robert', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CERN', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Committee for a Constructive Tomorrow', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Manhattan Institute', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wall Street Journal', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T22:25:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/750aba8b-b6c9-5d79-85d9-b45865bdd799', 'word_count': 667, 'uri': 'nyt://article/750aba8b-b6c9-5d79-85d9-b45865bdd799'}, {'abstract': 'While the number of protesters remains small in many cities, organizers have been quick to establish social-media hubs for each. Here is a collection of some of the most active.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/occupy-anytown-u-s-a/', 'snippet': 'While the number of protesters remains small in many cities, organizers have been quick to establish social-media hubs for each. Here is a collection of some of the most active.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As unions tentatively throw their support behind the Occupy Wall Street protests in New York, new cities are beginning to set up their own versions. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Occupy Anytown, U.S.A.', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T22:27:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. David Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96622a14-d949-5065-933e-7c38f21e54c5', 'word_count': 638, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96622a14-d949-5065-933e-7c38f21e54c5'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Critic, in his farewell column, sends inquiring diners to restaurants that are sure to please potential in-laws.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/dining/pleasing-finicky-parents-and-nostalgic-texans-hey-mr-critic.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Critic, in his farewell column, sends inquiring diners to restaurants that are sure to please potential in-laws.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The mailman dropped off a bunch of questions for me to answer this week. They came from people looking for restaurants to please their parents, and to please themselves. I answered them, but not before handing in a change-of-address card. This is my final column before I take on a new assignment at The New York Times. It has been a great pleasure to serve you. Now, let’s eat!', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge'