 'subject', 'value': 'iPod', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPad', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T22:35:51+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'ContinuousNews', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'Brent McDonald|Emily B. Hager|Nadia Sussman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brent', 'middlename': 'Mcdonald', 'lastname': 'Emily', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/f0a4ba07-8042-5564-87a4-2028928b6b03', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/f0a4ba07-8042-5564-87a4-2028928b6b03'}, {'abstract': 'Four young and highly schooled pianists are blazing new trails in the New York jazz scene.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/arts/music/four-young-pianists-on-the-rise-in-the-jazz-scene.html', 'snippet': 'Four young and highly schooled pianists are blazing new trails in the New York jazz scene.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If drummers are the engines of jazz, then pianists are often its mapmakers. It’s not just that they’re the bandleaders and the composers; they’re also the content providers, the song finders, the place setters.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07PIANISTS1_SPAN/07PIANISTS1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07PIANISTS1_SPAN/07PIANISTS1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07PIANISTS1_SPAN/JPPIANISTS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07PIANISTS1_SPAN/07PIANISTS1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07PIANISTS1_SPAN/07PIANISTS1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New Pilots at the Keyboard', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Pilots at the Keyboard', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Manhattan School of Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnegie Hall', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Kris (1980- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T22:37:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e388910f-a4ed-5ec6-95d8-ad60483d90e3', 'word_count': 2402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e388910f-a4ed-5ec6-95d8-ad60483d90e3'}, {'abstract': 'Virtuoso Resources on the Upper West Side rents instruments to players as young as 2 and has an alluring collection of musical mementos.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/a-salon-for-musicians-big-and-small/', 'snippet': 'Virtuoso Resources on the Upper West Side rents instruments to players as young as 2 and has an alluring collection of musical mementos.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Having learned to play the violin as a boy, my husband has long harbored fantasies that our 3-year-old daughter, Meenakshi, will someday follow his footsteps into a school orchestra. While researching how she could get started, I stumbled upon Virtuoso Resources. New York has no shortage of places that stock instruments for children, but for young people eyeing the violin, viola or cello, this Upper West Side atelier offers an unusual grace note: a touch of musical history.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Salon for Musicians Big and Small', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Musical Instruments', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Upper West Side (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T22:37:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shivani Vora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shivani', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ac45a0c-499e-5cbd-b9b6-bd1929f4152d', 'word_count': 184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ac45a0c-499e-5cbd-b9b6-bd1929f4152d'}, {'abstract': 'An excerpt of a new biography of Walter Payton has sparked outcries, but critics should remember this dedicated player’s complicated life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/us/remembering-payton-with-eyes-open.html', 'snippet': 'An excerpt of a new biography of Walter Payton has sparked outcries, but critics should remember this dedicated player’s complicated life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I don’t believe the reaction to Jeff Pearlman would have been as harsh if he had written off our lake as an oversized mud puddle or sanctimoniously declared that a fully loaded pizza is as bad-tasting as it is bad for you.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21B', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/us/07CNCSPORTS2/07CNCSPORTS2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 674, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/us/07CNCSPORTS2/07CNCSPORTS2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/07/us/07CNCSPORTS2/07CNCSPORTS2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Remembering Payton, With Eyes Open', 'kicker': 'Chicago News Cooperative | Sports', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Remembering Payton, With Eyes Open', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Payton, Walter', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pearlman, Jeff', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T22:38:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/607e6799-b30d-5c7c-b19d-d40e1c14283f', 'word_count': 873, 'uri': 'nyt://article/607e6799-b30d-5c7c-b19d-d40e1c14283f'}, {'abstract': '“The Little Prince,” the 1943 novella by Antoine de Saint-Exupéry, comes to the stage in the Bristol Riverside Theater production at the New Victory Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/theater/reviews/the-little-prince-at-new-victory-theater-review.html', 'snippet': '“The Little Prince,” the 1943 novella by Antoine de Saint-Exupéry, comes to the stage in the Bristol Riverside Theater production at the New Victory Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Little Prince,” the 1943 novella by Antoine de Saint-Exupéry, is not just one of the most beloved children’s stories of all time, it is also a parable celebrating the wisdom of the innocent. After all, it’s about a boy from another planet who teaches an aviator who has crashed in the desert what is important in life. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/PRINCE/PRINCE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 765, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/PRINCE/PRINCE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/PRINCE/PRINCE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'That Odd Boy and the Pilot, Still Together in the Desert', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'The Little Prince'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'That Odd Boy and the Pilot, Still Together in the Desert', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Saint-Exupery, Antoine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T22:42:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Zinoman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinoman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7192697-7b1b-5bf4-bd84-883379f6bc75', 'word_count': 331, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7192697-7b1b-5bf4-bd84-883379f6bc75'}, {'abstract': 'The new Web site of the Metropolitan Museum of Art allows for an incredibly impressive view of the works there.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/arts/design/the-metropolitan-museums-new-web-site.html', 'snippet': 'The new Web site of the Metropolitan Museum of Art allows for an incredibly impressive view of the works there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is something you can do on an ordinary visit to the Metropolitan Museum of Art: view a Versace evening dress from 1991, made of silk and glass, that displays a crowd of heads from Warhol’s James Dean and Marilyn Monroe paintings, and look at the 16th-century armor of George Clifford, third earl of Cumberland, made of steel with elaborate fire-etched gilding. Then you can contemplate Bonnard’s eerily vibrant 1939 painting “The Terrace at Vernonnet,” before turning to a 16th-century violin made in Cremona, Italy, by Andrea Amati, whose maple sides are inscribed with a Latin couplet stating, “By this bulwark alone religion stands and will stand.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07MUSEUM1_SPAN/07MUSEUM1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07MUSEUM1_SPAN/07MUSEUM1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07MUSEUM1_SPAN/JPMUSEUM1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 581, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07MUSEUM1_SPAN/07MUSEUM1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07MUSEUM1_SPAN/07MUSEUM1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Met Museum, Virtual Virtue', 'kicker': 'Web Site Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Met Museum, Virtual Virtue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Campbell, Thomas P', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T22:45:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Rothstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/99d10fca-4cb9-57a6-96c8-4d45be2e5bbe', 'word_count': 1337, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99d10fca-4cb9-57a6-96c8-4d45be2e5bbe'}, {'abstract': 'In Emilio Estevez’s film “The Way,” an ophthalmologist walks the Way of St. James.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/movies/the-way-directed-by-emilio-estevez-review.html', 'snippet': 'In Emilio Estevez’s film “The Way,” an ophthalmologist walks the Way of St. James.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One thing you quickly realize when you sit down to watch “The Way”: Martin Sheen is a very compelling actor. Another thing you realize more slowly as the film goes along: His oldest son, Emilio Estevez, is a very sensitive director.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07WAY-SUB/07WAY-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07WAY-SUB/07WAY-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07WAY-SUB/07WAY-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07WAY-SUB/07WAY-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07WAY-SUB/07WAY-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Trek From Loss and Grief to a Life Given Greater Meaning', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'The Way'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Estevez, Emilio', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheen, Martin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Unger, Deborah Kara', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T22:45:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/be7d77f3-f675-59a4-822e-b3c4757d620d', 'word_count': 358, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be7d77f3-f675-59a4-822e-b3c4757d620d'}, {'abstract': '“Picasso’s Drawings, 1890-1921,” at the Frick Collection, follows his development from tentative schoolboy to dominant modernist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/arts/design/lines-that-kept-moving-and-knew-no-boundaries.html', 'snippet': '“Picasso’s Drawings, 1890-1921,” at the Frick Collection, follows his development from tentative schoolboy to dominant modernist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maybe every museum, regardless of focus, should know the box-office thrill of staging a Picasso exhibition. In 2006 the Whitney Museum — of American Art, mind you — pulled it off with “Picasso and American Art,” a somewhat flawed effort that examined his influence on a host of major and minor American artists.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07PICASSO/PICASSO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 775, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07PICASSO/07PICASSO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07PICASSO/07PICASSO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lines That Kept Moving and Knew No Boundaries', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lines That Kept Moving And Knew No Boundaries', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Picasso, Pablo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Frick Collection', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T22:46:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1307dc6c-b28a-5c05-862d-0a4fdf6c80d4', 'word_count': 1271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1307dc6c-b28a-5c05-862d-0a4fdf6c80d4'}, {'abstract': '“Dana Schutz: If the Face Had Wheels” offers a survey of the artist’s work from the last 10 years at the Neuberger Museum of Art in Purchase, N.Y.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/arts/design/dana-schutz-at-neuberger-museum-review.html', 'snippet': '“Dana Schutz: If the Face Had Wheels” offers a survey of the artist’s work from the last 10 years at the Neuberger Museum of Art in Purchase, N.Y.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PURCHASE, N.Y. — These are some of the things Dana Schutz has painted: A portrait of herself as an elephant. A cannibal devouring his own face. The autopsied corpse of Michael Jackson. A giant on a dissecting table. A woman stripping a wood floor with her teeth. The last man on earth.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07SHUTZ/SHUTZ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 806, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07SHUTZ/07SHUTZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07SHUTZ/07SHUTZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fantastic and Grisly, Envisioned', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Fantastic and Grisly, Envisioned', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schutz, Dana', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Neuberger Museum of Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-06T22:49:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8882defb-8880-54f3-926e-7df28c8711bd', 'word_count': 881, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8882defb-8880-54f3-926e-7df28c8711bd'}, {'abstract': 'An exhibition of 19 paintings by Eva Hesse, done when she was just 24 and left without a clear mentor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/arts/design/eva-hesse-spectres-1960-at-the-brooklyn-museum-review.html', 'snippet': 'An exhibition of 19 paintings by Eva Hesse, done when she was just 24 and left without a clear mentor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The art world tends to see Eva Hesse as an Athena-like figure, an adult from birth. Hesse was just 34 when she died of a brain tumor, leaving behind a prodigious body of work that continues to inform contemporary sculpture.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/arts/07HESSE1/07HESSE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1019, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name'