5a1c-a396-c3773954a546', 'word_count': 318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/875b643b-c84b-5a1c-a396-c3773954a546'}, {'abstract': 'Mateo Gil’s western, “Blackthorn,” imagines that Butch Cassidy didn’t die in a hail of bullets and instead survived and lived till a ripe old age in Bolivia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/movies/blackthorn-starring-sam-shepard-review.html', 'snippet': 'Mateo Gil’s western, “Blackthorn,” imagines that Butch Cassidy didn’t die in a hail of bullets and instead survived and lived till a ripe old age in Bolivia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What if Butch Cassidy didn’t die romantically in a hail of bullets in his Paul Newman prime? “Blackthorn,” an elegiac, old-man’s western, takes as its starting point another, equally romantic myth: that he survived and lived till a ripe old age in Bolivia. It’s 1927 when the movie begins, and Butch (Sam Shepard), who calls himself James Blackthorn, decides to end his long exile and return home. On the way he becomes entangled with a hunted Spaniard (Eduardo Noriega). Adventures ensue.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07BLACK_SPAN/07BLACK_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07BLACK_SPAN/07BLACK_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07BLACK_SPAN/07BLACK_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07BLACK_SPAN/07BLACK_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thum