Bits blog post about the increasing amount of data being shared on Facebook.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/opinion/sharing-too-much-online.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to a recent Bits blog post about the increasing amount of data being shared on Facebook.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sharing Too Much Online', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sharing Too Much Online', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T00:06:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4113492-6182-5281-8a88-855d9f464672', 'word_count': 164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4113492-6182-5281-8a88-855d9f464672'}, {'abstract': '“Everyday Sunshine,” directed by Lev Anderson and Chris Metzler, recounts the rise and fall of the Los Angeles ska-punk rockers Fishbone.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/movies/everyday-sunshine-the-story-of-fishbone-review.html', 'snippet': '“Everyday Sunshine,” directed by Lev Anderson and Chris Metzler, recounts the rise and fall of the Los Angeles ska-punk rockers Fishbone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the heels of “Beats, Rhymes & Life,” Michael Rapaport’s documentary on the hip-hop ensemble A Tribe Called Quest, comes “Everyday Sunshine,” a fascinating account of another musical group’s rise and fall, here the Los Angeles ska-punk rockers Fishbone. Near-rise might be more accurate, as Fishbone, despite a reputation for breathless live performance (vividly apparent in fleeting clips), has never enjoyed the mainstream breakthrough of regional peers like the Red Hot Chili Peppers.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07EVERY_SPAN/07EVERY_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07EVERY_SPAN/07EVERY_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07EVERY_SPAN/07EVERY_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 753, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07EVERY_SPAN/07EVERY_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07EVERY_SPAN/07EVERY_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Black Rock Band Struggling in a White Milieu', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Everyday Sunshine'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fishbone (Music Group)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T00:08:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/42c4a508-9b0d-5d14-8926-785aaa7d3132', 'word_count': 292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42c4a508-9b0d-5d14-8926-785aaa7d3132'}, {'abstract': 'Chuck Blazer announced that he would step down Dec. 31 from his position as general secretary of the Concacaf region.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/sports/soccer/blazer-quits-regional-post.html', 'snippet': 'Chuck Blazer announced that he would step down Dec. 31 from his position as general secretary of the Concacaf region.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chuck Blazer announced that he would step down Dec. 31 from his position as general secretary of the Concacaf region. Blazer, 66, has served as the No. 2 man in the region since 1990. Last May, Blazer went public with information that his boss, Jack Warner, had arranged a meeting at which Mohammed bin Hammam, the head of the Asian confederation, offered bribes for votes. “I like to think that I accomplished a lot and now it’s time to move on,” Blazer said. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Blazer Quits Regional Post', 'kicker': 'Sports Briefing | Soccer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'American Quits Regional Post', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bribery and Kickbacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Federation of Association Football (FIFA)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blazer, Chuck', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whistle-Blowers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warner, Jack (1943- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T00:08:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e8b5419-ba9a-5095-bc08-0b10a256c154', 'word_count': 83, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e8b5419-ba9a-5095-bc08-0b10a256c154'}, {'abstract': 'Current and former employees said the mood at Apple headquarters after Steve Jobs’s death was a combination of sadness and business as usual.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/on-apples-campus-shock-and-sadness/', 'snippet': 'Current and former employees said the mood at Apple headquarters after Steve Jobs’s death was a combination of sadness and business as usual.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While the world publicly mourned the death of Steve Jobs on Thursday, Apple employees did so more quietly, as befits a company that prizes secrecy. Some employees said the mood at the company’s headquarters in Cupertino, Calif., was a combination of sadness and business as usual.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Apple’s Campus, Shock and Sadness', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jobs, Steven P', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T00:09:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Richtel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richtel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/562bb169-90df-5659-b250-d412f408b73e', 'word_count': 330, 'uri': 'nyt://article/562bb169-90df-5659-b250-d412f408b73e'}, {'abstract': 'The thaw in relations with the United States follows a political transition in Myanmar that has raised the possibility that the new government will ease restrictions on basic freedoms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/world/asia/united-states-aims-to-encourage-change-in-myanmar.html', 'snippet': 'The thaw in relations with the United States follows a political transition in Myanmar that has raised the possibility that the new government will ease restrictions on basic freedoms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The United States is considering a significant shift in its long-strained relationship with the autocratic government of Myanmar, including relaxing restrictions on financial assistance and taking other steps to encourage what senior American officials describe as startling political changes in the country. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/world/07myanmar1/07myanmar1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/world/07myanmar1/07myanmar1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/07/world/07myanmar1/07myanmar1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Detecting a Thaw in Myanmar, U.S. Aims to Encourage Change', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Detecting a Thaw in Myanmar, U.S. Aims to Encourage Change', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Myanmar', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shwe, Than', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aung San Suu Kyi, Daw', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thein Sein', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T00:16:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Lee Myers and Thomas Fuller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Myers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fuller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f723a006-68fb-574d-abd8-e891dcfc4e5c', 'word_count': 1363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f723a006-68fb-574d-abd8-e891dcfc4e5c'}, {'abstract': 'Malpractice reform, if it is done right, is a good thing, but it is best addressed outside the deficit-reduction framework.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/opinion/not-the-job-of-the-supercommittee.html', 'snippet': 'Malpractice reform, if it is done right, is a good thing, but it is best addressed outside the deficit-reduction framework.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The American Medical Association and other physicians’ organizations have asked the Congressional “supercommittee” on deficit reduction to impose limits on malpractice lawsuits. The committee has far bigger problems to tackle, and its task of finding $1.2 trillion in deficit savings will be difficult enough. It needs to be wary of getting dragged into this and other such special pleadings. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Not Their Job', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Not Their Job', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Malpractice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reform and Reorganization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Joint Select Committee on Deficit Reduction', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN MEDICAL ASSN', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T00:17:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/9dc6be00-3a97-5680-8f21-105b07bfaf7a', 'word_count': 292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9dc6be00-3a97-5680-8f21-105b07bfaf7a'}, {'abstract': 'In the documentary “Superheroes,” real-life caped crusaders do everything from help the homeless to accost drug dealers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/movies/superheroes-directed-by-michael-barnett-review.html', 'snippet': 'In the documentary “Superheroes,” real-life caped crusaders do everything from help the homeless to accost drug dealers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fighters for truth, justice and American eccentricity are celebrated in Michael Barnett’s “Superheroes,” a benign documentary portrait of costumed crusaders across the country, chasing evildoers. Among them are Zimmer, leader of the New York Initiative, a team of crime busters conducting late-night “bait patrols” on Brooklyn streets; Mr. Xtreme, a paunchy San Diego security guard camping in his van and wearing a helmet, a cape and goggles; the aging, ebullient Master Legend of Orlando, Fla.; and Thanatos, a 62-year-old in Vancouver, British Columbia, with a black hat, a fearsome green countenance and jeans.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07SUPER_SPAN/07SUPER_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07SUPER_SPAN/07SUPER_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07SUPER_SPAN/07SUPER_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07SUPER_SPAN/07SUPER_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/07/movies/07SUPER_SPAN/07SUPER_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Real-Life Caped Crusaders on a Mission', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Superheroes'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T00:22:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f611b2db-036d-538f-89a9-038bd76474ff', 'word_count': 318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f611b2db-036d-538f-89a9-038bd76474ff'}, {'abstract': 'Antitax pledges are beginning to worry lawmakers who fear that they are interpreted too broadly and hamper efforts to rewrite the nation’s tax code.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/us/politics/anti-tax-pledges-lose-their-sheen-as-eyes-turn-to-reform.html', 'snippet': 'Antitax pledges are beginning to worry lawmakers who fear that they are interpreted too broadly and hamper efforts to rewrite the nation’s tax code.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Representative Jason Chaffetz of Utah has signed a pledge never to raise taxes. He signed another pledge too, one that made it nearly impossible to vote for a bill to raise the nation’s debt ceiling. But right before that vote over the summer, in a meeting with scores of his Republican colleagues, he stood up and proclaimed that he would never sign another pledge.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tax Pledges Lose Allure as Eyes Turn to Reform', 'kicker': 'Congressional Memo', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tax Pledges Lose Allure As Eyes Turn to Reform', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Norquist, Grover G', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T00:25:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Steinhauer', 'person': [{'firstname': 