on of the article.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/opinion/07OPEDpolan/07OPEDpolan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/07/opinion/07OPEDpolan/07OPEDpolan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/opinion/07OPEDpolan/07OPEDpolan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 751, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/opinion/07OPEDpolan/07OPEDpolan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/07/opinion/07OPEDpolan/07OPEDpolan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Against Nostalgia', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Against Nostalgia', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jobs, Steven P', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T01:55:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Daisey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Daisey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cc36b61-904c-5a91-bfee-50f395e91a17', 'word_count': 845, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cc36b61-904c-5a91-bfee-50f395e91a17'}, {'abstract': 'Industry experts said the biography of Steve Jobs could easily sell millions of copies in print, audio and e-book editions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/business/media/book-business-has-big-hopes-for-biography-of-steve-jobs.html', 'snippet': 'Industry experts said the biography of Steve Jobs could easily sell millions of copies in print, audio and e-book editions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It took only hours on Wednesday night for Walter Isaacson’s forthcoming biography of Steve Jobs to shoot up the best-seller list on Amazon.com, eventually moving from No. 384 to the No. 1 spot. On Thursday morning, a book buyer at Powell’s Books in Portland, Ore., quintupled its order of the book, to 500 copies. Simon & Schuster, the publisher, said it was accelerating the publication, moving the release date to Oct. 24 from Nov. 21. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/business/BIOGRAPHY-1/BIOGRAPHY-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1011, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Book Business Sees a Bonanza in a Forthcoming Biography', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Book Business Sees a Bonanza in a Forthcoming Biography', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jobs, Steven P', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Isaacson, Walter', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Simon & Schuster Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T01:56:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7d5b6f2-c7fc-5aeb-a085-8a2586347161', 'word_count': 717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7d5b6f2-c7fc-5aeb-a085-8a2586347161'}, {'abstract': 'A group called Friends of the Children identifies kids who face multiple risks and mentors them for 12 years.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/for-children-at-risk-mentors-who-stay/', 'snippet': 'A group called Friends of the Children identifies kids who face multiple risks and mentors them for 12 years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Samuel was out of control. He cursed at his teacher, refused to do school work, attacked other kids in the schoolyard — and Samuel was still in kindergarten. His home life was chaotic. He’d never met his father. His mother had emotional and drug problems and was unable to care for him. His grandmother did her best. His older brother was involved in violent crime and had been in and out of jail. He taught Samuel to smoke marijuana when he was 6 years old.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Children at Risk, Mentors Who Stay', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mentors', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'CAMPBELL, DUNCAN', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Friends of the Children', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Institute of Child Health and Human Development', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T02:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Bornstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bornstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4f8a4b1-3e40-562e-9c17-e49d03fe89b0', 'word_count': 1942, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4f8a4b1-3e40-562e-9c17-e49d03fe89b0'}, {'abstract': 'A special puzzle from Kevin G. Der.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/06/man/', 'snippet': 'A special puzzle from Kevin G. Der.', 'lead_paragraph': 'UPDATE to the Administrivial Alert! The tribute puzzle is now available for solving in the timed applets. Thank you for your patience!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday: A Great Man', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jobs, Steven P', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T02:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/510f226f-d14e-58b9-876c-5b4b4cc7acff', 'word_count': 522, 'uri': 'nyt://article/510f226f-d14e-58b9-876c-5b4b4cc7acff'}, {'abstract': 'As executives in music, film and publishing learned, a deal with Steve Jobs meant inclusion in one of the most important digital marketplaces on the planet. But the deals were made on Mr. Jobs’s terms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/business/media/master-of-the-media-marketplace-and-its-demanding-gatekeeper.html', 'snippet': 'As executives in music, film and publishing learned, a deal with Steve Jobs meant inclusion in one of the most important digital marketplaces on the planet. But the deals were made on Mr. Jobs’s terms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last November, Steven P. Jobs finally achieved his long-sought goal of bringing the Beatles to iTunes. But years before he made the deal, he began working on the promotional campaign for the music, personally designing advertisements that would sit in virtual mothballs as long as necessary.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/business/MEDIA/MEDIA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 325, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/07/business/MEDIA/MEDIA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 325}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/business/MEDIA/MEDIA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 575, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Master of the Media Marketplace, and Its Demanding Gatekeeper', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Moving On, Minus Its Genius: Master of the Media Marketplace, and Its Demanding Gatekeeper', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jobs, Steven P', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T02:04:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ca6950d-06a1-5c38-b2f1-da6c7eac9f66', 'word_count': 1121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ca6950d-06a1-5c38-b2f1-da6c7eac9f66'}, {'abstract': 'The Yankees are counting on A. J. Burnett’s effort in Game 4 to remind him of how good he can be.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/sports/baseball/yankees-hope-burnett-gained-confidence-along-with-win.html', 'snippet': 'The Yankees are counting on A. J. Burnett’s effort in Game 4 to remind him of how good he can be.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A. J. Burnett’s explanation for his game-winning performance in Game 4 on Tuesday was that he got the ball back from the catcher “and just let it go again.” It was a simple approach that could lead him to more successes with the Yankees.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/sports/pins2/pins2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 959, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Yankees Hope Burnett Gained Confidence Along With Win', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Yankees Hope Burnett Gained Confidence Along With Win', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burnett, A J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Detroit Tigers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leyland, Jim', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T02:04:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2404346e-3d95-5d2b-95ac-df3e905ee7bb', 'word_count': 824, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2404346e-3d95-5d2b-95ac-df3e905ee7bb'}, {'abstract': 'A man who the police believe killed three co-workers and was fatally shot by law enforcement officials on Thursday, ending a manhunt across several Silicon Valley cities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/us/quarry-shooting-suspect-killed-in-california.html', 'snippet': 'A man who the police believe killed three co-workers and was fatally shot by law enforcement officials on Thursday, ending a manhunt across several Silicon Valley cities.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A man who the police believe killed three co-workers and wounded seven other people was fatally shot by law enforcement officials on Thursday, ending a manhunt that had spread out across several Silicon Valley cities. After a 24-hour search that involved dogs, helicopters and hundreds of law officers, three sheriff’s deputies on patrol spotted a man crouching behind a car in Sunnyvale, about 40 miles south of San Francisco, officials said. Sheriff Laurie Smith of Santa Clara County said all three deputies fired at the man after he showed a gun “in a threatening manner” and they recognized him as Shareef Allman, 47. The authorities say Mr. Allman walked into a Cupertino limestone quarry where he worked on Wednesday and opened fire. He then fled and wounded a seventh person while trying to steal her car for his escape, the authorities said. The man shot by the sheriff’s deputies was pronounced dead at the scene. The coroner had not confirmed his identity as of Thursday evening, but Sheriff Smith said the body matched the description of Mr. Allman. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'California: Shooting Suspect Killed', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | WEST', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California: Shooting Suspect Killed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T02:06:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malia Wollan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wollan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2169512-5e98-50ed-87f7-fd75ce75a95f', 'word_count': 179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2169512-5e98-50ed-87f7-fd75ce75a95f'}, {'abstract': 'A growing number of analysts estimate Goldman will slip into the red when it announces third-quarter results.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/business/a-question-of-trimming-pay-at-goldman-sachs-breakingviews.html', 'snippet': 'A growing number of analysts estimate Goldman will slip into the red when it announces third-quarter results.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A rare loss potentially looms for Goldman Sachs. A growing number of analysts estimate that the Wall Street titan will slip into the red when it announces third-quarter results.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/business/Views/Views-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 973, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/business/Views/Views-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/07/business/Views/Views-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Question of Trimming Pay at Goldman Sachs', 'kicker': 'Reuters Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Trimming Pay at Goldman', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T02:06:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By ANTONY CURRIE and PETER THAL LARSEN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Antony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'CURRIE', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'Thal', 'lastname': 'LARSEN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e428222a-18ee-5521-ad21-f51471ac04e0', 'word_count': 693, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e428222a-18ee-5521-ad21-f51471ac04e0'}, {'abstract': 'Tomas Transtromer is a Swedish poet known for his sophisticated simplicity and turbulent stillness.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/arts/tomas-transtromer-an-appreciation.html', 'snippet': 'Tomas Transtromer is a Swedish poet known for his sophisticated simplicity and turbulent stillness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Swedish Academy bestows the Nobel Prize on a Scandinavian poet, it is hard not to be skeptical. After all, the academy has managed to award the prize to three Swedish poets alone, and the last such poet — Harry Martinson in 1974 — was actually a member of the academy at the time. But it would be wrong to scold the Swedes for elevating their countryman Tomas Transtromer. Mr. Transtromer is not only a first-rate artist, but his selection, announced Thursday, corrects an almost 15-year drought for poetry. (The last Nobel laureate to be known mostly for poetry was Wislawa Szymborska, who won in 1996.)', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/07/world/07poet/07poet-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 726, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Following Words Through a Labyrinth', 'kicker': 'An Appreciation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Following Words Through a Labyrinth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nobel Prizes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sweden', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Transtromer, Tomas (1931-2015)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T02:07:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Orr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Orr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d91e5b84-53c0-57cc-9658-2ebaf874b292', 'word_count': 657, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d91e5b84-53c0-57cc-9658-2ebaf874b292'}, {'abstract': 'Banks that charge customers to use debit cards are trying to rationalize one of the largest illegal transfers of wealth from consumers to banks in American history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/opinion/debit-card-fees-are-robbery.html', 'snippet': 'Banks that charge customers to use debit cards are trying to rationalize one of the largest illegal transfers of wealth from consumers to banks in American history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN Bank of America told its customers recently that it would start charging them $5 a month to use debit cards, it argued that it was forced to make that change because of regulations that altered the economics of the cards. Other banks agreed. The chief executive of JPMorgan Chase, Jamie Dimon, put the effects of the regulations this way: “If you’re a restaurant and you can’t charge for the soda, you’re going to charge more for the burger.” Both banks were responding to the Federal Reserve’s actions to limit the interchange fees banks charge stores each time a debit card is used for a purchase. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Charging for Debit Cards Is Robbery', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Charging for Debit Cards Is Robbery', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of America Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debit Cards', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T02:08:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lloyd Constantine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lloyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Constantine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/87f1cefb-2d9f-556c-ac7e-a2554cd1577d', 'word_count': 747, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87f1cefb-2d9f-556c-ac7e-a2554cd1577d'}, {'abstract': 'There has been incredible innovation in information technology, but the pace of change has slowed in other sectors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/opinion/brooks-where-are-the-jobs.html', 'snippet': 'There has been incredible innovation in information technology, but the pace of change has slowed in other sectors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let’s imagine that someone from the year 1970 miraculously traveled forward in time to today. You could show her one of the iPhones that Steve Jobs helped create, and she’d be thunderstruck. People back then imagined wireless communication (Dick Tracy, Star Trek), but they never imagined you could funnel an entire world’s worth of information through a pocket-sized device. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-articleLarge-v2.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subTyp