'The evidence, to date, is not conclusive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Has freer trade decreased or increased poverty and inequality in India? The debate has persisted, almost from the very beginning of India’s embrace of the global economy in 1991.Curiously, also exactly 20 years ago, Americans were engaged in a similar battle of ideas. Mapping its intellectual contours helps give us a fix on the current Indian situation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Has Globalization Helped India’s Poor?', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Friedman, Thomas L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North American Free Trade Agreement', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T08:55:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivek Dehejia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivek', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dehejia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8f394a5-5864-5eb4-b16f-0069f2b08c5e', 'word_count': 918, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8f394a5-5864-5eb4-b16f-0069f2b08c5e'}, {'abstract': 'Who are the Nobel laureates this year in physics, chemistry, medicine and literature?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/2011-nobel-prize-winners-announced/', 'snippet': 'Who are the Nobel laureates this year in physics, chemistry, medicine and literature?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Note: This edition of 6 Q’s uses four articles; each question links to the piece in which answers to that question can be found.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '2011 Nobel Prize Winners Announced', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T08:56:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca Ruiz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ruiz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fcb155c4-c4f3-5206-b6c3-270a893edc54', 'word_count': 126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fcb155c4-c4f3-5206-b6c3-270a893edc54'}, {'abstract': 'Searching for signs of home in footage of the Libyan uprising.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/magazine/lives-smoke-on-the-water.html', 'snippet': 'Searching for signs of home in footage of the Libyan uprising.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A couple of natural-gas flares served as the community weather vane throughout my childhood in the coastal town of Marsa Brega, Libya. We looked to the black smoke that billowed from them morning and night to determine what the beach would be like that day. If the smoke blew out to sea, that meant scorching winds off the Sahara and water like glass, ideal conditions for spear fishing and Sunfish sailing. If it blew inland, it signaled a strong wind off the Mediterranean and rolling breakers, great for body surfing.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09lives/09lives-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 920, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09lives/mag-09lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09lives/mag-09lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Smoke on the Water', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Smoke on the Water', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T09:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil MacFarquhar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacFarquhar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff4582c4-e7fe-5c69-ac57-c81c8bf3061f', 'word_count': 752, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff4582c4-e7fe-5c69-ac57-c81c8bf3061f'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/magazine/reply-all-libya.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One thing that shines clear in all this is the incredible strength and character of the ordinary people of Libya. They have shown the most outstanding examples of courage and humanity I have ever seen in my life, and I will remember this to the end of my days. They deserve peace and prosperity, and I hope they find it soon.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Libya, the Most Surreal Country on Earth', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Most Surreal Country On Earth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T09:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/845c0bc7-2b67-5834-b45c-d1a580e21567', 'word_count': 181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/845c0bc7-2b67-5834-b45c-d1a580e21567'}, {'abstract': 'Reality TV. People in crisis. Going on “The Amazing Race.” And creating the most relatable weirdos in Hollywood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/magazine/stuff-mike-white-likes.html', 'snippet': 'Reality TV. People in crisis. Going on “The Amazing Race.” And creating the most relatable weirdos in Hollywood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2004, Mike White was working on a TV show for Fox, and it was not going well. He’d just had four movies he’d written come out in as many years, among them the critically acclaimed “The Good Girl,” starring Jennifer Aniston, and the commercial hit “School of Rock,” which he wrote for his friend Jack Black, and he felt stressed and overworked. Now White and the network were butting heads over the tone and direction of his new show, which was about a dysfunctional family, but which, White says, the network was trying to push closer to family-friendliness. White felt stuck and misled. “I felt like I was throwing a party, and the party sucked,” he says.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09white1_span/09white1_span-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 804, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09white1_span/09white1_span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09white1_span/09white1_span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stuff Mike White Likes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stuff Mike White Likes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'WHITE, MIKE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T09:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carina Chocano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chocano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Biography', '_id': 'nyt://article/05294055-0b23-5def-bbf1-989912d72a50', 'word_count': 2888, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05294055-0b23-5def-bbf1-989912d72a50'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/magazine/reply-all-mindy-kaling.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mindy Kaling is a wonderful writer, and she’s right on target about catharsis not being art. It’s self-indulgence. Since when did women’s comedy become all about which pretty face could use the dirtiest words? Talk about a Pyrrhic victory for women’s comedy.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mindy Kaling', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mindy Kaling', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaling, Mindy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T09:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/47df6671-f118-5874-8cd5-6de459105326', 'word_count': 50, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47df6671-f118-5874-8cd5-6de459105326'}, {'abstract': 'Unemployment rights; property wrongs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/magazine/the-ethicist-friends-with-benefits.html', 'snippet': 'Unemployment rights; property wrongs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is it unethical for a relatively wealthy person to apply for and receive unemployment compensation, even if he or she meets all the requirements? Is the answer different in times like the present, when government resources are extremely strained? ANONYMOUS, NEW HOPE, PA.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Friends With Benefits', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Friends With Benefits', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment Insurance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T09:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/e82a813b-a84c-5a5e-aeb5-344d39c93b7c', 'word_count': 942, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e82a813b-a84c-5a5e-aeb5-344d39c93b7c'}, {'abstract': 'How the U.N. was persuaded to alter the course of history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/magazine/a-state-is-born-in-palestine.html', 'snippet': 'How the U.N. was persuaded to alter the course of history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sixty-four years ago, in August 1947, the United Nations Special Committee on Palestine presented to the General Assembly a startling and unexpected report, calling for an end to the British Mandate of Palestine and division of most of the territory into two independent states, with the Jewish state occupying the majority of the land. What came next, of course, is well known — a vote in the General Assembly on Nov. 29, 1947, in favor of partition, and the war that immediately followed. The decision is viewed in the Arab world as “the great crime,” and Palestinian leaders, including the current president of the Palestinian Authority, Mahmoud Abbas, regard it as the original sin that led to the catastrophe, the nakba, that befell their nation — a disaster they now want the General Assembly to remedy. What is not widely known is how a possibly pro-Arab committee, or at least one that was supposed to be neutral, came to issue a report that led directly to the establishment of the state of Israel. What happened on that committee’s trip to Palestine, and how were the minds of its members changed in a way that so radically altered history?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09lede_span/mag-09lede-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09lede_span/mag-09lede-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 368}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09lede_span/mag-09lede-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 814, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09lede_span/mag-09lede-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/09/magazine/09lede_span/mag-09lede-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A State is Born in Palestine', 'kicker': 'First', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A State is Born In Palestine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Palestinian Authority', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Assembly (UN)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T09:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ronen Bergman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ronen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bergman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2be3fcf-05a4-5b61-a5c5-4abe284c8a3f', 'word_count': 1330, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2be3fcf-05a4-5b61-a5c5-4abe284c8a3f'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/magazine/reply-all-billy-beane.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Moneyball” fails to note one huge factor in the A’s success — pitching. Mark Mulder, Barry Zito and Tim Hudson were dominant pitchers, but there is little in the book or the movie to suggest how Beane got them, what statistics he saw in them or how they managed to have a collective E.R.A. under 3.00. Get a starting crew like that, and you don’t need much O.B.P.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Billy Beane', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Billy Beane', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beane, Billy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T09:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/763ac76c-f15c-56fd-bf47-45eb04869959', 'word_count': 209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/763ac76c-f15c-56fd-bf47-45eb04869959'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/magazine/reply-all-the-greed-police.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Roger Lowenstein raises the question: Should S.E.C. regulators concentrate on insider-trader cases, which are relatively insignificant but give small investors confidence, or on preventing systemic risk in the financial system? The answer is that it should do both. Regulators are low-paid (in comparison with those they investigate) and are often lawyers who lack the skills to understand the type of securities (e.g., credit default swaps) that led to our recent economic collapse.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Greed Police', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Greed Police', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Securities and Commodities Violations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T09:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/58728bc0-61e9-5519-9a01-dd958486f96e', 'word_count': 266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58728bc0-61e9-5519-9a01-dd958486f96e'}, {'abstract': 'Sebastian Vettel, the championship leader, crashed, and Jenson Button, second in the series, was fastest in both practice sessions. Meanwhile, Bernie Ecclestone bought 3,000 tickets for the tsunami victims.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/vettels-little-accident-in-practice/', 'snippet': 'Sebastian Vettel, the championship leader, crashed, and Jenson Button, second in the series, was fastest in both practice sessions. Meanwhile, Bernie Ecclestone bought 3,000 tickets for the tsunami victims.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SUZUKA, Japan – There was an interesting symmetry in the two first practice sessions Friday in preparation for the Japanese Grand Prix in Suzuka: Jenson Button, the only driver who is still capable of winning the drivers’ championship other than the series’ leader, finished fastest of the day in both the first and second sessions, while Sebastian Vettel, the man who needs only one more point to clinch the title, crashed in the first session.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vettel’s Little Accident in Practice', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Button, Jenson', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ecclestone, Bernie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vettel, Sebastian', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Formula One', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T09:12:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Spurgeon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spurgeon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2633b116-cc7e-58d3-b51c-d9320f94d9b5', 'word_count': 251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2633b116-cc7e-58d3-b51c-d9320f94d9b5'}, {'abstract': 'Sebastian Vettel, the championship leader, crashed, and Jenson Button, second in the series, was fastest in both practice sessions. Meanwhile, Bernie Ecclestone bought 3,000 tickets for the tsunami victims.', 'web_url': 'https://formulaone.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/vettels-little-accident-in-practice/', 'snippet': 'Sebastian Vettel, the championship leader, crashed, and Jenson Button, second in the series, was fastest in both practice sessions. Meanwhile, Bernie Ecclestone bought 3,000 tickets for the tsunami victims.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SUZUKA, Japan – There was an interesting symmetry in the two first practice sessions Friday in preparation for the Japanese Grand Prix in Suzuka: Jenson Button, the only driver who is still capable of winning the drivers’ championship other than the series’ leader, finished fastest of the day in both the first and second sessions, while Sebastian Vettel, the man who needs only one more point to clinch the title, crashed in the first session.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vettel’s Little Accident in Practice', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Button, Jenson', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ecclestone, Bernie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vettel, Sebastian', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Formula One', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T09:12:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Spurgeon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spurgeon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdf1f936-37b9-59f9-a5c0-d15d3bbb9097', 'word_count': 251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdf1f936-37b9-59f9-a5c0-d15d3bbb9097'}, {'abstract': 'President Ellen Johnson Sirleaf of Liberia, her compatriot Leymah Gbowee and Tawakkol Karman of Yemen won the Nobel Peace Prize on Friday for their nonviolent activism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/world/nobel-peace-prize-johnson-sirleaf-gbowee-karman.html', 'snippet': 'President Ellen Johnson Sirleaf of Liberia, her compatriot Leymah Gbowee and Tawakkol Karman of Yemen won the Nobel Peace Prize on Friday for their nonviolent activism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The Nobel Peace Prize for 2011 was awarded on Friday to three women from Africa and the Arab world in acknowledgment of their nonviolent role in promoting peace, democracy and gender equality. The winners were President Ellen Johnson Sirleaf of Liberia — the first woman to be elected president in modern Africa — her compatriot, the peace activist Leymah Gbowee, and Tawakkol Karman of Yemen, a pro-democracy campaigner.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/world/08nobel2-span/08nobel2-span-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 253, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/08/world/08nobel2-span/08nobel2-span-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 253}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nobel Peace Prize Awarded to Three Activist Women', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nobel Peace Prize Awarded to Three Activist Women', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson Sirleaf, Ellen', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gbowee, Leymah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karman, Tawakkol', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nobel Prizes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yemen', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Liberia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T09:30:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Cowell, Laura Kasinof and Adam Nossiter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kasinof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nossiter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0c58bd6-ce92-5912-a266-8e0ed00a56e5', 'word_count': 1974, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0c58bd6-ce92-5912-a266-8e0ed00a56e5'}, {'abstract': 'Steve Jobs told the world to “think different.” What are your own guiding principles?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/what-is-your-personal-credo/', 'snippet': 'Steve Jobs told the world to “think different.” What are your own guiding principles?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steve Jobs is being eulogized the world over as a leader, innovator and risk-taker whose mantras included the Apple motto “Think different.” What are your own guiding principles?  ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Is Your Personal Credo?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T09:40:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23671f54-4db8-5013-9081-6173e73ab31f', 'word_count': 378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23671f54-4db8-5013-9081-6173e73ab31f'}, {'abstract': 'A rematch of last year’s playoff game between the Packers and the Falcons highlights the weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/week-5-n-f-l-matchups-another-turning-point/', 'snippet': 'A rematch of last year’s playoff game between the Packers and the Falcons highlights the weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Packers (4-0) at Falcons (2-2)Sunday, 8:20 p.m.Line: Packers by 6.Tramon Williams’s end-of-half interception return for the Packers against the Falcons in last year’s playoffs may be remembered as a turning point in N.F.L. history. The Falcons were the better team entering the game, and while the Packers held a 21-14 lead before the play, the Falcons were driving and appeared likely to score. Williams’s interception turned the game into a rout, and the Packers have been unbeatable ever since, while the Falcons have replaced them as the team with a young quarterback and lots of talent that cannot quite get its act together.Of course, for some that turning point arrived a little late. “I’m really surprised that it kind of took him so long,” Brett Favre said of the three interminable years it took Aaron Rodgers to lead the Packers to the Super Bowl. Who knew that through all of those quasi-retirements, through his stints with the Jets and the Vikings, Favre was just impatiently waiting for Rodgers to close the deal? Favre did not sound as if \xa0he was trying to insult Rodgers — he has simply lost the ability to compliment people who are not him — but teammates and analysts have rushed to Rodgers’s defense, which he does not really need.It had been peaceful months since the last self-serving Favre rant. What took him so long? Pick: Packers', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Week 5 N.F.L. Matchups: Another Turning Point', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T10:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Tanier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f467d4aa-691e-5bf6-884c-e522cb94dd85', 'word_count': 1980, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f467d4aa-691e-5bf6-884c-e522cb94dd85'}, {'abstract': 'Investment in early childhood education seems to produce a variety of payoffs, including more children who graduate from college, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/from-kindergarten-to-college-completion/', 'snippet': 'Investment in early childhood education seems to produce a variety of payoffs, including more children who graduate from college, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Judith Scott-Clayton is an assistant professor at Teachers College, Columbia University.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Kindergarten to College Completion', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Head Start Program', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T10:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Scott-Clayton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott-Clayton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/765497e5-02bb-584b-bd89-fda6f524cd3a', 'word_count': 718, 'uri': 'nyt://article/765497e5-02bb-584b-bd89-fda6f524cd3a'}, {'abstract': 'Political news from today’s Times, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/the-early-word-double-dip/', 'snippet': 'Political news from today’s Times, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Today’s Times', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Double Dip', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T10:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Southall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Southall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3c62345-c3b5-521d-8a2c-5b88770c969f', 'word_count': 461, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3c62345-c3b5-521d-8a2c-5b88770c969f'}, {'abstract': 'In their final conversation previewing the seasons of the local N.H.L. teams, Jeff Z. Klein and Christopher Botta ponder whether the Devils are ready to return to dominance.', 'web_url': 'https://slapshot.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/2-on-1-with-healthy-parise-and-new-coach-are-devils-the-devils-again/', 'snippet': 'In their final conversation previewing the seasons of the local N.H.L. teams, Jeff Z. Klein and Christopher Botta ponder whether the Devils are ready to return to dominance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In their final conversation previewing the 2011-12 seasons of the local N.H.L. teams, Jeff Z. Klein and Christopher Botta ponder whether the Devils are ready to return to regular-season dominance.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '2-on-1: With Healthy Parise and New Coach, Are the Devils the Devils Again?', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parise, Zach', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Jersey Devils', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T10:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/766e3742-7540-5404-83f3-c284e9679080', 'word_count': 971, 'uri': 'nyt://article/766e3742-7540-5404-83f3-c284e9679080'}, {'abstract': 'A Taj Hotels property in London is taking reservations for a 1,832 square-foot suite designed to evoke the Jaguar brand.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/for-nearly-8000-per-night-a-bed-in-the-jaguars-den/', 'snippet': 'A Taj Hotels property in London is taking reservations for a 1,832 square-foot suite designed to evoke the Jaguar brand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Depending on your perspective, it’s either an 1,832 square-foot hotel room or, in the words of a recently distributed media release, “a way to strengthen synergy” between two British brands. Those brands happen to fall in the portfolio of Tata Group, the parent company of Tata Motors.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Nearly $8,000 Per Night, a Bed in the Jaguar’s Den', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HOTELS AND MOTELS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jaguar Cars Ltd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tata Group', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tata Motors Ltd', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T10:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Czap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Czap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f3173c2-cd57-53c9-b2a7-799b4aeba395', 'word_count': 268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f3173c2-cd57-53c9-b2a7-799b4aeba395'}, {'abstract': 'A new exhibition at the Grand Palais in Paris focuses its attention on the history of toys, from antiquity to the present day.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/toy-exhibition-in-paris-explores-meaning-behind-playthings/', 'snippet': 'A new exhibition at the Grand Palais in Paris focuses its attention on the history of toys, from antiquity to the present day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new exhibition at the Grand Palais in Paris focuses its attention on the history of toys, from antiquity to the present day. Gathering 1,000 playthings of all types, “Of Toys and Men,” on show through Jan. 23, aims to go beyond a simple timeline of dolls and teddy bears, and looks at the stereotypes represented in this miniature version of the grown-up world.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Toy Exhibition in Paris Explores Meaning Behind Playthings', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Toys', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Grand Palais', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T10:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lauren Houssin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lauren', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Houssin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/138fca13-c004-5b87-94ab-ac60fd2b08be', 'word_count': 286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/138fca13-c004-5b87-94ab-ac60fd2b08be'}, {'abstract': 'When a camera’s autofocus is not working, there are a few things to check besides the system settings.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/qa-diagnosing-a-cameras-lack-of-focus/', 'snippet': 'When a camera’s autofocus is not working, there are a few things to check besides the system settings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Diagnosing a Camera’s Lack of Focus', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T10:18:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79a694b8-bbf1-5137-b379-9392a7936908', 'word_count': 220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79a694b8-bbf1-5137-b379-9392a7936908'}, {'abstract': 'How the common puzzle came to be, and how it came to The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/who-made-that-crossword-puzzle/', 'snippet': 'How the common puzzle came to be, and how it came to The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the items that appeared in The New York Sunday World on Dec. 21, 1913: woman in Yonkers is robbed at gunpoint, aviator Glenn Curtiss vows to make the world’s first transatlantic flight and phone stocks rise after AT&T relinquishes its holdings of the Western Union Telegraph Company to mollify the Department of Justice. Next to these stories, the lighthearted Fun supplement introduced that day would not seem to be terribly newsworthy. Yet featured in “Fun” was the world’s first crossword puzzle, introduced as the “Word-Cross.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made That Crossword Puzzle?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T10:25:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Greenbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bfc0cbb-040a-5f6b-8a60-68e32c9b6cda', 'word_count': 462, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bfc0cbb-040a-5f6b-8a60-68e32c9b6cda'}, {'abstract': 'The primary campaign is already highlighting the lack of consensus within the party about the direction of United States foreign policy.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/republican-candidates-start-to-engage-on-foreign-policy/', 'snippet': 'The primary campaign is already highlighting the lack of consensus within the party about the direction of United States foreign policy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Rick Perry appeared unprepared for a debate question last month about how he would respond if Pakistan’s nuclear weapons fell into the hands of the Taliban. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Republican Candidates Start to Engage on Foreign Policy', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bachmann, Michele M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huntsman, Jon M Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Ron', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T10:40:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard W. Stevenson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Stevenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd2420aa-8be7-5956-b4eb-e729939d42c9', 'word_count': 894, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd2420aa-8be7-5956-b4eb-e729939d42c9'}, {'abstract': 'When the sisters took over, “it was kind of a free-for-all and nobody knew what to do.”', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/confused-a-family-business-turns-to-a-coach/', 'snippet': 'When the sisters took over, “it was kind of a free-for-all and nobody knew what to do.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Susan Parker and Erica Rosenfeld, who are sisters, have run BariJay together since 2008. But during the last meeting of our business group, Ms. Parker said they have only recently settled into clearly defined roles that allow them to work together happily and effectively at their company, which manufactures bridesmaid and prom dresses. In the beginning Ms. Parker said, “It was kind of a free-for-all and nobody knew what to do.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Confused, a Family Business Turns to a Coach', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T11:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adriana Gardella', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adriana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gardella', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd17f327-fa3f-5a84-ad34-2aa69cc1946b', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd17f327-fa3f-5a84-ad34-2aa69cc1946b'}, {'abstract': 'The actor Michael Fassbender talks about his role as a sex addict in the drama “Shame,” showing at the New York Film Festival.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/michael-fassbenders-destructive-journey/', 'snippet': 'The actor Michael Fassbender talks about his role as a sex addict in the drama “Shame,” showing at the New York Film Festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The actor Michael Fassbender is enjoying quite a year in his film career. After a Charlotte Brontë-inspired spring and a summer as a superhero turned villain, Mr. Fassbender settles into the fall with two serious films screening at the New York Film Festival. In “A Dangerous Method,” he plays the psychiatrist Carl Jung opposite Viggo Mortensen’s Sigmund Freud. And in another psychological drama, “Shame,” he takes on the role of Brandon, a New York man struggling with sex addiction. In this video interview, Mr. Fassbender talks about how he approached his role in “Shame” and how he defined the character.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Michael Fassbender’s Destructive Journey', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York Film Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fassbender, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T11:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30f3bc84-0029-545e-9818-a31560f93c47', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30f3bc84-0029-545e-9818-a31560f93c47'}, {'abstract': "The world's advanced economies are on the brink of a major slowdown, threatening the Japanese economy, the governor of the Bank of Japan said.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/business/global/japans-central-bank-sounds-warning-on-global-economy.html', 'snippet': "The world's advanced economies are on the brink of a major slowdown, threatening the Japanese economy, the governor of the Bank of Japan said.", 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — The Japanese central bank sounded the alarm over the risks facing the world economy, even as it left its monetary policy unchanged Friday, underscoring the gravity of a global economic slowdown over which policy makers may have little control.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/business/yen/yen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 786, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': "Japan's Central Bank Sounds Warning on Global Economy", 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Japan, Facing Billions More for Rebuilding, Looks for Ways to Pay for It', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T11:10:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type