', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T12:45:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Gonzalez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonzalez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25f594b4-cdce-5998-bb1d-27fd619e7da3', 'word_count': 592, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25f594b4-cdce-5998-bb1d-27fd619e7da3'}, {'abstract': 'The Labor Department said the economy added 103,000 jobs in September, but Fitch Ratings downgraded the government debt of Spain and Italy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/business/daily-stock-market-activity.html', 'snippet': 'The Labor Department said the economy added 103,000 jobs in September, but Fitch Ratings downgraded the government debt of Spain and Italy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After trading in a narrow range for most of the day, the major Wall Street indexes closed slightly lower on Friday as investors weighed modestly positive news about the American labor market against more evidence of troubles in Europe. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shares Fall After Europe Debt Downgrades', 'kicker': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shares Fall After Europe Debt Downgrades', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T12:50:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Brustein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brustein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/efa5b776-a66d-57dc-ad0d-ef990f757d8d', 'word_count': 679, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efa5b776-a66d-57dc-ad0d-ef990f757d8d'}, {'abstract': 'Am I one of them?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/elitist-me/', 'snippet': 'Am I one of them?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aarrgghh! Yves Smith totally misreads what I was saying in today’s column, which presumably means that I didn’t write it very well. When I said that it was the job of policy intellectuals to fill in the details for the Occupy Wall Street protestors, I didn’t mean “don’t worry your pretty little heads about it, we’ll work it out”. I meant job literally as in responsibility: people like Joe Stiglitz and me have an obligation to work on this, helping to translate what justifiably angry citizens are saying into more fleshed-out proposals. That doesn’t mean taking the public out of the loop; it means putting whatever expertise you have to work on the public’s behalf.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Elitist Me?', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T12:55:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c3ff1b5-e5db-5c90-86e8-5f18d6635205', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c3ff1b5-e5db-5c90-86e8-5f18d6635205'}, {'abstract': 'A panel advises against prostate screening for healthy men, why the latest iPhone looks the same, hotels join the flash mob fad and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/friday-reading-panel-advises-no-prostate-test-for-healthy-men/', 'snippet': 'A panel advises against prostate screening for healthy men, why the latest iPhone looks the same, hotels join the flash mob fad and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Reading: Panel Advises No Prostate Test for Healthy Men', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T12:56:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8050c611-2703-5d9c-968b-5caef8a7b7f7', 'word_count': 99, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8050c611-2703-5d9c-968b-5caef8a7b7f7'}, {'abstract': 'Fill in the blanks in the article “Jobs’s Death Draws Outpouring of Grief and Tributes.” Use your own words and phrases, or choose from a scrambled list of the words or phrases that were removed.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/fill-in-tributes-to-steve-jobs/', 'snippet': 'Fill in the blanks in the article “Jobs’s Death Draws Outpouring of Grief and Tributes.” Use your own words and phrases, or choose from a scrambled list of the words or phrases that were removed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Directions: Fill in the blanks in the article “Jobs’s Death Draws Outpouring of Grief and Tributes.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fill-In | Tributes to Steve Jobs', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T12:59:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/707e6033-10af-5ad9-865c-18a7e1c5fb4b', 'word_count': 649, 'uri': 'nyt://article/707e6033-10af-5ad9-865c-18a7e1c5fb4b'}, {'abstract': 'A professional boxer turned designer discusses his respected men’s-wear line.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/the-lowdown-arashi-yanagawa/', 'snippet': 'A professional boxer turned designer discusses his respected men’s-wear line.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Suffice it to say that, in Japanese, Arashi Yanagawa’s first name means “big storm.” The son of a taxi driver, Yanagawa made a name for himself as a professional boxer over a four-year career that included winning the Japanese national championships. Having satisfied his father’s deepest aspirations for his son, Yanagawa moved on to his new passion: fashion design. With no schooling or training, he simply started making clothes, founding the brand John Lawrence Sullivan (named after the Irish boxer) and learning as he went. Seven years later, JLS is one of the more respected men’s-wear companies in Japan and Yanagawa maintains a solid reputation as an excellent designer. Now, he is turning his sights on men’s-wear for women.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Lowdown | Arashi Yanagawa', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T13:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By IO Tillett Wright', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Io', 'middlename': 'Tillett', 'lastname': 'Wright', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f93d99ef-0559-5bfb-84c7-b26bc3cc9d25', 'word_count': 364, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f93d99ef-0559-5bfb-84c7-b26bc3cc9d25'}, {'abstract': 'The very destructive ECB.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/eurotrashers/', 'snippet': 'The very destructive ECB.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kantoos has a righteous rant about the ECB, and the destructiveness of its tight-money obsession. He’s right: if the euro cracks up, the ECB will bear a large share of the blame.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Eurotrashers', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T13:05:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6271d8a4-a710-5e79-a2bc-2ce58dffcb23', 'word_count': 212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6271d8a4-a710-5e79-a2bc-2ce58dffcb23'}, {'abstract': "Mike White's new show on HBO, starring Laura Dern.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/magazine/100000001097433/trailer-for-enlightened.html', 'snippet': "Mike White's new show on HBO, starring Laura Dern.", 'lead_paragraph': "Mike White's new show on HBO, starring Laura Dern.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/06/magazine/video-mag-enlightened/video-mag-enlightened-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/06/magazine/video-mag-enlightened/video-mag-enlightened-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/06/magazine/video-mag-enlightened/video-mag-enlightened-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/06/magazine/video-mag-enlightened/video-mag-enlightened-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Trailer for "Enlightened"', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T13:11:25+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/f8b2b11e-ed37-5754-b326-eb695006f861', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/f8b2b11e-ed37-5754-b326-eb695006f861'}, {'abstract': 'Back to 2007 levels.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/commodity-prices-redux/', 'snippet': 'Back to 2007 levels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Remember commodity prices? Not too long ago, the usual suspects were pointing to rising commodity prices as evidence that runaway inflation was just around the corner. Commodity prices were Exhibit A in claims that Bernanke was “debasing the dollar”; Paul Ryan called for monetary policy that would stabilize the price of a basket of commodities (and therefore require deflation in America whenever China experienced a boom, but hey, never mind).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Commodity Prices Redux', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T13:20:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd2aa19b-66c4-5417-bdfd-84b2da8f09a5', 'word_count': 223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd2aa19b-66c4-5417-bdfd-84b2da8f09a5'}, {'abstract': 'Other quarterbacks have had impressive starts over the years, but six quarterbacks are ahead of record pace this time.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/why-dan-marinos-record-is-going-down/', 'snippet': 'Other quarterbacks have had impressive starts over the years, but six quarterbacks are ahead of record pace this time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Luis DeLoureiro, a friend and contributor to the Fifth Down, is emphatic.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Dan Marino’s Record Is Going Down', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T13:30:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/210fe39e-2999-57d2-8ae6-6edf51a01fb0', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/210fe39e-2999-57d2-8ae6-6edf51a01fb0'}, {'abstract': 'Groupon continued to respond to criticism over its financial disclosures, once again amending the regulatory filing for its highly anticipated public offering.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/appeasing-critics-groupon-revises-i-p-o-disclosure/', 'snippet': 'Groupon continued to respond to criticism over its financial disclosures, once again amending the regulatory filing for its highly anticipated public offering.', 'lead_paragraph': '8:56 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Appeasing Critics, Groupon Revises I.P.O. Disclosure', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Groupon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tencent', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T13:32:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli and Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3b9f259-f9f4-57f8-a800-92c17fe78066', 'word_count': 552, 'uri': 'nyt://