ain': 'Changes for Me and Motherlode', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T15:48:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0765f041-1778-5499-9beb-f99989eed4ba', 'word_count': 558, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0765f041-1778-5499-9beb-f99989eed4ba'}, {'abstract': 'Harry Shearer, who provides the voices of dozens of “Simpsons” characters including Montgomery Burns, Ned Flanders and Kent Brockman, said he would accept a pay cut of 70 percent or more if Fox were willing to let him share in the profits from the show.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/simpsons-voice-actor-speaks-out-about-negotiations-with-fox/', 'snippet': 'Harry Shearer, who provides the voices of dozens of “Simpsons” characters including Montgomery Burns, Ned Flanders and Kent Brockman, said he would accept a pay cut of 70 percent or more if Fox were willing to let him share in the profits from the...', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a star of “The Simpsons,” Harry Shearer provides the voices of dozens of characters on that Fox animated comedy, from the nefarious plutocrat Montgomery Burns to Homer’s gentle next-door neighbor, Ned Flanders. And on Friday Mr. Shearer became the first performer on that long-running series to give voice to the current demands of its acting ensemble, which is locked in a tense negotiation with the 20th Century Fox Television studio while the fate of “The Simpsons” hangs in the balance. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Simpsons’ Voice Actor Speaks Out About Negotiations With Fox', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shearer, Harry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twentieth Century Fox', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T15:54:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e79a76d-6121-546c-b67a-095bf378e2d4', 'word_count': 434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e79a76d-6121-546c-b67a-095bf378e2d4'}, {'abstract': 'An American diplomat apologized for the alleged rape of an South Korean woman by an American soldier, as the U.S. military imposed a monthlong late-night curfew on its soldiers there.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/world/asia/in-south-korea-american-official-apologizes-over-rape-case.html', 'snippet': 'An American diplomat apologized for the alleged rape of an South Korean woman by an American soldier, as the U.S. military imposed a monthlong late-night curfew on its soldiers there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea — A senior American diplomat apologized on Friday in connection with the rape of an 18-year-old South Korean woman for which an American serviceman has been accused, and the United States military imposed a monthlong late-night curfew on its troops in South Korea.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In South Korea, American Official Apologizes Over Rape Case', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Korea: American Official Apologizes for Rape', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee Myung-bak', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Campbell, Kurt M', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roh Moo Hyun', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:02:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c77b19d-7b0a-56c6-b6e5-8df9d45ae613', 'word_count': 600, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c77b19d-7b0a-56c6-b6e5-8df9d45ae613'}, {'abstract': 'Detroit is celebrating its Tigers, particularly the heroics of utility man Don Kelly and pitcher Doug Fister.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/detroit-exhales-celebrates-advance-to-a-l-c-s/', 'snippet': 'Detroit is celebrating its Tigers, particularly the heroics of utility man Don Kelly and pitcher Doug Fister.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — After a tense, deciding Game 5, the Tigers have escaped New York to reach the American League Championship Series to play Texas and, as expected, are dominating the local conversation. Fans and media alike are celebrating the tough series win over the Yankees.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Detroit Exhales, Celebrates Advance to A.L.C.S.', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dombrowski, Dave', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fister, Doug', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Don T', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leyland, Jim', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Young, Delmon', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Detroit Tigers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:05:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne C. Gerstner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Gerstner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4dc11d1-70ae-56f5-ac85-552e39735777', 'word_count': 345, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4dc11d1-70ae-56f5-ac85-552e39735777'}, {'abstract': 'The Institute will add to its vaunted labs program projects that focus on the intersection of film and new media.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/sundance-adds-new-media-projects/', 'snippet': 'The Institute will add to its vaunted labs program projects that focus on the intersection of film and new media.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sundance has expanded its vaunted filmmaker labs program to include projects that focus on the convergence of film and new media technologies. (Think Matthew Barney.) The first New Frontier Story Lab will start Oct. 23 at Sundance’s headquarters in the mountains outside Salt Lake City. Keri Putnam, executive director of the Sundance Institute, called the intersection of film and new media “a new and important field where we felt independent voices needed nurturing and resources.” The inaugural New Frontier workshop, underwritten by Coca-Cola and McCann, will include 11 artists working on projects like “18 Days in Egypt,” an interactive documentary project that will tell the story of the recent Egyptian revolution through a collage of videos, photos, tweets and other electronic media.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sundance Adds ‘New Media’ Projects', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:15:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ddde1b0-60a8-598e-8337-8fb0f9c9f26d', 'word_count': 122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ddde1b0-60a8-598e-8337-8fb0f9c9f26d'}, {'abstract': 'Paul G. Allen, a founder of Microsoft, bought the co-op at 4 East 66th Street.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/big-ticket-sold-for-25000000/', 'snippet': 'Paul G. Allen, a founder of Microsoft, bought the co-op at 4 East 66th Street.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The $25 million sale of a penthouse in an East Side co-op to Paul G. Allen, a founder of the Microsoft Corporation, was the highest of the week, according to city property records.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '