dline': {'main': 'Most Leave Illinois Town After Fiery Train Derailment', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Most Leave Illinois Town After Fiery Train Derailment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroad Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethanol', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evacuations and Evacuees', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Illinois', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:31:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Davey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aeec45c4-4337-5ffe-bd8d-85f82f6c17da', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aeec45c4-4337-5ffe-bd8d-85f82f6c17da'}, {'abstract': 'Renee-Nicole Douceur, a worker at the isolated Amundsen-Scott research station at the South Pole, has been awaiting a rescue plane since she suffered a stroke on Aug. 27.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/science/08southpole.html', 'snippet': 'Renee-Nicole Douceur, a worker at the isolated Amundsen-Scott research station at the South Pole, has been awaiting a rescue plane since she suffered a stroke on Aug. 27.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Renee-Nicole Douceur, the winter manager at the Amundsen-Scott research station at the South Pole, was sitting at her desk on Aug. 27 when she suffered a stroke.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/science/08southpole/08southpole-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/science/08southpole/08southpole-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/08/science/08southpole/08southpole-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Worker at South Pole Station Pushes for a Rescue After a Stroke', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Worker at South Pole Station Pushes for a Rescue After a Stroke', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Pole', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Raytheon Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stroke', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:33:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Douglas Quenqua', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Quenqua', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8c5990b-2590-5f4a-844c-01f63ab366e8', 'word_count': 857, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8c5990b-2590-5f4a-844c-01f63ab366e8'}, {'abstract': 'Russell Banks’s protagonist, a paroled sex offender living under a Florida causeway, befriends a mysterious sociologist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/books/review/lost-memory-of-skin-by-russell-banks-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Russell Banks’s protagonist, a paroled sex offender living under a Florida causeway, befriends a mysterious sociologist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a country now famous for its second acts — the philandering politician, the reformed insider trader, Charlie Sheen — virtually the only American denied another chance is the sexual offender, branded in perpetuity, his whereabouts tracked by the government, a modern-day boogeyman unable by law in many states to live anywhere even remotely habitable. In his 12th novel, “Lost Memory of Skin,” the formidable Russell Banks bravely explores this specific plight, trying to find humanity in people who are feared and despised, frequently for good reason, and raising questions not only about the often horrendous public treatment of these outcasts but also about the ways the larger culture may contribute to their crimes.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/05/books/review/schulman/schulman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 954, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/05/books/review/schulman/schulman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/05/books/review/schulman/schulman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Russell Banks Imagines a Paroled Sex Offender’s Future', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Moral Wilderness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Banks, Russell', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:33:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helen Schulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f510a03a-66f9-5738-9a41-b4e37b87ebc0', 'word_count': 1295, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f510a03a-66f9-5738-9a41-b4e37b87ebc0'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to a recent review of Touré’s book “Who’s Afraid of Post-Blackness: What It Means to Be Black Now.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/books/review/post-blackness.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to a recent review of Touré’s book “Who’s Afraid of Post-Blackness: What It Means to Be Black Now.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Post-Blackness', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Post-Blackness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:33:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/73242ae1-7483-54d9-975d-93bbe403dd18', 'word_count': 199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73242ae1-7483-54d9-975d-93bbe403dd18'}, {'abstract': 'The new novels of both Russell Banks and Helen Schulman feature young people grappling with the moral and legal consequences of sex.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/books/review/up-front-helen-schulman.html', 'snippet': 'The new novels of both Russell Banks and Helen Schulman feature young people grappling with the moral and legal consequences of sex.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the mid-1980s, when Helen Schulman was in graduate school, she took a literary seminar with Russell Banks. “I don’t remember ever raising my hand, so I doubt he would remember me at all,” she said via e-mail. “But when asked to review his new novel, ‘Lost Memory of Skin,’ I jumped at the chance, because of my deep respect for his work and because I thought there were still things I could learn from him. His treatment of the character of the Kid — a virginal sex offender living in a tent under a causeway — is a case in point.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/02/books/review/02upfront/02upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 856, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/02/books/review/02upfront/02upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/02/books/review/02upfront/02upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front: Helen Schulman', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schulman, Helen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:33:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8413e1a4-c649-5cc3-b813-59f34b3d2a24', 'word_count': 298, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8413e1a4-c649-5cc3-b813-59f34b3d2a24'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to a recent essay by Geoffrey Nunberg on the past controversy created by dictionaries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/books/review/not-another-word.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to a recent essay by Geoffrey Nunberg on the past controversy created by dictionaries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Not Another Word', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Not Another Word', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:34:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/76654858-cecb-5d38-9c7b-4ab30658be0f', 'word_count': 382, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76654858-cecb-5d38-9c7b-4ab30658be0f'}, {'abstract': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Roger Ebert’s memoir “Life Itself.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/books/review/looks-arent-everything.html', 'snippet': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Roger Ebert’s memoir “Life Itself.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Looks Aren’t Everything', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Looks Aren’t Everything', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:34:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/409628f8-bc09-5fdf-91c2-da9248c9824c', 'word_count': 188, 'uri': 'nyt://article/409628f8-bc09-5fdf-91c2-da9248c9824c'}, {'abstract': 'Damir Sagolj was part of a group of journalists invited to document the food crisis in North Korea’s farm belt late in September. His images, captured on a government-led tour, present a stark view of a rarely-seen region.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/a-view-of-hunger-in-north-korea/', 'snippet': 'Damir Sagolj was part of a group of journalists invited to document the food crisis in North Korea’s farm belt late in September. His images, captured on a government-led tour, present a stark view of a rarely-seen region.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Damir Sagolj, a Bangkok-based Reuters photographer, was among a group of journalists invited last week by North Korea’s Economy and Trade Information Center to document the food crisis in the country’s farm belt. North Korea has appealed for food aid after a harsh winter and a series of summer floods and storms, but so far, only 30 percent of a United Nations aid target has been met.  ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A View of Hunger in North Korea', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:34:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kerri MacDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kerri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b05572cc-fdbd-51bf-9230-b4c1b4d7120c', 'word_count': 955, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b05572cc-fdbd-51bf-9230-b4c1b4d7120c'}, {'abstract': 'Bruce Jay Friedman — writer, editor, actor — reflects on an era.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/books/review/lucky-bruce-by-bruce-jay-friedman-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Bruce Jay Friedman — writer, editor, actor — reflects on an era.', 'lead_paragraph': '“You’d think that someone born in the thick of the Great Depression would have a sad story to tell,” Bruce Jay Friedman writes, “but mine isn’t one of them.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/09LELAND/09LELAND-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 409, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/09LELAND/09LELAND-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 409}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/09LELAND/09LELAND-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 699, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/09LELAND/09LELAND-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/09LELAND/09LELAND-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside Bruce Jay Friedman’s Pulp Arcadia', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Good Enough Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Friedman, Bruce Jay', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Careers and Professions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:36:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Leland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a3b04d1-278f-5ffe-9d40-cc18848bcbbc', 'word_count': 898, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a3b04d1-278f-5ffe-9d40-cc18848bcbbc'}, {'abstract': 'Julia Scheeres follows the experiences of five Peoples Temple members who went to Jonestown.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/books/review/a-thousand-lives-by-julia-scheeres-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Julia Scheeres follows the experiences of five Peoples Temple members who went to Jonestown.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Julia Scheeres describes her new book, “A Thousand Lives,” as the “untold story” of Jonestown, the Peoples Temple settlement in the jungles of Guyana where on Nov. 18, 1978, the Rev. Jim Jones orchestrated the deaths of more than 900 Americans. Well, not exactly untold. Over the past three decades, the massacre has been explored in numerous books and even several television documentaries. Yet the story does bear retelling, not least for those too young to recall the shock that swept the world at the time, but also because Scheeres adds insights harvested from some 50,000 pages of letters, journals and other documents found in Jonestown and recently released by the F.B.I. The result is a gripping account of how decent people can be taken in by a charismatic and crazed tyrant.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/09RIDING/09RIDING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 794, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/09RIDING/09RIDING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/09RIDING/09RIDING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Jonestown Massacre Revisited', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Demonic Almighty', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jonestown (Guyana)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suicides and Suicide Attempts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scheeres, Julia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poisoning and Poisons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:37:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Riding', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Riding', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/df2c018a-2558-54f5-8ead-1d572f3aa518', 'word_count': 892, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df2c018a-2558-54f5-8ead-1d572f3aa518'}, {'abstract': 'Developments in physics have the potential to radically change our understanding of the world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/books/review/knocking-on-heavens-door-by-lisa-randall-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Developments in physics have the potential to radically change our understanding of the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lisa Randall is a professor of physics at Harvard and one of the more original theorists at work in the profession today. In the fancifully titled “Knocking on Heaven’s Door,” her second book for a popular audience, she has two avowed aims: first, to explain where physics might be headed now that the Large Hadron Collider — the enormous particle accelerator on the Swiss-French border — is finally up and running; and second, to air her views on the nature of science, its fraught relations with religion, and the role of beauty as a guide to scientific truth. Her book thus alternates between the nitty-gritty of particle physics and meditations of a more rarefied sort. Stitching the whole thing together are passages recounting the author’s globe-trotting adventures: accepting the key to the city from the mayor of Padua, chatting up a scientifically curious actor on a flight to Los Angeles, attending the Barcelona premiere of an opera about physics for which she had written the libretto. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/09HOLT/09HOLT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 723, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/09HOLT/09HOLT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/09HOLT/09HOLT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Will the Large Hadron Collider Explain Everything?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Disturbances in the Field', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Large Hadron Collider', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Physics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gravitation and Gravity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Particle Accelerators', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Randall, Lisa', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:38:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Holt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/df1da4c5-c931-5e6a-808d-ff7181ae2522', 'word_count': 1221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df1da4c5-c931-5e6a-808d-ff7181ae2522'}, {'abstract': 'In this novel, an editor from the Caribbean makes a life in Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/books/review/boundaries-by-elizabeth-nunez-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In this novel, an editor from the Caribbean makes a life in Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Imagine this dystopian scene from the publishing world: Windsor, a New York industry giant, has been struggling to stay afloat. Among its moneymaking stratagems is Equiano, an imprint for writers of color. There is nary a Toni Morrison title in sight. Raine, “the queen of black exotica,” and the thug-life raconteur B. Benton become the imprint’s best-selling authors. “Urban lit” and “ghetto lit,” chick lit’s gritty cousins, begin to rake in millions for Windsor, whose blond editor in chief heads a staff of sycophants convinced that soft-core porn sells books.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/SLUTZKY-sub/SLUTZKY-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 819, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/SLUTZKY-sub/SLUTZKY-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/SLUTZKY-sub/SLUTZKY-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Caribbean Editor Tries to Give ‘Urban Lit’ a Makeover', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Islands', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nunez, Elizabeth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Caribbean Area', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:39:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zoë Slutzky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zoë', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Slutzky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f3709cd-37fc-5f05-aee5-01c3480e6133', 'word_count': 624, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f3709cd-37fc-5f05-aee5-01c3480e6133'}, {'abstract': 'In this novel, a programmer unmoored by the death of his grandfather returns to the town where he spent childhood summers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/books/review/luminous-airplanes-by-paul-la-farge-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In this novel, a programmer unmoored by the death of his grandfather returns to the town where he spent childhood summers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the startling things about getting older is that somewhere along the line, members of your generation ascend to public life: they run for office, land jobs that aren’t modified by the word “assistant,” write novels. In this last instance, they do the rest of us a service by tracing the trajectory that a swath of people cut through history, and vice versa.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/Schultz/Schultz-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/Schultz/Schultz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/Schultz/Schultz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Novel of Flying Machines, Apocalyptics and the San Francisco Internet Boom', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Looking Up, Looking Back', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:41:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Schulz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/db43b094-3d88-5ba9-910f-f335e83462ed', 'word_count': 1278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db43b094-3d88-5ba9-910f-f335e83462ed'}, {'abstract': 'The figures in a famous photograph of the segregated South come to complicated life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/books/review/elizabeth-and-hazel-by-david-margolick-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'The figures in a famous photograph of the segregated South come to complicated life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In September 1957, Elizabeth Eckford and Hazel Bryan were captured in the sickening news photograph that prompted this book. Will Counts, a sharecropper’s son and young hire at The Arkansas Democrat, took the picture, laying bare the disgrace of the Jim Crow South. In it, Elizabeth, a sheltered, scholarly teenager in a homemade dress, walks past Little Rock Central High School while Hazel, a student there, marches behind her, spewing racist abuse.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/09FINNERTY/09FINNERTY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/09FINNERTY/09FINNERTY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/09FINNERTY/09FINNERTY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/09/books/review/09FINNERTY/09FINNERTY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Photo That Exposed Segregation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Aftermath', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eckford, Elizabeth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Segregation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Margolick, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brown v Board of Education (Supreme Court Decision)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Little Rock (Ark)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Little Rock Central High School', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T16:41:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Finnerty', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finnerty', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b11a7e7c-abcd-552a-a535-49776c05a1b1', 'word_count': 773, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b11a7e7c-abcd-552a-a535-49776c05a1b1'}, {'abstract': 'Eisenstein and more Eistenstein, Lev Kuleshov and Mikhail Kalatozov are among the directors represented on a new boxed set of Soviet-era cinema.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/movies/homevideo/landmarks-of-early-soviet-film-come-to-dvd.html', 'snippet': 'Eisenstein and more Eistenstein, Lev Kuleshov and Mikhail Kalatozov are among the directors represented on a new boxed set of Soviet-era cinema.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a time in the mid-to-late 1920s the art of the cinema meant only one thing to the serious-minded film critics of America and Europe: Soviet-style montage, or the art of cutting shots together in a way that would produce ideas and emotions beyond those expressed in the images themselves.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The N