act number, “He Is an English Man,” kicks off the new PBS Arts Fall Festival.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/arts/09PBS1_span/09PBS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/09/arts/09PBS1_span/09PBS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/arts/09PBS1_span/09PBS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 647, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/arts/09PBS1_span/09PBS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/09/arts/09PBS1_span/09PBS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From PBS, a Sampler of the Arts', 'kicker': 'Television', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From PBS, A Sampler Of the Arts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting Service', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T18:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de4f5ca2-2b00-5a23-8e3c-dcc83f52775f', 'word_count': 1073, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de4f5ca2-2b00-5a23-8e3c-dcc83f52775f'}, {'abstract': 'The retro-soul singer Mayer Hawthorne describes his favorite performers, including Hall and Oates and Black Opera and the singer Barbara Mason.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/arts/music/mayer-hawthorne-faves-barbara-mason-3-sounds-black-opera.html', 'snippet': 'The retro-soul singer Mayer Hawthorne describes his favorite performers, including Hall and Oates and Black Opera and the singer Barbara Mason.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE smooth retro-soul singer Mayer Hawthorne was born just a few years ago in Los Angeles, the alter-ego of the D.J. and producer Andrew Mayer Cohen, 32. On a lark he recorded a few tracks in his bedroom, convincingly styling himself as a 1960s Motown crooner despite the fact that he is a white kid from Ann Arbor, Mich. His debut, “A Strange Arrangement,” was a surprise seller for the indie label Stones Throw in 2009. Now he’s signed to a major label, Universal Republic, which will release his sophomore album, “How Do You Do,” on Tuesday. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/arts/09PLAYLIST1/09PLAYLIST1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/arts/09PLAYLIST1/09PLAYLIST1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/09/arts/09PLAYLIST1/09PLAYLIST1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Soul Selections for Hip-Hop Heads', 'kicker': 'Playlist | Mayer Hawthorne', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Soul Selections for Hip-Hop Heads', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hawthorne, Mayer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T18:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/e15939ba-3761-5e7b-abc8-913800086026', 'word_count': 1018, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e15939ba-3761-5e7b-abc8-913800086026'}, {'abstract': 'What we’re reading this weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://bittman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/food-links-for-the-info-famished/', 'snippet': 'What we’re reading this weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’re keeping tabs on Denmark’s “fat tax” (which took effect last Saturday). Here are my thoughts, and those of the Washington Post. Also, New Zealand is in the grip of an obesity epidemic, but health minister Tony Ryall has ruled out a fat tax, citing the economy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Food Links for the Info-Famished', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Labor Department (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "McDonald's Corporation", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Michelin Group', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India'