g a (dude-heavy) crowd.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Modern Bromance', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T19:30:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Whitney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Whitney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb9854f3-35aa-5e56-84c7-855558395a42', 'word_count': 256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb9854f3-35aa-5e56-84c7-855558395a42'}, {'abstract': 'News media coverage of the Occupy Wall Street protests has increased since demonstrators clashed with the police, and is beginning to rival coverage given to Tea Party protests in 2009.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/police-clashes-spur-coverage-of-wall-street-protests/', 'snippet': 'News media coverage of the Occupy Wall Street protests has increased since demonstrators clashed with the police, and is beginning to rival coverage given to Tea Party protests in 2009.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Occupy Wall Street protests, by design, began with little in the way of concrete goals or strategy. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Police Clashes Spur Coverage of Wall Street Protests', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea Party Movement', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T19:31:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1fcdcc3-6d54-5997-b70b-41dbd6d259e7', 'word_count': 892, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1fcdcc3-6d54-5997-b70b-41dbd6d259e7'}, {'abstract': 'As the battle is joined over fees, the big banks want to make you pay, and the most aggressive of the little institutions want to pay you.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/your-money/brokerage-and-bank-accounts/consumers-caught-in-middle-of-checking-account-wars-your-money.html', 'snippet': 'As the battle is joined over fees, the big banks want to make you pay, and the most aggressive of the little institutions want to pay you.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, Senator Dick Durbin took to the Senate floor and called for a run on Bank of America. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/your-money/brokerage-and-bank-accounts/08money/08money-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 799, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/your-money/brokerage-and-bank-accounts/08money/08money-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/08/your-money/brokerage-and-bank-accounts/08money/08money-thu