aves to denounce Bank of America’s new $5 monthly debit card fee this week, I got curious about what was going to become of the various rewards checking accounts I’ve written about in the last year or two.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'PerkStreet, Kasasa and the War Over Your Checking Account', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debit Cards', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of America Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-07T20:32:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Lieber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lieber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/721ed4c2-06c8-56ea-bc05-35d025ca4bec', 'word_count': 237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/721ed4c2-06c8-56ea-bc05-35d025ca4bec'}, {'abstract': 'Three women win the 2011 Nobel Peace Prize: Liberian president Ellen Johnson-Sirleaf, her compatriot Leymah Gbowee and Yemeni pro-democracy activist Tawakul Karman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/multimedia/100000001099122/timescast--october-7-2011.html', 'snippet': 'Three women win the 2011 Nobel Peace Prize: Liberian president Ellen Johnson-Sirleaf, her compatriot Leymah Gbowee and Yemeni pro-democracy activist Tawakul Karman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three women win the 2011 Nobel Peace Prize: Liberian president Ellen Johnson-Sirleaf, her compatriot Leymah Gbowee and Yemeni pro-democracy activist Tawakul Karman.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'