, 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/nyregion/Y-JP-LAYOFFS/Y-JP-LAYOFFS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/08/nyregion/Y-JP-LAYOFFS/Y-JP-LAYOFFS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '672 School Jobs Are Lost in Largest Single-Agency Layoff Under Bloomberg', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '672 School Jobs Are Lost in Largest Single-Agency Layoff Under Bloomberg', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Layoffs and Job Reductions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-08T00:34:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fernanda Santos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fernanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Santos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7df0c47d-6fbb-5247-8564-f81934c7442e', 'word_count': 1022, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7df0c47d-6fbb-5247-8564-f81934c7442e'}, {'abstract': 'The choreographer Akram Khan’s first full-length solo work opened on Tuesday night at Sadler’s Wells in London.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/arts/dance/desh-from-akram-khan-at-sadlers-wells-review.html', 'snippet': 'The choreographer Akram Khan’s first full-length solo work opened on Tuesday night at Sadler’s Wells in London.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The ever-present tensions between history and identity, the collective past and the personal present; the paradoxical state of being that is the condition of the second-generation immigrant. These ideas are at the heart of Akram Khan’s finest choreography. And “Desh,” his first full-length solo work, which opened here at Sadler’s Wells on Tuesday, is undoubtedly among the best pieces that Mr. Khan has created. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/arts/DESH/DESH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 290, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/08/arts/DESH/DESH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 290}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/arts/DESH/DESH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 495, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/arts/DESH/DESH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/08/arts/DESH/DESH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Silk Monsoons Cascade Into a Homeland Tale', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Silk Monsoons Cascade Into a Homeland Tale', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Khan, Akram', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-08T00:37:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roslyn Sulcas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roslyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sulcas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/abd5deff-b3c7-5c87-9747-2aa8eb537176', 'word_count': 575, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abd5deff-b3c7-5c87-9747-2aa8eb537176'}, {'abstract': 'A revival of David Gordon’s “Dancing Henry Five” acknowledges its own insufficiency to portray “Henry V.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/arts/dance/david-gordons-dancing-henry-five-review.html', 'snippet': 'A revival of David Gordon’s “Dancing Henry Five” acknowledges its own insufficiency to portray “Henry V.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONTCLAIR, N.J. — How can this demonstrate that? How can art demonstrate life or history or another work of art? With the speech “O for a muse of fire,” Shakespeare starts his play “Henry V” with words about the challenge for theater of trying to represent war. (“Think, when we talk of horses, that you see them.”) When Laurence Olivier made his 1944 film of “Henry V” he quickly bypassed the issue of theater, showing his audience real horses, real arrows, real armor, real grass; and he commissioned music by William Walton to help make the movie exciting and affecting.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/arts/GORDON/GORDON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/arts/GORDON/GORDON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/08/arts/GORDON/GORDON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ships, Steeds and Kings, on Two Legs', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ships, Steeds and Kings, on Two Legs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gordon, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-08T00:39:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee6cdda6-0250-5ff2-93c8-ca6a0980730d', 'word_count': 747, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee6cdda6-0250-5ff2-93c8-ca6a0980730d'}, {'abstract': 'Varian Fry and Ernst Hanfstaengl’s 1935 encounter and their actions during World War II spurred Richard Kaplan to tackle their lives and histories in the film “Varian and Putzi.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/movies/varian-and-putzi-a-20th-century-tale-review.html', 'snippet': 'Varian Fry and Ernst Hanfstaengl’s 1935 encounter and their actions during World War II spurred Richard Kaplan to tackle their lives and histories in the film “Varian and Putzi.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'On July 17, 1935, The New York Times published a statement by Varian Fry in which that young American, the editor of Living Age magazine, described anti-Jewish rioting that had taken place in Berlin two nights earlier. His account was horrific, and not only because of what it foreshadowed: ordinary Germans joining Nazi thugs in what he characterized as a “holiday mood” of bloody anti-Semitic violence. “Old men and young men,” Fry wrote, “boys, Storm Troops, police, young girls of the domestic servant type, well-bred women, some even in the forties and over — all seemed to be having a good time.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/arts/TWENTY1/TWENTY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 922, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/arts/TWENTY1/TWENTY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/08/arts/TWENTY1/TWENTY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Horrors of a Coming War Painted in a 1935 Meeting', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Varian and Putzi'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Horrors of a Coming War Painted in a 1935 Meeting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-08T00:41:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/95442162-7c4f-5040-b258-46fff7a02b41', 'word_count': 625, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95442162-7c4f-5040-b258-46fff7a02b41'}, {'abstract': 'Tiger Woods, the former No. 1-ranked golfer, started the day in danger of missing consecutive cuts for the first time in his pro career.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/sports/golf/tiger-woods-makes-the-cut-and-expects-victory.html', 'snippet': 'Tiger Woods, the former No. 1-ranked golfer, started the day in danger of missing consecutive cuts for the first time in his pro career.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN MARTIN, Calif. — The fog that delayed the start of Tiger Woods’s second round for over two and half hours on Friday eventually lifted, and so did the gloom enveloping his game. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/sports/SUB-GOLF/SUB-GOLF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 634, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/sports/SUB-GOLF/SUB-GOLF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/08/sports/SUB-GOLF/SUB-GOLF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Woods Plays Better and Has the Same Expectation: Victory', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Better Day for Woods in Return', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woods, Tiger', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Casey, Paul', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-08T00:44:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88c02cc4-94b2-58ea-8460-fca72e10fdbe', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88c02cc4-94b2-58ea-8460-fca72e10fdbe'}, {'abstract': 'In a sign of potential conflict to come, the musicians of the New York Philharmonic have voted to authorize their representatives to call a strike, two orchestra members said on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/arts/music/philharmonic-union-authorizes-strike.html', 'snippet': 'In a sign of potential conflict to come, the musicians of the New York Philharmonic have voted to authorize their representatives to call a strike, two orchestra members said on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a sign of potential conflict to come, the musicians of the New York Philharmonic, above, have voted to authorize their representatives to call a strike, two orchestra members said on Friday. The players spoke on condition of anonymity because orchestra leaders ordered the members not to discuss the matter. The musicians’ contract expired on Sept. 20, but they have continued to perform, not an unusual practice. Several musicians say the atmosphere seems tenser than in the past, amid fears that a diminished contract could induce players to leave for teaching jobs or other top-tier orchestras like the Boston Symphony Orchestra, the Los Angeles Philharmonic and the Chicago Symphony. At the same time, the orchestra has 16 openings. Members of the orchestra’s negotiating committee declined to comment. In a statement the orchestra’s management said the strike authorization vote, held last week, was “standard procedure” and had no effect on its desire to reach an agreement. “While there are serious issues under consideration, we are hopeful that a new agreement will be reached, and that the orchestra will continue to perform according to the published concert schedule,” management said. Both sides “are currently engaged in negotiations,” it added, but orchestra officials declined to elaborate or to provide details on contacts between the sides.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Philharmonic Union Authorizes Strike', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Philharmonic Union Authorizes Strike', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-08T00:44:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a041199-3fa4-5604-af55-10b781dce12e', 'word_count': 215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a041199-3fa4-5604-af55-10b781dce12e'}, {'abstract': 'Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg has become increasingly critical of the Wall Street protests, even while repeatedly defending the right of people to demonstrate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/nyregion/for-bloomberg-occupy-wall-street-evokes-vietnam-era-protests.html', 'snippet': 'Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg has become increasingly critical of the Wall Street protests, even while repeatedly defending the right of people to demonstrate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The simmering discontent that has provoked the Occupy Wall Street protests has prompted numerous historical comparisons. Some are calling the movement a liberal version of the Tea Party. Others see a repeat of the 1930s populism that accompanied the New Deal.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/nyregion/BLOOMBERG/BLOOMBERG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/08/nyregion/BLOOMBERG/BLOOMBERG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/08/nyregion/BLOOMBERG/BLOOMBERG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Mayor, ‘Occupy Wall Street’ Evokes Protests From Vietnam Era', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Bloomberg, ‘Occupy Wall Street’ Evokes Protests From Vietnam Era', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vietnam War', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-08T00:45:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f346a301-580d-5ccb-8653-ca482dcf46ca', 'word_count': 675, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f346a301-580d-5ccb-8653-ca482dcf46ca'}, {'abstract': 'Just in time for people to catch up with “The Walking Dead,” the AMC series that begins its second season on Oct. 16, the first season is now streaming on Netflix.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/arts/television/life-for-walking-dead.html', 'snippet': 'Just in time for people to catch up with “The Walking Dead,” the AMC series that begins its second season on Oct. 16, the first season is now streaming on Netflix.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just in time for people to catch up with “The Walking Dead,” the AMC series that begins its second season on Oct. 16, the first season is now streaming on Netflix. The drama, about survivors of a zombie infestation, is the most prominent part of a multiyear content-licensing deal announced Friday by Netflix and AMC. The arrangement is the latest display of Netflix’s determination to license back-seasons of top television shows, diversifying its streaming service beyond feature films. This year the company struck a deal with Lionsgate, the producer of AMC’s “Mad Men,” to stream previous seasons of that show. Some other AMC Networks shows will also be streamed on Netflix “just prior to the premiere of subsequent seasons,” according to a news release. Those include “Portlandia” on IFC, “Braxton Family Values” on WE and “All on the Line With Joe Zee” on the Sundance Channel. Netflix did not disclose the terms of the deal, but it has been willing to pay substantial amounts for other shows and films.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Life for ‘Walking Dead’', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Life for ‘Walking Dead’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Netflix Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMC Networks Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-08T00:45:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d92f9198-b9fc-507d-9f6d-8d8cd32857f3', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d92f9198-b9fc-507d-9f6d-8d8cd32857f3'}, {'abstract': 'Prosecutors said they would concentrate on large, for-profit operations that use the medical law as a cover for large-scale drug operations, with marijuana being sent across state lines.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/us/california-to-crack-down-on-medical-marijuana.html', 'snippet': 'Prosecutors said they would concentrate on large, for-profit operations that use the medical law as a cover for large-scale drug operations, with marijuana being sent across state lines.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Federal officials on Friday warned dozens of marijuana dispensaries throughout California to shut down or face civil and criminal action as part of a major crackdown on the state’s growing medical marijuana industry.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Attorneys in California Set Crackdown on Marijuana', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. Attorneys In California Set Crackdown On Marijuana', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medical Marijuana', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Attorneys', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-08T00:47:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Medina', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Medina', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15ea75ee-f472-5468-b74d-ee790611c790', 'word_count': 686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15ea75ee-f472-5468-b74d-ee790611c790'}, {'abstract': 'The good that Democratic health care reforms do for Americans is undeniable. It is heartening to see President Obama defend the policy with no apologies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/opinion/the-president-embraces-obamacare.html', 'snippet': 'The good that Democratic health care reforms do for Americans is undeniable. It is heartening to see President Obama defend the policy with no apologies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As part of his newfound assertiveness, President Obama is using the term “Obamacare,” the Republicans’ sneering dismissal of the Democratic health care reforms. We are skeptical that he will be able to turn it into a term of approbation. But it is heartening to see him finally out there defending his pioneering policy with no apologies and an uncharacteristic feistiness. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Embracing His Inner ‘Obamacare’', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Embracing His Inner ‘Obamacare’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reform and Reorganization', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-08T00:47:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/e924927e-fce9-54ca-90eb-b6d8f0b489e8', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e924927e-fce9-54ca-90eb-b6d8f0b489e8'}, {'abstract': 'Red Light New Music played works using with toneless effects, by Christopher Cerrone, Chaya Czernowin and others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/07/arts/music/red-light-new-music-plays-christopher-cerrone-review.html', 'snippet': 'Red Light New Music played works using with toneless effects, by Christopher Cerrone, Chaya Czernowin and others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Red Light New Music’s thematic concerts can seem either inventive or fanciful, depending on whether a listener is persuaded that the works at hand touch on the program’s unifying notion. The group’s season opener at the Leonard Nimoy Thalia on Monday evening was called “In a Landscape: Music as Map,” and it was meant, according to the season prospectus, to include works “influenced by physical environments, from the arctic to the urban to the volcanic.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Musical Themes, Covering Landscapes', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Red Light New Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-08T00:50:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a15e79a3-eb8b-5d6f-8c13-a1515c2cf0be', 'word_count': 546, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a15e79a3-eb8b-5d6f-8c13-a1515c2cf0be'}, {'abstract': 'Senator Charles E. Grassley on Friday joined with dozens of academic researchers, consumer groups and journalism organizations in protesting a decision to pull off the Web a database o