l in 2014 has finally begun.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Act IV for U.S. and Klinsmann', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klinsmann, Jurgen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Major League Soccer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "United States Men's National Soccer Team", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Miami (Fla)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-08T14:04:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e92b581d-65db-5b5d-94bb-dd4a6f60003d', 'word_count': 557, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e92b581d-65db-5b5d-94bb-dd4a6f60003d'}, {'abstract': 'Self-fulfilling panic, illustrated.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/the-backwards-s-of-doom-wonkish/', 'snippet': 'Self-fulfilling panic, illustrated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Greg Ip reports on a Bank Credit Analyst analysis of the euro crisis that emphasizes the possibility of multiple equilibria; it’s similar in spirit to what I’ve been saying, but with a niftier picture:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Backwards S Of Doom (Wonkish)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-08T14:06:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cedd3047-ba64-503b-84e4-dd2d74dfc13f', 'word_count': 95, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cedd3047-ba64-503b-84e4-dd2d74dfc13f'}, {'abstract': 'On China and US manufacturing.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/08/re-shoring/', 'snippet': 'On China and US manufacturing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ugly new term, but apparently the new thing; it refers to companies moving production back to America from China. The fact that such stories are being heard is support for those (including me) demanding action on the renminbi, because they show that there is indeed a margin at which changes in Chinese dollar costs move production back to the United States, and hence that the undervalued renminbi directly costs US jobs, as well as having the indirect effects I’ve written about recently. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Re-Shoring', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-08T14:13:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4af50f63-a3dc-576f-8a9d-3c2237649d85', 'word_count': 83, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4af50f63-a3dc-576f-8a9d-3c2237649d85'}, {'abstract': 'Next up for the Big City Book Club is “This Beautiful Life,” the latest novel by Helen Schulman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/nyregion/big-city-book-club-privilege-and-strife.html', 'snippet': 'Next up for the Big City Book Club is “This Beautiful Life,” the latest novel by Helen Schulman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Next up for the Big City Book Club is “This Beautiful Life,” the latest novel by Helen Schulman. The book revolves around a digital pornography scandal set amid the immense privileges and antagonisms of the New York private-school world. Join Ginia Bellafante, the Big City columnist, for a live online discussion of the novel on Tuesday, Oct. 18, from 1 p.m. to 4 p.m. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/nyregion/16CLUB/16CLUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/nyregion/16CLUB/16CLUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/09/nyregion/16CLUB/16CLUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Big City Book Club: Privilege and Strife', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Coming: Privilege and Strife', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schulman, Helen', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-08T14:34:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da747c55-641b-595d-8950-182737a48820', 'word_count': 64, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da747c55-641b-595d-8950-182737a48820'}, {'abstract': 'The Tarrytown Music Hall is offering a series of concerts that feature some of the leading writers and interpreters of songs from the ‘60s and ‘70s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/nyregion/judy-collins-and-stephen-stills-among-singers-at-tarrytown.html', 'snippet': 'The Tarrytown Music Hall is offering a series of concerts that feature some of the leading writers and interpreters of songs from the ‘60s and ‘70s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR music lovers of a certain age and inclination, the late-1960s love affair between Judy Collins and Stephen Stills remains an epic tale, and the piece Mr. Stills wrote about it — “Suite: Judy Blue Eyes” — retains its hallowed place in the canon. Little wonder, then, that Ms. Collins, in a recent conversation, grew animated when discussing the song. ', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/nyregion/09ARTSWE-1_SPAN/09ARTSWE-1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/09/nyregion/09ARTSWE-1_SPAN/09ARTSWE-1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/nyregion/09ARTSWE-1_SPAN/09ARTSWE-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/09/nyregion/09ARTSWE-1_SPAN/09ARTSWE-1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/09/nyregion/09ARTSWE-1_SPAN/09ARTSWE-1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Leading Interpreters of a Turbulent Era', 'kicker': 'Arts | Westchester', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Leading Interpreters of a Turbulent Era', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Westchester County (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Folk Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Sixties', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tarrytown (NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-08T15:16:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Lutz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lutz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1d63939-3660-5962-8d31-590d65588add', 'word_count': 1007, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1d63939-3660-5962-8d31-590d65588add'}, {'abstract': 'Seventeen artists, some as famous as their subject, have works in “15 Minutes: Homage to Andy Warhol” at the Pollock-Krasner House and Study Center in East Hampton.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/nyregion/15-minutes-homage-to-andy-warhol-in-east-hampton-art-review.html', 'snippet': 'Seventeen artists, some as famous as their subject, have works in “15 Minutes: Homage to Andy Warhol” at the Pollock-Krasner House and Study Center in East Hampton.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Andy Warhol was obsessed with fame. You might say that it was his only consistent medium, and that everything else he did — painting, film, sculpture, publishing — was just a vehicle for examining its properties and effects. Now the Pollock-Krasner House and Study Center has taken his most famous utterance, “In the future, everyone will be world-famous for 15 minutes,” and used it as a springboard to create an exhibition. ', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url