', 'kicker': 'Sports of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Giants Still Own City’s Hearts, but Jets Are Closing In Fast', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Rex', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Franchises', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Records and Achievements', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-09T21:36:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By William C. Rhoden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Rhoden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8c6942b-bbfe-5286-ab4e-b64a111a1816', 'word_count': 1203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8c6942b-bbfe-5286-ab4e-b64a111a1816'}, {'abstract': 'The rapper Mos Def joined the Brooklyn Philharmonic in a performance of Frederic Rzewski’s “Coming Together,” written after the 1971 Attica prison uprising.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/arts/music/brooklyn-philharmonic-festival-with-mos-def-review.html', 'snippet': 'The rapper Mos Def joined the Brooklyn Philharmonic in a performance of Frederic Rzewski’s “Coming Together,” written after the 1971 Attica prison uprising.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When an orchestra wants to play outside it usually ends up in a park, a pastoral setting for picnicking and music that is familiar and not too demanding: Bernstein’s “West Side Story” Suite, say, or Mozart’s “Jupiter” Symphony. The audience is generally as white as the wine.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/Brook/Brook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 284, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/Brook/Brook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 284}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/Brook/Brook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 484, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rap in and of Brooklyn, With the Rumble of the Subway Trains', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rap in and of Brooklyn, With the Rumble of the Subway Trains', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Philharmonic', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mos Def', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rzewski, Frederic', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-09T21:48:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8feed1e0-2d0d-57d1-8fe2-01c9ff8d8cbd', 'word_count': 509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8feed1e0-2d0d-57d1-8fe2-01c9ff8d8cbd'}, {'abstract': '“Unauthorized: The Harvey Weinstein Project,” from the director Barry Avrich, is a virtual tribute to the man who co-founded Miramax Films.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/movies/unauthorized-the-harvey-weinstein-project-review.html', 'snippet': '“Unauthorized: The Harvey Weinstein Project,” from the director Barry Avrich, is a virtual tribute to the man who co-founded Miramax Films.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Taking its cue from a subject who’s always been able to sell sizzle sans steak, “Unauthorized: The Harvey Weinstein Project” is a bit of bait and switch. While its title implies a tawdry tell-all, this documentary actually delivers a virtual tribute to Mr. Weinstein, who co-founded Miramax Films, helped revolutionize American independent cinema and cultivated a reputation as a dyspeptic bull in the china shop of art-house sensibilities. Mr. Weinstein may not have authorized the director and producer Barry Avrich’s rather pedestrian film. But he won’t be throwing any telephones.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Harvey Weinstein Is Under the Microscope Again', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Unauthorized: The Harvey Weinstein Project'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miramax Films', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weinstein, Harvey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-09T21:49:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fe6e929-3cba-5a82-91d7-bdba56262b6e', 'word_count': 420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fe6e929-3cba-5a82-91d7-bdba56262b6e'}, {'abstract': 'For the first show of its season, the Composer Portraits series at the Miller Theater devoted a program to Tobias Picker, providing an overview of his development.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/arts/music/tobias-picker-at-the-miller-theater-review.html', 'snippet': 'For the first show of its season, the Composer Portraits series at the Miller Theater devoted a program to Tobias Picker, providing an overview of his development.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The philosophy behind the Composer Portraits series at the Miller Theater of Columbia University runs contrary to the kind of program you will find on almost every other concert stage in town, any day of the week. Instead of mixing works by different composers to form juxtapositions that illuminate each (or not), the Miller series generally provides a handful of pieces by a single composer, providing in its best instances a useful and nuanced overview of one artist’s development.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/Picker/Picker-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 808, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Composer’s Repertory in Broad Brush Strokes', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Composer’s Repertory In Broad Brush Strokes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Picker, Tobias', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-09T21:55:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5dbad3e2-25fa-5dd1-a659-4d3114026152', 'word_count': 517, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5dbad3e2-25fa-5dd1-a659-4d3114026152'}, {'abstract': '“Enlightened,” a new HBO series about a woman recovering from a nervous breakdown, embraces the absurdities of its subject with enough compassion to avoid outright parody.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/arts/television/enlightened-stars-laura-dern-on-hbo-review.html', 'snippet': '“Enlightened,” a new HBO series about a woman recovering from a nervous breakdown, embraces the absurdities of its subject with enough compassion to avoid outright parody.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is anger management, but does anyone ever do anything about excessive joy?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/jp-enlightened/jp-enlightened-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 343, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/jp-enlightened/jp-enlightened-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 343}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/jp-enlightened/jp-enlightened-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 592, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/jp-enlightened/jp-enlightened-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/jp-enlightened/jp-enlightened-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bad Day? Some Om Will Cure It', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bad Day? Some Om Will Cure It', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dern, Laura', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'WHITE, MIKE', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-09T22:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alessandra Stanley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alessandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stanley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8452b4aa-523f-5b02-9faa-8de44b82c6df', 'word_count': 884, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8452b4aa-523f-5b02-9faa-8de44b82c6df'}, {'abstract': 'Ian C. Livengood delivers a therapeutic dose of puzzle.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/deelish/', 'snippet': 'Ian C. Livengood delivers a therapeutic dose of puzzle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY’S PUZZLE — This one went down very smoothly. A nice Monday grid that fell in great big chunks, and as soon as I sussed out the CC part of the theme, my brain said, “Oh, I really hope there are 10 of these! ‘Ten cc’ would be such a great theme!”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday: Dee-lish!', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-09T22:00:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7aa7cf89-b58b-5882-9def-e362091f4581', 'word_count': 195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7aa7cf89-b58b-5882-9def-e362091f4581'}, {'abstract': 'The 49th New York Film Festival is fostering a sense of spontaneity, with a mystery sneak preview of an as-yet-unannounced film.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/movies/new-york-film-festival-includes-a-sneak-preview.html', 'snippet': 'The 49th New York Film Festival is fostering a sense of spontaneity, with a mystery sneak preview of an as-yet-unannounced film.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In its 49th incarnation, well into respectable middle age, the New York Film Festival has experienced a growth spurt and a burst of youthful energy. The Film Society of Lincoln Center, which hosts the festival, expanded its footprint last summer, with the opening of the Elinor Bunin Munroe Film Center, a street-level miniplex across West 65th Street from the lofty terrace of the Walter Reade Theater. The festival’s offerings have expanded accordingly, with more classic films and retrospectives, a new documentary program and a larger selection of Views From the Avant-Garde, in addition to the 27 features in the main slate.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/Festival-1/Festival-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 933, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/Festival-1/Festival-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/Festival-1/Festival-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Film Fest Gleams Mysterious This Year', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Film Fest Gleams Mysterious This Year', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York Film Festival', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-09T22:05:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f14a2a7-2d10-513f-9995-aee43bbe1850', 'word_count': 1140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f14a2a7-2d10-513f-9995-aee43bbe1850'}, {'abstract': 'Data will include retail sales for September and the August trade deficit, and companies including Alcoa, JPMorgan Chase and Google will report quarterly earnings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/business/economic-reports-for-the-week-of-oct-10.html', 'snippet': 'Data will include retail sales for September and the August trade deficit, and companies including Alcoa, JPMorgan Chase and Google will report quarterly earnings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ECONOMIC REPORTS Data will include the trade deficit for August and weekly jobless claims (Thursday); and retail sales for September, import prices for September, the Thomson Reuters/University of Michigan consumer sentiment index for October and business inventories for August (Friday). ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Economic Reports for the Week Ahead', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Looking Ahead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-09T22:12:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/928b377c-647c-55bf-b698-3d3c6776238d', 'word_count': 217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/928b377c-647c-55bf-b698-3d3c6776238d'}, {'abstract': 'The deal is the latest by Chinese energy companies flocking to acquire stakes in Canada’s oil-rich resources.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/sinopec-to-buy-daylight-energy-for-2-1-billion/', 'snippet': 'The deal is the latest by Chinese energy companies flocking to acquire stakes in Canada’s oil-rich resources.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sinopec of China agreed on Sunday to buy Daylight Energy, a Canadian oil and natural gas producer, for about $2.1 billion in cash, as Chinese companies increasingly flock to Canada’s oil-rich resources.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sinopec to Buy Daylight Energy for $2.1 Billion', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sinopec Corp', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-09T22:16:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ef3ab43-8278-5dc1-9c25-82b317aa59ff', 'word_count': 277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ef3ab43-8278-5dc1-9c25-82b317aa59ff'}, {'abstract': 'When you have a 4-4 fit in a no-trump contract, you will get at most four tricks from the suit. But if that is your trump suit, you will usually get an extra trick by ruffing a loser in one hand or the other.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/crosswords/bridge/bridge-an-opening-lead-is-steered-by-the-bidding.html', 'snippet': 'When you have a 4-4 fit in a no-trump contract, you will get at most four tricks from the suit. But if that is your trump suit, you will usually get an extra trick by ruffing a loser in one hand or the other.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you have a 4-4 fit in a no-trump contract, you will get at most four tricks from the suit. But if that is your trump suit, you will usually get an extra trick by ruffing a loser in one hand or the other. This is why players like 4-4 trump fits. However, methods like Stayman that search for a 4-4 fit suffer from one disadvantage: if a 4-4 fit is not found, the defenders have been given free information about declarer’s hand.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tipoff From the Bidding Results in a Different Lead', 'kicker': 'Bridge', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Tipoff From the Bidding Results in a Different Lead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-09T22:18:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0cd26af-b638-5961-861e-07cfde6d9fc7', 'word_count': 433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0cd26af-b638-5961-861e-07cfde6d9fc7'}, {'abstract': 'Three weeks into the new television season, prime-time schedule changes are beginning to pile up, as four new shows effectively have been canceled', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/arts/television/more-tv-shows-canceled.html', 'snippet': 'Three weeks into the new television season, prime-time schedule changes are beginning to pile up, as four new shows effectively have been canceled', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three weeks into the new television season, prime-time schedule changes are beginning to pile up, as four new shows effectively have been canceled. CBS confirmed Saturday that it had halted production on the sitcom “How to Be a Gentleman,” with David Hornsby and Kevin Dillon. That came a day after the network decided to move the sixth season of “Rules of Engagement” into its Thursday time slot. “Gentleman” joins the NBC sitcom “Free Agents” and the CW reality show “H8R,” both of which were pulled from their slots on Thursday. Earlier in the week NBC made “The Playboy Club” the first victim of the fall season; the new drama “Prime Suspect” will run in its 10 p.m. Monday time slot until Oct. 31, when the newsmagazine “Rock Center With Brian Williams” starts.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More TV Shows Canceled', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More TV Shows Canceled', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-09T22:22:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/2105af07-3732-5558-bb29-f87b19150b16', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2105af07-3732-5558-bb29-f87b19150b16'}, {'abstract': 'A reader responds to an article on private security companies profiting from the detention of immigrants.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/opinion/immigrant-detainees.html', 'snippet': 'A reader responds to an article on private security companies profiting from the detention of immigrants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Immigrant Detainees', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Immigrant Detainees', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration Detention', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-09T22:27:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/93de14c2-3909-5d8f-bd6a-9f22faf33f1e', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93de14c2-3909-5d8f-bd6a-9f22faf33f1e'}, {'abstract': 'Laissez-faire won’t work.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/financial-romanticism/', 'snippet': 'Laissez-faire won’t work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One line I’ve been seeing in various places, including comments here, is the claim that the real way to deal with Wall Street is laissez-faire economics: no more bailouts! On this view, policy makers should raise their right hand in the air, place their left hand on a copy of Atlas Shrugged, and swear in the name of A is A that they will never again step in to rescue failing banks. And all will be well with the world.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Financial Romanticism', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-09T22:27:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7f9dcc3-8fbc-555b-a88d-c54f13ece1a5', 'word_count': 349, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7f9dcc3-8fbc-555b-a88d-c54f13ece1a5'}, {'abstract': 'Two early films from the British-Ghanian documentarian John Akomfrah are on view at the Maysles Cinema in Harlem, one eyeing black history, the other the future.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/movies/seven-songs-for-malcolm-x-the-last-angel-of-history-review.html', 'snippet': 'Two early films from the British-Ghanian documentarian John Akomfrah are on view at the Maysles Cinema in Harlem, one eyeing black history, the other the future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two early films from the British-Ghanian documentarian John Akomfrah are on view at the Maysles Cinema in Harlem, one eyeing black history, the other the future. Both are thought-provoking, and they coincide with the premiere of this director’s latest work, “The Nine Muses,” at the Museum of Modern Art.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/Seven/Seven-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 378, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/Seven/Seven-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 378}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/Seven/Seven-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1024, '