aterial': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b117b42-62ad-5ba9-a22b-1948b100615f', 'word_count': 336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b117b42-62ad-5ba9-a22b-1948b100615f'}, {'abstract': 'The main raison d’être of the Broadway revival of Terence Rattigan’s “Man and Boy” is the occasion it gives Frank Langella to explore the pathology of power.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/theater/reviews/man-and-boy-with-frank-langella-review.html', 'snippet': 'The main raison d’être of the Broadway revival of Terence Rattigan’s “Man and Boy” is the occasion it gives Frank Langella to explore the pathology of power.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Frank Langella plays good, he’s fine; but when he’s bad, he’s a wonder. Having ditched the halo he wore as the sainted Sir Thomas More in “A Man for All Seasons” in 2008, Mr. Langella — the only actor to star on Broadway as both Dracula and Richard Nixon — has stepped back into the dark side in style. And this time he’s a villain that New York audiences should really love to hate: a soulless financier who wreaks havoc with other people’s money.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/MAN/MAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/MAN/MAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 391}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/MAN/MAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 713, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/MAN/MAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/MAN/MAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Art of Wreaking Havoc With Other People’s Money', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Man and Boy'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Art of Wreaking Havoc With Other People’s Money', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rattigan, Terence', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roundabout Theater Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Langella, Frank', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Driver, Adam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T02:01:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a60aa215-6e73-5913-9aa6-611f1bfd0e48', 'word_count': 1266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a60aa215-6e73-5913-9aa6-611f1bfd0e48'}, {'abstract': 'A team of elderly baseball players, a delay on a train and other slices of life in the city in Metropolitan Diary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/nyregion/a-good-samaritan-fumbles-with-an-umbrella.html', 'snippet': 'A team of elderly baseball players, a delay on a train and other slices of life in the city in Metropolitan Diary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DEAR DIARY:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Good Samaritan Fumbles With an Umbrella', 'kicker': 'Metropolitan Diary', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Good Samaritan Fumbles With an Umbrella', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T02:05:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/705d7f63-0027-5e99-b122-905e1187e488', 'word_count': 666, 'uri': 'nyt://article/705d7f63-0027-5e99-b122-905e1187e488'}, {'abstract': 'Mean streets, epic waves and that oceanic feeling. A short film.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/09/freud-on-the-beach-wave-theory/', 'snippet': 'Mean streets, epic waves and that oceanic feeling. A short film.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Being and Nothingness,” Jean-Paul Sartre recommends skiing as a model of human behavior. But the trouble with skiing, says Sartre (with a slightly paranoid swerve) is that you leave tracks behind you. “The ideal form of sliding,” he argues, “would therefore be one that leaves no trace: that is, sliding over water”‘ He might have been thinking of water-skiing, of course, but I have always liked to suppose it was surfing he had in mind.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Freud on the Beach: Wave Theory', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surfing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freud, Sigmund', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sartre, Jean-Paul', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T02:05:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organiza