nal': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/c43736ea-1507-5527-869e-6b75c37fd9e2', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/c43736ea-1507-5527-869e-6b75c37fd9e2'}, {'abstract': 'Top stories Monday morning.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/newswallah-in-the-english-language-press-monday-2/', 'snippet': 'Top stories Monday morning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The government has agreed to make public the audio recordings of the proceedings of the Joint Drafting Committee constituted to frame the Lokpal Bill, in response to a query filed by an activist under the Right to Information Act, The Hindu reports.The anti-corruption activist Anna Hazare is “above Parliament and had the right to threaten to go on fast to get bills passed,” his adviser Arvind Kejriwal said Sunday, marking a radical change from the civil society group’s position so far, The Times of India reports. Kejriwal justified his decision to campaign against the Congress Party in the an upcoming state election, “saying it was the ruling party’s duty to ensure passage of the Jan Lokpal Bill”, the paper reports.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Newswallah: In the English-Language Press Monday', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hazare, Anna', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Indian National Congress', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T04:31:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nikhila Gill', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nikhila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gill', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94636284-9df5-506f-a72b-1639b089ac3c', 'word_count': 434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94636284-9df5-506f-a72b-1639b089ac3c'}, {'abstract': 'Maria Velez-Clarke reflected on how her school has tried to maintain its emphasis on collaborative learning, arts education and democracy in the accountability era.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/education/10office.html', 'snippet': 'Maria Velez-Clarke reflected on how her school has tried to maintain its emphasis on collaborative learning, arts education and democracy in the accountability era.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maria Velez-Clarke has for 18 years been principal of the Children’s Workshop School in the East Village, Public School 361, one of several small schools started in the 1990s by people who had worked at the widely praised Central Park East School.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/nyregion/office/office-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/10/nyregion/office/office-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/nyregion/office/office-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/nyregion/office/office-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/10/nyregion/office/office-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Keeping a ‘Pity Party’ Short, and Pushing Hard to Improve', 'kicker': 'Principal’s Office: Maria Velez-Clarke', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Keeping a ‘Pity Party’ Short, and Pushing Hard to Improve', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Principals (School)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East Village (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T04:41:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b98c00a5-3d03-570e-9a4f-dfd71046db6b', 'word_count': 793, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b98c00a5-3d03-570e-9a4f-dfd71046db6b'}, {'abstract': 'Lisa Edelstein, along with Nina Arianda and Dallas Roberts, show up this week on “The  Good Wife,” but is the show on slow boil?', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/good-wife-watch-the-return-of-cuddy-er-lisa-edelstein/', 'snippet': 'Lisa Edelstein, along with Nina Arianda and Dallas Roberts, show up this week on “The  Good Wife,” but is the show on slow boil?', 'lead_paragraph': 'So just how quickly can the writers and producers of “The Good Wife” move? In Sunday night’s episode, “Get a Room,” Eli Gold did crisis management on an outbreak of listeria possibly caused by Wisconsin cheese. The current real-life listeria outbreak caused by Colorado cantaloupes became national news less than two weeks ago. That’s some fancy dancing. (Of course, there was an E. coli outbreak in Wisconsin in mid-September, so maybe it was just a matter of subbing “listeriosis” in the script.) The resolution was ripped from a different headline: a pie in a chief executive’s face, a la Rupert Murdoch.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Good Wife’ Watch: The Return of Cuddy, Er, Lisa Edelstein', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T05:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92cf6047-16ba-5063-87ba-826872bb36ba', 'word_count': 829, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92cf6047-16ba-5063-87ba-826872bb36ba'}, {'abstract': 'Can India produce a Steve Jobs? Samanth Subramanian asks a question on many lips in India after the death of the iconic chief executive.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/where-is-indias-steve-jobs/', 'snippet': 'Can India produce a Steve Jobs? Samanth Subramanian asks a question on many lips in India after the death of the iconic chief executive.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where is India’s Steve Jobs?', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jobs, Steven P', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Infosys Technologies Ltd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bangalore (India)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T05:45:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samanth Subramanian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samanth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Subramanian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/299d713c-fa92-57c2-93ea-cb7aa0d4e5e0', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/299d713c-fa92-57c2-93ea-cb7aa0d4e5e0'}, {'abstract': 'In an effort to track the long-term health effects of the nuclear disaster at Fukushima, Japan has begun a survey of local children for thyroid abnormalities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/world/asia/japan-studies-radiation-effects-on-children.html', 'snippet': 'In an effort to track the long-term health effects of the nuclear disaster at Fukushima, Japan has begun a survey of local children for thyroid abnormalities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — In an effort to track the long-term health effects of the nuclear disaster at Fukushima, Japan has begun a survey of local children for thyroid abnormalities, a problem associated with exposure to radiation.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Japan, a Long-Term Study on Radiation Leaks’ Effects', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Japan, a Long-Term Study On Radiation Leaks’ Effects', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Thyroid Gland', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radiation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T09:31:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk