 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susannah L. Griffee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susannah', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Griffee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6669d19-66b3-574c-a56e-74a40d98d8aa', 'word_count': 801, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6669d19-66b3-574c-a56e-74a40d98d8aa'}, {'abstract': 'Things will get moving in Amsterdam when the annual Amsterdam Dance Event takes over the city from Oct. 19 to 22, with 700 DJ’s and musicians performing in 52 venues around the city.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/festival-explores-amsterdam-club-life-by-day-and-night/', 'snippet': 'Things will get moving in Amsterdam when the annual Amsterdam Dance Event takes over the city from Oct. 19 to 22, with 700 DJ’s and musicians performing in 52 venues around the city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Things will get moving in Amsterdam when the annual Amsterdam Dance Event takes over the city from Oct. 19 to 22, with 700 D.J.’s and musicians performing in 52 venues around the city.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Festival Explores Amsterdam Club Life by Day and Night', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disc Jockeys', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'NIGHTCLUBS AND CABARETS', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AMSTERDAM (NETHERLANDS)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T10:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Valerie Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Valerie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66631f23-ecd2-5727-bd37-9ba8dd4505a2', 'word_count': 234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66631f23-ecd2-5727-bd37-9ba8dd4505a2'}, {'abstract': 'On the day that an article is published about how infrequently kickers miss extra points, two kickers miss extra points.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/so-much-for-perfection-two-extra-points-missed/', 'snippet': 'On the day that an article is published about how infrequently kickers miss extra points, two kickers miss extra points.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Do I owe kickers Mike Nugent and Olindo Mare an apology? On Sunday, the Times published an article  I wrote on how infrequently kickers miss extra points.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'So Much for Perfection: Two Extra Points Missed', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T10:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Seminara', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seminara', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3c74cf6-41c0-5f74-bcd1-14f555719346', 'word_count': 326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3c74cf6-41c0-5f74-bcd1-14f555719346'}, {'abstract': 'I interviewed Tony Blair in his suite at New York’s Carlyle Hotel on the steamy afternoon of September 23rd. He was in New York for the meeting of the UN General Assembly in his capacity as Middle East Envoy, a post announced just hours after he stepped down as British Prime Minister in 2007.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/tony-blair-they-keep-insinuating-and-i-keep-saying-its-not-true/', 'snippet': 'I interviewed Tony Blair in his suite at New York’s Carlyle Hotel on the steamy afternoon of September 23rd. He was in New York for the meeting of the UN General Assembly in his capacity as Middle East Envoy, a post announced just hours after he s...', 'lead_paragraph': 'I interviewed Tony Blair in his suite at New York’s Carlyle Hotel on the steamy afternoon of Sept. 23. He was in New York for the meeting of the U.N. General Assembly in his capacity as Middle East envoy, a post announced just hours after he stepped down as British prime minister in 2007. Blair had a short window between official meetings to sit for an interview to promote the paperback publication of his 2010 memoir, “A Journey.” Sadly he did not have enough time — nor did the Carlyle have an extra room — to allow photographer Christian Oth and his team to set their seamless backdrop anywhere or anytime else. So there we were, a trim, tanned, tieless former prime minister, his press secretary, an aide, a book publicist, a photographer and his two assistants and a reporter, all occupying one room in which the air conditioning seemed not to be functioning. At the conclusion of the interview, a flustered Cherie Blair rushed in to join the party, apparently after overhearing from the next room too many questions concerning reports that had run in the conservative tabloid that has dogged the Blairs for decades. “I couldn’t resist coming in after hearing all that Daily Mail nonsense,” she declared. Here’s what was left on the cutting room floor.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tony Blair: ‘They Keep Insinuating and I Keep Saying it’s Not True’', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blair, Tony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Conservative Party (Great Britain)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of the World', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T10:06:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6dbf989-24b6-5610-b9f6-1bef6e61dcfb', 'word_count': 978, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6dbf989-24b6-5610-b9f6-1bef6e61dcfb'}, {'abstract': 'Wedding band musicians endure increasing competition and little respect.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/when-the-music-stops/', 'snippet': 'Wedding band musicians endure increasing competition and little respect.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wedding season is just around the corner, and soon the streets of Delhi will be full of marching brass bands in colorful, shiny, ill-fitting jackets and pants. No Indian wedding seems to be complete without sonorous trumpets and drums playing classic Hindustani songs or the latest Bollywood item numbers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When the Music Stops', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Delhi (India)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T10:20:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Poh Si Teng', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Poh', 'middlename': 'Si', 'lastname': 'Teng', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/376490f1-eed1-513e-aabd-32706da73486', 'word_count': 64, 'uri': 'nyt://article/376490f1-eed1-513e-aabd-32706da73486'}, {'abstract': 'The Giants’ recent games seem to boil down to one person: the raw receiver Victor Cruz.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/the-victor-cruz-show-the-good-and-the-ugly/', 'snippet': 'The Giants’ recent games seem to boil down to one person: the raw receiver Victor Cruz.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Giants \xa0games boil down to Victor Cruz. At least it has seemed that way recently. He caught two touchdown passes to help beat the Eagles. He lived to tell the tale of  laying the ball on the turf even though he wasn’t touched against Arizona. And he had a spectacular catch against the Seahawks but also played a role in two crucial turnovers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Victor Cruz Show, the Good and the Ugly', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cruz, Victor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T10:30:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88473963-0118-5234-88a5-dc4c6146c0db', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88473963-0118-5234-88a5-dc4c6146c0db'}, {'abstract': 'Plus: How effective is your e-mail marketing?', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/this-week-in-small-business-a-40-percent-chance/', 'snippet': 'Plus: How effective is your e-mail marketing?', 'lead_paragraph': 'What’s affecting me, my clients and other small-business owners this week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week in Small Business: A 40 Percent Chance', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wall Street (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T11:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gene Marks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8edc43d-3a89-5d66-b6fa-b577a5af54d8', 'word_count': 1550, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8edc43d-3a89-5d66-b6fa-b577a5af54d8'}, {'abstract': 'The Season 3 premiere of HBO’s “Bored to Death” on Monday reunites viewers with Jonathan, Ray and George in contemporary boho New York where they all struggle with various sorts of daddy issues.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/channel-surfing-bored-to-death/', 'snippet': 'The Season 3 premiere of HBO’s “Bored to Death” on Monday reunites viewers with Jonathan, Ray and George in contemporary boho New York where they all struggle with various sorts of daddy issues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s no other comedy on television quite like HBO’s “Bored to Death.” It’s simultaneously old-fashioned and amazingly up to the minute, a gumshoe fantasy set in a very real world: the Brooklyn brownstones and cafes and Manhattan co-op apartments and watering holes that shelter what passes for literary culture in 21st-century New York. The novelist Jonathan Ames, the show’s creator, works out his love-hate relationship to the city’s writing life (and those who live it) with a mix of whimsy and precise observation that can make even the most cleverly written network sitcoms — “Modern Family,” “Community” — seem mass produced and one-size-fits-all in comparison.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Channel Surfing: ‘Bored to Death’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AMES, JONATHAN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Danson, Ted', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Galifianakis, Zach', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T11:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ebbefdd2-e972-5a3c-82d9-43aac518e59d', 'word_count': 617, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ebbefdd2-e972-5a3c-82d9-43aac518e59d'}, {'abstract': 'In this week’s Complaint Box: The dangers posed by food delivery workers riding motorized bicycles.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/complaint-box-electric-delivery-bikes/', 'snippet': 'In this week’s Complaint Box: The dangers posed by food delivery workers riding motorized bicycles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like everyone else, I like to order pizza or other takeout food and have it at my door and still warm in 15 to 20 minutes. But a recent encounter with a deliveryman on a bicycle that had been souped up with an electric motor — the type that is becoming ubiquitous in neighborhoods throughout the city — alerted me to the perils to pedestrians that the drivers of these illegally motorized vehicles can cause.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Complaint Box | Electric Delivery Bikes', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Delivery Services', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T11:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jerry Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d9dd960-3970-567d-808b-fd1f27167728', 'word_count': 521, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d9dd960-3970-567d-808b-fd1f27167728'}, {'abstract': 'The Patriots put the finishing touches on the Jets’ miserable road swing by running the ball and daring the Jets to stop them. They couldn’t.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/final-dagger-on-0-3-road-trip-for-jets/', 'snippet': 'The Patriots put the finishing touches on the Jets’ miserable road swing by running the ball and daring the Jets to stop them. They couldn’t.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the Jets knew before Sunday’s game that they would hold Wes Welker in check for the most part while limiting Tom Brady to one touchdown pass and intercepting him once, they probably would have signed up for it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Final Dagger on  0-3 Road Trip for Jets', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Patriots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T11:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Bretherton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bretherton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4afda7ab-7981-5fbb-9b25-e8705576c028', 'word_count': 371, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4afda7ab-7981-5fbb-9b25-e8705576c028'}, {'abstract': 'The prime minister asks how a ship could have plowed into a well- documented reef in calm waters.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/on-our-radar-oil-spill-off-new-zealand/', 'snippet': 'The prime minister asks how a ship could have plowed into a well- documented reef in calm waters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Five days after a container ship hit a reef, clumps of oil begin washing up on New Zealand’s North Island, stirring fear that the fuel could foul delicate estuaries. Workers had begun pumping fuel from the ship but halted the effort after weather predictions of gale-force winds. [Associated Press]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: Oil Spill Off New Zealand', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T11:33:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dba05bfe-54c7-5883-af1b-eb011ec741de', 'word_count': 150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dba05bfe-54c7-5883-af1b-eb011ec741de'}, {'abstract': 'Thomas J. Sargent and Christopher A. Sims, two Americans, won the Nobel economics prize on Monday “for their empirical research on cause and effect in the macroeconomy.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/business/american-economists-share-nobel-prize.html', 'snippet': 'Thomas J. Sargent and Christopher A. Sims, two Americans, won the Nobel economics prize on Monday “for their empirical research on cause and effect in the macroeconomy.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Nobel in economic science was awarded on Monday to Thomas J. Sargent of New York University and Christopher A. Sims of Princeton University for their research on the cause and effect of government policies on the broader economy, a major concern of countries still struggling to address the aftermath of the recent financial crisis.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/business/NOBLE/NOBLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 658, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '2 American Professors Awarded Nobel in Economic Science', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '2 American Professors Are Awarded the Nobel in Economic Science', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sims, Christopher A', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nobel Prizes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sargent, Thomas J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economics', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T11:40:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a3aa9c7-c134-5c7c-99ef-ad3c1f4b6af9', 'word_count': 1026, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a3aa9c7-c134-5c7c-99ef-ad3c1f4b6af9'}, {'abstract': 'With the sector under pressure, Superior Energy Services agreed to buy a rival, Complete Production Services, for about $2.7 billion.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/superior-energy-to-acquire-complete-for-2-7-billion/', 'snippet': 'With the sector under pressure, Superior Energy Services agreed to buy a rival, Complete Production Services, for about $2.7 billion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Superior Energy Services, an oil field services company based in New Orleans, agreed to buy a rival, Complete Production Services, for about $2.7 billion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Superior Energy to Acquire Complete for $2.7 Billion', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T11:52:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'ne