': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b267c48-c2a5-5795-a5e5-4a531d25f8cc', 'word_count': 108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b267c48-c2a5-5795-a5e5-4a531d25f8cc'}, {'abstract': 'A day after the death of the Raiders owner Al Davis, his hand-picked roster of quirky players and speed freaks did him proud with a thrilling victory over the Texans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/sports/raiders-win-one-for-al-davis-leading-off.html', 'snippet': 'A day after the death of the Raiders owner Al Davis, his hand-picked roster of quirky players and speed freaks did him proud with a thrilling victory over the Texans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There might not have been a more inevitable result than the Raiders winning their game over the Texans on Sunday. There simply was no other possible ending. Because wherever you think the sports world’s No. 1 curmudgeon landed in the afterlife after his death on Saturday, Al Davis was going to make eternity unbearable for whoever is in charge had his beloved team not given him a proper sendoff victory. After years of raging at the N.F.L. machine, his raging might have produced actual thunder. Or an earthquake.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Winning One for Al Davis', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Winning One for Al Davis', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Milwaukee Brewers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Atlanta Falcons', 'rank': 5, 'major':