tname': 'Anahad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connor", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/97157c4a-77c2-5664-89aa-35cbbcc60a94', 'word_count': 266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97157c4a-77c2-5664-89aa-35cbbcc60a94'}, {'abstract': 'Groupon disclosed in a company blog post that it had begun deliberately undercounting the number of times a deal had been purchased by consumers.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/groupon-transparent-about-our-lack-of-transparency/', 'snippet': 'Groupon disclosed in a company blog post that it had begun deliberately undercounting the number of times a deal had been purchased by consumers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Groupon has made it clear time and again that it doesn’t quite want to act like the usual boring business. On Monday, the online coupon giant proved that yet again, by putting a new spin on transparency.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Groupon: ‘Transparent About Our Lack of Transparency’', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Groupon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T17:51:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d2585e3-e162-5ece-89c5-ec75a78b2f0d', 'word_count': 335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d2585e3-e162-5ece-89c5-ec75a78b2f0d'}, {'abstract': 'Scientists are teasing out the earliest differences between brains exposed to one language and brains exposed to two.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/health/views/11klass.html', 'snippet': 'Scientists are teasing out the earliest differences between brains exposed to one language and brains exposed to two.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once, experts feared that young children exposed to more than one language would suffer “language confusion,” which might delay their speech development. Today, parents often are urged to capitalize on that early knack for acquiring language. Upscale schools market themselves with promises of deep immersion in Spanish — or Mandarin — for everyone, starting in kindergarten or even before. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/science/11KLAS/11KLAS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 853, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/science/11KLAS/11KLAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/11/science/11KLAS/11KLAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hearing Bilingual: How Babies Sort Out Language', 'kicker': '18 and Under', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hearing Bilingual: How Babies Sort Out Language', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Language and Languages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T17:52:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Views', 'byline': {'original': 'By Perri Klass, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Perri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klass', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c82b4c19-d87b-5ddd-a1d0-a906a79484b5', 'word_count': 1024, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c82b4c19-d87b-5ddd-a1d0-a906a79484b5'}, {'abstract': 'At the County College of Morris in New Jersey, a history teacher showed little patience with Philip Garber Jr. when he raised his hand.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/education/11stutter.html', 'snippet': 'At the County College of Morris in New Jersey, a history teacher showed little patience with Philip Garber Jr. when he raised his hand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'RANDOLPH, N.J. — As his history class at the County College of Morris here discussed exploration of the New World, Philip Garber Jr. raised his hand, hoping to ask why China’s 15th-century explorers, who traveled as far as Africa, had not also reached North America. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/nyregion/11stutter/11stutter-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 347, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/11/nyregion/11stutter/11stutter-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 347}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/nyregion/11stutter/11stutter-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/nyregion/11stutter/11stutter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/11/nyregion/11stutter/11stutter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stutterer Speaks Up in Class; His Professor Says Keep Quiet', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stutterer Speaks Up in Class; His Professor Says Keep Quiet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stuttering', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'County College of Morris', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T17:54:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Pérez-Peña', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pérez-Peña', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ab86e85-7547-5e30-ade3-5eadec4b9260', 'word_count': 1141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ab86e85-7547-5e30-ade3-5eadec4b9260'}, {'abstract': 'Most observers had expected that the prohibitions would apply only to the activities in the United States of non-American banks. The draft rule blows this expectation out of the water, contends Douglas Landy of Allen & Overy.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/how-the-volcker-rule-may-affect-foreign-banks/', 'snippet': 'Most observers had expected that the prohibitions would apply only to the activities in the United States of non-American banks. The draft rule blows this expectation out of the water, contends Douglas Landy of Allen & Overy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Drafts of the proposed Volcker Rule, implementing Section 619 of the Dodd-Frank Act, have begun circulating in advance of a vote by the federal agencies to issue the proposal. The Volcker Rule would ban many forms of proprietary trading by banks and would strictly limit their investments in private equity and hedge funds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How the Volcker Rule May Affect Foreign Banks', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T17:55:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Douglas Landy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42100b58-375c-52f6-a989-3995fd582330', 'word_count': 612, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42100b58-375c-52f6-a989-3995fd582330'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Tough’s article ‘‘What if the Secret to Success Is Failure?’’ received a tremendous response from readers when it appeared in the Education Issue last month, including 270 comments on our Web site and 40 percent of the magazine’s page views.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/is-failure-the-secret-to-success-student-edition/', 'snippet': 'Paul Tough’s article ‘‘What if the Secret to Success Is Failure?’’ received a tremendous response from readers when it appeared in the Education Issue last month, including 270 comments on our Web site and 40 percent of the magazine’s page views.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paul Tough’s article ‘‘What if the Secret to Success Is Failure?’’ received a tremendous response from readers when it appeared in the Education Issue last month, including 270 comments on our Web site and 40 percent of the magazine’s page views. But we were very excited to see the article have a kind of second life over at The Learning Network, a New York Times blog for students and teachers. Our colleagues at The Learning Network used the article to kick off a new ‘‘Reading Club,’’ and there were 534 student comments on Tough’s article. The Reading Club forum encourages discussion, and it was pretty exciting to see students thoughtfully debate questions about social class and character; parental influence and character; and the difference between struggle and failure. There was a lot of respectful disagreement, though the comments I read seemed to be nearly unanimous on one point: No report cards for character, please.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Failure the Secret to Success? Student Edition', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tough, Paul', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T17:55:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vera Titunik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vera', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Titunik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b93ca162-f9cd-5b58-9f15-39ead328e735', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b93ca162-f9cd-5b58-9f15-39ead328e735'}, {'abstract': 'Steven P. Jobs inspired an artist to make unwanted Apple computers his canvas.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/the-laptops-dont-work-but-the-messages-get-through/', 'snippet': 'Steven P. Jobs inspired an artist to make unwanted Apple computers his canvas.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Laptops Don’t Work, but the Messages Get Through', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Laptop Computers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T17:57:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Barron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9291e6d-022e-54f9-a840-f72c3ca5ca16', 'word_count': 445, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9291e6d-022e-54f9-a840-f72c3ca5ca16'}, {'abstract': 'The actress will be honored for lifetime achievement.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/american-film-institute-to-give-award-to-shirley-maclaine/', 'snippet': 'The actress will be honored for lifetime achievement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alfred Hitchcock was one of the first to see Shirley MacLaine’s cinematic appeal, casting her in his 1955 film, “The Trouble With Harry.” Now, with more than 50 Hollywood movies to her name, Ms. MacLaine will receive the lifetime achievement award from the American Film Institute, which will be presented at a gala tribute in Los Angeles on June 7, the BBC reported.', 'sour