tract': 'The choreographers Heather Kravas and Jeremy Wade each presented a solo work at Danspace Project last week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/arts/dance/heather-kravas-and-jeremy-wade-in-solo-works-review.html', 'snippet': 'The choreographers Heather Kravas and Jeremy Wade each presented a solo work at Danspace Project last week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps you’ve seen them on YouTube, the young girls shaking their bodies in emulation of Beyoncé while making faces more like Shirley Temple’s. Perhaps you’ve wondered if they understand the erotic implications of their movements. Is this sexual pantomime damaging? Where will it lead?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/kravas/kravas-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/kravas/kravas-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/kravas/kravas-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/kravas/kravas-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/10/arts/kravas/kravas-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Flaunting Youthful Naïveté', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Flaunting Youthful Naïveté', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wade, Jeremy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kravas, Heather', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T20:05:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Seibert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seibert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/61822f04-c62d-5dd4-bb04-51da85bdce4e', 'word_count': 419, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61822f04-c62d-5dd4-bb04-51da85bdce4e'}, {'abstract': 'In this week’s Appraisal: Do you think a live-in center for people recovering from substance abuse, like one soon to open in TriBeCa, can thrive amid the city’s assorted threats to sobriety?', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/the-appraisal-sobering-up-amid-citys-lures/', 'snippet': 'In this week’s Appraisal: Do you think a live-in center for people recovering from substance abuse, like one soon to open in TriBeCa, can thrive amid the city’s assorted threats to sobriety?', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week the Appraisal visits TriBeCa Twelve, a soon-to-open home for young adults recovering from substance abuse. It will be run by Hazelden, the Minnesota-based operator of a network of rehab centers, and be housed in a planned luxury condo development on West Broadway that Hazelden bought after the development went belly up. The Columbia University psychiatry department is a partner in the effort. Although the popular image of live-in centers for people recovering from substance abuse runs more toward campus-like complexes in bucolic settings, where fresh air and natural beauty replace the lures of drugs and alcohol, this one — intended to serve as a transition from rehab to real life — is in the midst of a heavily traveled night life zone, near bars, clubs and restaurants that would seem to pose threats to a resident’s hard-won sobriety. Have you or has someone you know been in rehab or a similar situation and found the temptations of city life hard to resist? Or do you think that treatment centers need not be remote to be effective? Please share your thoughts and experiences.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Appraisal: Sobering Up Amid City’s Lures', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'APPRAISALS AND VALUATION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Therapy and Rehabilitation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia University', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Minnesota', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'TriBeCa (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T20:11:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Diane Cardwell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Diane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cardwell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d39b1e82-6028-5713-afe9-f40b5a6600c7', 'word_count': 184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d39b1e82-6028-5713-afe9-f40b5a6600c7'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers studying Nazca boobies, ground-nesting birds native to the Galápagos Islands, found interesting correlations between early abuse and later behavior.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/science/11booby.html', 'snippet': 'Researchers studying Nazca boobies, ground-nesting birds native to the Galápagos Islands, found interesting correlations between early abuse and later behavior.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Although it has never been definitively proven, many social scientists believe that abused children are more likely to become abusers as adults. Now for the first time, there is evidence of the phenomenon in animals in the wild.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/science/11OBBOOBY_SPAN/11OBOX1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/11/science/11OBBOOBY_SPAN/11OBOX1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/science/11OBBOOBY_SPAN/11OBOX1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/science/11OBBOOBY_SPAN/11OBOX1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/11/science/11OBBOOBY_SPAN/11OBOX1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Birds, a Possible Clue to the Cycle of Abuse', 'kicker': 'Observatory', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Birds, a Possible Clue to the Cycle of Abuse', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Aggression (Behavior)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10