age', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/world/AFRICA/AFRICA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/11/world/AFRICA/AFRICA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ex-President of Cape Verde Wins Good-Government Prize', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ex-President of Cape Verde Wins Good-Government Prize', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Contests and Prizes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cape Verde', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T20:50:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Nossiter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nossiter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7217224a-2ffb-55dc-b4c0-566fb424d95e', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7217224a-2ffb-55dc-b4c0-566fb424d95e'}, {'abstract': 'The sausage race at the Brewers’ home games is the ultimate gag in Wisconsin, a state known for its brats, cheese and beer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/sports/baseball/in-brewers-sausage-race-theyre-all-wieners.html', 'snippet': 'The sausage race at the Brewers’ home games is the ultimate gag in Wisconsin, a state known for its brats, cheese and beer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MILWAUKEE — They appeared from behind the left-field fence in the middle of the sixth inning, five tall, costumed wieners with heads like Easter Island statues but wearing hats. The Miller Park crowd of 43,613 cheered and whistled Sunday as the Milwaukee Brewers’ famous racing sausages lined up on the warning track and took off.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/sports/baseball/11sausages-pic/11sausages-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/11/sports/baseball/11sausages-pic/11sausages-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/sports/baseball/11sausages-pic/11sausages-pic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/sports/baseball/11sausages-pic/11sausages-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/11/sports/baseball/11sausages-pic/11sausages-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Milwaukee, Sausages Aren’t Just for Tailgating', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Milwaukee, Sausages Aren’t Just for Tailgating', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Milwaukee Brewers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wisconsin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sausages', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T20:54:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pat Borzi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pat', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borzi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd8e3d69-f168-59fd-bef8-8077fd79532d', 'word_count': 1212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd8e3d69-f168-59fd-bef8-8077fd79532d'}, {'abstract': 'Norman Norell has always been something of a quiet genius of American fashion.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/normans-conquest/', 'snippet': 'Norman Norell has always been something of a quiet genius of American fashion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the American fall collections of 1960, Norman Norell did something seemingly too radical for a man who ate most of his meals at Schrafft’s and Hamburg Heaven. He had his models paint their faces in the bohemian-urchin style of Paris in the ’20s, with darkened eyes and red lips, finished off with shingle haircuts. Then he put the girls in pants. Technically these were split skirts, but who cares? The effect was the same. Long before Yves Saint Laurent issued le smoking, square Norman was there. “They may seem a little avant-garde at first,” he told The Times after his 1960 show, for which he received a standing ovation, “but I really do believe in the look.” He had uncanny instincts for anticipating change: the elongated sweater look, the return of waistlines in the mid-’60s, the dolman-sleeve rage of the early ’70s. He showed leopard patterns in the ’40s. The flapper makeup told a lot about Norell as well: stage-struck at an early age, he designed costumes in the ’20s at the Astoria studio of Paramount Pictures, dressing Gloria Swanson in “Zaza,” and later worked on Broadway musicals and burlesque shows before moving to ladies’ fashion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Norman’s Conquest', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dresses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Norell, Norman', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T21:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13ba9b01-ecc7-590e-a840-8b238c24db9f', 'word_count': 595, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13ba9b01-ecc7-590e-a840-8b238c24db9f'}, {'abstract': '“Breaking Bad,” which just ended its fourth season on Sunday, had its premiere on Jan. 20, 2008. It started with a straightforward premise: A mild-mannered chemistry teacher named Walter White finds out he has cancer, and so he decides, in order to build up a fortune to leave to his family, to start cooking meth.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/the-incredible-good-luck-of-breaking-bad/', 'snippet': '“Breaking Bad,” which just ended its fourth season on Sunday, had its premiere on Jan. 20, 2008. It started with a straightforward premise: A mild-mannered chemistry teacher named Walter White finds out he has cancer, and so he decides, in order t...', 'lead_paragraph': '‘‘Breaking Bad,’’ which just ended its fourth season on Sunday, had its premiere on Jan. 20, 2008. It started with a straightforward and rather narrow premise: A mild-mannered chemistry teacher named Walter White finds out he has cancer, and so he decides, in order to build up a fortune to leave to his family, to start cooking meth.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Incredible Good Luck of ‘Breaking Bad’', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'se