eported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f8d8cb2-f2d2-5fae-b731-0dc6bce522d9', 'word_count': 732, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f8d8cb2-f2d2-5fae-b731-0dc6bce522d9'}, {'abstract': 'The National Dance Institute’s first permanent home, in a modern building on 147th Street, is what city officials, community leaders and educators hope will become a beacon for the arts in a changing Harlem.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/arts/dance/national-dance-institute-center-celebrates-harlem-home.html', 'snippet': 'The National Dance Institute’s first permanent home, in a modern building on 147th Street, is what city officials, community leaders and educators hope will become a beacon for the arts in a changing Harlem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For 35 years the National Dance Institute has been a gypsy, in the words of its founder, Jacques d’Amboise, the former New York City Ballet principal dancer. The institute rented and borrowed space here and there as it brought dance, performance and arts education to thousands of New York City public school students.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/arts/NDI1/NDI1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/11/arts/NDI1/NDI1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 391}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/arts/NDI1/NDI1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/arts/NDI1/NDI1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/11/arts/NDI1/NDI1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dance Institute, Centered in Harlem', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dance Institute, Centered in Harlem', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Dance Institute', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'HARLEM (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T22:16:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77e8f339-4dac-5ef1-8cf7-be5c80560ff8', 'word_count': 719, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77e8f339-4dac-5ef1-8cf7-be5c80560ff8'}, {'abstract': 'Charles Askegard gave his final performance with New York City Ballet on Sunday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/arts/dance/charles-askegard-of-city-ballet-gives-final-performance.html', 'snippet': 'Charles Askegard gave his final performance with New York City Ballet on Sunday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sunday afternoon at New York City Ballet, the dancer Charles Askegard played four consecutive roles: a noble cavalier in formal attire (the pas de deux from George Balanchine’s “Diamonds”); an unadorned dancer in white T-shirt and black tights (Balanchine’s “Episodes”); a harsh messenger of death in gray shirt and black trousers (Jerome Robbins’s “In Memory of...”); and finally an American cowboy (Balanchine’s “Western Symphony”). With this display of range, Mr. Askegard made his farewell to City Ballet.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/arts/Askegard/Askegard-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 690, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/arts/Askegard/Askegard-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/11/arts/Askegard/Askegard-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Goodbye, Understated but Riveting in Its Duets', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Goodbye, Understated But Riveting In Its Duets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Ballet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Askegard, Charles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T22:18:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fb8ac0f-2160-50f6-90bc-928dfb0580e1', 'word_count': 654, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fb8ac0f-2160-50f6-90bc-928dfb0580e1'}, {'abstract': 'Direct democracy in California was meant to bring the people into the governance process, but voters have become consumers of television sound-bite campaigns.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/opinion/how-to-fix-californias-democracy-crisis.html', 'snippet': 'Direct democracy in California was meant to bring the people into the governance process, but voters have become consumers of television sound-bite campaigns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stanford, Calif.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Fix California’s Democracy Crisis', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Fix California&#8217;s Democracy Crisis', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Referendums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting and Voters', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T22:22:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By JAMES S. FISHKIN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'FISHKIN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/6981650b-07ee-5c89-b08b-94f72169e585', 'word_count': 1005, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6981650b-07ee-5c89-b08b-94f72169e585'}, {'abstract': 'The “Sunday Night Football” game between the Green Bay Packers and the Atlanta Falcons dominated the night en route to another Sunday ratings victory for NBC.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/arts/television/nbc-and-packers-cruise-to-victory.html', 'snippet': 'The “Sunday Night Football” game between the Green Bay Packers and the Atlanta Falcons dominated the night en route to another Sunday ratings victory for NBC.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the Fox broadcast of Game 2 of the American League Championship Series postponed because of rain, the “Sunday Night Football” game between the Green Bay Packers and the Atlanta Falcons dominated the night en route to another Sunday ratings victory for NBC. (The Packers defeated the Falcons, 25-14). The competing shows, meanwhile, continued to struggle, especially on ABC. As little as two weeks ago the network was looking at a successful debut of its series “Pam Am,” as the premiere episode was sampled by nearly 11 million total viewers. Three episodes later, the series has fallen sharply week-to-week, with another 27 percent drop this week in the 18-to-49-year-old demographic and a dwindling total viewership of 6.4 million. The numbers weren’t much better for “Desperate Housewives,” which tied a series low in the 18-to-49 category for a new episode.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'NBC and Packers Cruise to Victory', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'NBC and Packers Cruise to Victory', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T22:25:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/85408703-5be5-53c4-a80c-d7a03eac3c31', 'word_count': 140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85408703-5be5-53c4-a80c-d7a03eac3c31'}, {'abstract': 'A recent study in the wild suggests that male crickets linger after mating to protect females from predation rather than to fend off rivals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/science/11cricket.html', 'snippet': 'A recent study in the wild suggests that male crickets linger after mating to protect females from predation rather than to fend off rivals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Biologists have long thought of the male cricket as an insect version of the stalker boyfriend. In laboratory studies, a male cricket often lingers after copulating in an effort, researchers believed, to make sure his mate does not go searching for a different partner or reject the slow-release package of sperm he has just deposited.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/science/11OBCRICKETS_SPAN/11OBOX3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cricket Hovers After Sex to Ensure Mate’s Safety', 'kicker': 'Observatory', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cricket Hovers After Sex To Ensure Mate’s Safety', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Current Biology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crickets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reproduction (Biological)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T22:25:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ritchie S. King', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ritchie', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'King', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f2d4808-9c41-58ee-b0e9-3637906fceda', 'word_count': 297, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f2d4808-9c41-58ee-b0e9-3637906fceda'}, {'abstract': 'De mortuis nihil nisi bonum. Don’t rip the dead. It seems to be the generous sentiment behind most of the coverage following the deaths of two Bay Area titans in the last week, Steve Jobs and Al Davis.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/10/post-games/', 'snippet': 'De mortuis nihil nisi bonum. Don’t rip the dead. It seems to be the generous sentiment behind most of the coverage following the deaths of two Bay Area titans in the last week, Steve Jobs and Al Davis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'De mortuis nihil nisi bonum. Don’t rip the dead. It seems to be the generous sentiment behind most of the coverage following the deaths of two Bay Area titans in the last week, Steve Jobs and Al Davis. And who can argue with that? Jobs was Apple; Davis was the Oakland Raiders; each transformed his industry. Who doesn’t love an iPad (or appreciate Apple’s market capitalization)? Who doesn’t thrill to the high-arcing ‘‘vertical’’ bomb that results in a 60-yard touchdown?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Post Games', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Al', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oakland (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-10T22:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dean Robinson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robinson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a88533ce-4274-50b6-9483-7034df4594d9', 'word_count': 193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a88533ce-4274-50b6-9483-7034df4594d9'}, {'abstract': 'George Vecsey joined Jurgen Klinsmann and the United States national team on Monday during its visit to the September 11 Memorial in Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https