: 'Democrats scored a small but significant victory for the cause of progress in the Senate late last week when they voted to prohibit one of the many delaying tactics that keep the chamber tied up in pointless partisan arguments. It was a long way from desperately needed filibuster reform, but it showed that sufficiently frustrated senators can take action to prevent the Senate from being a total dead weight.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chipping Away at Gridlock', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chipping Away at Gridlock: Senate Democrats vote to fight back against endless Republican delaying tactics', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Filibusters and Debate Curbs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reid, Harry', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McConnell, Mitch', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T00:40:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/dcff5eb3-d6e3-5f37-b37d-67c1bf385dc8', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dcff5eb3-d6e3-5f37-b37d-67c1bf385dc8'}, {'abstract': 'In the past, when passengers were removed from flights, only fellow travelers knew; now social media exposes these disputes to a worldwide audience.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/business/on-youtube-a-debate-over-the-right-to-fly-with-your-pants-down.html', 'snippet': 'In the past, when passengers were removed from flights, only fellow travelers knew; now social media exposes these disputes to a worldwide audience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Airlines have long been able to remove disruptive passengers from their airplanes. But while in the past only fellow travelers knew what had happened, now social media exposes these disputes to a worldwide audience.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/business/Right1/Right1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/business/Right1/Right1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/11/business/Right1/Right1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fliers’ vs. Airlines’ Rights', 'kicker': 'Itineraries', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fliers’ vs. Airlines’ Rights', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customer Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T00:42:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Negroni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Negroni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec19b3d6-07ab-5069-b106-60d4efa38bfd', 'word_count': 997, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec19b3d6-07ab-5069-b106-60d4efa38bfd'}, {'abstract': 'Jesse Itzler, co-founder of Marquis Jet, which sells access to NetJets planes, is not crazy about flying, but he once flew to snowy Utah to raise some business.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/business/selling-private-jet-service-with-chattering-teeth.html', 'snippet': 'Jesse Itzler, co-founder of Marquis Jet, which sells access to NetJets planes, is not crazy about flying, but he once flew to snowy Utah to raise some business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ALMOST all of my business flights are commercial, not private. I’ll be the first to admit that I’m not a good flier.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/business/Flier/Flier-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No Car, No Hotel, No Luggage. But He Got the Account.', 'kicker': 'Frequent Flier', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Car, No Hotel, No Luggage. But He Got the Account.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Aircraft', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Utah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sales', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Business Travel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NetJets', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T00:44:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse Itzler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e2832db-157a-535c-9c6a-b697b8e0ae04', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e2832db-157a-535c-9c6a-b697b8e0ae04'}, {'abstract': 'Instead of denouncing the killing in Syria, Russia and China have ensured it will continue after using their United Nations Security Council veto.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/opinion/enabling-president-assad-of-syria.html', 'snippet': 'Instead of denouncing the killing in Syria, Russia and China have ensured it will continue after using their United Nations Security Council veto.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a lot of shame to go around after the United Nations Security Council failed last week to pass a resolution condemning Syria’s brutal crackdown. Russia, which used its veto, clearly values its arms sales and other trade with Damascus over the lives of the more than 2,900 Syrians killed during pro-democracy protests. China, which followed Russia, clearly fears any popular movement. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Enabling Mr. Assad', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Enabling Mr. Assad: Instead of denouncing the killing in Syria, Russia and China have ensured it will continue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Security Council (UN)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embargoes and Economic Sanctions', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T00:46:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8cdea83-b94d-5460-bf82-daf78d8c5612', 'word_count': 394, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8cdea83-b94d-5460-bf82-daf78d8c5612'}, {'abstract': 'The warning that the P.S.A. test may cause men more harm than good is information that can guide patients and doctors to make better decisions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/opinion/questioning-prostate-cancer-tests.html', 'snippet': 'The warning that the P.S.A. test may cause men more harm than good is information that can guide patients and doctors to make better decisions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'American men now have an authoritative warning that getting a blood test for prostate cancer may push them toward needless tests and treatments that would do them more harm than good. The United States Preventive Services Task Force, after an exhaustive review of the scientific evidence, has suggested that healthy men should no longer receive the P.S.A. blood test to screen for a protein that may indicate cancer. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Questioning Prostate Cancer Tests', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Questioning Prostate Cancer Tests', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests (Medical)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostate Cancer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Preventive Services Task Force', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostate Specific Antigen', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T00:51:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/90af7a9d-f3e4-5142-a7a5-d0c87b51911a', 'word_count': 342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90af7a9d-f3e4-5142-a7a5-d0c87b51911a'}, {'abstract': 'The retail store also faces fines after regular cuts of pork were sold as organic in Chongqing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/business/global/wal-marts-in-china-city-closed-for-pork-mislabeling.html', 'snippet': 'The retail store also faces fines after regular cuts of pork were sold as organic in Chongqing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — The fiercely nationalistic municipal government of Chongqing in southwestern China ordered all 13 Wal-Mart stores there to close for 15 days and fined the company $420,000 in a labeling dispute that has broader political implications for Western companies operating in China.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chinese City Shuts Down 13 Wal-Marts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chinese City Shuts Down 13 Wal-Marts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fines (Penalties)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wal-Mart Stores Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T00:51:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keith Bradsher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', 'qualifier': N