e': 'Quinn, Christine C', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T00:51:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Powell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Powell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac60f04f-336b-5c1d-b6ed-cf2d1d3b3653', 'word_count': 775, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac60f04f-336b-5c1d-b6ed-cf2d1d3b3653'}, {'abstract': 'The trial involving a former campaign aide to Mayor Bloomberg raises a larger question about unlimited housekeeping donations to a political party.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/opinion/new-yorks-housekeeping-money.html', 'snippet': 'The trial involving a former campaign aide to Mayor Bloomberg raises a larger question about unlimited housekeeping donations to a political party.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mayor Michael Bloomberg testified last week in a trial about whether a former campaign aide had absconded with the mayor’s $1.1 million contribution to the Independence Party. That party gave Mr. Bloomberg its line in 2009. While the court is focused on that question, the public should be asking a larger one. Mainly, why could the mayor write such a large personal check to the party?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York’s ‘Housekeeping’ Money', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New York’s ‘Housekeeping’ Money', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'n