arring-douglas-and-damon-for-hbo/', 'snippet': 'Michael Douglas will play Liberace, and Matt Damon will play Scott Thorson, his live-in lover, in a biographical film, “Behind the Candelabra,” which Steven Soderbergh will direct.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You spend enough years in Las Vegas and eventually its kitschy charm leaves an indelible mark on you. Having made three movies about Danny Ocean and his Rat Pack-like team of casino-heist experts, as well as critically acclaimed films like “Traffic,” “Erin Brockovich” and “Sex, Lies and Videotape,” Steven Soderbergh has turned to the only subject that was left for him: a biographical movie about Liberace. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cue the Kitsch: Soderbergh to Make Liberace Film Starring Douglas and Damon for HBO', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cable Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Damon, Matt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Douglas, Michael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'LaGravenese, Richard', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Liberace, Wladziu Valentino', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Soderbergh, Steven', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weintraub, Jerry', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Las Vegas (Nev)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T17:49:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b76b814-8095-56e0-8517-354a7534b058', 'word_count': 329, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b76b814-8095-56e0-8517-354a7534b058'}, {'abstract': 'The fastest growing group of gamers are children ages 2 to 5. They and their older brothers and sisters also cajole their parents into buying games, making them an important cohort for game marketers.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/from-the-crib-to-the-console/', 'snippet': 'The fastest growing group of gamers are children ages 2 to 5. They and their older brothers and sisters also cajole their parents into buying games, making them an important cohort for game marketers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Video game use is exploding among the youngest among us, according to a new survey.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Playing From the Crib to the Console', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T17:53:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Richtel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richtel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbf32af0-5ab4-5bc3-9a7f-32f6d35a913a', 'word_count': 222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbf32af0-5ab4-5bc3-9a7f-32f6d35a913a'}, {'abstract': 'A teacher asks a student with an extreme stutter to stop talking in class and submit written questions instead.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/should-a-stutterer-speak-in-class/', 'snippet': 'A teacher asks a student with an extreme stutter to stop talking in class and submit written questions instead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Philip Garber Jr.\xa0 is 16 and taking two college classes. He is thoughtful, engaged and eager to contribute to class discussion — a dream student in many respects. But because his stutter is extreme, it takes him far longer than other students to ask a question or make a comment. So, as Richard Pérez-Peña writes in today’s New York Times, his history teacher has asked him not to.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Should a Stutterer Speak in Class?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T18:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Karin Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a757a6a0-fdd0-5a55-9534-24ac7481c0d5', 'word_count': 330, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a757a6a0-fdd0-5a55-9534-24ac7481c0d5'}, {'abstract': 'Would the country have been better off with McCain in the White House?', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/dreams-of-a-mccain-presidency/', 'snippet': 'Would the country have been better off with McCain in the White House?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The most interesting part of Ezra Klein’s lengthy “what went wrong” assessment of the Obama administration’s economic policy is the way it raises the possibility that a John McCain presidency might have delivered a more effective response to the Great Recession. With McCain in the White House and a Democratic majority in Congress, Klein implies, we might have had a smaller, better-targeted stimulus package (perhaps more like the one that Alice Rivlin recommended) joined to a more robust intervention in the housing market (like the one McCain’s campaign proposed in the autumn of 2008). Jonathan Chait takes this idea and uses it to make a broader argument that a McCain presidency would have forestalled the popular backlash against deficit-financed stimulus:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dreams of a McCain Presidency', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T18:05:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc3dde18-917a-5c29-a467-3e986421da12', 'word_count': 706, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc3dde18-917a-5c29-a467-3e986421da12'}, {'abstract': 'Israel and Hamas have reached a tentative agreement on a proposal to exchange Palestinian prisoners for Gilad Shalit, an Israeli soldier who has been held since 2006.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/world/middleeast/possible-deal-near-to-free-captive-israeli-soldier.html', 'snippet': 'Israel and Hamas have reached a tentative agreement on a proposal to exchange Palestinian prisoners for Gilad Shalit, an Israeli soldier who has been held since 2006.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — Israel and Hamas announced an agreement on Tuesday to exchange more than 1,000 Palestinian prisoners for an Israeli soldier held captive in Gaza for five years, a deal brokered by Egypt that seemed likely to shake up Middle East politics at a time when the region is immersed in turmoil.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/world/middleeast/SHALIT/SHALIT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/12/world/middleeast/SHALIT/SHALIT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/world/middleeast/SHALIT/SHALIT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 602, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/world/middleeast/SHALIT/SHALIT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/12/world/middleeast/SHALIT/SHALIT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Israel and Hamas Agree to Swap Prisoners for Soldier', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Deal With Hamas Will Free Israeli Held Since 2006', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shalit, Gilad', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hamas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Netanyahu, Benjamin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T18:07:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ethan Bronner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ethan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bronner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a10fbd5-c436-5819-998d-5b38333b1004', 'word_count': 1310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a10fbd5-c436-5819-998d-5b38333b1004'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama continued plugging his jobs plan, even as White House officials acknowledged that the bill’s prospects in the Senate were tenuous.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/obama-pushes-jobs-plan-as-senate-prepares-to-vote/', 'snippet': 'President Obama continued plugging his jobs plan, even as White House officials acknowledged that the bill’s prospects in the Senate were tenuous.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PITTSBURGH — President Obama continued the drumbeat for his jobs plan on Tuesday, even as White House officials acknowledged that the bill’s prospects in the Senate were tenuous and Mr. Obama himself vowed not to wait for Congress to advance ideas to get American back to work.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Pushes Jobs Plan as Senate Prepares to Vote', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T18:12:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helene Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/366dbb9a-db2a-5ef9-8b98-9ca762dff6fd', 'word_count': 608, 'uri': 'nyt://article/366dbb9a-db2a-5ef9-8b98-9ca762dff6fd'}, {'abstract': '“Montclair is a food-savvy town, and I think there is a lot of curiosity now about edge cuisine,” said Tre Ghoshal, the chef and owner.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/the-avante-garde-arrives-in-montclair-at-adara/', 'snippet': '“Montclair is a food-savvy town, and I think there is a lot of curiosity now about edge cuisine,” said Tre Ghoshal, the chef and owner.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Does Montclair, N.J., hunger for a restaurant whose mission statement promises to explore “the relationship between food, art and technology”? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Avante-Garde Arrives in Montclair, at Adara', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T18:13:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/258606b0-72c3-59cf-9f07-24d17834ca1a', 'word_count': 517, 'uri': 'nyt://article/258606b0-72c3-59cf-9f07-24d17834ca1a'}, {'abstract': '', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2007/08/01/health/666112ProstatectomySeries_index.html', 'snippet': '', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2007/08/01/health/adam/thumbnails/10053t.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2007/08/01/health/adam/thumbnails/10053t.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Prostatectomy  - series', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T18:13:46+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Health', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/a7b4ad18-f54b-59c6-bb3d-55ee227a059f', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/a7b4ad18-f54b-59c6-bb3d-55ee227a059f'}, {'abstract': 'The university hopes that starting a joint-degree program with City College will enhance its bid to create an engineering school in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/stanford-teams-with-city-college-think-engineering-school/', 'snippet': 'The university hopes that starting a joint-degree program with City College will enhance its bid to create an engineering school in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the chess match to determine which university will get a chance to build a graduate school of applied sciences on public land in New York City, Stanford is trying what might be called a C.C.N.Y. stratagem.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stanford Teams With City College (Think Engineering School)', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City College of New York', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stanford University', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T18:15:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGeehan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGeehan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/825c81d1-20d8-58bd-aff8-1211494154af', 'word_count': 399, 'uri': 'nyt://article/825c81d1-20d8-58bd-aff8-1211494154af'}, {'abstract': 'A former shoemaker makes a big return to the footwear world.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/brightness-rises-patrick-coxs-third-act/', 'snippet': 'A former shoemaker makes a big return to the footwear world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Patrick Cox’s Wannabe loafer, a design-y take on the traditional schoolboy shoe, was a smash hit in the early ’90s — almost a million pairs were sold every year. “I was just a kid in England doing this thing that became gigantic and global,” says Cox, who was 31 at the time. Stores opened in London, then Paris and New York, and the chunky, heeled shoes all flew out the door. Then came what Cox now calls his Second Album Syndrome. “Wannabe was so huge,” he remembers. “So big. So unexpected.” What could he possibly do to top it? Though the company chugged along for a few years, Cox eventually sold it in 2007. Faced with a noncompete clause, he traveled for a bit and eventually opened a bakery called\xa0Cox Cookies and Cake  on the naughty side of Soho in London, selling baked goods that cheekily made references to the peep shows and X-rated video stores that flanked his storefront.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brightness Rises | Patrick Cox’s Third Act', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T18:17:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Pask', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pask', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dcbe7dc-80ed-5c8c-b6e9-0263e7c84eb0', 'word_count': 356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dcbe7dc-80ed-5c8c-b6e9-0263e7c84eb0'}, {'abstract': 'More photos of Thomas Keller’s four-star restaurant in Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/10/12/dining/20111012-REST.html', 'snippet': 'More photos of Thomas Keller’s four-star restaurant in Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More photos of Thomas Keller’s four-star restaurant in Manhattan.',