'}, {'abstract': 'A historical perspective on philosophy by Raymond Geuss, a professor of philosophy at the University of Cambridge.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/opinion/100000001105361/raymond-geuss.html', 'snippet': 'A historical perspective on philosophy by Raymond Geuss, a professor of philosophy at the University of Cambridge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A historical perspective on philosophy by Raymond Geuss, a professor of philosophy at the University of Cambridge.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/opinion/video-geuss-final/video-geuss-final-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/11/opinion/video-geuss-final/video-geuss-final-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/opinion/video-geuss-final/video-geuss-final-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/11/opinion/video-geuss-final/video-geuss-final-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Raymond Geuss', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T21:24:10+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Opinion', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'None None', 'person': [{'firstname': 'None', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'None', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/35a89c51-4341-5b6d-b1d3-52a31effd0b9', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/35a89c51-4341-5b6d-b1d3-52a31effd0b9'}, {'abstract': 'Donna Johnson recounts growing up among a traveling caravan of evangelists, holding tent revivals in Southern backwaters, where miracles were in high demand.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/books/holy-ghost-girl-a-memoir-by-donna-m-johnson-review.html', 'snippet': 'Donna Johnson recounts growing up among a traveling caravan of evangelists, holding tent revivals in Southern backwaters, where miracles were in high demand.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0It was after midnight at the dawn of the 1960s. Donna Johnson was 3, her brother 1, her father gone. Her mother had sold everything — “knickknacks, flatware, all of her slacks” — to join a traveling caravan of evangelists led by the preacher Brother David Terrell. Holding tent revivals in a new town every few weeks, they were essentially carnies, but instead of hawking funnel cakes, they were selling God. \xa0', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/arts/12book/12book-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 558, 'width': 557, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/arts/12book/12book-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/12/arts/12book/12book-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Blind Faith That Is Shaken Under a Revival Tent', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Blind Faith That Is Shaken Under a Revival Tent', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T21:26:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susannah Meadows', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susannah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Meadows', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a9eeaf4-5a9d-5b5b-821e-f4fca7b6148f', 'word_count': 947, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a9eeaf4-5a9d-5b5b-821e-f4fca7b6148f'}, {'abstract': 'A forged Robert Motherwell painting was branded with the equivalent of a scarlet letter on Tuesday in a legal settlement involving three art galleries and the Dedalus Foundation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/arts/design/motherwell-painting-declared-a-forgery.html', 'snippet': 'A forged Robert Motherwell painting was branded with the equivalent of a scarlet letter on Tuesday in a legal settlement involving three art galleries and the Dedalus Foundation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A forged Robert Motherwell painting was branded with the equivalent of a scarlet letter on Tuesday in a legal settlement involving three art galleries and the Dedalus Foundation, which owns the copyrights to Motherwell’s works. “Spanish Elegy,” the painting at the center of the dispute, was sold to a European gallery, Killala Fine Art, for $650,000 in 2007 by Julian Weissman, who had previously worked as a salesman at Knoedler & Company in Manhattan. Before the sale Dedalus initially concluded that “Spanish Elegy” was authentic, but later backtracked and declared the work, supposedly painted by Motherwell in 1953, to be a fake. The Killala gallery said in its lawsuit that it had relied on the foundation’s assurance in making the purchase. Mr. Weissman was accused of acquiring the painting from Glafira Rosales, a dealer on Long Island, and of hiding its provenance, according to court papers. As part of the settlement, Ms. Rosales and her gallery will pick up most of the cost of reimbursing Killala for the price of the painting and Dedalus for its expenses. Mr. Weissman will pay the remaining portion. The foundation marked the back of “Spanish Elegy” (above) with a stamp in capital letters and indelible ink that says Dedalus “has determined that this painting is not an authentic work by Robert Motherwell but a forgery.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Motherwell Painting Declared a Forgery', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Motherwell Painting Declared a Forgery', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T21:31:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/734a5964-e465-5d91-80f3-982fd26ba089', 'word_count': 223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/734a5964-e465-5d91-80f3-982fd26ba089'}, {'abstract': 'An NBC report on the similarities between the national and Massachusetts health care plans is timed perfectly for Mitt Romney’s opponents.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/romney-readies-for-health-care-attacks-at-debate/', 'snippet': 'An NBC report on the similarities between the national and Massachusetts health care plans is timed perfectly for Mitt Romney’s opponents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HANOVER, N.H. — If there’s one thing that Mitt Romney is bracing for at Tuesday night’s debate, it is an assault on his health care record as governor of Massachusetts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney Readies for Health Care Attacks at Debate', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NBC News', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T21:31:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a073f83-2acc-5ed4-872a-e85d565c4381', 'word_count': 446, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a073f83-2acc-5ed4-872a-e85d565c4381'}, {'abstract': 'Mariusz Kwiecien, the Polish baritone who was cast in the title role of the Metropolitan Opera’s new production of “Don Giovanni,” which opens on Thursday night, underwent surgery for a herniated disk on Tuesday, his manager said.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/baritone-in-don-giovanni-taken-to-hospital/', 'snippet': 'Mariusz Kwiecien, the Polish baritone who was cast in the title role of the Metropolitan Opera’s new production of “Don Giovanni,” which opens on Thursday night, underwent surgery for a herniated disk on Tuesday, his manager said.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Mariusz Kwiecien, the Polish baritone who was cast in the title role of the Metropolitan Opera’s new production of “Don Giovanni,” which opens on Thursday night, underwent surgery for a herniated disk on Tuesday, his manager said. “The doctors feel that Mariusz’s prognosis is excellent,” said the manager, William Guerri, of Columbia Artists Management. But it was unclear when he might return to the stage. “It’s a wait-and-see proposition,” Mr. Guerri said. Mr. Kwiecien felt pain during the opening scene of Monday’s dress rehearsal, the manager said. He was taken by paramedics to a hospital, said the Met spokesman, Peter Clark. The Met tapped Peter Mattei, who was on hand for a production of Rossini’s “Barbiere di Siviglia,” to replace Mr. Kwiecien for the opening of “Don Giovanni.” The opera has 16 more performances into March, although Mr. Kwiecien was scheduled to be in the cast only through Nov. 11.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Baritone in ‘Don Giovanni’ Taken to Hospital', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mattei, Peter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rossini, Gioacchino', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia Artists Management Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T21:38:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6132085b-9d34-5a58-b5fa-bcaac5024a4a', 'word_count': 151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6132085b-9d34-5a58-b5fa-bcaac5024a4a'}, {'abstract': 'One occupational hazard of testing skin-care products is overdosing on irritants. The Samurai’s next mission: skin restoration.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/samurai-shopper-the-wages-of-skin/', 'snippet': 'One occupational hazard of testing skin-care products is overdosing on irritants. The Samurai’s next mission: skin restoration.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ouch. My face hurts. I know, it’s killing you too — oldest joke in the book. But it’s no joke: one occupational hazard of testing skin-care products is overdosing on irritants. Chemicals can get under your skin, and even natural substances — essential oils, for example — can wreak havoc: lavender’s aroma can be therapeutic and calming, but don’t ply your face with lavender oil. Ditto minty or citrusy oils and anything acidic, unless buried in a gentle formula. Hypoallergenics can also be problematic. Many contain potential endocrine disrupters and other nasty components. Besides, allergies are as varied as fingerprints; there’s no one-size-fits-all solution to irritated skin.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Samurai Shopper | The Wages of Skin', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T21:39:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By S.S. Fair', 'person': [{'firstname': 'S.', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Fair', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d87f84df-161c-584a-9d24-44434a027f53', 'word_count': 422, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d87f84df-161c-584a-9d24-44434a027f53'}, {'abstract': 'As his federal trial began in Detroit, it remained uncertain what defense Umar Farouk Abdulmutallab, who is representing himself, intends to present.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/us/prosecution-says-suspect-admitted-trying-to-blow-up-plane.html', 'snippet': 'As his federal trial began in Detroit, it remained uncertain what defense Umar Farouk Abdulmutallab, who is representing himself, intends to present.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — Even before the plane had landed, a badly burned Umar Farouk Abdulmutallab was admitting to a fellow passenger that, yes, he had just been trying to ignite an explosive device, a prosecutor said Tuesday as Mr. Abdulmutallab’s trial on charges of trying to blow up a plane in 2009 began.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/us/BOMBER/BOMBER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 871, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/us/BOMBER/BOMBER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/12/us/BOMBER/BOMBER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Says Man Admitted Plot to Blow Up Passenger Jet', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. Says Man Admitted Plot To Blow Up Passenger Jet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Abdulmutallab, Umar Farouk', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T21:40:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Davey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8735d874-6fe4-519f-915c-0e7a39cfaa39', 'word_count': 768, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8735d874-6fe4-519f-915c-0e7a39cfaa39'}, {'abstract': 'The Bolshoi’s “Esmeralda” is the latest in live, high-definition ballet to be shown in movie theaters worldwide, and the production is vivid.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/arts/dance/bolshois-esmeralda-is-vivid-on-a-movie-screen.html', 'snippet': 'The Bolshoi’s “Esmeralda” is the latest in live, high-definition ballet to be shown in movie theaters worldwide, and the production is vivid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Presentations of live, high-definition ballet in movie theaters worldwide, keeping abreast with those of opera and theater, have become a trend in the last year or so. Just now, however, only one ballet company is making the most of them: the Bolshoi in Moscow. During the 2010-11 season it screened performances of “Don Quixote,” “Coppélia” and “Swan Lake”; in the 2011-12 season it is presenting six more, starting last Sunday with “Esmeralda.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/arts/12esmeralda/12esmeralda-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 958, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/arts/12esmeralda/12esmeralda-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/12/arts/12esmeralda/12esmeralda-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Gypsy Love Story, Now With a Happy Ending', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Gypsy Love Story, Now With a Happy Ending', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Ballet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Paris Opera Ballet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bolshoi Ballet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T21:42:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/35132e14-b6af-5d59-9a82-bd4613b38c20', 'word_count': 694, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35132e14-b6af-5d59-9a82-bd4613b38c20'}, {'abstract': 'The newest song by Kathleen Edwards, who happens to be the girlfriend of Justin Vernon, as just as good as Vernon said it would be.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/justin-vernon-the-good-boyfriend/', 'snippet': 'The newest song by Kathleen Edwards, who happens to be the girlfriend of Justin Vernon, as just as good as Vernon said it would be.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the surprising revelations in Jon Caramanica’s magazine profile of Justin Vernon (the man behind the band Bon Iver) in June — like the existence of a tattoo of the state of Wisconsin on his collarbone — was Vernon’s relationship with the songwriter Kathleen Edwards. According to Vernon, Edwards helped keep him sane, and he, in return, was helping produce her latest record. Vernon, as you would expect, said nice things about his girlfriend: ‘‘I would always try to write songs like her,’’ he told Caramanica. ‘‘I was never that good.’’', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Justin Vernon, the Good Boyfriend', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Courtship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vernon, Justin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bon Iver', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wisconsin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T21:43:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Wm. Ferguson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Wm', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d56aefc2-97e7-5779-8136-6522f5918801', 'word_count': 128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d56aefc2-97e7-5779-8136-6522f5918801'}, {'abstract': 'Wherever one stands on the meaning or significance of the Oct. 7 attacks by the Haqqani network on American-Afghan outposts, two young enlisted soldiers in a bunker appeared to have stopped the crucial component of the attack.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/a-closer-look-at-the-haqqani-anniversary-attack-on-american-afghan-outposts/', 'snippet': 'Wherever one stands on the meaning or significance of the Oct. 7 attacks by the Haqqani network on American-Afghan outposts, two young enlisted soldiers in a bunker appeared to have stopped the crucial component of the attack.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FORWARD OPERATING BASE ORGUN-E, Afghanistan – Last Friday, on the 10th anniversary of the start of the Afghan war, at least several dozen fighters from the Haqqani insurgent network launched a complex attack against multiple American-Afghan outposts near the Pakistan border.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Closer Look at the Haqqani Anniversary Attack on American-Afghan Outposts', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assaults', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Haqqani Network', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T21:45:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By C.J. Chivers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Chivers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a79bdc4a-c33b-50d7-9f6d-c5ed01b3900f', 'word_count': 1421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a79bdc4a-c33b-50d7-9f6d-c5ed01b3900f'}, {'abstract': 'The electronic music producer Deadmau5 performed at Roseland Ballroom last week and his protégé, Skrillex performed at Starland in Sayreville, N.J.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/10/13/arts/music/1013DEADMAU.html', 'snippet': 'The electronic music producer Deadmau5 performed at Roseland Ballroom last week and his protégé, Skrillex performed at Starland in Sayreville, N.J.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The electronic music producer Deadmau5 performed at Roseland Ballroom last week and his protégé, Skrillex performed at Starland in Sayreville, N.J.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/13/arts/music/1013DEADMAU-slide-YU4P/1013DEADMAU-slide-YU4P-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Electronic Master and Pupil', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T21:51:42+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/007c9f94-8579-57a3-aacc-e7e9e0f89371', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/007c9f94-8579-57a3-aacc-e7e9e0f89371'}, {'abstract': 'The electronic music producers Deadmau5 and Skrillex performed sold-out shows in the New York area.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/arts/music/electronic-music-that-plays-to-the-senses-both-sound-and-sight.html', 'snippet': 'The electronic music producers Deadmau5 and Skrillex performed sold-out shows in the New York area.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most of the time, it might not matter what Skrillex is doing with his hands. Every time the bass dropped during his outrageous, frenetic, head-and-body-abusing set at the Starland Ballroom in Sayreville, N. J., on Sunday night, it rendered most other input moot. The bass was a warhead, landing with a boom, sticking around with a wobble.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/arts/JPDEADMAU2/JPDEADMAU2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/12/arts/JPDEADMAU2/JPDEADMAU2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/arts/JPDEADMAU2/JPDEADMAU2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 672, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/arts/JPDEADMAU2/JPDEADMAU2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/12/arts/JPDEADMAU2/JPDEADMAU2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Electronic Music That Plays to the Senses, Both Sound and Sight', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Electronic Music That Plays to the Senses, Both Sound and Sight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T21:55:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3cc5467-fb03-5575-a6ff-8b334eee25f5', 'word_count': 883, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3cc5467-fb03-5575-a6ff-8b334eee25f5'}, {'abstract': 'Matt Read went straight from college to the Flyers of the N.H.L.–and scored his first goal on Saturday against the Devils.', 'web_url': 'https://slapshot.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/from-bemidji-state-to-flyers-rookie/', 'snippet': 'Matt Read went straight from college to the Flyers of the N.H.L.–and scored his first goal on Saturday against the Devils.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEWARK — Before the Flyers played in New Jersey on Saturday, a few of Matt Read’s teammates gave him some advice in the visiting team locker room.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Bemidji State to Flyers Rookie', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'NCAA Ice Hockey Championships', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brodeur, Martin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Laviolette, Peter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN HOCKEY LEAGUE', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Minnesota Wild', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Hockey League', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Jersey Devils', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Philadelphia Flyers', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ontario (Canada)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T21:58:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Botta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Botta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/543410a0-f248-5d07-a024-3d54caec7206', 'word_count': 691, 'uri': 'nyt://article/543410a0-f248-5d07-a024-3d54caec7206'}, {'abstract': 'A new bakery is a throwback to a time when places with homey cakes and pastries tempted many neighborhoods.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/dining/plenty-bakery-opens-on-upper-east-side.html', 'snippet': 'A new bakery is a throwback to a time when places with homey cakes and pastries tempted many neighborhoods.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Plenty is a new bakery, but it’s a throwback to a time when places with homey cakes and pastries tempted many neighborhoods. The owner, Jessica Weiss, pictured, speaks lovingly about the crumb cake, rugelach and such that her father and grandmother baked. She grew up baking on Long Island, in the Five Towns, but until recently ran and owned a tutoring company for standardized tests. She did some catering jobs before opening Plenty, which has a few tables and also serves soups, salads and sandwiches. Gradually, the inventory will expand with black-and-white cookies and more layer cakes.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/dining/12STUFF_BAKE/12STUFF_BAKE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/12/dining/12STUFF_BAKE/12STUFF_BAKE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/dining/12STUFF_BAKE/12STUFF4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/dining/12STUFF_BAKE/12STUFF_BAKE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/12/dining/12STUFF_BAKE/12STUFF_BAKE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Plenty Bakery Opens on Upper East Side', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Plenty, as in Baked Goods', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bakeries and Baked Products', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T22:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/899ae411-c59b-5984-ab66-ed9c5b34f272', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/899ae411-c59b-5984-ab66-ed9c5b34f272'}, {'abstract': 'Steven Judelson and his wife, Natalie, evaporate seawater to make salt with a Hamptons pedigree.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/dining/sea-salt-from-the-surf-off-amagansett.html', 'snippet': 'Steven Judelson and his wife, Natalie, evaporate seawater to make salt with a Hamptons pedigree.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many weekend mornings find Steven Judelson, above, wearing waders and splashing into the surf off the beaches of Amagansett to haul five-gallon buckets of water from the sea. Then he and his wife, Natalie, evaporate it to make sea salt with a Hamptons pedigree. The Judelsons, two well-traveled lawyers who are passionate about food, had seen sea salts from around the world in markets and thought, “Why not out here?” ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/dining/12STUFF_SALT2/12STUFF2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/dining/12STUFF_SALT2/12STUFF_SALT2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/12/dining/12STUFF_SALT2/12STUFF_SALT2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sea Salt Harvested From the Surf Off Amagansett', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sea Salt Harvested From the Surf Off Amagansett', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T22:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d2f95a9-0cb0-50ef-9bb2-c9bad1ec592d', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d2f95a9-0cb0-50ef-9bb2-c9bad1ec592d'}, {'abstract': 'Quinn Popcorn uses organic ingredients and a simple food-grade parchment paper bag instead of a pouch treated with chemical compounds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/dining/microwave-popcorn-minus-the-chemicals.html', 'snippet': 'Quinn Popcorn uses organic ingredients and a simple food-grade parchment paper bag instead of a pouch treated with chemical compounds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kristy and Coulter Lewis were dissatisfied with the ingredients and additives in microwave popcorn. Quinn Popcorn, named for their baby son, uses organic ingredients and a simple food-grade parchment paper bag instead of a pouch treated with compounds like perfluoro-octanoic and metals to keep the bag from absorbing grease and increase the amount of corn that pops. You add Quinn Popcorn’s oil and seasoning mixture after the corn is popped, so grease-proofing the bag is not an issue. And if a few kernels may be duds, what pops is delicious.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/dining/12STUFF_POPCORN/12STUFF3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 695, 'width': 1024, 'su