12/movies/darryl-robertss-america-the-beautiful-2-review.html', 'snippet': 'Darryl Roberts’s documentary “America the Beautiful 2” takes aim at the modeling industry’s skewed approach to beauty and his own personal battle with the bulge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Darryl Roberts’s scattered documentary “America the Beautiful 2” treads similar territory as his “America the Beautiful” from 2008. (Was it really that big a hit?) Among its deserving concerns are the punishing demands for weight loss in the modeling profession, the dire toll of plastic surgery and anorexia, and other manifestations of how the prevailing aesthetic abuses women (and, it suggests, men). A persistent refrain is its view of the body mass index as an insidiously excessive criterion for health.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/movies/america-span/america-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/11/movies/america-span/america-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/movies/america-span/america-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/movies/america-span/america-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/11/movies/america-span/america-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Challenging an Unhealthy Focus on Weight and Aesthetics', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'America the Beautiful 2'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weight', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T22:27:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d82f3214-021d-5fe3-af46-564af5366bd4', 'word_count': 326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d82f3214-021d-5fe3-af46-564af5366bd4'}, {'abstract': 'The real lovers behind a Village sculpture.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/love-in-the-time-of-stonewall/', 'snippet': 'The real lovers behind a Village sculpture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1979, George Segal, the famous Pop artist, was commissioned to create a sculpture to commemorate New York City’s Stonewall uprising in 1969 — the seminal (although not the only) event to kick-start the gay liberation movement. The bronze sculpture, covered with a white patina, was finally unveiled in Christopher Park in Greenwich Village in 1992, after years of controversy. It depicts a life-size male couple standing a few feet away from another life-size female couple sitting on a park bench. Beth Suskin, my partner of 35 years, and I, Leslie Cohen, were the models for this sculpture. This is our love story.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Love in the Time of Stonewall', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Segal, George', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T22:30:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By LESLIE COHEN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leslie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'COHEN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd0e7338-c567-55f7-acc7-a41e027e58ab', 'word_count': 3429, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd0e7338-c567-55f7-acc7-a41e027e58ab'}, {'abstract': 'A talk show featuring Rosie O’Donnell and a series of old episodes of “The Oprah Winfrey Show” are intended to revitalize a network that has gotten off to a slow start.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/two-new-shows-on-oprah-winfrey-network/', 'snippet': 'A talk show featuring Rosie O’Donnell and a series of old episodes of “The Oprah Winfrey Show” are intended to revitalize a network that has gotten off to a slow start.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oprah Winfrey’s 10-month-old cable channel had a reset of sorts on Monday with the introduction of “The Rosie Show,” a 7 p.m. talk show by Rosie O’Donnell, and “Oprah’s Lifeclass,” a remix of old episodes of “The Oprah Winfrey Show,” at 8 p.m.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Two New Shows on Oprah Winfrey Network', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Donnell, Rosie", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winfrey, Oprah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Discovery Channel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oprah Winfrey Network', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T22:31:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48df5bf4-648b-5ab1-b829-a7b4629a1463', 'word_count': 322, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48df5bf4-648b-5ab1-b829-a7b4629a1463'}, {'abstract': 'A Stanford University student finds that your online travel — your clickstream, as it’s poetically known — is not always anonymous. It can often be traced right back to rather precise parts of you.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/stanford-researcher-finds-lots-of-leaky-web-sites/', 'snippet': 'A Stanford University student finds that your online travel — your clickstream, as it’s poetically known — is not always anonymous. It can often be traced right back to rather precise parts of you.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Web is porous. Remarkable information trickles in from everywhere. It also sometimes spills out without its users knowing exactly where or how. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stanford Researcher Finds Lots of Leaky Web Sites', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T22:32:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'