for-abolition/', 'snippet': 'From the beginning of the Civil War, American newspapers fought over what it would mean.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One Saturday in late September 1861, Manhattan witnessed a scene that was becoming commonplace in countless New England towns and cities: well over 1,000 soldiers marched through the city, in the words of one observer, “Singing reverently and enthusiastically in praise of John Brown!” Abolitionism was hardly a universal idea, but the name on everyone’s lips was that of its most fiery, violent advocate. “No military hero of the present war,” the New York Independent noted, “has thus been honored.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A War Not for Abolition', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T22:34:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By R. Blakeslee Gilpin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'R.', 'middlename': 'Blakeslee', 'lastname': 'Gilpin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b75711e3-8921-50ff-965c-18ff089ae353', 'word_count': 1429, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b75711e3-8921-50ff-965c-18ff089ae353'}, {'abstract': 'How about me? Or better, Keynes.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/11/who-you-gonna-bet-on-october-2011/', 'snippet': 'How about me? Or better, Keynes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sorry, but if I am not for myself, who will be for me? For readers new to this, back in 2010 Business Week ran a story contrasting my pessimistic views with the bullish outlook of hedgie John Paulson, and making it clear that only a fool would believe the views of some bearded professor.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who You Gonna Bet On, October 2011', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-11T22:39:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/808320f3-7460-5b31-833b-09b08ef9ef4c', 'word_count': 148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/808320f3-7460-5b31-833b-09b08ef9ef4c'}, {'abstract': 'The groups of protesters inspired by “Occupy Wall Street” are shifting into new postures and responses.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/10/11/us/20111012_PROTESTS.html', 'snippet': 'The groups of protesters inspired by “Occupy Wall Street” are shifting into new postures and responses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The groups of protesters inspired by “Occupy Wall Street” are shifting into new postures and responses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/us/20111012_PROTESTS-slide-HMDL/20111012_PROTESTS-slide-HMDL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/11/us/20111012_PROTESTS-slide-HMDL/20111012_PROTESTS-slide-HMDL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/1