 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77ac12e8-eef6-5602-aee1-46540ebed7db', 'word_count': 778, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77ac12e8-eef6-5602-aee1-46540ebed7db'}, {'abstract': 'The government’s legal memo on Anwar al-Awlaki’s death is a step, but not enough for a momentous decision to kill one of its own citizens.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/opinion/justifying-the-killing-of-an-american.html', 'snippet': 'The government’s legal memo on Anwar al-Awlaki’s death is a step, but not enough for a momentous decision to kill one of its own citizens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Obama administration apparently spent months considering the legal implications of targeting Anwar al-Awlaki, the American citizen who was killed in Yemen last month after being accused of being a terrorist organizer. It prepared a detailed and cautious memorandum to justify the decision — a refreshing change from the reckless legal thinking of the Bush administration, which rationalized torture, claimed unlimited presidential powers and drove the country’s fight against terrorists off the rails.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Justifying the Killing of an American', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Justifying the Killing of an American: The government’s legal memo on Anwar al-Awlaki’s death is a step, but not enough of one', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Awlaki, Anwar al-', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name':