kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T08:47:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dcf7bd1c-8edd-56b1-a833-476e2f9b2c06', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dcf7bd1c-8edd-56b1-a833-476e2f9b2c06'}, {'abstract': 'Sugar with your coffee? An international photography project examines how some everyday items exact an unseen toll half a world away.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/the-true-price-with-a-hidden-cost/', 'snippet': 'Sugar with your coffee? An international photography project examines how some everyday items exact an unseen toll half a world away.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The next time you dip your spoon into the sugar bowl or grab a few free packets for your morning coffee, think about this: Untold numbers of people had to cross borders illegally, live in squalid conditions, and endure back-breaking and dangerous work for slave wages for your convenience.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The True Price, With a Hidden Cost', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T09:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Niko Koppel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Niko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Koppel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9296b66c-2f5c-52b8-8344-9e4da7c9a4ce', 'word_count': 583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9296b66c-2f5c-52b8-8344-9e4da7c9a4ce'}, {'abstract': 'The story behind one of their biggest legislative fights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/magazine/why-facebook-is-after-your-kids.html', 'snippet': 'The story behind one of their biggest legislative fights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In May, Consumer Reports announced that 7.5 million kids age 12 and younger are on Facebook. The magazine called this “troubling news,” in no small part because their presence is at odds with federal law, which bars Web sites from collecting personal data about kids under 13 without permission from their parents. “Clearly, using Facebook presents children and their friends and families with safety, security and privacy risks,” Consumer Reports concluded.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/magazine/16lede_span/mag-16lede-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 322, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/16/magazine/16lede_span/mag-16lede-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 322}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/magazine/16lede_span/mag-16lede-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/magazine/16lede_span/mag-16lede-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/magazine/16lede_span/mag-16lede-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Facebook Is After Your Kids', 'kicker': 'First', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Young And The Friended', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Children's Online Privacy Protection Act", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Friendship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T09:01:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Bazelon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bazelon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8122315f-8c58-5988-9be8-d24d0d5a2764', 'word_count': 1168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8122315f-8c58-5988-9be8-d24d0d5a2764'}, {'abstract': 'The Republican elite tries to take its party back.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/magazine/does-anyone-have-a-grip-on-the-gop.html', 'snippet': 'The Republican elite tries to take its party back.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It wasn’t that long ago that Republican moneymen and operatives in Washington were moping around K Street like Eeyore in the Hundred Acre Wood, lamenting their party’s extremist image and casting about for a candidate with a chance of beating Barack Obama in 2012. Citing what he called the “near self-immolation” of House Republicans during the debt-ceiling fiasco, Bill Kristol, editor of The Weekly Standard, worried in early August that a “large number of Republican primary voters, and even more independent general-election voters, will be wary of supporting a Republican candidate in 2012 if the party looks as if it’s in the grip of an infantile form of conservatism.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/13/us/politics/13debate/13debate-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 701, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/13/us/politics/13debate/13debate-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/13/us/politics/13debate/13debate-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Does Anyone Have a Grip on the G.O.P.?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '"You\'re Nuts!"', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kibbe, Matt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Crossroads', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea Party Movement', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weber, Vin', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rove, Karl', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Malek, Frederic V', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daniels, Mitchell E Jr', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lobbying and Lobbyists', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T09:01:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Bai', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bai', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c7b9192-7d42-5ce5-af25-808c0e1bc9ee', 'word_count': 7122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c7b9192-7d42-5ce5-af25-808c0e1bc9ee'}, {'abstract': 'Can you calculate the cost of educating a student at a particular private school from pre-kindergarten to graduation?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/test-yourself-math-oct-12-2011/', 'snippet': 'Can you calculate the cost of educating a student at a particular private school from pre-kindergarten to graduation?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Test your math skills with this question created by Scott Matthews at Math for America from The Times Magazine article from last month, “What if the Secret to Success Is Failure?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | Math, Oct. 12, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T09:37:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f7f2bf4-dfa3-584d-9a5d-10aafdaea11f', 'word_count': 58, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f7f2bf4-dfa3-584d-9a5d-10