runner.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Caucus Video: Romney, Center Stage', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debates (Political)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T19:45:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8604597b-5f71-5f22-ad99-50f30dd45e60', 'word_count': 19, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8604597b-5f71-5f22-ad99-50f30dd45e60'}, {'abstract': 'CBS has 20 of the top 30 shows among the broadcast networks in total viewership.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/cbs-is-top-network-standing/', 'snippet': 'CBS has 20 of the top 30 shows among the broadcast networks in total viewership.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So far, CBS has done pretty well for itself over the course of this still-young fall season. How well? According to Nielsen ratings data from Sept. 19 through Oct. 9, CBS has 20 of the top 30 shows among the broadcast networks in total viewership, making CBS the first network to accomplish such a feat. It was also the number one network in average viewership and in the 18-to-49-year-old demographic. Stalwarts “NCIS” and “NCIS: LA” certainly helped the network’s cause on Tuesday, garnering 18.7 million viewers and 15.4 million respectively, although the two dramas hit season lows in the 18-to-49 demo. If it can hold steady, ABC will have a new entry in the top 30 with the new Tim Allen comedy “Last Man Standing.” The two-episode series premiere was sampled by nearly 13 million total viewers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CBS Is Top Network Standing', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T19:47:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d19f4ff9-cb19-5e99-811b-3d0285f0b4f6', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d19f4ff9-cb19-5e99-811b-3d0285f0b4f6'}, {'abstract': 'Gotham Extra: If the City Council passes a so-called living wage law over the mayor’s objections, the speaker and mayor should empower an ideologically balanced panel to examine the law’s effects.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/gotham-extra-in-living-wage-talks-warfare-trumps-discourse/', 'snippet': 'Gotham Extra: If the City Council passes a so-called living wage law over the mayor’s objections, the speaker and mayor should empower an ideologically balanced panel to examine the law’s effects.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Long ago, I passed boiling summers driving taxis in New York City (an experience that no doubt still summons nightmares for my former passengers, not to mention wayward pedestrians). Other summers, I worked as a doorman, hauling bags and opening doors for royally snooty Park Avenue dowagers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Living Wage Talks, Warfare Trumps Discourse', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minimum Wage', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Giuliani, Rudolph W', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quinn, Christine C', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City Council (NYC)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Economic Development Corp', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Industrial Areas Foundation', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T19:50:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Powell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Powell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f7cbcbb-c5ae-58fe-9628-b054c2f426ef', 'word_count': 1074, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f7cbcbb-c5ae-58fe-9628-b054c2f426ef'}, {'abstract': 'BlackBerrys went out and bankers suffered.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/dealbook-wall-streets-crackberry-withdrawal/', 'snippet': 'BlackBerrys went out and bankers suffered.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The BlackBerry outage, which seemed contained to the places most afflicted by the sovereign debt crisis, had spread to the heart of American capitalism.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'DealBook: Wall Street’s CrackBerry Withdrawal', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'BlackBerry (Handheld Device)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T19:55:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83dc6fc4-ecc1-549e-91a5-4f1934e6144b', 'word_count': 86, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83dc6fc4-ecc1-549e-91a5-4f1934e6144b'}, {'abstract': 'Occupy Wall Street is again taking aim at JPMorgan Chase’s chief executive. One problem, though — their demonstration isn’t taking place outside his office.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/protesters-take-aim-at-jamie-dimon-but-miss-his-office/', 'snippet': 'Occupy Wall Street is again taking aim at JPMorgan Chase’s chief executive. One problem, though — their demonstration isn’t taking place outside his office.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The nearly month-old Occupy Wall Street protest has apparently settled on a favorite target for their ire, Jamie Dimon. On Tuesday, protestors marched to his apartment building in Manhattan’s Upper East Side, bearing a giant fake check critiquing the low taxes of America’s wealthiest individuals.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Protesters Take Aim at Dimon, but Miss His Office', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dimon, James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T19:56:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf000aa6-516e-54ed-996f-50dfa3af50c9', 'word_count': 290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf000aa6-516e-54ed-996f-50dfa3af50c9'}, {'abstract': '', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2007/08/01/health/100127Leglengtheningseries_index.html', 'snippet': '', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2007/08/01/health/adam/thumbnails/13971t.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2007/08/01/health/adam/thumbnails/13971t.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Leg Lengthening', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T19:57:06+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Health', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/ef26ce8b-e613-53d1-bb89-5f933d920a5d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/ef26ce8b-e613-53d1-bb89-5f933d920a5d'}, {'abstract': 'With the fall television season shaking out, the critics Mike Hale and Neil Genzlinger are taking your questions.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/tv-chat-ask-the-critics-3/', 'snippet': 'With the fall television season shaking out, the critics Mike Hale and Neil Genzlinger are taking your questions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Now that viewers have gotten a look at most of the new shows this season, the fall television picture is becoming clearer: sitcoms like “New Girl” (with Zooey Deschanel) and “2 Broke Girls” (with Kat Dennings) have gotten votes of confidence, Tim Allen’s “Last Man Standing” earned a spot in the Top 30, while “H8R,” “Free Agents” and “How to Be a Gentleman” are out or on their way. Did these shows deserve their fates, for better or worse? Speaking of comedies, HBO unveiled “Enlightened” and came back with a new season of “Bored to Death” and a male breast-feeding scene. Has HBO finally gone too far? The television critics Mike Hale and Neil Genzlinger have posted answers to your questions and comments. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'TV Chat: Ask the Critics', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T20:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/868a215a-ad92-59b7-abaa-f8473699a15f', 'word_count': 3107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/868a215a-ad92-59b7-abaa-f8473699a15f'}, {'abstract': 'A slide show depicting nose surgery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2007/08/01/health/100070Nosesurgeryseries_index.html', 'snippet': 'A slide show depicting nose surgery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A slide show depicting nose surgery.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2007/08/01/health/adam/thumbnails/10172t.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2007/08/01/health/adam/thumbnails/10172t.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headl