Web, college women have instant access to the latest fashion trends. Many take them seriously, leaving the disheveled student look behind.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JULIA FLYNN was darting to her classes at Columbia University last week in a Marc by Marc Jacobs daisy-patterned dress and high-heeled Chloé boots, her polished turnout accessorized with a Starbucks venti latte. “I’m really loving the whole Chanel, Valentino and McQueen shows,” she said. “They completely inspired me.” She had gleaned her information that very morning from Style.com.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/13/fashion/13CAMPUS/13CAMPUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/13/fashion/13CAMPUS/13CAMPUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/13/fashion/13CAMPUS/13CAMPUS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/13/fashion/13CAMPUS/13CAMPUS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/13/fashion/13CAMPUS/13CAMPUS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Campus as Runway', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Campus as Runway', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shoes and Boots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T21:05:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ruth La Ferla', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ruth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Ferla', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/200aba03-877c-5028-8050-0f670ae89e1f', 'word_count': 1262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/200aba03-877c-5028-8050-0f670ae89e1f'}, {'abstract': 'This has been a good week for “American Idol” on the music charts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/arts/music/idol-star-at-no-1.html', 'snippet': 'This has been a good week for “American Idol” on the music charts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This has been a good week for “American Idol” on the music charts. Scotty McCreery, who won this year’s competition, has opened at No. 1 on the latest Billboard chart with his first album, “Clear as Day” (Mercury Nashville/19 Recordings/Interscope), and reached numerous sales milestones. “Clear as Day,” which sold 197,000 copies in the United States, according to Nielsen SoundScan, had the biggest opening week in three years for an “Idol” winner’s first album, and it is also the first debut by an “Idol” champion to open at No. 1 since Ruben Studdard’s “Soulful,” in 2003. If that were not enough, Mr. McCreery, who turned 18 on Sunday, is also the first country act to open at No. 1 with a debut, and is the youngest man to have a first album start in the top slot, Billboard reported. Also this week, Adele’s unstoppable “21” (XL/Columbia) is No. 2 on the album chart with 112,000 sales, and her recent single, “Someone Like You,” remains the No. 1 digital song, with 218,000 downloads. The No. 2 spot on the digital songs chart is also unchanged from last week: Maroon 5’s “Moves Like Jagger,” featuring Christina Aguilera, had 186,000 downloads. Tony Bennett’s “Duets II” (Columbia) is the No. 3 album this week with 71,000. Other new titles in this week’s Top 10 include Feist’s “Metals” (Cherrytree/Interscope), at No. 7, and “Take a Back Road” (Curb), the fourth album by Rodney Atkins, at No. 8.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Idol’ Star at No. 1', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Idol’ Star at No. 1', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T21:16:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e60bf9d-1d21-505f-b930-c3015ada063e', 'word_count': 245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e60bf9d-1d21-505f-b930-c3015ada063e'}, {'abstract': 'The violinist Hilary Hahn, celebrating the release of her new album, performed Charles Ives’s violin sonatas and various hymns at the Stone in the East Village.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/arts/music/the-violinist-hilary-hahn-at-the-stone-review.html', 'snippet': 'The violinist Hilary Hahn, celebrating the release of her new album, performed Charles Ives’s violin sonatas and various hymns at the Stone in the East Village.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Welcome to the Stone, and to the Ives geek-out party,” the violinist Hilary Hahn announced brightly on Monday at the Stone, a tiny, boxy East Village new-music laboratory operated by the saxophonist and composer John Zorn. Ms. Hahn, a performer who draws sizable throngs to prominent auditoriums, was making a point by celebrating her admirable new CD, “Charles Ives: Four Sonatas,” of Ives’s violin sonatas at Mr. Zorn’s space, which seats fewer than 100. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/13/arts/HAHN/HAHN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 765, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/13/arts/HAHN/HAHN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/13/arts/HAHN/HAHN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Embracing Communal and Hymnal', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Embracing Communal And Hymnal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hahn, Hilary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T21:22:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/80fe24f2-99bb-57b6-b408-30f35f8c8e12', 'word_count': 419, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80fe24f2-99bb-57b6-b408-30f35f8c8e12'}, {'abstract': 'Macroeconomics is not a morality play.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/i-am-not-your-mirror-image/', 'snippet': 'Macroeconomics is not a morality play.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Russ Roberts tries to debunk my explanation of the reasons to believe in a broadly Keynesian view of the world — and reveals more than he intended.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'I Am Not Your Mirror Image', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T21:23:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f2912d2-0085-53a4-8211-74d6671cf763', 'word_count': 773, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f2912d2-0085-53a4-8211-74d6671cf763'}, {'abstract': "The high-energy, risk-taking traits of attention deficit disorder may be part of the nation's pioneer DNA. Or maybe we're just overmedicated.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/roomfordebate/2011/10/12/are-americans-more-prone-to-adhd/', 'snippet': "The high-energy, risk-taking traits of attention deficit disorder may be part of the nation's pioneer DNA. Or maybe we're just overmedicated.", 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are Americans More Prone to A.D.H.D.?', 'kicker': 'Room for Debate', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T21:25:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2f696dc-d4b8-50c8-a313-b9d77b7c0f0a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2f696dc-d4b8-50c8-a313-b9d77b7c0f0a'}, {'abstract': 'Is love blind or merely obstinate? Two women, two tales of indifference.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/romancing-the-reich/', 'snippet': 'Is love blind or merely obstinate? Two women, two tales of indifference.', 'lead_paragraph': 'They met in 1929, in the photographer’s shop where she worked, in Munich. He was 40, she was 17. He devoured her with his eyes, she said. Soon after, he had her family investigated, to check that her bloodlines were Aryan. He was Adolf Hitler, an amateur artist whose charisma as a reactionary orator had propelled him to the forefront of Germany’s National Socialist party. She was Eva Braun, an attractive blonde from a middle-class family who, after one year at a school run by nuns, was back home living with her parents. We know how the story ends, though we can’t help wondering why — was she completely lacking in intelligence, or morals, or both? In “Eva Braun: Life With Hitler,” translated from the German by Damion Searls (Alfred A. Knopf), Heike B. Görtemaker takes on a task few biographers — I’m tempted to say no biographer in her right mind — would tackle, so scant and unreliable is the material.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romancing the Reich', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Braun, Eva', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dodd, Martha', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gortemaker, Heike B', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hitler, Adolf', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Larson, Erik', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T21:30:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Brubach', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brubach', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b77e2ef2-2bf5-521e-b8ca-87a3fadc6c30', 'word_count': 1780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b77e2ef2-2bf5-521e-b8ca-87a3fadc6c30'}, {'abstract': '“Ordinary Witnesses,” choreographed by Rachid Ouramdane, expresses post-traumatic conditions in movement imagery, and in the process extends the notion of what dance can express.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/arts/dance/rachid-ouramdanes-ordinary-witnesses-review.html', 'snippet': '“Ordinary Witnesses,” choreographed by Rachid Ouramdane, expresses post-traumatic conditions in movement imagery, and in the process extends the notion of what dance can express.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What has dance got to do with genocide or torture? At first the mind recoils from any such link. Yet amazingly, the French-Algerian choreographer Rachid Ouramdane plunges in, evoking post-traumatic conditions in movement imagery, and in the process he extends the whole notion of what dance can express. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/13/arts/WITNESS/WITNESS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/13/arts/WITNESS/WITNESS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/13/arts/WITNESS/WITNESS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Expressing Trauma in Words and Spins', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Expressing Trauma in Words and Spins', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ouramdane, Rachid', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-12T21:30:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/32f712e3-d465-582a-a943-93f491960d89', 'word_count': 520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32f712e3-d465-582a-a943-93f491960d89'}, {'abstract': 'Solange Knowles answers fashion questions; Uniqlo opens, Cameo rings and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/10/13/fashion/20111013-BROWSING.html', 'snippet': 'Solange Knowles answers fashion questions; Uniqlo opens, Cameo rings and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Solange Knowles answers fashion questions; Uniqlo opens, Cameo rings and more.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/13/fashion/20111013-BROWSING-slide-UJIG/20111013-BROWSING-slide-UJIG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/13/fashion/20111013-BROWSING-slide-UJIG/20111013-BROWSING-slide-UJIG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/13/fashion/20111013-BROWSING-slide-UJIG/20111013-BROWSING-slide-UJIG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnai