all', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Newton, Cameron J', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bowl Games', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T00:25:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pete Thamel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pete', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thamel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2fff50e0-e8c2-5152-b11e-a883785a4a99', 'word_count': 474, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2fff50e0-e8c2-5152-b11e-a883785a4a99'}, {'abstract': 'There are now three iPhone 4S carriers. Here’s one way to calculate which one is best for you.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/how-to-pick-the-best-iphone-4s-carrier/', 'snippet': 'There are now three iPhone 4S carriers. Here’s one way to calculate which one is best for you.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’re in the market for an iPhone 4S, you may be wondering which carrier you should give your money to. After all, you now have a new choice to consider, Sprint, not to mention the ever-shifting landscape of each carrier’s plans and add-ons. Let’s try to break it down.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Pick the Best iPhone 4S Carrier', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Telephones and Telecommunications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AT&T Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sprint Nextel Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Verizon Communications Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T00:29:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Grobart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grobart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/162e867f-c092-5da4-bc65-61d370964d7a', 'word_count': 692, 'uri': 'nyt://article/162e867f-c092-5da4-bc65-61d370964d7a'}, {'abstract': 'I donated the three books I’d written to the cafe’s free bookcase, and then I drove myself crazy, waiting to see if anyone would take them home.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/12/a-shelf-obsessed-writer/', 'snippet': 'I donated the three books I’d written to the cafe’s free bookcase, and then I drove myself crazy, waiting to see if anyone would take them home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I can’t remember when I first became obsessed with the cafe’s free bookcase, but sometime in the last year it was suddenly so, and I was regularly making pilgrimages there. I took books home to read, and brought others in to replace them. I noticed which ones were snapped up quickly and which sat for too long. I rooted for the galley of Emily St. John Mandel’s smart novel “The Singer’s Gun” to find a good home, but felt a little smug about another novel — which sucked up all the press in the universe when it came out, even though it was not its author’s finest work, not by a long shot, and one might wonder if perhaps the author was even phoning it in — which no one touched for weeks. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Shelf-Obsessed Writer', 'kicker': 'Townies', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T00:30:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jami Attenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jami', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Attenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5097f25c-8961-5130-8a26-5a754dc3af19', 'word_count': 1777, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5097f25c-8961-5130-8a26-5a754dc3af19'}, {'abstract': 'A military inquiry into a shoot-down that caused the largest loss of American life during a single day of the war found the actions by those on the mission were sound.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/world/asia/report-finds-no-wrongdoing-in-afghanistan-helicopter-crash.html', 'snippet': 'A military inquiry into a shoot-down that caused the largest loss of American life during a single day of the war found the actions by those on the mission were sound.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON -- The official military investigation into a helicopter shoot-down that caused the largest loss of American life during a single day of the war in Afghanistan has found no wrongful actions by personnel involved in the mission, nor were there equipment failures.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Report Finds No Wrongdoing in Afghanistan Copter Crash', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Afghanistan: Copter Crash Inquiry Clears Personnel and Equipment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War 