had to have “a real respect for the thing in front of him.” Through his long career – photographing everything from coal miners, sharecroppers, railroad men and Puerto Rican canecutters – he conveyed a deep respect for not just the travails of Everyman, but a true appreciation of the dignity that lay within.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jack Delano’s American Sonata', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T09:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Gonzalez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonzalez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb45b3bb-5f2d-519a-9576-c7244b37ce04', 'word_count': 1175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb45b3bb-5f2d-519a-9576-c7244b37ce04'}, {'abstract': 'The Congressional supercommittee dealing with the budget deficit is contemplating the notion that tax cuts will provoke growth and more revenue — and that is a dangerous approach, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/can-tax-cuts-pay-for-themselves/', 'snippet': 'The Congressional supercommittee dealing with the budget deficit is contemplating the notion that tax cuts will provoke growth and more revenue — and that is a dangerous approach, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Simon Johnson, the former chief economist at the International Monetary Fund, is the co-author of “13 Bankers.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can Tax Cuts Pay for Themselves?', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mankiw, N Gregory', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Congressional Budget Office', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T09:00:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdb941c7-cd72-5008-a420-137cc3f46fc1', 'word_count': 1001, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdb941c7-cd72-5008-a420-137cc3f46fc1'}, {'abstract': 'She was a landscape architect in New York. He was a prison inmate in Arkansas. This is how they fell in love.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/magazine/a-death-row-love-story.html', 'snippet': 'She was a landscape architect in New York. He was a prison inmate in Arkansas. This is how they fell in love.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The letter came through a slot in the cell door. It was early 1996. Damien Echols looked at the postmark — Brooklyn, N.Y. — and the name on the return address: Lorri Davis. He opened the envelope and examined the messy handwriting.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '52', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/magazine/16prison1_span/16prison1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/16/magazine/16prison1_span/16prison1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/magazine/16prison1_span/16prison1_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/magazine/16prison1_span/16prison1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/magazine/16prison1_span/16prison1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Death-Row Love Story', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'My Dearest Damien', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Letters', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Love (Emotion)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'False Arrests, Convictions and Imprisonments', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Echols, Damien', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T09:01:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoffrey Gray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7604ab6d-77c5-503d-a86b-25f5c0b46bd4', 'word_count': 2917, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7604ab6d-77c5-503d-a86b-25f5c0b46bd4'}, {'abstract': 'Can you choose the verb that correctly completes a given sentence?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/test-yourself-english-oct-13-2011/', 'snippet': 'Can you choose the verb that correctly completes a given sentence?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The question was written by Shannon Doyne. It comes from the article, “Realignment of Star Power.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | English, Oct. 13, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T09:09:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bb0f00e-e59a-50ba-9eda-23cf6da6b91d', 'word_count': 43, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bb0f00e-e59a-50ba-9eda-23cf6da6b91d'}, {'abstract': 'Do you feel comfortable raising your hand during a class discussion?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/do-you-participate-in-class/', 'snippet': 'Do you feel comfortable raising your hand during a class discussion?', 'lead_paragraph': 'October 13, 10:40 p.m. | Updated  A history professor at the County College of Morris in Randolph, N.J., asked a student with a stutter not to raise his hand during class because she felt his stuttering took up valuable class time (although the professor later disputed that account). What do you think about the professor’s decision not to allow the student to participate verbally in class? What has been your experience with class participation?  ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Do You Participate in Class?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T09:15:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susannah L. Griffee and Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susannah', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Griffee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c917d21-018a-57e2-af55-c9052689678f', 'word_count': 488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c917d21-018a-57e2-af55-c9052689678f'}, {'abstract': 'The Indian market for comics and graphic novels is booming and there is no better proof of this than the second Comic Convention. An “Express” version will run Oct. 22 and 23 at the World Trade Center in Mumbai.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/mumbai-comic-convention-spotlights-local-talent/', 'snippet': 'The Indian market for comics and graphic novels is booming and there is no better proof of this than the second Comic Convention. An “Express” version will run Oct. 22 and 23 at the World Trade Center in Mumbai.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Indian market for comics and graphic novels is booming, and there is no better proof of this than the second Comic Con India. The first such convention took place in Delhi earlier this February and will remain the key event in future while Mumbai will see an “Express” version, running Oct. 22 and 23 at the World Trade Center (Cuffe Parade; 91-22-6638-7272; comicconindia.com/Express), that will travel to different cities each year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mumbai Comic Convention Spotlights Local Talent', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graphic Novels', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mumbai (India)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T10:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charukesi Ramadurai', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charukesi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ramadurai', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3acda1a-7798-5f02-99cb-408205da5663', 'word_count': 257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3acda1a-7798-5f02-99cb-408205da5663'}, {'abstract': 'The whole idea of statistics is to find a mathematical representation of what happened in the game. The truth is, some turnovers aren’t really turnovers.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/n-f-l-needs-fair-method-for-recording-interception-statistics/', 'snippet': 'The whole idea of statistics is to find a mathematical representation of what happened in the game. The truth is, some turnovers aren’t really turnovers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the final play in the first half of Sunday’s game between the Jets and the Patriots, Tom Brady threw a quick out to tight end Aaron Hernandez. Jets cornerback Antonio Cromartie intercepted the pass near the Patriots’ goal line and ran the ball back to the 41-yard-line as the clock expired.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'N.F.L. Needs Fair Method for Recording Interception Statistics', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T10:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Benoit', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benoit', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/950fd827-f24c-58fa-bc0e-fc3c650a4be6', 'word_count': 397, 'uri': 'nyt://article/950fd827-f24c-58fa-bc0e-fc3c650a4be6'}, {'abstract': 'It was one of those New York “it” buildings with a superprime, recession-proof location. So how did it end up nearly empty?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/magazine/what-went-wrong-at-one-madison-park.html', 'snippet': 'It was one of those New York “it” buildings with a superprime, recession-proof location. So how did it end up nearly empty?', 'lead_paragraph': '“Where else in Manhattan can you get this?” shouted Ira Shapiro as he stood high above the streets of Manhattan on the penthouse terrace of One Madison Park. It was a bright blue spring morning, and he stretched out his arms to show off the view, a clear shot in three directions: north to the Empire State Building, south to the Brooklyn Bridge and west over the Hudson River to Jersey City. Music drifted upward from the bandstand in Madison Square Park, a lovely expanse of trees and paths, 57 stories below. It was by far the tallest building on the block. “We wanted to change the skyline of Manhattan,” Shapiro said. “And we did.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '84', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/magazine/16madison2/mag-16Madison-t_CA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/magazine/16madison2/16madison2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'cr