nt_headline': 'New Zealand: Oil Spill Is Nation’s Worst', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Zealand', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil Spills', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T12:45:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Hutchison', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hutchison', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1c43a28-a11c-5f19-9eed-bfad0240c756', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1c43a28-a11c-5f19-9eed-bfad0240c756'}, {'abstract': 'That word does not mean what you think it means.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/varieties-of-uncertainty/', 'snippet': 'That word does not mean what you think it means.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some of the usual suspects have lately been touting a research paper by Baker et al that supposedly shows that policy uncertainty is an important factor in restraining the economy, and claiming that this vindicates the Republican view. Larry Mishel sets the record straight.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Varieties Of Uncertainty', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T12:52:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe4022c0-c0c9-5acd-ae9b-cd827668ce21', 'word_count': 199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe4022c0-c0c9-5acd-ae9b-cd827668ce21'}, {'abstract': 'Cameron’s delusions.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/low-rates-as-a-sign-of-failure/', 'snippet': 'Cameron’s delusions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brad DeLong and Atrios pile on to David Cameron for suggesting that his austerity program, which has rather notably failed to deliver growth, is responsible for Britain’s low borrowing costs. As they say, British rates are low for the same reason US rates are low — not as a reward for fiscal virtue, but because everyone now expects the economy to stay depressed, and policy rates near zero, for years to come.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Low Rates As A Sign Of Failure', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T13:01:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By PAUL KRUGMAN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'KRUGMAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83cad29d-1923-50d9-8e88-a2e9ed69bc77', 'word_count': 126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83cad29d-1923-50d9-8e88-a2e9ed69bc77'}, {'abstract': 'Laura Marling, a 21-year-old British sensation, is perhaps the quintessential folk singer of her generation.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/timely-laura-marling-will-of-the-wisp/', 'snippet': 'Laura Marling, a 21-year-old British sensation, is perhaps the quintessential folk singer of her generation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Songs simmer awhile inside her head before choosing their own moment to froth out fully formed. Or so has claimed Laura Marling, a 21-year-old British sensation who is perhaps the quintessential folk singer of her generation. It’s a romantic idea, this ghostly pale lass with wispy blond hair transmuting somehow — perhaps via inspiration from the Muses — her own admittedly tangled emotions into divinely inspired songs to be sung by her divinely bestowed voice. And it’s an idea bolstered by the fact that her first two albums earned her a Mercury Prize nomination each. Marling’s newly released third effort, “A Creature I Don’t Know,” poetically titled like all her albums, trades some of her teenage urgency for a more muscular, acrobatic lyricism — the result being 10 tracks that feel evolved, or more grown-up even. In any case, it’s clear that when Marling found this new sound, the Muses did not abandon her.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Timely | Laura Marling, Will of the Wisp', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Folk Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marling, Laura', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T13:05:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jacob Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jacob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/692a3f36-4221-5f2e-aad9-9202ee607dc8', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/692a3f36-4221-5f2e-aad9-9202ee607dc8'}, {'abstract': 'In the 1990s, Mike Judge unleashed a pair of giggling cultural commentators. Now they’re back, returning to a very different culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/magazine/the-eternal-adolescence-of-beavis-and-butt-head.html', 'snippet': 'In the 1990s, Mike Judge unleashed a pair of giggling cultural commentators. Now they’re back, returning to a very different culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some 15 years ago, these were two of the best-known laughs in America. One begins with an uhhhh that gives way to an airy, affected heh-heh-heh — it could belong to a snob who has had one too many electroshock treatments — while the other is more guttural, a throat-clearing hnhnhnhn that sometimes shifts to a higher register and back again, like Scooby-Doo being strangled. The first belongs to Butt-Head, the second to Beavis, and in their animated show, which MTV broadcast from 1993 to 1997, that laughter punctuates just about everything the two boneheaded teenagers utter. It’s so automatic that I imagined it had actually been automated — that an engineer pressed a button every time their laughter was called for, and voilà: uhhhh-heh-heh-heh, or hnhnhnhn, or both overlapping.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/magazine/16judge2/mag-16Judge-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 759, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/magazine/16judge2/mag-16Judge-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/magazine/16judge2/mag-16Judge-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Eternal Adolescence of Beavis and Butt-Head', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Dumbest Guys In The Room', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Judge, Mike', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T13:25:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Olsson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Olsson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ebe7e658-e6c2-5675-b444-e1637fa89dbb', 'word_count': 3530, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ebe7e658-e6c2-5675-b444-e1637fa89dbb'}, {'abstract': 'An exhibition at the Los Angeles County Museum of Art takes visitors on a journey through California in all its midcentury glory.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/seeing-things-california-design-1930-1965/', 'snippet': 'An exhibition at the Los Angeles County Museum of Art takes visitors on a journey through California in all its midcentury glory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog480\',appendTo: \'fullScreenSlideShow\',showHeadLine: \'false\', renderstyle: "embeddedSlideShowTmag"}', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeing Things | ‘California Design, 1930-1965’', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T14:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Hodge', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6bd4410-bbb5-5ac1-856f-05a4e1f9ebf7', 'word_count': 605, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6bd4410-bbb5-5ac1-856f-05a4e1f9ebf7'}, {'abstract': 'The Bridge Project’s production of “Richard III,” starring Mr. Spacey and directed by Sam Mendes, will be followed by “Being Shakespeare,” a one-man drama about the playwright starring Simon Callow, and Harold Pinter’s “The Caretaker,” starring Jonathan Pryce.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/spaceys-richard-iii-arrives-in-bams-new-winter-spring-season/', 'snippet': 'The Bridge Project’s production of “Richard III,” starring Mr. Spacey and directed by Sam Mendes, will be followed by “Being Shakespeare,” a one-man drama about the playwright starring Simon Callow, and Harold Pinter’s “The Caretaker,” starring Jo...', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a certain Shakespearean monarch has said, “An honest tale speeds best, being plainly told,” and in the meantime a not-so-plain production of “Richard III” will make its way to the Brooklyn Academy of Music in January as part of the 2012 winter-spring season. The Bridge Project’s presentation of the play, starring Kevin Spacey as the “deformed, unfinsh’d” Duke of Gloucester and directed by Sam Mendes, will be presented at the Harvey Theater from Jan. 10 through March 4 with its official opening night scheduled for Jan. 18, the academy said Thursday. The production was first staged over the summer at the Old Vic Theater in London (where Ben Brantley wrote that Mr. Spacey “gives fierce and flashy physical life to every twist of a power-mad man’s corkscrew mind”) and will be the final season of the Bridge Project, a three-year series of collaborations among the academy, the Old Vic and Neal Street, Mr. Mendes’s company.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spacey’s ‘Richard III’ Arrives in BAM’s New Winter-Spring Season', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bridge Project, The', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Academy of Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T14:13:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1e98d13-0d02-5c21-9480-e62d201bcc04', 'word_count': 309, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1e98d13-0d02-5c21-9480-e62d201bcc04'}, {'abstract': 'Experts are blaming deforestation, urban sprawl and bad planning for turning an unusually heavy monsoon season in Thailand into a disaster.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/world/asia/a-natural-disaster-in-thailand-guided-by-human-hand.html', 'snippet': 'Experts are blaming deforestation, urban sprawl and bad planning for turning an unusually heavy monsoon season in Thailand into a disaster.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BANGKOK — As some of Thailand’s worst flooding in half a century bears down on Bangkok — submerging cities, industrial parks and ancient temples as it comes — experts in water management are blaming human activity for turning an unusually heavy monsoon season into a disaster. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/world/14thailand_337/14thailand_337-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 706, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As Thailand Floods Spread, Experts Blame Officials, Not Rains', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Thailand Floods Spread, Experts Blame Officials, Not Rains', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Thailand', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bangkok (Thailand)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disasters and Emergencies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T14:13:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Mydans', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mydans', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4719d5ed-39aa-5949-a7e5-b5e77a8814e9', 'word_count': 826, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4719d5ed-39aa-5949-a7e5-b5e77a8814e9'}, {'abstract': 'An interactive guide to the government’s biggest victory yet in a widening investigation of insider trading. The prosecution of Raj Rajaratman, the chief of Galleon, has focused attention on hedge funds and expert networks.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/interactive-insider-trading-at-galleon/', 'snippet': 'An interactive guide to the government’s biggest victory yet in a widening investigation of insider trading. The prosecution of Raj Rajaratman, the chief of Galleon, has focused attention on hedge funds and expert networks.', 'lead_paragraph': '1:45 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Interactive: Insider Trading at Galleon', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T14:25:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Owles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Owles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42b4b0da-9923-5589-8634-edf5d67eb12a', 'word_count': 63, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42b4b0da-9923-5589-8634-edf5d67eb12a'}, {'abstract': 'The Taxi and Limousine Commission says it will consider a plan to do away with the smaller “off-duty” lights in favor of a single, central beacon, but City Room wonders: Don’t you have a better idea?', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/vacant-taxi-or-occupied-that-is-the-question/', 'snippet': 'The Taxi and Limousine Commission says it will consider a plan to do away with the smaller “off-duty” lights in favor of a single, central beacon, but City Room wonders: Don’t you have a better idea?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The humble, occasionally head-scratching taxicab roof light is coming in for a makeover. New Yorkers, what would you like to see sitting atop your cab?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vacant Taxi or Occupied? That Is the Question', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxicabs and Taxicab Drivers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taxi and Limousine Commission', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T14:26:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19619ada-2197-5a00-a2f0-fe3d8b8d7e29', 'word_count': 273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19619ada-2197-5a00-a2f0-fe3d8b8d7e29'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/what-were-reading-289/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'St. Lawrence University: Each fall, 12 undergraduates are chosen to spend the semester in Arcadia, a camp site deep inside Adirondack National Park where they fend for themselves for four months, studying geology, botany and survival skills. Two current students wrote this account of what they grow, cook (in their yurt kitchen), and eat in the wild. — Julia Moskin', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T14:30:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6459df0-7b79-5a26-bf0b-3ccfa48a3a26', 'word_count': 471, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6459df0-7b79-5a26-bf0b-3ccfa48a3a26'}, {'abstract': 'Figures on trade in both of the world’s largest economies signaled slower growth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/business/economy/us-and-china-data-highlight-weakness-in-global-economy.html', 'snippet': 'Figures on trade in both of the world’s largest economies signaled slower growth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The weakness in the global economy was underscored by reports published Thursday about the balance of trade in the United States and China. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. and China Data Highlight Weakness in Global Economy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. and China Data Highlight Weakness in Global Economy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value'