[], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T15:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By ROBERT S. BOYNTON', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'BOYNTON', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/61cc8acf-b285-52c0-b61b-58ed614cd392', 'word_count': 988, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61cc8acf-b285-52c0-b61b-58ed614cd392'}, {'abstract': 'Elaine May conducts “an in-depth interview” with Ethan Coen and Woody Allen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/theater/elaine-may-interviews-ethan-coen-and-woody-allen.html', 'snippet': 'Elaine May conducts “an in-depth interview” with Ethan Coen and Woody Allen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE producers of “Relatively Speaking” (which opens at the Brooks Atkinson on Oct. 20) have asked me to conduct an in-depth interview with Ethan Coen and Woody Allen, with whom it turns out I have written three one-act plays.\xa0 I have done this by submitting questions to both men who will presumably answer them in depth.\xa0 I’ve interviewed Woody Allen before but I have only recently met Ethan Coen and we have never spoken although our silence is friendly.\xa0 My challenge will be to ask the probing questions that will reveal the deeper, more complex nature of both men in a way that will not hurt ticket sales. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/arts/16RELATIVELY_SPAN/16RELATIVELY_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/16/arts/16RELATIVELY_SPAN/16RELATIVELY_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/arts/16RELATIVELY_SPAN/16RELATIVELY_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/arts/16RELATIVELY_SPAN/16RELATIVELY_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/arts/16RELATIVELY_SPAN/16RELATIVELY_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Allen, Coen and May: 3 Wits, One Show', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Allen, Coen and May: 3 Wits, One Show', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALLEN, WOODY', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coen, Ethan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'May, Elaine', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T15:01:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By ELAINE MAY', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MAY', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8b89afc-4ae9-57ff-82d9-a17c65565c12', 'word_count': 1188, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8b89afc-4ae9-57ff-82d9-a17c65565c12'}, {'abstract': 'With the release of “Mylo Xyloto” this month, Coldplay has had to prove itself a worthy inheritor to the biggest-band-in-the-world title and savior of the commercial viability of rock.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/arts/music/chris-martin-of-coldplay-discusses-mylo-xyloto.html', 'snippet': 'With the release of “Mylo Xyloto” this month, Coldplay has had to prove itself a worthy inheritor to the biggest-band-in-the-world title and savior of the commercial viability of rock.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chris Martin, the lead singer of Coldplay, is one of the world’s biggest rock stars, a species for whom tardiness is all but a right. Yet he was full of apologies when he popped through the door of a Midtown Manhattan restaurant, no entourage in sight, for a recent interview.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/arts/16COLDPLAY1_SPAN/16COLDPLAY1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/16/arts/16COLDPLAY1_SPAN/16COLDPLAY1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/arts/16COLDPLAY1_SPAN/16JPCOLDPLAY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 715, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/arts/16COLDPLAY1_SPAN/16COLDPLAY1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/arts/16COLDPLAY1_SPAN/16COLDPLAY1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chris Martin of Coldplay Asks: What Would Bruce Do?', 'kicker': 'Arts & Leisure', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Would Bruce Do?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Coldplay', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value'