 'subject', 'value': 'Gymnastics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Volleyball', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T17:09:23+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/1a20af77-a9c2-5a80-95ef-8e5c7348150d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/1a20af77-a9c2-5a80-95ef-8e5c7348150d'}, {'abstract': 'The recent Good to Go show at the Bowery Poetry Club was a mishmash of performance types.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/notes-from-the-underground-good-to-go/', 'snippet': 'The recent Good to Go show at the Bowery Poetry Club was a mishmash of performance types.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The recent Good to Go show at the Bowery Poetry Club was a mishmash of performance types. The evening’s host, the Canadian comedy star and television personality Sabrina Jalees, kept the audience entertained with hilarious in-character stand-up sketches, and Ben Lerman, an unassuming guy with a ukulele, sang an unexpectedly gripping story about being Ben Lerman — and playing the ukulele. The show was peppered with original choreography from the Good to Go Girls — Jessy Smith, Molly Merkler and Jillian Hollis (shown here) — a bubbly trio of classically trained dancers whose skits toyed with physical comedy and burlesque. All in all, it was a surprisingly professional and well-curated event from one of downtown’s few remaining homes for the obscure.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Notes From the Underground | Good to Go', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T17:15:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By IO Tillett Wright', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Io', 'middlename': 'Tillett', 'lastname': 'Wright', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca730d41-35e4-508b-ac8c-7a39b4947395', 'word_count': 121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca730d41-35e4-508b-ac8c-7a39b4947395'}, {'abstract': 'As a parent, you imagine your children will adopt your excellent tastes, and it can be disheartening when they develop an enthusiasm for Hannah Montana or ‘‘The Real Housewives of New Jersey’’ (‘‘Gilligan’s Island’’ was so much better).', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/my-three-favorite-recommendations-from-my-teenage-daughter/', 'snippet': 'As a parent, you imagine your children will adopt your excellent tastes, and it can be disheartening when they develop an enthusiasm for Hannah Montana or ‘‘The Real Housewives of New Jersey’’ (‘‘Gilligan’s Island’’ was so much better).', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a parent, you imagine your children will adopt your excellent tastes, and it can be disheartening when they develop an enthusiasm for Hannah Montana or ‘‘The Real Housewives of New Jersey’’ (‘‘Gilligan’s Island’’ was so much better). But they can also lead you to some pretty great discoveries. To wit:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'My Three Favorite Recommendations from My Teenage Daughter', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Laughter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Condon, Zach', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dunbar, Robin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Brien, Tim", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Beirut (Music Group)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T17:31:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vera Titunik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vera', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Titunik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/18bbbb76-4e0b-5eff-9e4f-a7a1322f1200', 'word_count': 208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18bbbb76-4e0b-5eff-9e4f-a7a1322f1200'}, {'abstract': 'Everyone has her own shopping pattern for Bloomingdale’s, the mother ship on Lexington Avenue and 59th Street, but mine turns out to be not so original. I always head to the contemporary brands on the second floor.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/at-redone-bloomingdales-hit-the-escalators/', 'snippet': 'Everyone has her own shopping pattern for Bloomingdale’s, the mother ship on Lexington Avenue and 59th Street, but mine turns out to be not so original. I always head to the contemporary brands on the second floor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EVERYONE has her own shopping pattern for Bloomingdale’s, the mother ship on Lexington Avenue and 59th Street, but mine turns out to be not so original. I always head to the contemporary brands on the second floor, skip the third entirely (a bland sea of office clothes) and the cruise designer stuff on the fourth. I have my favorite corners — J Brand and Theory — but I never ventured into the low-ceilinged, crammed denim area. Just too uninviting.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Redone Bloomingdale’s, Hit the Escalators', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T17:48:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d210414f-66f4-5cbf-b7ea-ad2e84139dd9', 'word_count': 510, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d210414f-66f4-5cbf-b7ea-ad2e84139dd9'}, {'abstract': 'In the last presidential campaign, Herman Cain wrote that Mitt Romney was Republican voters’  “best hope.”', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/in-2008-cain-endorsed-romney/', 'snippet': 'In the last presidential campaign, Herman Cain wrote that Mitt Romney was Republican voters’  “best hope.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'As he competes with Mitt Romney for the Republican presidential nomination, Herman Cain is quick to tick off his list of what makes Mr. Romney unelectable. But Conor Friedersdorf of The Atlantic points out that in 2008, Mr. Cain ignored those same objections and endorsed Mr. Romney over several other candidates, including Representative Ron Paul of Texas and Senator John McCain of Arizona, who went on to win the nomination.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In 2008, Cain Endorsed Romney', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T17:48:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Southall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Southall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/780f92be-8ea0-5e53-b4f8-7f5d424c5a3e', 'word_count': 199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/780f92be-8ea0-5e53-b4f8-7f5d424c5a3e'}, {'abstract': 'Pacific Standard Time, an art and design festival in Los Angeles, makes a