89dd-342f329b5204'}, {'abstract': '“New Photography 2011,” an exhibition of six artists at the Museum of Modern Art, makes for a useful if not revelatory crossfire of ideas.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/arts/design/new-photography-2011-features-6-artists-at-moma-review.html', 'snippet': '“New Photography 2011,” an exhibition of six artists at the Museum of Modern Art, makes for a useful if not revelatory crossfire of ideas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the 1980s photography mutated into a monster that threatened to swallow fine art altogether. In the hands of artists like Cindy Sherman, Jeff Wall, Gregory Crewdson, Andreas Gursky and legions of copycats, photography parsed the day’s most urgent questions about representation, propaganda, truth and reality. But in the ensuing decades, the answers became increasingly routine, and today the beast that art photography was finds itself tired and toothless. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/arts/14PHOTOGRAPHY/PHOTOGRAPHY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 821, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/arts/14PHOTOGRAPHY/14PHOTOGRAPHY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/14/arts/14PHOTOGRAPHY/14PHOTOGRAPHY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Exploring Truth (and Fiction) Through a Camera Lens', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Exploring Truth (and Fiction) Through a Camera Lens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Modern Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davey, Moyra', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T21:28:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3007a9e3-b56b-5b45-8736-5d4a9376d27d', 'word_count': 818, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3007a9e3-b56b-5b45-8736-5d4a9376d27d'}, {'abstract': 'A compact retrospective of Nicholas Krushenick’s work, at the Gary Snyder Gallery, showcases the artist’s fusion of Pop, Surrealism and abstraction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/arts/design/nicholas-krushenick-a-survey.html', 'snippet': 'A compact retrospective of Nicholas Krushenick’s work, at the Gary Snyder Gallery, showcases the artist’s fusion of Pop, Surrealism and abstraction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gary Snyder Gallery', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nicholas Krushenick: ‘A Survey’', 'kicker': 'Art in Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nicholas Krushenick: ‘A Survey’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T21:29:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1c67e94-2520-535d-a063-cb0bd2e8afd3', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1c67e94-2520-535d-a063-cb0bd2e8afd3'}, {'abstract': 'For the first time in 30 years, the median monthly mortgage payment is about the same as the median rental payment.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/rent-vs-buy/', 'snippet': 'For the first time in 30 years, the median monthly mortgage payment is about the same as the median rental payment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For years my colleague David Leonhardt has been helping people calculate whether it makes more sense to rent or buy a home, based on the relative costs of each decision. This week, the economists at Capital Economics noticed an interesting phenomenon related to this tradeoff. For the first time in three decades, the median monthly mortgage payment is about the same as the median rental payment:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rent vs. Buy', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Renting and Leasing (Real Estate)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T21:30:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d04e11f-4bd4-551a-bdf7-6a8f33e83afd', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d04e11f-4bd4-551a-bdf7-6a8f33e83afd'}, {'abstract': 'Gabriel Orozco’s exhibition at Marian Goodman Gallery provides only a glimpse of the magic that is the basis of Mr. Orozco’s reputation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/arts/design/gabriel-orozco-corplegados-and-particles.html', 'snippet': 'Gabriel Orozco’s exhibition at Marian Goodman Gallery provides only a glimpse of the magic that is the basis of Mr. Orozco’s reputation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Marian Goodman Gallery', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gabriel Orozco: ‘Corplegados and Particles’', 'kicker': 'Art in Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gabriel Orozco: ‘Corplegados and Particles’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T21:30:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/065861a1-68dc-5fbc-9a77-a78a6ded275a', 'word_count': 313, 'uri': 'nyt://article/065861a1-68dc-5fbc-9a77-a78a6ded275a'}, {'abstract': 'If you’re at the game or on the couch and in need of real college football analysis or even some wise cracks — or have some of your own to share — Joe Drape and Zach Hillesland will be live-blogging all day on Saturday, with on-site feeds from Oklahoma State-Texas and Michigan-Michigan State. Join the party.', 'web_url': 'https://thequad.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/college-football-all-day-long/', 'snippet': 'If you’re at the game or on the couch and in need of real college football analysis or even some wise cracks — or have some of your own to share — Joe Drape and Zach Hillesland will be live-blogging all day on Saturday, with on-site feeds from Okl...', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’re at the game or on the couch and in need of real college football analysis or even some wisecracks — or have some of your own to share — Joe Drape and Zach Hillesland will be live-blogging all day on Saturday, with on-site feeds from Pete Thamel at Oklahoma State-Texas and Pete Bigelow at Michigan-Michigan State. Join the party and impress your friends.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'College Football All … Day … Long', 'kicker': 'The Quad', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T21:34:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34c22131-76a8-529d-8afc-807379cc86d3', 'word_count': 65, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34c22131-76a8-529d-8afc-807379cc86d3'}, {'abstract': 'John Lasseter, the chief creative officer of Pixar, is taking reader questions.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/ask-pixars-john-lasseter-your-questions/', 'snippet': 'John Lasseter, the chief creative officer of Pixar, is taking reader questions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pixar Animation Studios, the home of Woody and Buzz, not to mention James P. Sullivan and Nemo, is celebrating its 25th anniversary this year. And it’s been an impressive run — not one box-office dud among its 12 features (though some critics, including A. O. Scott of this paper, registered their disappointment with this year’s sequel to “Cars”). The company, however, is not one to rest on its laurels. There’s a full slate of Pixar films to come, including “Brave,” the first of its features with a female protagonist. And “Finding Nemo” and “Monsters Inc.” will return to theaters in 2012 and 2013 for limited 3-D runs. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ask Pixar’s John Lasseter Your Questions', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T21:39:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5aa70af1-a9fd-5181-85b7-525f89ef5831', 'word_count': 227, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5aa70af1-a9fd-5181-85b7-525f89ef5831'}, {'abstract': 'The 1960 New York School of Printing, now the High School of Graphic Communication Arts, was an architecturally ambitious design with an auditorium shaped like a guitar and a wall of glass block.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/realestate/streetscapes-hells-kitchen-the-slipcovered-school.html', 'snippet': 'The 1960 New York School of Printing, now the High School of Graphic Communication Arts, was an architecturally ambitious design with an auditorium shaped like a guitar and a wall of glass block.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE old New York School of Printing, on 49th Street through to 50th, west of Ninth Avenue, is a modernistic surprise in the tenement miscellany of Hell’s Kitchen. Opened in 1960, it was designed by Hugh Kelly and B. Sumner Gruzen in two parts, a fluid, guitar-box auditorium set off by a stern, rectangular sweep of glass blocks and steel swing-out windows. Concealed under a plastic screen years ago, the glass blocks were exposed for a while this year, only to be covered up again.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/realestate/16STREETS1/streets-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 