s the inaugural recipient of the Rudolph Nureyev Prize for New Dance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Along with Houston Ballet’s debut at the Joyce Theater on Tuesday evening was another first: the presentation of the inaugural recipient of the Rudolph Nureyev Prize for New Dance, Jorma Elo’s “ONE/end/ONE.” I wish Mr. Elo’s ballet had been one to remember; alas, the grant, worth $25,000, is off to a weak start. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/arts/14houston-span/14houston-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 331, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/14/arts/14houston-span/14houston-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 331}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/arts/14houston-span/14houston-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 578, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/arts/14houston-span/14houston-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/14/arts/14houston-span/14houston-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Acrobatics, Doll-Like Movements and Coy Gestures', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Acrobatics, Doll-Like Movements and Coy Gestures', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-13T22:05:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/afcb2e3b-9ced-5994-b719-3379a08be58a', 'word_count': 407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/afcb2e3b-9ced-5994-b719-3379a08be58a'}, {'abstract': 'Suzanne Farrell, one of the foremost interpreters of Balanchine’s work, is back in New York with her own ballet company.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/arts/dance/suzanne-farrell-ballet-at-joyce-theater.html', 'snippet': 'Suzanne Farrell, one of the foremost interpreters of Balanchine’s work, is back in New York with her own ballet company.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THERE was a time in the not-so-distant past when the idea of Suzanne Farrell anywhere but onstage seemed impossible. For more than two decades she was George Balanchine’s muse, willowy, glamorous and alluringly distant, the face of not only New York City Ballet but also of ballet in America. Then, after she retired in 1989, she seemed to disappear, rarely spotted around a city where she had at one time seemed omnipresent. She once told a journalist that, in between coaching gigs, she enjoyed doing nothing more than playing cards and reading mystery novels.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/arts/14FARRELL/14FARRELL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 805, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/arts/14FARRELL/14FARRELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/14/arts/14FARRELL/14FARRELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Muse Rekindles Tha