” means when an application freezes up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Getting a Response From Stalled Software', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T00:20:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4bf848f9-3179-5f40-972a-3366fbf51ed3', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4bf848f9-3179-5f40-972a-3366fbf51ed3'}, {'abstract': 'Owners of adventure bikes want machines that will go anywhere, even if there’s no actual chance they’ll go to the Andes or even the Dakar Rally.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/automobiles/tough-bikes-for-suburban-adventurers.html', 'snippet': 'Owners of adventure bikes want machines that will go anywhere, even if there’s no actual chance they’ll go to the Andes or even the Dakar Rally.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BY the laws of probability, you wouldn’t expect many Timbuktu-bound motorcyclists to begin their world tours from your local Starbucks parking lot. But that’s where you’re likely to see their circumnavigation-ready machines, just about any Sunday morning, in just about any blue-state suburb from Scituate, Mass., to Santa Monica, Calif. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/automobiles/14bikes/14bikes-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 723, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}