 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frank, Barney', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T01:53:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/57730ef5-c1d5-5d20-8f41-82860a3e0121', 'word_count': 819, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57730ef5-c1d5-5d20-8f41-82860a3e0121'}, {'abstract': 'The rebuilding of ground zero offers a lesson in how to marry monumental vision with pragmatic know-how.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/opinion/the-thing-itself.html', 'snippet': 'The rebuilding of ground zero offers a lesson in how to marry monumental vision with pragmatic know-how.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ground zero in Lower Manhattan is a mass grave. So when it came time to rebuild the World Trade Center, the whole enterprise was enshrouded with passion and symbolism. The developers wanted a project that would proudly assert the American spirit. They wanted to send a message that the terrorist damage would not last. They wanted it to commemorate the tragedy and celebrate the revival. Everything, therefore, had to be big: the country’s tallest building, the most expensive commuter rail station, the costliest memorial. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-articleLarge-v2.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-thumbLarge-v2.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Thing Itself', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Thing Itself', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': '1 World Trade Center (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National September 11 Memorial and Museum', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Port Authority of New York and New Jersey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Area Planning and Renewal', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ward, Christopher O', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T01:57:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/91bc4c2d-9f81-5e01-8f29-a04bf308e7a0', 'word_count': 816, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91bc4c2d-9f81-5e01-8f29-a04bf308e7a0'}, {'abstract': 'St. Louis finally found a closer in the former catcher Jason Motte, and a July trade brought more pitchers to improve the bullpen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/sports/baseball/cardinals-bullpen-takes-shape-just-in-time.html', 'snippet': 'St. Louis finally found a closer in the former catcher Jason Motte, and a July trade brought more pitchers to improve the bullpen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ST. LOUIS — The final out secured Wednesday night, St. Louis Cardinals reliever Jason Motte patted his fist into his glove softly and repeatedly, as if patiently waiting for a teammate to toss him a ball. No pointing skyward to celebrate his four-out save in Game 3 of the National League Championship Series. No fist-pumping. Nothing demonstrative. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/sports/bullpen/bullpen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 654, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After Year of Tinkering, Cardinals’ Bullpen Takes Shape, Just in Time', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Year of Tinkering, Cardinals&#8217; Bullpen Takes Shape, Just in Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trades (Sports)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'St Louis Cardinals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'la Russa, Tony', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Milwaukee Brewers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T01:57:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pat Borzi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pat', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borzi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5f42634-94d7-51f7-bc64-c0846ff616c6', 'word_count': 878, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5f42634-94d7-51f7-bc64-c0846ff616c6'}, {'abstract': 'A puzzle from Caleb Madison and his J.A.S.A. crossword puzzle class.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/13/point-2/', 'snippet': 'A puzzle from Caleb Madison and his J.A.S.A. crossword puzzle class.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRIDAY’S PUZZLE — The last time we saw Caleb Madison at the helm of the J.A.S.A. class, (Across Lite, Solution, blog) he was encouraging them to flaunt their “IT” factor. Now, with Mr. Madison off getting a good education at Yale, the constructor Ian Livengood has taken over teaching the class, and they continue to construct. Good for them, I say, because they continue to crank out some very nice puzzles. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday: Pilot’s Point?', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T02:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f941f450-3778-508b-b531-a023c08597f1', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f941f450-3778-508b-b531-a023c08597f1'}, {'abstract': 'If Medicaid’s equal access provision goes unenforced, providers will not participate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/opinion/killing-medicaid-the-california-way.html', 'snippet': 'If Medicaid’s equal access provision goes unenforced, providers will not participate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VIRTUALLY all of the debate over the health care legislation enacted last year has focused on the constitutionality of the individual mandate, the requirement that, by 2014, nearly all Americans either purchase health insurance or pay a fine if they fail to do so. The Supreme Court now seems likely to decide the fate of the mandate, perhaps as early as June.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Killing Medicaid the California Way', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Killing Medicaid the California Way', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Health and Human Services Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T02:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce C. Vladeck and Stephen I. Vladeck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Vladeck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': 'I.', 'lastname': 'Vladeck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f24ab4c7-bb0b-5867-934b-c6905bddca39', 'word_count': 900, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f24ab4c7-bb0b-5867-934b-c6905bddca39'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Ritchie, the inventor of the C programming language and co-developer of the Unix operating system, influenced all facets of technology.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/technology/dennis-ritchie-programming-trailblazer-dies-at-70.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Ritchie, the inventor of the C programming language and co-developer of the Unix operating system, influenced all facets of technology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dennis M. Ritchie, who helped shape the modern digital era by creating software tools that power things as diverse as search engines like Google and smartphones, was found dead on Wednesday at his home in Berkeley Heights, N.J. He was 70. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/business/RITCHIE-obit/RITCHIE-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 615, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/business/RITCHIE-obit/RITCHIE-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/14/business/RITCHIE-obit/RITCHIE-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dennis Ritchie, Trailblazer in Digital Era, Dies at 70', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dennis Ritchie, 70, Dies; Trailblazer in Digital Era', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unix (Operating System)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bell Laboratories', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T02:01:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Lohr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lohr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/7661baa3-81fd-5052-9764-f0e0809df428', 'word_count': 804, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7661baa3-81fd-5052-9764-f0e0809df428'}, {'abstract': '“The Mountaintop,” with Samuel L. Jackson as the Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King Jr., takes place the day before Dr. King was assassinated in the motel where he spent his last night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/theater/reviews/the-mountaintop-with-samuel-l-jackson-angela-bassett.html', 'snippet': '“The Mountaintop,” with Samuel L. Jackson as the Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King Jr., takes place the day before Dr. King was assassinated in the motel where he spent his last night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even before the first flash of lightning — and there will be plenty of that before evening’s end — an ominous electricity crackles through the opening moments of “The Mountaintop,” Katori Hall’s surprisingly thin new play about a monumental subject, which opened on Thursday at the Bernard B. Jacobs Theater. It’s not that what we’re looking at appears in itself to be fraught with significance. It’s only a generic, dirt-toned motel room, depressingly familiar in its seediness.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/arts/14MOUNTAIN_SPAN/14MOUNTAIN_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/14/arts/14MOUNTAIN_SPAN/14MOUNTAIN_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/arts/14MOUNTAIN_SPAN/14MOUNTAIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/arts/14MOUNTAIN_SPAN/14MOUNTAIN_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/14/arts/14MOUNTAIN_SPAN/14MOUNTAIN_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'April 3, 1968. Lorraine Motel. Evening.', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'The Mountaintop'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'April 3, 1968. Lorraine Motel. Evening.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Samuel L', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hall, Katori', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bassett, Angela', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leon, Kenny', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T02:01:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/59a00df1-2b68-5f8e-96ec-4c5661952449', 'word_count': 1336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59a00df1-2b68-5f8e-96ec-4c5661952449'}, {'abstract': 'New York City Transit has acted to improve performance, but maintenance problems still cause trouble for older or disabled riders.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/nyregion/subway-elevators-and-escalators-still-break-down-often.html', 'snippet': 'New York City Transit has acted to improve performance, but maintenance problems still cause trouble for older or disabled riders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the Delancey Street subway station in Manhattan, the escalator works so infrequently that Karen Lee Benson tries to avoid using that stop unless her husband is with her. Consider the alternative: a 60-stair hike to the street with her two sons under 3 and their strollers. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/nyregion/ELEVATOR/ELEVATOR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 718, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/14/nyregion/ELEVATOR/ELEVATOR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStanda