upport newly released prisoners are a good investment. The Senate should follow the House’s lead and pay for the Second Chance Act.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With state prison costs driven mainly by recidivism, the federal government must do all it can to support programs like the Second Chance Act, which guides newly released prisoners to drug treatment, mental health care, housing and jobs to keep them from going right back to jail. The House, which supports continued funding, must hold fast against a Senate appropriations bill that would zero out this important program. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Second Chances After Prison', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Second Chances After Prison', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wolf, Frank R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Second Chance Act', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Council of State Governments', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T02:06:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/9592561d-9e1f-548f-908a-af1f394e835c', 'word_count': 325, 'uri': 'nyt://a