an Cain has undergone this week over his economic plan. Also, campaign finance filings are due to the Federal Election Commission on Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oct. 14, 2011 - A look at the intense criticism Herman Cain has undergone this week over his economic plan. Also, campaign finance filings are due to the Federal Election Commission on Saturday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/13/us/politics/video-caucus-101411/video-caucus-101411-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/13/us/politics/video-caucus-101411/video-caucus-101411-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/13/us/politics/video-caucus-101411/video-caucus-101411-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/13/us/politics/video-caucus-101411/video-caucus-101411-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Caucus | Cain Under Fire', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:24:20+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'Ben Werschkul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werschkul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/c8dc4aa6-87c3-5462-839d-b0efa8422f12', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/c8dc4aa6-87c3-5462-839d-b0efa8422f12'}, {'abstract': 'Republican presidential hopeful Herman Cain’s new book “This Is Herman Cain! My Journey to the White House” hit the top 10 on Amazon just one day after official publication.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Republican presidential hopeful Herman Cain’s new book “This Is Herman Cain! My Journey to the White House” hit the top 10 on Amazon just one day after official publication.', 'lead_paragraph': ' CALL 999: Does the road to the White House run through Barnes & Noble? Herman Cain, the long-shot Republican presidential hopeful who took first place in last month’s Florida straw poll, certainly seems to think so. Since “This Is Herman Cain! My Journey to the White House” was released on Oct. 4, the former Godfather’s Pizza executive has been spending an awful lot of time in bookstores, signing copies and doing riffs on exclamation-point-happy policy prescriptions like “Natural resources are there for a reason. Use them!”(Anyone else want to hear those guys from the Miller Lite commercials stage a shout-off between Cain’s “Nine, nine, nine!” slogan — a reference to his flat-tax plan — and the Occupy Wall Street movement’s “We are the 99 percent”?) It took Cain about three months and a few professional collaborators to write the rather slender book, but less than one day after official publication to hit the top 10 on Amazon. According to his publisher, Threshold Editions, the book has already gone back for a sixth printing, with some 100,000 copies in circulation.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/23insidelist/23insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/23insidelist/23insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:27:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1a99637-d595-59ab-af22-4959811df547', 'word_count': 632, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1a99637-d595-59ab-af22-4959811df547'}, {'abstract': 'Jill Abramson shows how life with a puppy enriches middle age.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/books/review/the-puppy-diaries-by-jill-abramson-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Jill Abramson shows how life with a puppy enriches middle age.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let’s be real. It’s hard to keep a straight face writing about puppies. Animal behaviorists like Alexandra Horowitz may apply hard science to shed light on why your shiba inu isn’t interested in the television or your corgi mix likes to eat manure. The good poe\xadt — John Galsworthy, Rudyard Kipling — will wrest a sigh from the reader’s maudlin breast with paeans to loyal companions long departed. But most dog-crazy writers avoid spilling too much ink on the subject. We’re wary of fashioning sentences that are a prose equivalent of the idiotic high-pitched babble we direct at our animals all day long. (Full family disclosure: my husband routinely cradles our 85-pound Labrador in his arms and sings to her, as he did when she was a puppy, the “Baby Mine” song from “Dumbo.”) If we’re not sentimental, we’ll surely be unoriginal. It’s like writing about sex. The opportunity to humiliate oneself lurks around every paragraph.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16STYRON/16STYRON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 386, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16STYRON/16STYRON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 386}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16STYRON/16STYRON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16STYRON/16STYRON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16STYRON/16STYRON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Golden Retriever Who Came to Stay', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Come. Stay.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABRAMSON, JILL', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:29:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Styron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Styron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cca8f340-01a1-5ae4-80e5-8ff15984b17c', 'word_count': 1433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cca8f340-01a1-5ae4-80e5-8ff15984b17c'}, {'abstract': 'A French artist brings her intensely autobiographical work to an uptown hotel.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/the-suite-life-sophie-calle-at-the-lowell-hotel/', 'snippet': 'A French artist brings her intensely autobiographical work to an uptown hotel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before boarding a flight from Paris this week, Sophie Calle stuffed her suitcase with a selection of choice possessions that hold personal significance, including one red and one white wedding dress, a wig, a framed portrait of Freud, a sprawled stuffed cat and an issue of TeleStar with Peter Falk on the cover, circa 1986. Upon arrival in New York, the French artist, best known for her intensely autobiographical work, proceeded to unpack these items at the Lowell Hotel where she has taken over a suite for “Room,” an installation commissioned by French Institute Alliance Française as part of Crossing the Line, an annual festival of contemporary art. Calle’s inventory also includes a few specialty items she sourced locally, like a burnt mattress, a baby stroller and a Remington Noiseless Portable typewriter. The artist herself will also appear from time to time over the show’s run.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Suite Life | Sophie Calle at the Lowell Hotel', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HOTELS AND MOTELS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Calle, Sophie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lowell Hotel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:30:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Feldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Feldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad4ae0da-f973-5ece-bd5d-77605efd6bee', 'word_count': 306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad4ae0da-f973-5ece-bd5d-77605efd6bee'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Epstein, who taught at Northwestern and edited the American Scholar from 1975 to 1997, discusses the changing role of the public intellectual.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/books/review/up-front-joseph-epstein.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Epstein, who taught at Northwestern and edited the American Scholar from 1975 to 1997, discusses the changing role of the public intellectual.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joseph Epstein, who taught at Northwestern from 1974 to 2002 and edited The American Scholar from 1975 to 1997, reviews two books about the Medill family on the cover. In 1972, he wrote a much-discussed essay about William F. Buckley Jr. and his position as America’s most famous intellectual.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/UPFRONT/UPFRONT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 724, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/UPFRONT/UPFRONT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/UPFRONT/UPFRONT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front: Joseph Epstein', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_hea