'A look at the intense criticism Herman Cain has undergone this week over his economic plan. Also, campaign finance filings are due on Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/caucus-video-attacks-on-9-9-9-campaign-finance-preview/', 'snippet': 'A look at the intense criticism Herman Cain has undergone this week over his economic plan. Also, campaign finance filings are due on Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A look at the intense criticism Herman Cain has weathered this week over his economic plan. Also, campaign finance filings are due to the Federal Election Commission on Saturday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Caucus Video: Attacks on 9-9-9; Campaign Finance Preview', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cain, Herman', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Election Commission', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:32:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Werschkul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werschkul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23ad9900-9f3d-52d3-a11b-ccede86208bf', 'word_count': 31, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23ad9900-9f3d-52d3-a11b-ccede86208bf'}, {'abstract': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Mary Gabriel’s “Love and Capital: Karl and Jenny Marx and the Birth of a Revolution.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/books/review/loves-labors.html', 'snippet': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Mary Gabriel’s “Love and Capital: Karl and Jenny Marx and the Birth of a Revolution.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Love’s Labors', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Love’s Labors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:32:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/acf4e6d1-1fd0-5574-b5a2-4efbabe82ef5', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/acf4e6d1-1fd0-5574-b5a2-4efbabe82ef5'}, {'abstract': 'Three books explore the brain’s astonishing capability, as well as its flaws and limitations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/books/review/is-the-brain-good-at-what-it-does.html', 'snippet': 'Three books explore the brain’s astonishing capability, as well as its flaws and limitations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The human brain gets a lot of press these days, but not all the publicity has been good. Its reviews are reminiscent of Barack Obama’s during the 2008 presidential campaign, when one side said he was a socialist Muslim foreigner and the other thought he was a savior from on high. To its detractors, the brain is a kludge, a hacked-up device beset with bugs, biases and self-\xaddeceptions that undermine our decision making and well-being at every turn. To its admirers, it contains vast potential we can all unlock to improve our lives, thanks to “neural plasticity” that enables the adult nervous system to change in more dramatic ways than previously thought. Lately, a growing army of Chicken Littles retorts that this very plasticity has been hijacked by the Internet and other forms of technological crack that are rewiring our brains into a state of continual distraction and intellectual torpor.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16CHABRIS/16CHABRIS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 771, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16CHABRIS/16CHABRIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16CHABRIS/16CHABRIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is the Brain Good at What It Does?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Think Again', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davidson, Cathy N', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Linden, David J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buonomano, Dean', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:33:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher F. Chabris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Chabris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bffd00d-6d47-53a4-9ddb-407fc2ec6b00', 'word_count': 2026, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bffd00d-6d47-53a4-9ddb-407fc2ec6b00'}, {'abstract': 'The irresistible rise of the robot-book.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/books/review/do-androids-dream-of-electric-authors.html', 'snippet': 'The irresistible rise of the robot-book.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One day, I stumbled across a book on Amazon called “Saltine Cracker.” It didn’t make sense: who would pay $54 for a book entirely about perforated crackers? The book was co-edited by someone called Lambert M. Surhone — a name that sounds like one of Kurt Vonnegut’s inventions. According to Amazon, Lambert M. Surhone has written or edited more than 100,000 titles, on every subject from beekeeping to the world’s largest cedar bucket. He was churning out books at a rate that was simply not possible for a human being.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16Kennedy/16Kennedy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16Kennedy/16Kennedy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16Kennedy/16Kennedy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16Kennedy/16Kennedy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16Kennedy/16Kennedy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Do Androids Dream of Electric Authors?', 'kicker': 'The Mechanic Muse', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I, Robot-Book', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wikipedia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Artificial Intelligence', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robots and Robotics', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:34:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pagan Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pagan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54637d09-3b64-56e8-830c-506ccd5417c9', 'word_count': 1146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54637d09-3b64-56e8-830c-506ccd5417c9'}, {'abstract': 'Life after college poses challenges for the three friends in Jeffrey Eugenides’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/books/review/the-marriage-plot-by-jeffrey-eugenides-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Life after college poses challenges for the three friends in Jeffrey Eugenides’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was no predicting where Jeffrey Eugenides would go after his first two novels, so different were they in tone and form. “The Virgin Suicides” — humid, dreamlike, entranced — comes off as a single thought. “Middlesex,” a chatty multigenerational saga that winds its way from Turkey to Michigan to San Francisco to Berlin, sweeping together the burning of Smyrna, the rise and fall of Detroit, the immigrant experience, the Nation of Islam, the sins of Nixon and, of course, the lore and genetics of intersexuality, has as many moving parts as a Rube Goldberg machine. “The Virgin Suicides,” edged with antic wit and edging toward the surreal, glances in Nabokovian contempt at the petty preoccupations of “rangers and realists.” “Middlesex,” for all the novelty of its hermaphroditic protagonist, is straight-up realism, start to finish.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/books/review/NEWmarriageillo/NEWmarriageillo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/12/books/review/NEWmarriageillo/NEWmarriageillo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/12/books/review/NEWmarriageillo/NEWmarriageillo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/12/books/review/NEWmarriageillo/NEWmarriageillo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jeffrey Eugenides on Liberal Arts Graduates in Love', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Graduates', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eugenides, Jeffrey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brown University', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robinson, Marilynne', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:36:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Deresiewicz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Deresiewicz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/763b7508-fca3-5b1e-9ec7-5e58d16bcf9a', 'word_count': 1204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/763b7508-fca3-5b1e-9ec7-5e58d16bcf9a'}, {'abstract': 'Maile Meloy’s first novel for young readers combines historical fiction with fantasy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/books/review/the-apothecary-by-maile-meloy-illustrated-by-ian-schoenherr-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Maile Meloy’s first novel for young readers combines historical fiction with fantasy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maile Meloy, author of two novels and two story collections that have been praised for their meticulous realism, has written her first book for young readers, and defying expectations of admirers such as myself, it’s a fantasy. Though a longtime fan of Meloy, I couldn’t help approaching “The Apothecary,” a story of teenage spies and transformative elixirs, with trepidation.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/Goddu/Goddu-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 446, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/Goddu/Goddu-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 446}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/Goddu/Goddu-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 773, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/Goddu/Goddu-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/Goddu/Goddu-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Child of the Blacklist Becomes a Teenage Spy', 'kicker': 'Young Adult ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spy Kids', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Meloy, Maile', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Magic and Magicians', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:37:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Krystyna Poray Goddu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Krystyna', 'middlename': 'Poray', 'lastname': 'Goddu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/878569ca-49f5-5349-a303-b7b28fbbb8aa', 'word_count': 745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/878569ca-49f5-5349-a303-b7b28fbbb8aa'}, {'abstract': 'This monster forces a boy to confront his despair over his mother’s terminal illness.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/books/review/it-takes-a-monster-to-learn-how-to-grieve.html', 'snippet': 'This monster forces a boy to confront his despair over his mother’s terminal illness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I opened the envelope on my doorstep containing this book, I immediately had to sit down. “A Monster Calls” is about coming to terms with grief. And it is based on the last story idea from my first mentor: brassy, big-hearted Siobhan Dowd, then a human rights campaigner, who was kind — or crazy — enough to hire me as an intern in 1997 when I was 18 years old. She died four years ago from breast cancer at the age of 47.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/Bruder/Bruder-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 385, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/Bruder/Bruder-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 385}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/Bruder/Bruder-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 657, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/Bruder/Bruder-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/Bruder/Bruder-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'It Takes a Monster to Learn How to Grieve', 'kicker': 'Young Adult', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Facing His Fears', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grief (Emotion)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ness, Patrick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dowd, Siobhan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:37:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Bruder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c0a0bf0-63e6-5924-97bb-ffbc7bae6eb0', 'word_count': 746, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c0a0bf0-63e6-5924-97bb-ffbc7bae6eb0'}, {'abstract': 'An enigmatic art student in Prague becomes a pawn in a centuries-old war.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/books/review/daughter-of-smoke-and-bone-by-laini-taylor-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'An enigmatic art student in Prague becomes a pawn in a centuries-old war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Any book that opens with “Once upon a time” is inviting high expectations. It’s a phrase that inevitably evokes fairy tales and leather-bound classics about epic adventures, setting up the anticipation that readers will discover worlds filled with magic. And it may be a cliché, but it’s not necessarily an unwelcome one.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/Philpot/Philpot-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/Philpot/Philpot-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/Philpot/Philpot-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chimeras, Angels and a Girl in Prague', 'kicker': 'Young Adult', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unearthly Delights', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taylor, Laini', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:38:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsey Philpot', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Philpot', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb7cae34-9114-55a2-ade1-082c77cd38ab', 'word_count': 859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb7cae34-9114-55a2-ade1-082c77cd38ab'}, {'abstract': 'Pamela Paul reviews new children’s books about Halloween and spooky creatures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/10/15/books/review/bookshelf-boo.html', 'snippet': 'Pamela Paul reviews new children’s books about Halloween and spooky creatures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pamela Paul reviews new children’s books about Halloween and spooky creatures.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/15/books/review/bookshelf-boo-slide-03MJ/bookshelf-boo-slide-03MJ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 688, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/15/books/review/bookshelf-boo-slide-03MJ/bookshelf-boo-slide-03MJ-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 688}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/15/books/review/bookshelf-boo-slide-03MJ/bookshelf-boo-slide-03MJ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 893, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {