l', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/Bookshelf5/Bookshelf5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bookshelf: Night', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bookshelf: Night', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ray, Mary Lyn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Almond, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pinkney, Jerry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:38:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pamela Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pamela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d40be348-7886-5103-81c2-a1fcfac09f56', 'word_count': 555, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d40be348-7886-5103-81c2-a1fcfac09f56'}, {'abstract': 'Three Ceylonese schoolboys on a sea journey to England take delight in their eccentric companions at the ship’s worst dining table.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/books/review/the-cats-table-by-michael-ondaatje-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Three Ceylonese schoolboys on a sea journey to England take delight in their eccentric companions at the ship’s worst dining table.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once, on holiday, a friend and I descended a narrow iron ladder into a floodlit cavern and followed a guide into a small boat. As he rowed us through eerily motionless waters under a low, stalactite-fanged ceiling, the overhead lights went out, and only his flashlight pierced the darkness. In its beam we could see the play of the variegated colors across the crystal-crusted walls, and the slow-moving flickers of eyeless fish, our vision completely dependent on his single beam. Reading Michael Ondaatje’s mesmerizing new novel, “The Cat’s Table,” is like being guided, just as surely and just as magically, through the author’s lustrous visions. As he did in his great 1992 novel, “The English Patient,” which won the Man Booker Prize and became an Academy Award-winning film, Ondaatje conjures images that pull strangers into the vivid rooms of his imagination, their detail illumined by his words.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16schillinger/16schillinger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1018, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16schillinger/16schillinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16schillinger/16schillinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Michael Ondaatje’s Passage From Ceylon', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Empire’s Children I', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ondaatje, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:40:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liesl Schillinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liesl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schillinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2531acf-c5fc-510f-9eb8-a2a8e03766f5', 'word_count': 1265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2531acf-c5fc-510f-9eb8-a2a8e03766f5'}, {'abstract': 'Did George Keats’s move to America inspire his brother John’s greatest poetry?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/books/review/the-keats-brothers-by-denise-gigante-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Did George Keats’s move to America inspire his brother John’s greatest poetry?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Suppose you wanted to write a novel about John Keats, everyone’s favorite English Romantic poet, whose travels in “realms of gold” were purely imaginary, and who died in 1821, poor and spitting blood, at the obscenely young age of 25. Faced with such narrative restrictions, you might be tempted to invent a brother who was everything that Keats was not: practical, rich, footloose, married with a child, healthy enough to reach middle age. Why not send this imaginary brother across the Atlantic to the “rank-grown forests” (as Keats wrote in a later poem) of America, Kentucky, say, and invent encounters with luminous figures like John James Audubon and giant catfish and backwoods swindlers living on “bear sandwiches greased with bear fat”? A lively correspondence between the two brothers, spiced with verses, might serve to delineate the striking differences in their situations, and cast a reciprocal light on the poetry and the prairies.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/BENFEY2/BENFEY2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 857, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/BENFEY2/BENFEY2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/BENFEY2/BENFEY2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Keats Brother on the American Frontier', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ode to Siblings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gigante, Denise', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keats, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:41:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Benfey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benfey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d982ee3-c575-52fb-9ed0-0027f8fc74ab', 'word_count': 1120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d982ee3-c575-52fb-9ed0-0027f8fc74ab'}, {'abstract': 'Jeremi Suri argues that nation-building is central to American history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/books/review/libertys-surest-guardian-by-jeremi-suri-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Jeremi Suri argues that nation-building is central to American history.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Nation-building” is in bad odor these days, among the foreign policy cognoscenti and the general public alike. Weariness with the long struggles in Afghanistan and Iraq has produced a grand opposition alliance of isolationists, realists, populists and left-liberal anti-interventionists, who all agree it is past time to give up the hopeless dream. As Peggy Noonan recently put it, “We should not occupy their lands, run their governments or try to bribe them into bonhomie.” American soldiers should not be “social workers.” When she visited Afghanistan earlier this year, Noonan asked an American general how the war was going and was appalled to hear his answer: “Great. We just opened a new hospital.” Noonan lamented that the American soldier was no longer allowed to be “a warrior in a warrior army.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16KAGAN/16KAGAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16KAGAN/16KAGAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16KAGAN/16KAGAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 772, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16KAGAN/16KAGAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/16KAGAN/16KAGAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nation-Building, Our National Pastime', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Refurbishing Republics', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Suri, Jeremi', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:42:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Kagan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kagan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f185ea9-86e3-56a3-aafa-15225cce8b85', 'word_count': 1204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f185ea9-86e3-56a3-aafa-15225cce8b85'}, {'abstract': 'In this novel, an assimilated Austrian Jew chooses a vicious, and symbolic, husband.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/books/review/until-the-dawns-light-by-aharon-appelfeld-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In this novel, an assimilated Austrian Jew chooses a vicious, and symbolic, husband.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Philip Roth, writing in these pages two decades ago, called the virtuosic Israeli writer Aharon Appelfeld “a displaced writer of displaced fiction, who has made of displacement and disorientation a subject uniquely his own.” Indeed, “Until the Dawn’s Light,” Appelfeld’s masterly and finely wrought 16th novel, begins with a displacement. The year is 1912; a young mother, Blanca Hammer, is fleeing by train with her 4-year-old son, Otto, from some unnamed menace, toward an unknown destination. “It all transpired quickly and with frightening precision,” Appelfeld writes, “as though they were no longer their own masters but in the hands of the railways, which treated them mercifully and moved them from place to place, almost without pain.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/ORRINGER/ORRINGER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/ORRINGER/ORRINGER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 429}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/ORRINGER/ORRINGER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 763, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/ORRINGER/ORRINGER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/ORRINGER/ORRINGER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Marriage of Semite and Anti-Semite', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Married to the Enemy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Appelfeld, Aharon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTI-SEMITISM', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:42:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Orringer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Orringer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9881fed-9a3d-5276-9e50-891bd0a5c0a1', 'word_count': 1352, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9881fed-9a3d-5276-9e50-891bd0a5c0a1'}, {'abstract': 'New mystery novels from James Lee Burke, S. J. Rozan, Ruth Rendell and John Connolly.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/books/review/crime-mystery-novels-by-james-lee-burke-s-j-rozan-ruth-rendell-and-john-connolly-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'New mystery novels from James Lee Burke, S. J. Rozan, Ruth Rendell and John Connolly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'James Lee Burke presses onward with his singular mission to rewrite the American western in FEAST DAY OF FOOLS (Simon & Schuster, $26.99). The southwest Texas landscape is familiar genre territory, and the stand-up protagonist, Sheriff Hackberry Holland, is the latest in a long line of principled lawmen. But the good guys don’t ride horses anymore, the bad guys have traded their six-guns for automatic weapons and a country club sprawls across part of the old Outlaw Trail.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/CRIME/CRIME-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 726, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/CRIME/CRIME-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/books/review/CRIME/CRIME-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Blood in the Sand', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Blood in the Sand', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burke, James Lee', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rendell, Ruth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Connolly, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rozan, S J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:43:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e800f74-4ebf-55ca-96cb-5e5a9b82f80b', 'word_count': 916, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e800f74-4ebf-55ca-96cb-5e5a9b82f80b'}, {'abstract': 'Three hedge funds filed an involuntary petition to put the debtor, which happens to be incorporated in the Cayman Islands, into bankruptcy.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/the-curious-c-d-o-bankruptcy/', 'snippet': 'Three hedge funds filed an involuntary petition to put the debtor, which happens to be incorporated in the Cayman Islands, into bankruptcy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Something odd is going on in New Jersey.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Curious C.D.O. Bankruptcy', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collateralized Debt Obligations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:43:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen J. Lubben', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Lubben', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56ba6043-bc13-5b21-9d89-5824d505b3f2', 'word_count': 545, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56ba6043-bc13-5b21-9d89-5824d505b3f2'}, {'abstract': 'A study of dark matter ends in marriage for two physicists.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/fashion/weddings/ruth-toner-and-simon-fiorucci-vows.html', 'snippet': 'A study of dark matter ends in marriage for two physicists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IF love is supposed to be ephemeral, elusive and maybe also undiscovered by its experimenters, then the wedding of Ruth Bushnell Toner and Simon David Fiorucci may have hit love at its source. After all, the couple met while looking for dark matter, one of the most ephemeral and elusive (and in fact, undiscovered) phenomena in physics.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/fashion/weddings/VOWS-1/VOWS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/16/fashion/weddings/VOWS-1/VOWS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/fashion/weddings/VOWS-1/VOWS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 747, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/fashion/weddings/VOWS-1/VOWS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/fashion/weddings/VOWS-1/VOWS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ruth Toner and Simon Fiorucci', 'kicker': 'Vows', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ruth Toner and Simon Fiorucci', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vows (Times Column)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:43:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Strauss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strauss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/627b1576-d779-54f2-9dbc-6265bad3230f', 'word_count': 1126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/627b1576-d779-54f2-9dbc-6265bad3230f'}, {'abstract': 'Craig Thompson’s dark graphic novel draws on “The Thousand and One Nights,” Orientalist paintings and R. Crumb.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/books/review/habibi-written-and-illustrated-by-craig-thompson-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Craig Thompson’s dark graphic novel draws on “The Thousand and One Nights,” Orientalist paintings and R. Crumb.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Craig Thompson’s “Habibi” is a work of fantasy about being ashamed of one’s fantasies. This anxiety is native to comics, or at least to the broad strain of American comics written by white males. The work of R. Crumb, for example, is a gallery of self-loathing — portraits that register an artist’s recoil from his own noxious imaginings. Thompson’s work is much prettier than anything drawn by Crumb, but the anxieties are similar. If “Habibi” had been content to mine this narrow but rich vein of adolescent angst it might have been a comic’s comic, or at least an instructive book for young adults. Instead, it aspires to address questions of spirituality, environmental degradation and religious pluralism. The result is frankly a mess, though it does include some arresting pictures.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/13/books/review/creswell/creswell-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 690, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/13/books/review/creswell/creswell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/13/books/review/creswell/creswell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Graphic Novel as Orientalist Mash-Up', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Alas, Babylon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:43:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robyn Creswell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Creswell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ee48c4a-7234-518a-8ee3-db9e4e0fa2ea', 'word_count': 1026, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ee48c4a-7234-518a-8ee3-db9e4e0fa2ea'}, {'abstract': 'We are teaming with FantasyPros.com to analyze the toughest fantasy matchups for Week 6. Vote on who you think is the right pick.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/fantasy-football-week-6s-toughest-picks/', 'snippet': 'We are teaming with FantasyPros.com to analyze the toughest fantasy matchups for Week 6. Vote on who you think is the right pick.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jason and Justin Sablich are here to help you with your fantasy football team. The Sablich brothers will provide fantasy football advice throughout the season on this blog and on Twitter (@5thDownFantasy). ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fantasy Football: Week 6’s Toughest Picks', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fantasy Sports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Donald', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fitzpatrick, Ryan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Andre', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, Donald', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Alex', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Torain, Ryan', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:45:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Sablich and Jason Sablich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sablich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sablich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8655b093-86ee-523b-9172-94754c75f659', 'word_count': 668, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8655b093-86ee-523b-9172-94754c75f659'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE BETTER ANGELS OF OUR NATURE: Why Violence Has Declined,  by Steven Pinker (Viking, $40.) Are humans essentially good or bad? Has the past century seen moral progress or a moral collapse? Pinker addresses these questions and more.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice: Recent Books Of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T17:47:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed06fb89-a00f-5604-942d-47a73653953d', 'word_count': 278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed06fb89-a00f-5604-942d-47a73653953d'}, {'abstract': 'The most important document for determining financial aid for college is a form that recognizes only heterosexual marriage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/15/your-money/for-children-of-same-sex-couples-a-student-aid-maze.html', 'snippet': 'The most important document for determining financial aid for college is a form that recognizes only heterosexual marriage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It took five attempts for one prospective college student and her mother to fill out the 106-question federal form that would determine whether she would be eligible for financial aid. And that was not just because the form was frustratingly complicated. What tripped them up was the fact that the student had two legal mothers — and the form had room for only one. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/15/business/15aid-sfspan/15aid-sfspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/15/business/15aid-sfspan/15aid-sfspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit':