ection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Keh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8b524f6-3ec3-5765-9198-2de79180bd2b', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8b524f6-3ec3-5765-9198-2de79180bd2b'}, {'abstract': 'Leading writers from China and India will meet for panel discussions in New York early next month as part of a new initiative called “The ‘Chindia’ Dialogues.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/15/arts/china-india-program-at-asia-society.html', 'snippet': 'Leading writers from China and India will meet for panel discussions in New York early next month as part of a new initiative called “The ‘Chindia’ Dialogues.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Leading writers from China and India will meet for panel discussions in New York early next month as part of a new initiative called “The ‘Chindia’ Dialogues.” The event, to run Nov. 3 through 6 at Asia Society, will focus on cultural and social trends in both countries as well as their interaction with each other. The Chinese-born writers who will take part include Ha Jin, Su Tong and Yu Hua; on the Indian side, Amitav Ghosh, Amit Chaudhuri and Siddhartha Deb are among those scheduled to speak. In addition, the American scholars Jonathan Spence and Orville Schell will participate, and separate musical performances by musicians from each country are also scheduled.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China-India Program at Asia Society', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China-India Program At Asia Society', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Asia Society', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T23:23:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d944b3cb-bf70-5a21-9762-91949b70f636', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d944b3cb-bf70-5a21-9762-91949b70f636'}, {'abstract': 'Teenagers, according to recent brain studies, are quite possibly the most fully, crucially adaptive human beings around. Whatever.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/14/teen-spirit-smells-like-victory/', 'snippet': 'Teenagers, according to recent brain studies, are quite possibly the most fully, crucially adaptive human beings around. Whatever.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you have — or have ever had — a teenager in your home or, really, if you have ever known a teenager, then chances are there’s been an occasion when you wondered, What was he thinking? (Or if he was thinking.) That’s why you should check out National Geographic’s October cover article by David Dobbs, “The New Science of the Teenage Brain.” It’s full of fascinating things. Even Shakespeare is enlisted to sum up the problem of teenagedom, through a character who says, of 10- to 23-year-olds, “There is nothing in the between but getting wenches with child, wronging the ancientry, stealing, fighting.” The bard neglects drinking, drug doing, driving while drinking or drug doing, cutting classes, indulging all sorts of cyber shenanigans, playing too much of that damn “Halo” video game — wait, I sound like the ancientry, don’t I? — but you get the idea. Teenagers are crazy.*', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Teen Spirit Smells Like . . . Victory!', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-14T23:24:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dean Robinson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robinson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/531622d5-de1a-5111-8bf8-81dee62448d9', 'word_count': 861, 'uri': 'nyt://article/531622d5-de1a-5111-8bf8-81dee62448d9'}, {'abstract': 'A new study suggests that our perception of race is altered by the clothing people wear.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/16/fashion/when-color-is-reflected-in-a-janitors-outfit-studied.html', 'snippet': 'A new study suggests that our perception of race is altered by the clothing people wear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE GIST Our perception of race is altered by the clothing people wear.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/fashion/16STUDIED/16STUDIED-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1002, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/16/fashion/16STUDIED/16STUDIED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/16/fashion/16STUDIED/16STUDIED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Race Reflected by Your Outfit?', 'kicker': 'Studied', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Is Race Reflected By Your Outfit?'