tract': 'Among Ms. Mengers’s clients over the years were Barbra Streisand, Steve McQueen and Michael Caine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/17/movies/sue-mengers-hollywood-agent-dies-at-79.html', 'snippet': 'Among Ms. Mengers’s clients over the years were Barbra Streisand, Steve McQueen and Michael Caine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Sue Mengers, a powerful agent who represented stars like Barbra Streisand and Steve McQueen and helped shape Hollywood’s vibrant revival in the 1970s before suddenly retiring to become an interested observer and party hostess to the changing film industry, died on Saturday at her home in Beverly Hills, Calif. She was 79.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/17/arts/mengers-obit/mengers-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/17/arts/mengers-obit/mengers-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/17/arts/mengers-obit/mengers-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/17/arts/mengers-obit/mengers-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/17/arts/mengers-obit/mengers-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sue Mengers, Hollywood Agent, Dies at 79', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sue Mengers, Hollywood Agent, Dies at 79', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hollywood (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGENTS (BUSINESS)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-16T22:53:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/be912902-8924-565c-9a1d-73c5a9a0855f', 'word_count': 741, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be912902-8924-565c-9a1d-73c5a9a0855f'}, {'abstract': 'Starting Tuesday, the company will hold its annual conference for software developers, to persuade them to build apps for new BlackBerrys and PlayBook tablets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/17/technology/research-in-motion-pins-hopes-on-its-next-os.html', 'snippet': 'Starting Tuesday, the company will hold its annual conference for software developers, to persuade them to build apps for new BlackBerrys and PlayBook tablets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OTTAWA — After months of misery underlined by the declining market share of its BlackBerry phone in North America, a profoundly unsuccessful move into tablet computers and, just last week, a prolonged failure of its service for millions of users worldwide, Research in Motion has a new opportunity to convince the world that it remains relevant.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/17/business/RIMM/RIMM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 790, 'width': 777, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Battered, RIM Pins Hopes on Next Wave of Devices', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Battered, RIM Pins Hopes on Next Wave of Devices', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'BlackBerry (Handheld Device)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tablet Computers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Research In Motion Ltd', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-16T22:53:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Austen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45b9d774-f6bf-5c9f-9f01-2d6b264576fd', 'word_count': 1050, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45b9d774-f6bf-5c9f-9f01-2d6b264576fd'}, {'abstract': 'A reader responds to an editorial on the growing problem of online courses being vulnerable to student aid fraud.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/17/opinion/academic-fraud-online.html', 'snippet': 'A reader responds to an editorial on the growing problem of online courses being vulnerable to student aid fraud.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Academic Fraud Online', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Academic Fraud Online', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Learning', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-16T23:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/a26f644b-90ae-5914-af2c-60a2ff490518', 'word_count': 142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a26f644b-90ae-5914-af2c-60a2ff490518'}, {'abstract': 'An ancient volume of Archimedes, at one point used as a prayer book, is restored and on view at the Walters Art Museum.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/17/arts/secrets-of-archimedes-at-walters-in-baltimore-review.html', 'snippet': 'An ancient volume of Archimedes, at one point used as a prayer book, is restored and on view at the Walters Art Museum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BALTIMORE — “The Archimedes Palimpsest” could well be the title of a Robert Ludlum thriller, though its plot’s esoteric arcana might also be useful for Dan Brown in his next variation on “The Da Vinci Code.” It features a third-century B.C. Greek mathematician (Archimedes) known for his playful brilliance; his lost writings, discovered more than a hundred years ago in an Istanbul convent; and various episodes involving plunder, pilferage and puzzling forgeries. The saga includes a monastery in the Judaean desert, a Jewish book dealer trying to flee Paris as the Nazis closed in, a French freedom fighter and an anonymous billionaire collector.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/17/arts/17SECRETS/17SECRETS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/17/arts/17SECRETS/17SECRETS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'