N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALITO, SAMUEL A JR', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-17T15:16:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/904ade93-b5cd-54dc-ba0e-17728734eb94', 'word_count': 927, 'uri': 'nyt://article/904ade93-b5cd-54dc-ba0e-17728734eb94'}, {'abstract': 'The Kenyan military sent hundreds of soldiers into Somalia over the weekend, and the Shabab militant group vowed to attack Kenya in retaliation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/world/africa/somali-militants-vow-to-attack-kenya.html', 'snippet': 'The Kenyan military sent hundreds of soldiers into Somalia over the weekend, and the Shabab militant group vowed to attack Kenya in retaliation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NAIROBI, Kenya — A big battle may be shaping up in southern Somalia between the Kenyan military, which took the unusual step of sending hundreds of soldiers into Somalia over the weekend, and the Shabab militant group, a ruthless franchise of Al Qaeda that vowed Monday to attack Kenya in retaliation.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Somali Militants Threaten Kenya Over Cross-Border Troops', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Somali Militants Threaten Kenya Over Cross-Border Troops', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Somalia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kenya', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Shabab', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-17T15:22:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeffrey Gettleman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gettleman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58a8420b-e545-51f6-aa60-33ef3a1401df', 'word_count': 760, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58a8420b-e545-51f6-aa60-33ef3a1401df'}, {'abstract': 'Sportswriters examined 49ers Coach Jim Harbaugh’s overzealous postgame handshake, and Lions Coach Jim Schwartz’s reaction to it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/sports/much-ado-about-handshake-after-49ers-lions-game-leading-off.html', 'snippet': 'Sportswriters examined 49ers Coach Jim Harbaugh’s overzealous postgame handshake, and Lions Coach Jim Schwartz’s reaction to it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s talk will, of course, be all about the handshake. When 49ers Coach Jim Harbaugh grabbed Lions Coach Jim Schwartz’s hand and gave him an overzealous jerk, prompting Schwartz to chase him down maniacally as they left the field Sunday, it became the perfect sports story for this era. It’s easy to argue about, was captured fully on video that can be replayed endlessly and is entirely silly. It had no impact on how the game turned out, much less anything deeper or more meaningful. Which means it will be debated until the N.F.L. can provide you another distraction. (Tim Tebow is starting next week!)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/18/sports/football/18leading-pic2/18leading-pic2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/18/sports/football/18leading-pic2/18leading-pic2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, '