ature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/d1e2247b-d7b8-522f-a680-9280455b8d26', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/d1e2247b-d7b8-522f-a680-9280455b8d26'}, {'abstract': 'Investigators’ reports offer a look into 14 episodes involving New York City educators.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/nyregion/how-cheating-cases-at-new-york-schools-played-out.html', 'snippet': 'Investigators’ reports offer a look into 14 episodes involving New York City educators.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A charter school teacher warned her third graders that a standardized test question was “tricky,” and they all changed their answers. A high school coach in Brooklyn called a student into the hallway and slipped her a completed answer sheet in a newspaper. In the Bronx, a principal convened Finish Your Lab Days, where biology students ended up copying answers for work they never did.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Cheating Cases, Teachers Who Took Risks or Flouted Rules', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Cheating Cases, Teachers Who Took Risks or Flouted Rules', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheating', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Condon, Richard J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suspensions, Dismissals and Resignations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-17T17:41:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sharon Otterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sharon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Otterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6026eb47-ab32-58ce-b160-50e132bd3729', 'word_count': 1490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6026eb47-ab32-58ce-b160-50e132bd3729'}, {'abstract': 'The Guggenheim Museum has entered into a licensing agreement with  a Vermont company that produces high-end commercial paints to create 200 interior and exterior wall colors drawn from well-known paintings in the museum’s collection and from wall colors that curators, artists and designers have used for exhibitions there.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/17/house-paints-from-the-guggenheim/', 'snippet': 'The Guggenheim Museum has entered into a licensing agreement with  a Vermont company that produces high-end commercial paints to create 200 interior and exterior wall colors drawn from well-known paintings in the museum’s collection and from wall ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'House paint has been a part of the modernist fine-art supply cabinet for more than a century, from Picasso to Pollock to Bridget Riley. Now, the Guggenheim Museum is hoping to generate a little revenue by turning the phenomenon back in the other direction. The museum has entered into a licensing agreement with Fine Paints of Europe, a Vermont company that produces high-end commercial paints,to create a set of 200 interior and exterior wall colors drawn from well-known paintings in the Guggenheim’s collection and from wall colors that curators, artists and designers (including Frank Lloyd Wright himself) have used for exhibitions over several decades.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'House Paints From the Guggenheim', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Guggenheim, Solomon R, Museum', 'ran