s cut offers a good chance to try grass-fed meat. It is ordinarily tougher, but the filet is steak at its most tender. And while the filet is often bland, grass-fed meat can have a stronger flavor. Here, it is seared, then roasted, if you can call it that: the beef cooks so fast you might just walk it past a hot oven.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19WINEPAIRING_SPAN/WinePairing-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19WINEPAIRING_SPAN/WinePairing-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19WINEPAIRING_SPAN/WinePairing-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19WINEPAIRING_SPAN/WinePairing-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19WINEPAIRING_SPAN/WinePairing-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pairings: Filet of Beef With Swiss Chard', 'kicker': 'Recipe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pairings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beef', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T00:29:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eefb36da-47fb-5d75-894b-e567c2d6e9a3', 'word_count': 458, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eefb36da-47fb-5d75-894b-e567c2d6e9a3'}, {'abstract': 'The panel tasted 20 St.-Joseph reds from recent vintages — wines whose aromas and flavors conjure up fall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/dining/reviews/st-josephs-from-recent-vintages-wine-review.html', 'snippet': 'The panel tasted 20 St.-Joseph reds from recent vintages — wines whose aromas and flavors conjure up fall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE weather has been so bizarre this year that I almost hesitate to associate wine with the seasons. But autumn seems to have finally begun in earnest, so I feel compelled — and relieved, actually — to dip into the St.-Joseph.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19WINE/Wine-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19WINE/19WINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19WINE/19WINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding Autumn in St.-Joseph', 'kicker': 'Wines of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Finding Autumn In Rhone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 1, 'm