ject', 'value': 'Electronic Books and Readers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barnes & Noble Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T10:57:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e8b10f8-3d87-55d4-b4b9-8413a231d1e0', 'word_count': 239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e8b10f8-3d87-55d4-b4b9-8413a231d1e0'}, {'abstract': 'Why did the administration back away from the first government-run insurance program for long-term care? “We can make it work,” said the actuary formerly in charge of developing the plan.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/behind-the-class-act-a-numbers-game/', 'snippet': 'Why did the administration back away from the first government-run insurance program for long-term care? “We can make it work,” said the actuary formerly in charge of developing the plan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let the post-mortem on the Class Act begin.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Behind the Class Act, a Numbers Game', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Long-Term Care Insurance', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T11:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90eabd1f-2754-5b31-952b-c7b6b032677e', 'word_count': 732, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90eabd1f-2754-5b31-952b-c7b6b032677e'}, {'abstract': 'The catastrophic loss wasn’t insured, but rather than taking legal action the owner rethought his data protection strategy.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/even-at-the-laugh-factory-losing-data-is-no-laughing-matter/', 'snippet': 'The catastrophic loss wasn’t insured, but rather than taking legal action the owner rethought his data protection strategy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jamie Masada will never forget the phone call last year that alerted him to the erasing of a chunk of his business and of his life. The call came from a manager at the online backup-storage company to which Mr. Masada had entrusted what might well have been the world’s most valuable collection of stand-up comedy video — 1,500 hours of acclaimed comics performing on the stage of Mr. Masada’s groundbreaking Hollywood comedy club, The Laugh Factory. Some of the video was historic, including Rodney Dangerfield’s earliest performances and stars like Dave Chapelle and Dane Cook honing their acts before hitting it big. And much of it existed only on the servers of the data storage company.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Even at the Laugh Factory, Losing Data Is No Laughing Matter', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data Storage', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Laugh Factory', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T11:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By David H. Freedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'H.', 'lastname': 'Freedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e65d697-07e9-5083-9825-528ffd55bd98', 'word_count': 999, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e65d697-07e9-5083-9825-528ffd55bd98'}, {'abstract': 'The amount of eastern Atlantic bluefin tuna traded on the global market in 2010 exceeded the official quota by 141 percent, according to an analysis commissioned by the Pew Environment Group.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/industry-flouts-bluefin-catch-limits-study-says/', 'snippet': 'The amount of eastern Atlantic bluefin tuna traded on the global market in 2010 exceeded the official quota by 141 percent, according to an analysis commissioned by the Pew Environment Group.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new study commissioned by the Pew Environment Group suggests that the fishing industry makes a mockery of efforts to regulate the catch of the endangered bluefin tuna in the Mediterranean.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Industry Flouts Bluefin Catch Limits, Study Says', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fishing, Commercial', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tuna', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Commission for the Conservation of Atlantic Tunas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mediterranean Sea', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N