h weather forces workers to stop pumping oil from a damaged container ship that seems to be on the verge of cracking apart completely and sinking off the coast of New Zealand. The government calls it the largest maritime spill in the country’s history. [nzherald.co.nz]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: A Listing, Leaking Ship', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T15:26:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1212eacb-908d-5301-83e0-cf66cd07ca2d', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1212eacb-908d-5301-83e0-cf66cd07ca2d'}, {'abstract': 'The year 1941 was singular for Alexander Calder. Now a show at the Pace Gallery brings together his sculpture of that time, which signified a turning point in his career.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/arts/design/alexander-calder-in-1941-at-the-pace-gallery.html', 'snippet': 'The year 1941 was singular for Alexander Calder. Now a show at the Pace Gallery brings together his sculpture of that time, which signified a turning point in his career.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some of the best-known photographs of Alexander Calder, taken in 1941 by his friend Herbert Matter, show Calder in the high-windowed studio he fashioned next to a cow pasture in Roxbury, Conn., surrounded by such an explosion of sculpture and sheet-metal scraps that it looks as if a small airplane had just crashed into the site.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/calder/calder-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024,