ion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dropbox, the online storage company, confirmed on Tuesday that it had raised $250 million in its latest fund-raising round, an investment that values the company at $4 billion, according to people close to the company.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dropbox Confirms $250 Million Investment', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data Storage', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venture Capital', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dropbox Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T15:46:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a622bbe1-9aa5-5c59-bfc7-848abd2b2e44', 'word_count': 195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a622bbe1-9aa5-5c59-bfc7-848abd2b2e44'}, {'abstract': 'Lens: Child Prisons', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/10/18/blogs/20111018-lens-prisons.html', 'snippet': 'Lens: Child Prisons', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lens: Child Prisons', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/18/blogs/20111018-lens-prisons-slide-XGXT/20111018-lens-prisons-slide-XGXT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lens: Child Prisons', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T15:55:51+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Blogs', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/4f0dc1cf-c036-5a8b-a373-6eb509bc025e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/4f0dc1cf-c036-5a8b-a373-6eb509bc025e'}, {'abstract': 'The Jets beat the Dolphins decisively, but the feeling persists that they did not play that well.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/much-needed-win-for-jets-but-what-does-it-mean/', 'snippet': 'The Jets beat the Dolphins decisively, but the feeling persists that they did not play that well.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The prevailing emotion in the Jets’ locker room late Monday night after they beat the hapless Miami Dolphins, 24-6, was that of relief. Never has being an average team with a 3-3 record felt so good.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Much-Needed Win for Jets, but What Does It Mean?', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T15:57:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shipgel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shipgel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a5915c9-2197-5aeb-acdc-7bda877d3838', 'word_count': 201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a5915c9-2197-5aeb-acdc-7bda877d3838'}, {'abstract': 'The Provençal version of pesto garnishes this simple, hearty soup.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/health/nutrition/21recipehealth.html', 'snippet': 'The Provençal version of pesto garnishes this simple, hearty soup.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is a big, simple soup made with winter vegetables – all diced small and thrown into a big pot with water and simmered for an hour. It’s garnished with the Provençal version of pesto, which does not contain any pine nuts. It makes a hearty meal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/18/science/21recipehealth/21recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/18/science/21recipehealth/21recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/18/science/21recipehealth/21recipehealth-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/18/science/21recipehealth/21recipehealth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1333, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/18/science/21recipehealth/21recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/18/science/21recipehealth/21recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/18/science/21recipehealth/21recipehealth-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Winter Vegetable Soupe au Pistou', 'kicker': 'Recipes for Health', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soups', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Herbs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T15:57:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Fitness & Nutrition', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Rose Shulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Shulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'recipe', '_id': 'nyt://article/e91a401c-0338-5f6f-8ffa-3e4dc1bc8cc1', 'word_count': 375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e91a401c-0338-5f6f-8ffa-3e4dc1bc8cc1'}, {'abstract': 'A Bruce Springsteen archive has outgrown the Asbury Park Public Library.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/a-boss-collection-for-a-new-jersey-university/', 'snippet': 'A Bruce Springsteen archive has outgrown the Asbury Park Public Library.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A collection of documents, articles and books about Bruce Springsteen’s career that has been growing for years at the Asbury Park Public Library will be moved to nearby Monmouth University in West Long Branch, N.J., the university said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Boss Collection for a New Jersey University', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Springsteen, Bruce', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Monmouth University', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Asbury Park (NJ)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T16:01:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ddcfbba6-d3b2-55b9-923f-ac385baaf0d4', 'word_count': 259, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ddcfbba6-d3b2-55b9-923f-ac385baaf0d4'}, {'abstract': 'There are more than 110 million instances of impaired driving each year in the United States, and 11,000 deaths, despite 30 years of drunk driving advocacy.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/friends-still-let-friends-drive-drunk/', 'snippet': 'There are more than 110 million instances of impaired driving each year in the United States, and 11,000 deaths, despite 30 years of drunk driving advocacy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sept. 15, Matthew Grape, 21, got into the passenger seat of a car with one of his Duke University fraternity brothers. The driver hit a tree, escaping with minor injuries, but killing his dear friend. According to local press reports, the driver was charged with impaired driving.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friends Still Let Friends Drive Drunk', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alcohol Abuse', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drunken and Reckless Driving', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T16:02:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Barron H. Lerner, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Barron', 'middlename': 'H.', 'lastname': 'Lerner', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/deca0310-9ac2-5a58-b861-4d0ac2fb8c1b', 'word_count': 757, 'uri': 'nyt://article/deca0310-9ac2-5a58-b861-4d0ac2fb8c1b'}, {'abstract': 'A Hawaiian novelist finds her book contract canceled after she self-publishes a book with Amazon.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/publishing-perils-in-the-digital-age/', 'snippet': 'A Hawaiian novelist finds her book contract canceled after she self-publishes a book with Amazon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'These are uncertain times for many established writers, as the case of the Hawaiian novelist Kiana Davenport shows. Until recently she had a deal with Riverhead Books, a literary line that is a division of Penguin, for a Civil War novel, “The Chinese Soldier’s Daughter.” Ms. Davenport received an initial $20,000 advance for the tale, which was scheduled for publication next summer. That wasn’t enough money to survive on, so the writer cast about for another source of income, and self-published a book of old stories on Amazon. The result: Her novel has been canceled, the publisher wants its money back, and her publishing career is in jeopardy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Publishing Perils in the Digital Age', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Penguin Group', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T16:16:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Streitfeld', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Streitfeld', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a6e13ae-5e1b-5b82-82cd-099b7ccfc674', 'word_count': 569, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a6e13ae-5e1b-5b82-82cd-099b7ccfc674'}, {'abstract': 'More views on what impedes big transformations, and why humans have a habit of waiting too long to make them.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/more-on-climate-and-energy-procrastination/', 'snippet': 'More views on what impedes big transformations, and why humans have a habit of waiting too long to make them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here are three reactions to my post about Machiavelli’s view of the impediments to making big changes in societies — as in the asymmetrical nature of the battle to take the carbon out of energy systems in the face of both societal inertia and intensive efforts by entrenched interests to maintain the status quo. The first two remarks came in a group e-mail exchange and so relate to each other.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More On Climate and Energy Procrastination', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Energy and Power', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'History (Academic Subject)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sociology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Linden, Eugene', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Machiavelli, Niccolo', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T16:23:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f047a347-0fe0-516f-8315-9257d7946fd9', 'word_count': 637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f047a347-0fe0-516f-8315-9257d7946fd9'}, {'abstract': 'A entertaining interactive Web site takes a crafty — and spooky — approach to play on people’s fears about privacy and social media.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/mysterious-site-creates-a-horror-movie-starring-you/', 'snippet': 'A entertaining interactive Web site takes a crafty — and spooky — approach to play on people’s fears about privacy and social media.', 'lead_paragraph': '4:10 p.m. | Updated   Adding some details on the origin of the site and interview with its creator.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mysterious Site Creates a Horror Movie, Starring You', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T16:27:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e69a4d4-11bb-5b11-8018-c42f2e6feda5', 'word_count': 416, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e69a4d4-11bb-5b11-8018-c42f2e6feda5'}, {'abstract': 'A NASA scientist is lauded for communicating about climate.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/a-deserved-award-for-gavin-schmidt-of-real-climate-and-nasa/', 'snippet': 'A NASA scientist is lauded for communicating about climate.', 'lead_paragraph': '5:23 p.m. | Updated Gavin Schmidt, the climate modeler at NASA and Columbia University who has long endured the slings and arrows that come with blogging on climate, has now gained a laurel for his efforts — the inaugural $25,000 Climate Communications Prize of the American Geophysical Union. [The geophysical union release is now posted.]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Deserved Award for Gavin Schmidt of Real Climate and NASA', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Aeronautics and Space Administration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T16:30:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d3112e4-6e50-5e03-8b3b-f4fd9f723374', 'word_count': 470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d3112e4-6e50-5e03-8b3b-f4fd9f723374'}, {'abstract': '“Captors,” play about Eichmann, has eye on Broadway after Boston world premiere.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/eichmann-drama-opening-in-boston-has-broadway-in-mind/', 'snippet': '“Captors,” play about Eichmann, has eye on Broadway after Boston world premiere.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The respected character actor Michael Cristofer will play Adolf Eichmann, locked in a battle of wills and wits with Mossad agents in 1960 Buenos Aires, in the new drama “Captors” at the Huntington Theater Company this fall, the company said on Tuesday. An outside team of commercial producers has already secured the rights to transfer the work to Broadway, which they hope to do during the 2011-12 season after the end of the Huntington run, a spokeswoman for the theater added.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Eichmann Drama Opening in Boston, With Broadway in Mind', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T16:30:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e701caed-44da-5cd8-b6de-f0cc1b43b8c1', 'word_count': 507, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e701caed-44da-5cd8-b6de-f0cc1b43b8c1'}, {'abstract': 'Not very, proponents of a shutdown of the nuclear plant insist; but that all depends on how heavy the demand for electricity in the New York metropolitan area turns out to be in coming years.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/how-essential-is-indian-point/', 'snippet': 'Not very, proponents of a shutdown of the nuclear plant insist; but that all depends on how heavy the demand for electricity in the New York metropolitan area turns out to be in coming years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I reported in Tuesday’s paper, opponents of the Indian Point nuclear power plant assert that its two reactors can be retired in the next few years because alternatives exist that pose less risk and would not cost substantially more. New York City’s position, however, is that retiring the reactors would raise prices sharply and reduce reliability. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Essential Is Indian Point?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Energy Efficiency', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indian Point Nuclear Power Plant (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consolidated Edison Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Natural Resources Defense Council', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nuclear Regulatory Commission', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T16:34:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34616c2c-174e-5f14-8117-6d3fc4b1fd94', 'word_count': 497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34616c2c-174e-5f14-8117-6d3fc4b1fd94'}, {'abstract': 'Inspired by his daughter, Daisy, McNairy’s women’s wear collection is charmingly boyish, but sexily so.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/mark-mcnairy-makes-us-smile-again/', 'snippet': 'Inspired by his daughter, Daisy, McNairy’s women’s wear collection is charmingly boyish, but sexily so.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gentleman of varying persuasions of style are already familiar with the name Mark McNairy. Shoes from the designer’s footwear collection,  Mark McNairy New Amsterdam, and tailored pieces from the Ivy League-inspired outfitter J. Press, where McNairy was formerly creative director, have made their way into an eclectic mix of closets, from traditional to trendy. When it was announced this summer that McNairy would introduce\xa0a complete line of subversive-preppy clothes and accessories for men in the spring, all those fans cheered, and now — it’s official — he’s doing women’s wear, too. Inspired by his daughter, Daisy, the 50-piece collection is charmingly boyish, but (very McNairy) sexily so, with oxford shirts paired with boyfriend boxers and rugby tanks over miniskirts. Echoes of some of the men’s line motifs — a daisy print, an exploded camouflage and a floral-on-plaid shirt — mix easily with more overtly feminine pieces, like the ditsy Laura Ashley-like button-down. “Basically, I make women’s clothes from a guy’s point of view,” McNairy\xa0says. “It has nothing to do with fashion — they’re items that transcend trend.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mark McNairy Makes Us Smile (Again)', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T16:35:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/458d6ffa-7656-5783-9d35-e27961d658b8', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/458d6ffa-7656-5783-9d35-e27961d658b8'}, {'abstract': 'Amazon struck a deal for the exclusive digital rights to a hundred graphic novels by DC Comics, leading Barnes & Noble and Books-a-Million to remove the novels from their shelves.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/technology/bookstores-drop-comics-after-amazon-deal-with-dc.html', 'snippet': 'Amazon struck a deal for the exclusive digital rights to a hundred graphic novels by DC 