 union officials, insurers, real estate experts and appraisers have raised questions about the potential impacts that oil and gas leases have on residential and farm mortgages. The following is a collection of documents that relate to these issues, with key sections highlighted and annotated to explain their meaning.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Documents: Mortgages and Gas Leases', 'kicker': 'Drilling Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T19:16:01+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/37c98054-f861-51dc-9a98-c443b46aef82', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/37c98054-f861-51dc-9a98-c443b46aef82'}, {'abstract': 'The richest Americans give a greater share of their income to charities than low- and middle-income Americans, but the mix of beneficiaries is different.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/which-americans-are-most-generous-and-to-whom/', 'snippet': 'The richest Americans give a greater share of their income to charities than low- and middle-income Americans, but the mix of beneficiaries is different.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The richest Americans give a greater share of their income to charities than low- and middle-income Americans do, but the mix of beneficiaries is decidedly different, according to congressional testimony from Frank J. Sammartino, the assistant director for tax analysis from the Congressional Budget Office.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Which Americans Are Most Generous, and to Whom', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank'