, 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5da13fe5-18d3-5c8b-8bde-21c8b1c91dfb', 'word_count': 437, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5da13fe5-18d3-5c8b-8bde-21c8b1c91dfb'}, {'abstract': 'The newly revamped Arts Club in Mayfair, replete with brand-name art and boldface names as members, is creating quite a stir.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/londons-latest-in-spot-the-arts-club/', 'snippet': 'The newly revamped Arts Club in Mayfair, replete with brand-name art and boldface names as members, is creating quite a stir.', 'lead_paragraph': 'London may be brimming with private members’ clubs, but its latest — the newly revamped Arts Club in Mayfair — is creating quite a stir. Founded in 1863 by a group of friends (including Charles Dickens), the formerly fusty establishment has reopened with Gwyneth Paltrow (an investor) as its creative director. The stylish Art Deco-inspired interior designed by David D’Almada features marble floors, cashmere walls and art by Tomas Saraceno, John Baldessari and George Condo. Boldface names like Cameron Diaz, Mario Testino and Charlotte Dellal have been spotted supping in the restaurant, and Paltrow is known to belt out a few tunes at Club Nouveau, the nightclub downstairs that’s run by Mark Ronson. Also on the mike at times are Ronnie Wood, Mica Paris and Noel Gallagher. It costs £1,000 to join, the same fee applies each year, and the first list closes on Oct. 31. Being famous and fabulous is not a requirement, but it doesn’t hurt to be either, or both.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'London’s Latest ‘In’ Spot | The Arts Club', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T20:24:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Earle-Levine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Earle-Levine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5abfab1e-f8b5-5fee-af40-5e09b4569ac4', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5abfab1e-f8b5-5fee-af40-5e09b4569ac4'}, {'abstract': 'Ah, Heritage.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/adventures-in-credibility/', 'snippet': 'Ah, Heritage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Menzie Chinn takes us through the “Real American Jobs Act”, which is every bit as ludicrous as one might imagine. What caught my eye, however, is who the GOP is leaning on for economic validation: namely the Heritage Foundation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Adventures In Credibility', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T20:25:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/408afef2-8445-5186-90aa-9626057f3938', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/408afef2-8445-5186-90aa-9626057f3938'}, {'abstract': 'An interview by Egyptian state television with Sgt. First Class Gilad Shalit provoked polar opposite reactions on both sides of the Israel-Egypt border.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/world/middleeast/shalit-interview-sharply-criticized-by-just-about-everyone.html', 'snippet': 'An interview by Egyptian state television with Sgt. First Class Gilad Shalit provoked polar opposite reactions on both sides of the Israel-Egypt border.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — An exclusive interview by Egyptian state television with Sgt. First Class Gilad Shalit of Israel\xa0 — fresh from captivity, and before he had seen his family — provoked strong but polar opposite reactions on both sides of the Israel-Egypt border.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/world/SHALIT/SHALIT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 679, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/world/SHALIT/SHALIT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/19/world/SHALIT/SHALIT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sharp Criticism for Egyptian Interview With Soldier', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sharp Criticism for Egyptian Interview With Soldier', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Prisoners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shalit, Gilad', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T20:26:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil MacFarquhar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacFarquhar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee8d9c55-edbe-5986-b34e-25fe3f60638c', 'word_count': 725, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee8d9c55-edbe-5986-b34e-25fe3f60638c'}, {'abstract': 'Ali Smith’s new novel, “There but for the,” plays with words and symbols in explaining a man unseen for most of the book, and to say something about loss and retention.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/books/there-but-for-the-by-ali-smith-review.html', 'snippet': 'Ali Smith’s new novel, “There but for the,” plays with words and symbols in explaining a man unseen for most of the book, and to say something about loss and retention.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Symbol alert! Ali Smith’s new novel opens with a perplexing prologue: a story within a story about a man on an exercise bike whose eyes and mouth are covered by what look like mailbox flaps. A small boy appears, jimmies the flaps off with a dinner knife and instructs the man in how to make a paper airplane. The man complies and discovers that “outside, on its top, it looks like a plain folded piece of paper. Inside, underneath, it is packed tight into itself with surprising neatness like origami, like a small machine.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/19book/19book-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 924, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/19book/19book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/19book/19book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After Hiding, He Becomes a Celebrity', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Hiding, He Becomes a Celebrity', 'name': None,