2011/10/18/attention-shoppers-thats-jacques-pepin-in-aisle-3/', 'snippet': 'A peek inside Jacques Pépin’s kitchen reveals supermarket ingredients and a garbage can he stole back in the 1960s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wait. Was that Kikkoman soy sauce on Jacques Pépin’s kitchen counter?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Attention, Shoppers: That’s Jacques Pépin in Aisle 3', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chefs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pepin, Jacques', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T21:25:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Gordinier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gordinier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42a844b7-d2be-57d3-8224-db6fa4155ab4', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42a844b7-d2be-57d3-8224-db6fa4155ab4'}, {'abstract': 'Awesome.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/tpc-does-herman-cain/', 'snippet': 'Awesome.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Tax Policy Center has the distributional analysis of 9-9-9. It’s awesome:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'TPC Does Herman Cain', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T21:27:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6027a579-465e-547c-84dd-828138a41ae0', 'word_count': 151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6027a579-465e-547c-84dd-828138a41ae0'}, {'abstract': 'For the spring collections, everyone from Ralph Lauren to L’Wren Scott and Diane Von Furstenberg chose to dress women in tones of mint, lemony yellow and sorbet pink.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/spring-ahead-electric-pastels/', 'snippet': 'For the spring collections, everyone from Ralph Lauren to L’Wren Scott and Diane Von Furstenberg chose to dress women in tones of mint, lemony yellow and sorbet pink.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Right now fall is favoring cool-factor shocks of bright, neon colors. But the spring collections took a different approach, revisiting pretty with pastels that, thankfully, aren’t too jejune. Everyone from Ralph Lauren to L’Wren Scott and Diane Von Furstenberg chose to dress women — grown women — in tones of mint, lemony yellow and sorbet pink. Meanwhile, at Marni, Christopher Kane and Rochas classic silhouettes had a retro tinge with swirls of ’60s-era daisies. Louis Vuitton delighted in remodeling things like boyish biker jackets in chickadee yellow and Easter egg blue. “I wanted to add another dimension to the neons and thought that washing out some of those vivid colors would add an interesting contrast,” says the designer Jill Stuart, whose Art Deco Miami dresses were coated in colors like macaroon pink and sea foam green.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spring Ahead | Electric Pastels', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T21:30:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54eb3534-c9c1-57d1-8e44-c1cfe3b5f71d', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54eb3534-c9c1-57d1-8e44-c1cfe3b5f71d'}, {'abstract': 'Since the recession started, 72 percent of women seeking abortion services have been mothers.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/is-choice-less-accepted-for-mothers/', 'snippet': 'Since the recession started, 72 percent of women seeking abortion services have been mothers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The subset of women in America who most frequently make use of abortion services, writes Lauren Sandler in Slate this week, are … mothers. In 2008, according to the Guttmacher Institute, 61 percent of women who terminated their pregnancies already had at least one child. And in the period since the economic woes of the recession started hitting families, the National Abortion Federation reported to Ms. Sandler, fully 72 percent of clients who came seeking abortion services were mothers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is ‘Choice’ Less Accepted for Mothers?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T21:32:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Warner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Warner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f44e30ac-c723-5d6b-aa7a-a53584f3b957', 'word_count': 575, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f44e30ac-c723-5d6b-aa7a-a53584f3b957'}, {'abstract': '“Intríngulis,” Carlo Albán’s one-man show at Intar Theater, is a modern immigration story told by someone who was in the country illegally and a cast member of “Sesame Street.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/theater/reviews/intringulis-at-intar-theater-review.html', 'snippet': '“Intríngulis,” Carlo Albán’s one-man show at Intar Theater, is a modern immigration story told by someone who was in the country illegally and a cast member of “Sesame Street.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carlo Albán: nice American boy. Carlo Albán: illegal immigrant. When he was in high school, Mr. Albán was both, not to mention frequently stoned and a regular cast member of “Sesame Street.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/INTRINGULIS/INTRINGULIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/INTRINGULIS/INTRINGULIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/INTRINGULIS/INTRINGULIS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 716, 'width': 959, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/INTRINGULIS/INTRINGULIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/INTRINGULIS/INTRINGULIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Carlo of ‘Sesame Street,’ on Being an Illegal Immigrant in Real Life', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Intríngulis'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Carlo of ‘Sesame Street,’ on Being an Illegal Immigrant in Real Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T21:33:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Saltz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saltz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/64b78859-a48c-583e-8bc4-821d7f4d2abd', 'word_count': 420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64b78859-a48c-583e-8bc4-821d7f4d2abd'}, {'abstract': 'The ethics committee says it will investigate whether Jesse Jackson Jr. broke House rules by publicly campaigning to be appointed to President Obama’s former Senate seat.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/house-reopens-case-against-rep-jackson-in-blagojevich-case/', 'snippet': 'The ethics committee says it will investigate whether Jesse Jackson Jr. broke House rules by publicly campaigning to be appointed to President Obama’s former Senate seat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The House Ethics Committee, after a two-year delay, has reopened its investigation into whether Representative Jesse Jackson Jr. conducted a “public campaign” in late 2008 to secure an appointment to President Obama’s former Senate seat in Illinois, and perhaps broke House rules in the process.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'House Reopens Case Against Jackson in Blagojevich Case', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T21:35:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Lipton', 'per