ne': {'original': 'By Jeff Gordinier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gordinier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f5a17df-fb63-50dc-8774-2e6c5c711d06', 'word_count': 1771, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f5a17df-fb63-50dc-8774-2e6c5c711d06'}, {'abstract': 'Mary Meeker’s latest Internet trends report is available online.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/mary-meekers-internet-trends/', 'snippet': 'Mary Meeker’s latest Internet trends report is available online.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mary Meeker, the former securities analyst who joined the Kleiner Perkins venture capital firm, is still producing her influential annual Internet trends report.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mary Meeker’s Internet Trends', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Meeker, Mary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T21:43:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Damon Darlin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Damon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Darlin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a91e617-e329-5e97-a645-c7613879fcd6', 'word_count': 45, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a91e617-e329-5e97-a645-c7613879fcd6'}, {'abstract': 'The Doris Duke Charitable Foundation will award $50 million over 10 years to more than 200 performing artists, dance companies, theaters and presenters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/arts/duke-foundation-to-aid-artists.html', 'snippet': 'The Doris Duke Charitable Foundation will award $50 million over 10 years to more than 200 performing artists, dance companies, theaters and presenters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Doris Duke Charitable Foundation will award $50 million over 10 years to more than 200 performing artists, dance companies, theaters and presenters, the foundation is to announce on Thursday. Rather than allotting funds for specific projects as foundations typically do, the Doris Duke Performing Artists Initiative will invest in artists’ future work with flexible multiyear cash awards of as much as $275,000, starting next year. The program will offer more support to artists through residencies. Artists cannot apply for the funds; they will be chosen through an anonymous peer-review process. But the residencies will be open to joint applications from both artists and organizations. The foundation hopes to encourage artists to take creative risks, explore new ideas and address needs like health care and retirement savings. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Duke Foundation To Aid Artists', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Duke Foundation To Aid Artists', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Duke, Doris, Charitable Foundation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T21:45:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/0776e6ff-b203-5a69-b395-0745c5b9cd53', 'word_count': 127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0776e6ff-b203-5a69-b395-0745c5b9cd53'}, {'abstract': 'Vladimir Lande conducted the St. Petersburg State Academic Symphony Orchestra at Alice Tully Hall on Monday evening.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/arts/music/st-petersburg-symphony-orchestra-at-alice-tully-hall-review.html', 'snippet': 'Vladimir Lande conducted the St. Petersburg State Academic Symphony Orchestra at Alice Tully Hall on Monday evening.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sorting out the world of Russian orchestras can seem like a trip down the rabbit hole. The young, energetic ensemble that performed at Alice Tully Hall on Monday evening was billed as the St. Petersburg Symphony Orchestra, a group directed by Alexander Titov but led here by Vladimir Lande, its principal guest conductor. But it is more properly the St. Petersburg State Academic Symphony Orchestra, a group founded in 1967 as the Orchestra of Ancient and Modern Music and known briefly (from 1985 until the end of the Soviet era) as the Leningrad State Orchestra. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/19pete/19pete-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Rare Rhapsody and Other Russian Pleasures', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Rare Rhapsody and Other Russian Pleasures', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'St Petersburg Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T21:54:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f4cadb2-0706-52a0-8623-1387de5d3ed7', 'word_count': 407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f4cadb2-0706-52a0-8623-1387de5d3ed7'}, {'abstract': 'The private papers of public individuals can reveal the most intimate and unexpected details of a person’s life.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/my-three-favorite-collections-of-private-papers/', 'snippet': 'The private papers of public individuals can reveal the most intimate and unexpected details of a person’s life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To check Debbie Nathan’s piece on the multiple-personality postergirl Sybil, a photo researcher and I headed to the special collections room at John Jay College of Criminal Justice, which houses transcripts of Sybil’s psychotherapy sessions. Going through the boxes of Sybil’s late biographer, Flora Rheta Schreiber, which also contain her personal letters and photographs, impressed me with the intimacy of such collections. Here are some striking private details from the archives of three great public figures.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'My Three Favorite Collections of Private Papers', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T21:55:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jillian Dunham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jillian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dunham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'ran