Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'PaperChef', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T22:31:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1cef2c0-7bc1-5a83-b160-c40d262047f3', 'word_count': 76, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1cef2c0-7bc1-5a83-b160-c40d262047f3'}, {'abstract': 'Giving a new twist to the notion of “try before you buy,” TastingRoom offers more than 50 sets of six sampler bottles of wine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/dining/sampling-wines-at-tastingroomcom.html', 'snippet': 'Giving a new twist to the notion of “try before you buy,” TastingRoom offers more than 50 sets of six sampler bottles of wine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Giving a new twist to the notion of “try before you buy,” TastingRoom offers more than 50 sets of six sampler bottles of wine. Once you try the wines, you can order full bottles of them.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19STUFF_WINESPAN/Stuff-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19STUFF_WINESPAN/Stuff-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19STUFF_WINESPAN/Stuff-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 757, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19STUFF_WINESPAN/Stuff-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19STUFF_WINESPAN/Stuff-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sip a Little Wine Now, Buy a Bottle Later', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sip a Little Now, Buy a Bottle Later', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T22:32:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a48df073-f56c-594c-9212-ff8d743a57d6', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a48df073-f56c-594c-9212-ff8d743a57d6'}, {'abstract': 'A curious meditation on the psychogeography of Manhattan real estate, at the Frieze Art Fair.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/frieze-frame-ken-okiishis-manhattan-transfer/', 'snippet': 'A curious meditation on the psychogeography of Manhattan real estate, at the Frieze Art Fair.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Frame section of the Frieze Art Fair, curated by Cecilia Alemani and Rodrigo Moura, made space for experimentation on the fair’s fringes in the form of 25 solo artist presentations presented by a younger batch of international galleries. Among them was Alex Zachary, one of New York’s most idiosyncratic galleries both in terms of space and location. (It has been operating out of a multilevel, formerly residential condominium a couple of blocks from the Metropolitan Museum of Art.) Fittingly, the artist Ken Okiishi’s project at Zachary’s Frame booth was a curious meditation on the psychogeography of Manhattan real estate and mythologies of uptown bohemia. Eulogizing the recently shuttered Lexington Avenue establishment Gino as a point of departure (which over time has hosted luminaries as diverse as Sophia Loren, I. M. Pei and Robin Byrd), Okiishi began by outfitting the booth in the iconic Scalamandré-designed zebra wallpaper that made its debut in the restaurant’s dining room circa 1930. He applied the same print to parasols bolted to the walls, whose slow and steady motorized twirling evoked bygone pageantry and ritziness. Faux-antique photographs of an early-20th-century apartment were hung beside the umbrellas; in actuality these were digital images lifted from the present-day Streeteasy.com listing for a unit on West 67th Street that was once home to Marcel Duchamp, from 1916 to 1918. East Side, West Side, Depression era, recession era: Okiishi’s project drew foggy connections between places and times that are factually disparate but psychically proximate. These artifacts repurposed as ready-mades contribute to a meandering mental map of the city that relies on legends like Duchamp (and Gino’s) as its compass.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Frieze Frame | Ken Okiishi’s Manhattan Transfer', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T22:32:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By KEVIN MCGARRY', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MCGARRY', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/914a25ee-b932-5863-abcb-5b0047a34375', 'word_count': 270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/914a25ee-b932-5863-abcb-5b0047a34375'}, {'abstract': 'Mine was a short life but an eventful one. So bizarre and unpredictable was it that I sometimes wonder if it was a dream, or a week of TV programming.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/arts/television/it-was-real-cradle-to-grave-or-was-it-just-tv.html', 'snippet': 'Mine was a short life but an eventful one. So bizarre and unpredictable was it that I sometimes wonder if it was a dream, or a week of TV programming.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mine was a short life but an eventful one. So bizarre and unpredictable was it that I sometimes wonder if it was a dream, a thing experienced yet not experienced, like a stadium rock concert that you mostly watch on the overhead screen because your seats are so far from the stage. To tell you the truth, I’m kind of glad it’s over, since it was exhausting. Let me tell you about it. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/tv-life-1/tv-life-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/tv-life-1/tv-life-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/tv-life-1/tv-life-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'It Was Real, Cradle to Grave. Or Was It Just TV?', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It Was Real, Cradle to Grave. Or Was It Just TV?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T22:32:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1035e11-b42e-5087-be7d-416c375b5189', 'word_count': 1111, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1035e11-b42e-5087-be7d-416c375b5189'}, {'abstract': '“Radioactive Wolves” on PBS offers the site of a nuclear accident in 1986 as a best-case demonstration that life abides, despite the human race’s efforts to eradicate it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/arts/television/radioactive-wolves-on-pbs-review.html', 'snippet': '“Radioactive Wolves” on PBS offers the site of a nuclear accident in 1986 as a best-case demonstration that life abides, despite the human race’s efforts to eradicate it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the months since the Japanese tsunami, we’ve heard a lot about Chernobyl as a worst-case example: here’s how bad Fukushima could have been. Now PBS’s “Nature” offers another vision: Chernobyl as a best-case demonstration that life abides despite the human race’s efforts to eradicate it. As long as the life in question isn’t ours, that is.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/WOLVES/WOLVES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/WOLVES/WOLVES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/WOLVES/WOLVES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 941, 'width': 705, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/WOLVES/WOLVES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/19/arts/WOLVES/WOLVES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Dead Zone of Chernobyl, Animal Kingdom Thrives', 'kicker': "Television Review | 'Nature: Radioactive Wolves'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Dead Zone of Chernobyl, Animal Kingdom Thrives', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting Service', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T22:37:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b097221d-4eff-5e08-91ee-a65a14fb4447', 'word_count': 511, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b097221d-4eff-5e08-91ee-a65a14fb4447'}, {'abstract': 'At a dinner party in Denmark decades ago, a preview of the Nordic cuisine championed by the chef René Redzepi today.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/dining/scandinavian-food-before-it-was-fashionable.html', 'snippet': 'At a dinner party in Denmark decades ago, a preview of the Nordic cuisine championed by the chef René Redzepi today.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NOT to gloat, of course, but the resurgence of interest in Scandinavian food is something I have been anticipating for a long, long time. Currently the cuisine is termed “Nordic,” as championed by the celebrated Copenhagen chef René Redzepi, who will discuss his inventive cooking style\xa0at the Union Square Greenmarket on Saturday. He and his disciples swear by ingredients that are local, seasonal and, preferably, wild. Nordic also has a nice exotic ring, summoning visions of Greenlanders and Laplanders fishing through ice, simmering up blubber confits and salt-curing chunks of Donner and Blitzen.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19DEGUSTIBUS_SPAN/DeGustibus-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 306, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19DEGUSTIBUS_SPAN/DeGustibus-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 306}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19DEGUSTIBUS_SPAN/DeGustibus-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 523, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19DEGUSTIBUS_SPAN/DeGustibus-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/19/dining/19DEGUSTIBUS_SPAN/DeGustibus-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Scandinavian Food Before It Was Fashionable', 'kicker': 'De Gustibus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nordic Once, Nordic Twice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T22:39:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mimi Sheraton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mimi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sheraton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5af312fa-84bc-54bc-ac2b-3c221659c024', 'word_count': 1080, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5af312fa-84bc-54bc-ac2b-3c221659c024'}, {'abstract': '“American Hoggers,” a reality show, focuses on the family business of the Campbell family of central Texas, which involves capturing and hunting the wild boars.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/arts/television/american-hoggers-on-ae-review.html', 'snippet': '“American Hoggers,” a reality show, focuses on the family business of the Campbell family of central Texas, which involves capturing and hunting the wild boars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before you tune in A&E’s newest show, think for a moment about the message you’re sending to Marg Helgenberger, Emily VanCamp and Mariska Hargitay. You’re telling these lovely and presumably hard-working actresses, who all have shows in the same time slot, “I’d rather look at hogs than look at you.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Get Away? Not by the Hairs on Their Chinny Chin Chins', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Get Away? Not by the Hairs On Their Chinny Chin Chins', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ARTS & ENTERTAINMENT NETWORK', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-18T22:41:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlenam