value': 'Brooklyn Heights (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T00:13:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marc Santora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marc', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Santora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/065c04fc-80d5-51a9-a7dc-f0e7d3a92862', 'word_count': 570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/065c04fc-80d5-51a9-a7dc-f0e7d3a92862'}, {'abstract': 'Youth courts do more than simply divert teenagers from juvenile justice: they actively create pro-social behavior.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/18/where-teens-find-the-jury-isnt-rigged/', 'snippet': 'Youth courts do more than simply divert teenagers from juvenile justice: they actively create pro-social behavior.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Friday, Fixes examined the Youth Court of the District of Columbia, a forum where first-time nonviolent teenage offenders are judged by others who have been in the same situation.\xa0\xa0 The D.C. Youth Court is one of the largest of some 1,000 youth courts around the country.\xa0\xa0 These courts are designed to help minor offenders avoid a criminal record and stay out of juvenile justice — traditionally an efficient production line for criminality.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Teenagers Find the Jury Isn’t Rigged', 'kicker': 'Opinionator', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jury System', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Juvenile Delinquency', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teenagers and Adolescence', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Youth', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T00:15:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90fd8aca-e0ef-50be-a2dd-fd6914a7632e', 'word_count': 1278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90fd8aca-e0ef-50be-a2dd-fd6914a7632e'}, {'abstract': 'Labor unions won the first round of a potentially significant challenge to pension reform on Tuesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/us/pension-reform-victory-for-unions-in-rhode-island.html', 'snippet': 'Labor unions won the first round of a potentially significant challenge to pension reform on Tuesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Labor unions won the first round of a potentially significant challenge to pension reform on Tuesday, with a Rhode Island state judge ruling that there is an implied contract between the state pension system and the employees who stand to benefit from it. In 2010, eight public employee unions sued the state, arguing that reducing pension benefits for workers with more than 10 years of service violated their contractual rights. The state had tried to have the suit dismissed. The ruling may complicate the state’s pension reform efforts, which will be the focus of a special legislative session next month.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rhode Island: Judge Rules for Unions in Pension Suit', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | New England', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rhode Island: Judge Rules for Unions in Pension Suit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rhode Island', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T00:16:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abby Goodnough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodnough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5b03414-a32e-5e1a-ba16-685e6f2fa3f5', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5b03414-a32e-5e1a-ba16-685e6f2fa3f5'}, {'abstract': 'Halfway through the city’s Million Trees campaign, even supporters worry about dead wood and other future hazards if maintenance money is lacking.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/nyregion/new-york-planting-a-million-treestoo-many-some-say.html', 'snippet': 'Halfway through the city’s Million Trees campaign, even supporters worry about dead wood and other future hazards if maintenance money is lacking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a city of steel and concrete, a single tree offers a burst of green, a cooling canopy and an antidote for frayed nerves. It seems as if nobody is against one tree. But try planting a million of them.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/nyregion/TREES/TREES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/nyregion/TREES/TREES-thumb