, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86729333-aa38-51dd-b557-017ca30765e2', 'word_count': 325, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86729333-aa38-51dd-b557-017ca30765e2'}, {'abstract': 'Several wild animals were still on the loose in an Ohio town on Wednesday after their owner had set them free and then killed himself, local authorities said.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/deputies-hunt-big-game-on-the-loose-in-ohio/', 'snippet': 'Several wild animals were still on the loose in an Ohio town on Wednesday after their owner had set them free and then killed himself, local authorities said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last Updated | 2:35 p.m. The authorities in the Ohio town where wild animals roamed overnight and early Wednesday said that by the afternoon all but two — a monkey and a wolf — had been accounted for. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Deputies Hunt Exotic Animals on the Loose in Ohio', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMALS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T06:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. David Goodman and Elizabeth A. Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3eb2ca54-34b9-5b3d-a627-e410e4c99ca4', 'word_count': 1110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3eb2ca54-34b9-5b3d-a627-e410e4c99ca4'}, {'abstract': 'A preview Wednesday’s games, Matchday 3, of the UEFA Champions League.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/champions-league-wednesday-preview-porto-leery-of-apoel/', 'snippet': 'A preview Wednesday’s games, Matchday 3, of the UEFA Champions League.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Don’t look now, but as the Champions League’s group stage comes its halfway point with eight games on Wednesday, the pride of Cyprus leads Group G. APOEL (Athlitikos Podosfairikos Omilos Ellinon Lefkosias in Greek, or Athletic Football Club of Greeks of Nicosia) goes to Portugal to face Porto leading the group by a point.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Champions League Wednesday Preview: Porto Leery of APOEL', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'UEFA Champions League (Soccer)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T06:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c35ecf67-063d-58b7-aca2-8bf7a3cd87c2', 'word_count': 942, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c35ecf67-063d-58b7-aca2-8bf7a3cd87c2'}, {'abstract': '12 rupees for a crunchy, spicy handful of heaven.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/the-worlds-best-vada-pav-vadkhal-edition-2/', 'snippet': '12 rupees for a crunchy, spicy handful of heaven.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A recent reporting trip took me down the road that connects the pulsing metropolis of Mumbai to the laid-back retreat of Goa. About 80 kilometers (50 miles) outside the city, in the town of Vadkhal, my traveling companion insisted we make a snack stop. I am never really averse to snack stops, but I was surprised because he had been rhapsodizing about the fresh, Konkan-style fish curry that awaited us at our destination.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The World’s Best Vada Pav, Vadkhal Edition', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mumbai (India)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T07:04:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lydia Polgreen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lydia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Polgreen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2989a67d-4e0c-52f3-9a0d-9a9c579cac7d', 'word_count': 361, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2989a67d-4e0c-52f3-9a0d-9a9c579cac7d'}, {'abstract': 'On Oct. 19, 1987, a day that became known as “Black Monday,” the stock market crashed as the Dow Jones Industrial Average plunged 508 points, or 22.6 percent in value, its largest single-day percentage drop.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/oct-19-1987-stock-market-crashes-on-black-monday/', 'snippet': 'On Oct. 19, 1987, a day that became known as “Black Monday,” the stock market crashed as the Dow Jones Industrial Average plunged 508 points, or 22.6 percent in value, its largest single-day percentage drop.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Oct. 19, 1987, a day that became known as “Black Monday,” the stock market crashed as the Dow Jones Industrial Average plunged 508 points, or 22.6 percent in value, its largest single-day percentage drop. The crash came after a two-week period in which the Dow dropped 15 percent.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oct. 19, 1987 | Stock Market Crashes on ‘Black Monday’', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T08:05:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/053b734d-3e0a-5c69-9ede-00bd6db9fe0d', 'word_count': 302, 'uri': 'nyt://article/053b734d-3e0a-5c69-9ede-00bd6db9fe0d'}, {'abstract': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/news-quiz-oct-19-2011/', 'snippet': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'See what you know about the news below. To prepare, you might scan the articles or summaries on today’s paper.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Quiz | Oct. 19, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T08:35:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tiffany Frasier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tiffany', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frasier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7e2646d-0f8a-5bc6-9f14-a4bf54ee046a', 'word_count': 20, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7e2646d-0f8a-5bc6-9f14-a4bf54ee046a'}, {'abstract': 'Despite one of the largest protests in Athens in months, Parliament approved a new austerity bill in principle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/world/europe/greek-workers-start-two-day-anti-austerity-strike.html', 'snippet': 'Despite one of the largest protests in Athens in months, Parliament approved a new austerity bill in principle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ATHENS — By now, it almost feels like a ritual: a strike and large demonstration disrupted by skirmishes and tear gas ahead of a parliamentary vote on new austerity measures that Greece needs to take to qualify for the next installment of aid the country needs to fend off default. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thousands in Greece Protest Austerity Bill', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Thousands in Greece Protest New Austerity Bill', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strikes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T08:41:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Donadio and Niki Kitsantonis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Donadio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Niki', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kitsantonis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3d653a5-57ee-5748-a8e9-4ec76059050b', 'word_count': 826, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3d653a5-57ee-5748-a8e9-4ec76059050b'}, {'abstract': 'British authorities have been trying to clear an encampment east of London for years, arguing that it is supposed to be kept as open countryside.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/world/europe/british-authorities-evict-travelers.html', 'snippet': 'British authorities have been trying to clear an encampment east of London for years, arguing that it is supposed to be kept as open countryside.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — After a long and bitter legal battle that has tested Britain’s reputation for tolerance, the authorities began clearing an encampment east of London on Wednesday, two days after the Court of Appeal upheld a ruling that occupation by its owners — so-called travelers — was unlawful.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/20/world/20britain-1319056437236/20britain-1319056437236-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/20/world/20britain-1319056437236/20britain-1319056437236-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/20/world/20britain-1319056437236/20britain-1319056437236-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 651, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/20/world/20britain-1319056437236/20britain-1319056437236-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/20/world/20britain-1319056437236/20britain-1319056437236-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'British Police Clear Encampment, Home to Hundreds, After Ruling', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'British Police Clear Encampment, Home to Hundreds, After Ruling', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evictions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T08:42:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Cowell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54c469f4-5671-55f2-8b2e-0b10a79652d4', 'word_count': 592, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54c469f4-5671-55f2-8b2e-0b10a79652d4'}, {'abstract': 'Lizzie Sadin spent eight years working on her project “Children Behind Bars,” which took her from Russia to Cambodia to document issues facing juvenile prisoners.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/around-the-world-young-and-imprisoned/', 'snippet': 'Lizzie Sadin spent eight years working on her project “Children Behind Bars,” which took her from Russia to Cambodia to document issues facing juvenile prisoners.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lizzie Sadin started researching her project “Children Behind Bars” in 1999\xa0— though it took her more than a year to be allowed to shoot inside a prison in Russia. By then, she had no\xa0plans to seek out prisons in other countries.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Around the World, Young and Imprisoned', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kerri MacDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kerri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8404a177-d9d2-51e5-b486-89f1287dcd42', 'word_count': 385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8404a177-d9d2-51e5-b486-89f1287dcd42'}, {'abstract': 'It afflicts us all. Because surety in our own judgments is part of being human.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/magazine/dont-blink-the-hazards-of-confidence.html', 'snippet': 'It afflicts us all. Because surety in our own judgments is part of being human.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many decades ago I spent what seemed like a great deal of time under a scorching sun, watching groups of sweaty soldiers as they solved a problem. I was doing my national service in the Israeli Army at the time. I had completed an undergraduate degree in psychology, and after a year as an infantry officer, I was assigned to the army’s Psychology Branch, where one of my occasional duties was to help evaluate candidates for officer training. We used methods that were developed by the British Army in World War II.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23kahneman_span/23kahneman_span-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 378, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23kahneman_span/23kahneman_span-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 378}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23kahneman_span/23kahneman_span-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 378, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23kahneman_span/23kahneman_span-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 378, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23kahneman_span/23kahneman_span-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23kahneman_span/23kahneman_span-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23kahneman_span/23kahneman_span-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Blink! The Hazards of Confidence', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Surety of Fools', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Memory', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T09:01:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Kahneman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kahneman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93e17b3d-49ca-5feb-ac3a-78692f2f69a5', 'word_count': 3423, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93e17b3d-49ca-5feb-ac3a-78692f2f69a5'}, {'abstract': 'Training Afghans to protect their homes and fight the Taliban seems to be working. The problem is that it’s also allowing them to fight their own personal wars.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/magazine/bad-guys-vs-worse-guys-in-afghanistan.html', 'snippet': 'Training Afghans to protect their homes and fight the Taliban seems to be working. The problem is that it’s also allowing them to fight their own personal wars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One afternoon this summer, in a park beside the Ajmil River, I sat with seven residents of Shahabuddin, a collection of villages in northern Afghanistan’s Baghlan Province. It was the first week of Ramadan, and the park was almost empty, but still the men — some middle-aged, others stooped and gray-bearded — whispered conspiratorially and became silent whenever anybody walked close. Several minutes into our conversation, one of the younger men, who’d been mutely worrying at a piece of grass, rose and abruptly stalked off. The others, embarrassed, apologized for him. “If Nur-ul Haq finds out we spoke to you,” one of them explained, “he will kill us.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23afghanistan_cham-slide-6DT1/23afghanistan_cham-slide-6DT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23afghanistan_cham-slide-6DT1/23afghanistan_cham-slide-6DT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23afghanistan_cham-slide-6DT1/23afghanistan_cham-slide-6DT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23afghanistan_cham-slide-6DT1/23afghanistan_cham-slide-6DT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23afghanistan_cham-slide-6DT1/23afghanistan_cham-slide-6DT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bad Guys vs. Worse Guys in Afghanistan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Bad Guys vs. The Worse Guys', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kandahar (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War and Guerrilla Warfare', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kabul (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T09:01:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Luke Mogelson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Luke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mogelson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d497643b-c4a8-5aac-af3e-ae3abb1df84b', 'word_count': 6253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d497643b-c4a8-5aac-af3e-ae3abb1df84b'}, {'abstract': 'Can you use the cost per pupil to calculate the amount needed for 20 million students to use a particular educational technology?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/test-yourself-math-oct-19-2011/', 'snippet': 'Can you use the cost per pupil to calculate the amount needed for 20 million students to use a particular educational technology?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Test your math skills with this question created by Patrick Honner at Math for America from the article, “Inflating the Software Report Card.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | Math, Oct. 19, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T09:08:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d954b4b4-1ab8-56b8-b65a-b641b2cd6794', 'word_count': 50, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d954b4b4-1ab8-56b8-b65a-b641b2cd6794'}, {'abstract': '6 Q’s About the News | Why is Atlanta a new contender for “zombie capital” of the United States?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/zombies-turn-up-the-heat-in-atlanta/', 'snippet': '6 Q’s About the News | Why is Atlanta a new contender for “zombie capital” of the United States?', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHY has Atlanta magazine dubbed that city the “zombie capital of the world”?WHY do you think so many people want to be extras on “The Walking Dead”?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Zombies Turn Up the Heat in Atlanta', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T09:33:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a132062b-2c52-5ce4-938c-24c39b21f597', 'word_count': 107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a132062b-2c52-5ce4-938c-24c39b21f597'}, {'abstract': 'Student Opinion | How does television affect your life and your family?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/what-role-does-television-play-in-your-life-and-the-life-of-your-family/', 'snippet': 'Student Opinion | How does television affect your life and your family?', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new study about children under the age of 2 states that television may be harmful to the children’s development. The study’s authors suggest parents refrain from letting their young children watch television, the same advice they issued in 1999. What age do you think is appropriate for children to begin watching television? Do you have rules in your home about how much television you can watch per day?  ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Role Does Television Play in Your Life and the Life of Your Family?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T09:50:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4418984f-1371-56f1-a2a3-6d3a31b5161c', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4418984f-1371-56f1-a2a3-6d3a31b5161c'}, {'abstract': 'Voters seem to have grown weary of Mr. Bloomberg as he enters the home stretch of his tenure, a new poll finds.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/poll-finds-some-believe-mayor-has-lost-focus/', 'snippet': 'Voters seem to have grown weary of Mr. Bloomberg as he enters the home stretch of his tenure, a new poll finds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A narrow majority of New York City voters believe that Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg has lost his focus midway through his third term, according to a poll released Wednesday morning by Quinnipiac University.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poll Finds Some Believe Mayor Has Lost Focus', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Quinnipiac University', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T10:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f083eb48-3089-57d1-91b8-3687716ebedc', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f083eb48-3089-57d1-91b8-3687716ebedc'}, {'abstract': 'With fewer people working and those employed working fewer hours, it’s not surprising at all that the nation’s total hours worked dropped significantly, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/are-employers-requiring-people-to-work-longer-hours/', 'snippet': 'With fewer people working and those employed working fewer hours, it’s not surprising at all that the nation’s total hours worked dropped significantly, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Casey B. Mulligan is an economics professor at the University of Chicago.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are Employers Requiring People to Work Longer Hours?', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Working Hours', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T10:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Casey B. Mulligan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Casey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/87ed5626-b976-586c-a83c-d752f76f5815', 'word_count': 551, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87ed5626-b976-586c-a83c-d752f76f5815'}, {'abstract': 'The racing simulator, released this month for the Microsoft Xbox 360, teases realistic on-road behavior from fantasy machines of a gamer’s own making.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/a-v-12-in-a-ford-ka-forza-4-makes-realism-customizable/', 'snippet': 'The racing simulator, released this month for the Microsoft Xbox 360, teases realistic on-road behavior from fantasy machines of a gamer’s own making.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a Web advertisement for Forza Motorsport 4, the new racing simulator for the Xbox 360, we hear Jeremy Clarkson say that car enthusiasts “are an endangered species.” Mr. Clarkson, the host of the BBC‘s “Top Gear” show, laments that lovers of speed and performance are being trapped in practical cars and commuter slogs. That, he says, is why a racing simulator like Forza provides sanctuary.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A V-12 in a Ford Ka? Forza 4 Makes Realism Customizable', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Xbox 360 (Video Game System)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Aston Martin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Broadcasting Corp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T10:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Schembari', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schembari', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc946c9a-0625-5e34-aecb-5785f2614db0', 'word_count': 497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc946c9a-0625-5e34-aecb-5785f2614db0'}, {'abstract': 'Cowboys Coach Jason Garrett shouldn’t shy away from consulting advanced  statistics and using them in specific game situations.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/jerry-jones-was-right-cowboys-should-have-passed/', 'snippet': 'Cowboys Coach Jason Garrett shouldn’t shy away from consulting advanced  statistics and using them in specific game situations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jason Garrett has received a lot of criticism for his decision to call three straight runs while up, 16-13, with 3 minutes 36 seconds to play in Sunday’s game in New England, perhaps the harshest of which came from his boss. “We rolled the dice at the end and went conservative,” the Cowboys’ owner, Jerry Jones, said,\xa0“and it bit us. You always second-guess whether or not we should have tried to run a little offense down there instead of running it three times.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jerry Jones Was Right; Cowboys Should Have Passed', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garrett, Jason', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, Jerry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dallas Cowboys', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T10:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Bales', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bales', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e10b8aa7-81a2-585b-b80b-94d2a925e001', 'word_count': 588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e10b8aa7-81a2-585b-b80b-94d2a925e001'}, {'abstract': 'The latest political news from today’s Times, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/the-early-word-combative-2/', 'snippet': 'The latest political news from today’s Times, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Today’s Times:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Combative', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debates (Political)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T10:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Southall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Southall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/416ab933-d397-54de-9786-5ea3f542f51a', 'word_count': 482, 'uri': 'nyt://article/416ab933-d397-54de-9786-5ea3f542f51a'}, {'abstract': 'Good food and drink rank high among Hong Kongers’ obsessions, so it’s appropriate that there is a whole month devoted to it.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/in-hong-kong-a-monthlong-celebration-of-food-and-wine/', 'snippet': 'Good food and drink rank high among Hong Kongers’ obsessions, so it’s appropriate that there is a whole month devoted to it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Good food and drink rank high among Hong Kongers’ obsessions, so it’s appropriate that there is a whole month devoted to it. Wine & Dine month kicks off with the Wine & Dine Festival, which runs Oct. 27 to 30 at a picturesque location on the West Kowloon promenade. Food and wine tastings may be purchased from over 200 exhibitors (general admission is free). New at the festival this year are Spanish and Japanese purveyors who promise themed food and entertainment. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Hong Kong, a Monthlong Celebration of Food and Wine', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Festivals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T10:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Chow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88d227df-4e88-5bc9-a029-dfd4391d450e', 'word_count': 246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88d227df-4e88-5bc9-a029-dfd4391d450e'}, {'abstract': 'How to use text messaging with social networks.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/tip-of-the-week-mobile-updates-to-your-social-network/', 'snippet': 'How to use text messaging with social networks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dedicated smartphone apps for social-media networks make it easy to post status updates on the go. Users with regular cellphones can also keep their friends in the loop by simply sending text messages directly to a phone number specified by the site. A few setup steps may be needed first to get the text-based posting activated (and every service may not work with every wireless carrier out there), but Facebook, Twitter and Google+ all have guides for getting started. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tip of the Week: Mobile Updates to Your Social Network', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subjec