h', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mydans', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04c83fdc-df4e-5355-a012-cf8165bc53ca', 'word_count': 502, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04c83fdc-df4e-5355-a012-cf8165bc53ca'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/morning-take-out-349/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wall Street Grapples with Diminished Reality \xa0|\xa0 The first loss at Goldman Sachs since the financial crisis points to what could be a more lasting shift in the financial industry, as the bank that was once Wall Street’s highest flier has been grounded.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T12:54:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f411507a-5713-5b6f-899c-d62e8578b241', 'word_count': 2077, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f411507a-5713-5b6f-899c-d62e8578b241'}, {'abstract': 'Four consumer advocacy and privacy organizations plan to file a complaint with the Federal Trade Commission on Wednesday charging PepsiCo and its Frito-Lay subsidiary of engaging in deceptive digital marketing practices.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/complaint-accuses-pepsi-of-deceptive-marketing/', 'snippet': 'Four consumer advocacy and privacy organizations plan to file a complaint with the Federal Trade Commission on Wednesday charging PepsiCo and its Frito-Lay subsidiary of engaging in deceptive digital marketing practices.', 'lead_paragraph': '11:30 a.m. | Updated Four consumer advocacy and privacy organizations filed a complaint with the Federal Trade Commission on Wednesday alleging that PepsiCo and its Frito-Lay subsidiary are engaging in deceptive digital marketing practices targeted at adolescents.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Complaint Accuses Pepsi of Deceptive Marketing', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Protection', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Trade Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Frito-Lay Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pepsico Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T13:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanzina Vega', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanzina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vega', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9cf103c4-d039-51d3-9a25-f20c48e0c7e6', 'word_count': 384, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9cf103c4-d039-51d3-9a25-f20c48e0c7e6'}, {'abstract': 'The Shopper defends herself against the inevitable onslaught of smell with a countervailing arsenal in her handbag.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/samurai-shopper-odor-eaters/', 'snippet': 'The Shopper defends herself against the inevitable onslaught of smell with a countervailing arsenal in her handbag.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once upon a time, washing with soap and water was considered radical hygiene. There was even a church injunction against water on the grounds that bathing spurred sensuality and indolence — talk about the odor of sanctity. At the same time, pestilential stench was a sign of Satan; bad odors indicated physical and moral decay. Alain Corbin’s “The Foul and the Fragrant” chronicles France in the 18th and 19th centuries when the masses stank to high heaven and perfumed gloves and sleeves inured the aesthete from those very rank rank and file. But the aristocracy was not entirely immune. Casanova, Corbin tells us, nearly fainted at the musky smell of an old nymphomaniac duchess. I sat next to her on the bus yesterday, and I nearly died too.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Samurai Shopper | Odor Eaters', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T13:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By S.S. Fair', 'person': [{'firstname': 'S.', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Fair', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b67aa0a1-92e6-51be-a902-076a0add8ada', 'word_count': 443, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b67aa0a1-92e6-51be-a902-076a0add8ada'}, {'abstract': 'The company said it would divide into two publicly traded entities.', 'web_url': 'https://prescriptions.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/abbott-plans-to-split-into-two-companies/', 'snippet': 'The company said it would divide into two publicly traded entities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated: 12:45 p.m. Abbott Laboratories said on Wednesday it would separate into two publicly traded companies, one focused on medical devices like heart stents and the other in what it called “research-based pharmaceuticals,” such as its blockbuster rheumatoid arthritis drug Humira.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Abbott Plans to Split Into Two Companies', 'kicker': 'Prescriptions', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medical Devices', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'White, Miles D', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Abbott Laboratories', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T13:01:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Japsen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Japsen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/868b51f6-2bcc-5ef6-9109-24e0a2f784c1', 'word_count': 780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/868b51f6-2bcc-5ef6-9109-24e0a2f784c1'}, {'abstract': 'Experiments aimed at using visual and sonic arts to convey tough, consequential concepts.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/fostering-insights-by-engagin-the-whole-brain/', 'snippet': 'Experiments aimed at using visual and sonic arts to convey tough, consequential concepts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s a fresh look at efforts to bring data to life using sound, motion and graphics. I’ve posted before on the largely untapped potential to communicate information and ideas in new ways. The images above of brain activity related to processing or speaking words (from the National Institutes of Health) convey how multimedia communication might more fully engage the brain.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fostering Insights by Engaging the Whole Brain', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (Secondary)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TED (Technology, Entertainment, Design)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T13:19:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/199e2d57-e8c1-55c1-ac50-710118a49532', 'word_count': 434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/199e2d57-e8c1-55c1-ac50-710118a49532'}, {'abstract': 'A big rush to buy computer servers means there may not be time for a new model of server, using non-Intel chips, to reach broad acceptance.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/rising-demand-may-save-intel-from-low-price-competitors/', 'snippet': 'A big rush to buy computer servers means there may not be time for a new model of server, using non-Intel chips, to reach broad acceptance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Intel has a message for its doubters: Time, in terms of a rush to build more big computing centers, is on its side.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rising Demand May Save Intel From Low-Price Competitors', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Chips', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data Centers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Enterprise Computing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Intel Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T13:19:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Quentin Hardy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Quentin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hardy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc585476-feca-5f29-82f5-5bc2649865da', 'word_count': 444, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc585476-feca-5f29-82f5-5bc2649865da'}, {'abstract': 'Sam Bradford’s rookie season in St. Louis  was noteworthy for just one thing: he threw the ball a lot. His second season isn’t going any better.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/withholding-judgment-on-sam-bradford/', 'snippet': 'Sam Bradford’s rookie season in St. Louis  was noteworthy for just one thing: he threw the ball a lot. His second season isn’t going any better.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chase Stuart, who contributes to the Fifth Down, took some criticism from Rams fans in August for writing on Pro-Football-Reference.com that Sam Bradford’s rookie season in 2010 was overrated. Stuart’s point was that football pundits were overrating Bradford — he was being called a surefire star — not that Bradford was a failure.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Withholding Judgment on Sam Bradford', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bradford, Sam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'St Louis Rams', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T13:30:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a571513e-4af4-5241-b8e4-c4bd675262e3', 'word_count': 551, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a571513e-4af4-5241-b8e4-c4bd675262e3'}, {'abstract': 'The owner of an exotic pet farm from which the animals escaped is found dead.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/on-our-radar-wild-animals-escape-in-ohio/', 'snippet': 'The owner of an exotic pet farm from which the animals escaped is found dead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lions, tigers, bears and other animals escaped from an exotic pet farm in Zanesville, Ohio, prompting the local authorities to issue a shoot-to-kill order that resulted in death for most of the animals. [Reuters]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: Wild Animals Escape in Ohio', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T13:54:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b840897-e26f-5b8d-b118-d40c42f7dc40', 'word_count': 195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b840897-e26f-5b8d-b118-d40c42f7dc40'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/what-were-reading-293/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wine Spectator: Matt Kramer advises newcomers to wine to avoid the hedonistic approach and go for the edge. — Eric Asimov', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T13:57:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f425b0e8-ef8f-5e53-be07-c876050a2eac', 'word_count': 521, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f425b0e8-ef8f-5e53-be07-c876050a2eac'}, {'abstract': 'An interview with Simon Kuper, the author of the new book “Soccer Men.”', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/q-a-with-soccer-men-author-simon-kuper/', 'snippet': 'An interview with Simon Kuper, the author of the new book “Soccer Men.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The British author Simon Kuper was born in Uganda and grew up in England, the Netherlands, the United States, Sweden and Jamaica. His soccer writing has taken him to even more far-flung corners of the globe — even on daring forays inside the minds of Diego Maradona and Wayne Rooney — for the profiles of what he calls the “rogues, geniuses and neurotics” of world soccer that make up his new book, “Soccer Men.” Kuper’s previous books include the prize-winning “Football Against the Enemy” and the groundbreaking “Soccernomics,” written with the economist Stefan Syzmanski. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q. and A. With ‘Soccer Men’ Author Simon Kuper', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T14:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Das', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Das', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90610108-56a8-5744-b056-b44c3edecbe7', 'word_count': 1355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90610108-56a8-5744-b056-b44c3edecbe7'}, {'abstract': 'A gathering of philosophy-related links from the past week.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/stone-links-5/', 'snippet': 'A gathering of philosophy-related links from the past week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A gathering of philosophy-related links from the past week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stone Links', 'kicker': 'Opinionator', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T14:07:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e731ddc-2939-5c40-92ff-dc907e3b6868', 'word_count': 181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e731ddc-2939-5c40-92ff-dc907e3b6868'}, {'abstract': 'The first batch of readers’ responses to some of the shows Tuesday night at CMJ Music Marathon, collected through Storify.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/cmj-reader-reviews-day-1-titus-andronicus-wild-flag-and-radical-dads/', 'snippet': 'The first batch of readers’ responses to some of the shows Tuesday night at CMJ Music Marathon, collected through Storify.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Critics from The New York Times will be squashed into many of the city’s clubs during the CMJ Music Marathon alongside other music fans and chroniclers. But we can’t be everywhere. This year, we’ve invited readers to join us in appraising CMJ shows on Twitter. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headli