rd books for the fall season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/books/review/this-seasons-board-books.html', 'snippet': 'Nine new board books for the fall season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHARLEY HARPER’S COLORS\xa0\xa0 By Charley Harper\xa0\xa0\xa0 24 pp. AMMO. $9.95.\xa0\xa0\xa0 (Board book; ages 1 to 3)\xa0\xa0\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/books/review/19childrenboard1SUB/19childrenboard1SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 308, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/19/books/review/19childrenboard1SUB/19childrenboard1SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 308}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/books/review/19childrenboard1SUB/19childrenboard1SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 526, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/19/books/review/19childrenboard1SUB/19childrenboard1SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/19/books/review/19childrenboard1SUB/19childrenboard1SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'This Season’s Board Books', 'kicker': "Children's Books | Board Book Roundup", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T16:28:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pamela Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pamela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/380de86e-4a1f-52cf-9d2b-4cf40f2b5ad7', 'word_count': 1000, 'uri': 'nyt://article/380de86e-4a1f-52cf-9d2b-4cf40f2b5ad7'}, {'abstract': 'Tom Kerridge is cooking some superb British food in his Michelin-starred gastropub.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/eurofile-britains-first-two-star-pub/', 'snippet': 'Tom Kerridge is cooking some superb British food in his Michelin-starred gastropub.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I do hope the food isn’t all fussy,” said my friend Edwina as we drove out of London on a beautiful Indian summer afternoon for Sunday lunch at the Hand and Flowers pub in Marlow, which is midway between Maidenhead and Henley-on-Thames in Buckinghamshire. Her slightly grumpy apprehension derived from the fact that Hand and Flowers had just received two Michelin stars, a first for any British pub. “I do loathe the term ‘gastropub,’” she added. “I bet this place will be full of horrid people wearing new Barbour jackets and Wellies, and don’t forget we must be home by 5 p.m.” Now lost in a sea of red wine was last night’s admission that she had a date with her married Spanish airline pilot.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Eurofile | Britain’s First Two-Star Pub', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Michelin Group', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T16:28:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexander Lobrano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexander', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lobrano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/faece4a6-a392-539c-8962-1ef504fcc4a2', 'word_count': 619, 'uri': 'nyt://article/faece4a6-a392-539c-8962-1ef504fcc4a2'}, {'abstract': 'Unemployment benefits are a necessary lifeline for people who lose their jobs, but — especially in a tough economy — they can also create big expenses for companies that are already struggling. Fortunately, there are ways to control the costs.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/are-you-managing-your-unemployment-insurance/', 'snippet': 'Unemployment benefits are a necessary lifeline for people who lose their jobs, but — especially in a tough economy — they can also create big expenses for companies that are already struggling. Fortunately, there are ways to control the costs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every Nov. 30, the State of Illinois sends out a statement that tells businesses what rate they will pay in the coming year for unemployment insurance. I view the rate as one indication of how I did this year as a manager. This time around, I’m anticipating a gift, because we had no unemployment claims this year, and I expect the rate to go down. In a bad year, the statement feels more like a reprimand, one that comes with a bill attached.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are You Managing Your Unemployment Insurance?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T16:29:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By JAY GOLTZ', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'GOLTZ', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5088ffcf-dd59-5a8c-8f7a-0d7778464f36', 'word_count': 1030, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5088ffcf-dd59-5a8c-8f7a-0d7778464f36'}, {'abstract': 'The World Series is a great event, but it is not a great culinary event.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/treading-lightly-beyond-peanuts-and-cracker-jack/', 'snippet': 'The World Series is a great event, but it is not a great culinary event.', 'lead_paragraph': 'All through the baseball playoffs, Mike Tanier will provide the Bats blog with daily updates on less-than-important developments from around the league.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Treading Lightly Beyond Peanuts and Cracker Jack', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Series', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T16:31:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Tanier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f472af3-a594-5ef5-9cb3-47dffbec6ecc', 'word_count': 949, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f472af3-a594-5ef5-9cb3-47dffbec6ecc'}, {'abstract': 'Public opinion on the movement is mixed. How reliable are polls in assessing patterns of human behavior?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/is-occupy-wall-street-being-overhyped/', 'snippet': 'Public opinion on the movement is mixed. How reliable are polls in assessing patterns of human behavior?', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Brooks: Gail, can we talk about the overhyping of the Occupy Wall Street movement?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Occupy Wall Street Being Overhyped?', 'kicker': 'Opinionator', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T16:34:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks and Gail Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Gail', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2022d15-37c4-536a-b53d-10572db755f9', 'word_count': 1086, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2022d15-37c4-536a-b53d-10572db755f9'}, {'abstract': 'An environmental activist describes his shifts on nuclear power and genetically modified crops.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/mark-lynas-explains-shifts-on-biotechnology-and-nuclear-power/', 'snippet': 'An environmental activist describes his shifts on nuclear power and genetically modified crops.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Keith Kloor has a fascinating interview with the British environmental writer and activist Mark Lynas on the Yale Environment 360 Web site. The exchange centers on Lynas’s new book, “The God Species: Saving the Planet in the Age of Humans,”\xa0and particularly on his conclusion that some technologies that have been anathema to environmentalists are vital to a smooth human journey in this century — particularly nuclear power and crops developed using biotechnology. Also notable is Lynas’s revised view of the “skeptical environmentalist” Bjorn Lomborg, whose face he once plastered with a pie. I encourage you to read it and weigh in here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Environmentalist Explains Shifts on Biotechnology and Nuclear Power', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetic Engineering', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T16:35:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a21938c6-79ea-5084-b7a4-48ca107f4b5a', 'word_count': 368, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a21938c6-79ea-5084-b7a4-48ca107f4b5a'}, {'abstract': 'UIST, pronounced “wist,” stands for User Interface Software and Technology. It’s the name of a research conference going on this week in Santa Barbara that showcases what’s going on among computer interface researchers at top computer science departments — MIT and Stanford are a major presence — and at companies like Google, Disney and Autodesk. Standing out among the presented research, and especially relevant to news organizations, are the emerging interfaces for crowdsourcing.', 'web_url': 'https://open.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/uist-2011-crowdsourcing-research/', 'snippet': 'UIST, pronounced “wist,” stands for User Interface Software and Technology. It’s the name of a research conference going on this week in Santa Barbara that showcases what’s going on among computer interface researchers at top computer science depa...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kyle Murray, a doctoral student in Computer Science at the University of Rochester, prepares to demo Legion, a crowdsourcing application toolkit.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'UIST 2011: Crowdsourcing Research', 'kicker': 'Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T16:46:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Stenger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stenger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/367206de-e8e4-5994-a419-6918d9bdc8e8', 'word_count': 786, 'uri': 'nyt://article/367206de-e8e4-5994-a419-6918d9bdc8e8'}, {'abstract': 'On Twitter, protesters in the Occupy movement explain what motivated them to join.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/why-occupy/', 'snippet': 'On Twitter, protesters in the Occupy movement explain what motivated them to join.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An article by Marc Lacey in Tuesday’s New York Times looked at the many different issues motivating protesters in America and abroad to join the growing Occupy Wall Street movement. Reporting on protesters around the country, Mr. Lacey found that not every group or individual that has embraced Occupy marches for the same reasons. Though many protesters seem united in feeling that a system designed to benefit the rich and connected is not working in their favor, many are also more concerned with specific challenges affecting their local communities, such as education and the environment. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Occupy?', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-19T16:49:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4125775f-2f84-53c6-96c8-ebe223773773', 'word_count': 148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4125775f-2f84-53c6-96c8-ebe223773773'}, {'abstract': 'The LeMay Museum in Tacoma, Wash., is scheduled to reopen in June 2012 in a new, 165,000-square-foot space.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/19/americas-car-museum-takes-shape-in-tacoma/', 'snippet': 'The LeMay Museum in Tacoma, Wash., is scheduled to reopen in June 2012 in a new, 165,000-square-foot space.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To hear David Madeira tell it, building a 165,000-square-foot, four-story museum to house the breadth of American automotive history is a solemn task and a mathematical quandary.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'America’s Car Museum Takes Shape in Tacoma', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antique and Classic Cars', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collectors and Collections', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N