: 'organizations', 'value': 'Haqqani Network', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T00:50:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Perlez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/58d640bd-02a2-598b-9370-0ff11b671ca3', 'word_count': 1145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58d640bd-02a2-598b-9370-0ff11b671ca3'}, {'abstract': 'The pain from Alabama’s harsh anti-immigration law isn’t felt just by the undocumented. Native Alabamans are paying a high price as well.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/opinion/its-what-they-asked-for.html', 'snippet': 'The pain from Alabama’s harsh anti-immigration law isn’t felt just by the undocumented. Native Alabamans are paying a high price as well.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alabama’s new anti-immigrant law, the nation’s harshest, went into effect last month (a few provisions have been temporarily blocked in federal court), and it is already reaping a bitter harvest of dislocation and fear. Hispanic homes are emptying, businesses are closing, employers are wondering where their workers have gone. Parents who have not yet figured out where to go are lying low and keeping children home from school. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s What They Asked For', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It’s What They Asked For', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Migrant and Foreign Workers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hispanic-Americans', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T00:52:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2899e563-1c93-5194-8c09-d984115da8e5', 'word_count': 526, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2899e563-1c93-5194-8c09-d984115da8e5'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama’s jobs council shouldn’t be advocating for special interests by asking Congress to end safeguards of the Sarbanes-Oxley Act.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/opinion/not-their-job.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama’s jobs council shouldn’t be advocating for special interests by asking Congress to end safeguards of the Sarbanes-Oxley Act.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama’s Council on Jobs and Competitiveness has some promising job growth ideas, including ways to speed up projects in energy and infrastructure. But the 27-member council — made up mostly of corporate executives, along with a handful of investors, labor leaders and academics — has indulged in outrageous special pleading, with its assault on Sarbanes-Oxley, the antifraud law passed in 2002 in response to Enron, WorldCom and the dot-com bust.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Not Their Job', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Not Their Job', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sarbanes-Oxley Act', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Coun