s', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T01:05:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c493c226-e639-5a96-a829-e7758ea9a55e', 'word_count': 511, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c493c226-e639-5a96-a829-e7758ea9a55e'}, {'abstract': 'There is much to be done in autumn, and yet the temptation is to just sit in the sun and listen to the hickory nuts falling.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/opinion/hastening-slowly-through-fall.html', 'snippet': 'There is much to be done in autumn, and yet the temptation is to just sit in the sun and listen to the hickory nuts falling.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Caterpillars have begun to appear outside my mudroom door. They look barely animate and thickly pelted, like moss creeping up the door frame in individual inches. I wonder about the feel of caterpillar fur, but some natural caution keeps me from touching them. They are a great improvement over the slugs and the millipedes that crept up the door all summer long. The slugs looked like thick, muddy drops of gelatinous rain. There is no analogy for the millipedes. I pretend not to know they are there.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hastening Slowly Through Fall', 'kicker': 'Editorial | The Rural Life', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hastening Slowly Through Fall', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insects', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seasons and Months', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rural Areas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T01:09:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Verlyn Klinkenborg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Verlyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klinkenborg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/bca3ed8d-7fe3-544a-bc2b-cbc4a304ad1c', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bca3ed8d-7fe3-544a-bc2b-cbc4a304ad1c'}, {'abstract': 'Many Republican strategists see recent remarks having a potentially negative impact in swing states with many Hispanic voters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/us/politics/immigration-talk-turns-off-some-hispanics.html', 'snippet': 'Many Republican strategists see recent remarks having a potentially negative impact in swing states with many Hispanic voters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today, Republican candidates are competing over who can talk the toughest about illegal immigration — who will erect the most impenetrable border defense; who will turn off “magnets” like college tuition benefits. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/20/us/IMMIG/IMMIG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/20/us/IMMIG/IMMIG-articleLarge.jpg',