: 'subject', 'value': 'Neck', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Protective Clothing', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T18:03:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By SAM BORDEN&nbsp;', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/653ff0fd-61b2-50bc-9394-94bf51bd2087', 'word_count': 1034, 'uri': 'nyt://article/653ff0fd-61b2-50bc-9394-94bf51bd2087'}, {'abstract': 'Leonard Cohen, the celebrated songwriter, dropped the news that he will release his first album since 2004 sometime next year.  The album, to be called “Old Ideas,” has been recorded and will contain 10 new songs.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/leonard-cohen-to-release-a-new-album-next-year/', 'snippet': 'Leonard Cohen, the celebrated songwriter, dropped the news that he will release his first album since 2004 sometime next year.  The album, to be called “Old Ideas,” has been recorded and will contain 10 new songs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Leonard Cohen, the celebrated songwriter, dropped the news that he will release his first album since 2004 sometime next year, Reuters reported. Mr. Cohen, in Spain to receive a literary award, said the album has already been recorded and contains 10 unreleased songs. It will be called “Old Ideas.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Leonard Cohen to Release a New Album Next Year', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohen, Leonard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T18:04:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cd6c01d-fe79-5f5b-a9be-adb1e643c0aa', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3cd6c01d-fe79-5f5b-a9be-adb1e643c0aa'}, {'abstract': 'Not now, maybe never.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/dirty-jobs/', 'snippet': 'Not now, maybe never.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It appears that Republican claims that they can create lots of jobs by weakening environmental regulation are keying off a study from the American Petroleum Institute (pdf). There are a lot of things to critique about this study, including the fact that it actually shows a relatively small number of energy jobs, which are then blown up by assuming a large multiplier. Funny how spending can only move money around, not increase total spending, if it comes from the government, and how the multiplier is a nonsense concept when applied to government spending, but totally valid when it involves oil companies …', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dirty Jobs', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T18:06:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f93f6347-0b73-514d-bc35-b855666d9030', 'word_count': 165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f93f6347-0b73-514d-bc35-b855666d9030'}, {'abstract': 'Joseph J. Lhota, who is known for his expertise in budgets and finance, has little background in transit.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/lhota-former-deputy-mayor-under-giuliani-is-named-to-head-m-t-a/', 'snippet': 'Joseph J. Lhota, who is known for his expertise in budgets and finance, has little background in transit.', 'lead_paragraph': '11:20 p.m. | Updated It is one of the more momentous tasks facing the Cuomo administration: turning around New York City’s ailing mass transit system, the largest in the country.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cuomo Picks Political Veteran to Lead M.T.A.', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lhota, Joseph J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Transportation Authority', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T18:11:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c94eeef-88b6-50d2-bfc0-d50dbba197b7', 'word_count': 980, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c94eeef-88b6-50d2-bfc0-d50dbba197b7'}, {'abstract': 'I’ll be around.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/i-like-to-watch/', 'snippet': 'I’ll be around.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since someone will probably notice: if you see a guy who looks like Tom Friedman, or maybe an employee of New Jersey Transit, in the Wall Street area later today, it might be me. I’m going to observe, not to participate, which as I’ve explained is out of bounds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'I Like To Watch', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T18:20:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5f54cb2-aac2-530d-a325-7b4dbacf1e20', 'word_count': 55, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5f54cb2-aac2-530d-a325-7b4dbacf1e20'}, {'abstract': 'In the third quarter, Abbott’s Niaspan failed to show the double-digit sales increases that the drug had previously generated.', 'web_url': 'https://prescriptions.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/cholesterol-drug-sales-slip-after-study/', 'snippet': 'In the third quarter, Abbott’s Niaspan failed to show the double-digit sales increases that the drug had previously generated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New Abbott sales data shows that physicians are reconsidering whether to prescribe a drug used to raise levels of good cholesterol after a study earlier this year questioned whether it reduced the risk of heart attacks.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cholesterol Drug Sales Slip After Study', 'kicker': 'Prescriptions', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cholesterol', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Institutes of Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T18:24:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Japsen', 'person': [{'firstname