lue': 'Murdoch, James R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Watson, Tom', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEWS CORP', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T19:47:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Chozick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65f821ea-14df-5006-9a17-cbf95bc10e3f', 'word_count': 432, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65f821ea-14df-5006-9a17-cbf95bc10e3f'}, {'abstract': 'A simple question from the jury strikes at the heart of the case against John F. Haggerty Jr.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/in-trial-of-political-consultant-jury-asks-who-is-the-victim/', 'snippet': 'A simple question from the jury strikes at the heart of the case against John F. Haggerty Jr.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As most lawyers would concede, trying to distill a deeper meaning from jurors’ questions during deliberations can be a futile exercise.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Trial of Political Consultant, Jury Asks, Who Is the Victim?', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haggerty, John F Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T19:50:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Eligon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eligon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9960ca23-e38f-5776-8a1c-82b250731733', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9960ca23-e38f-5776-8a1c-82b250731733'}, {'abstract': 'An army’s tools often long outlast an army’s creators, and can become instruments for forces those who handed out the weapons did not foresee.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/afghan-gun-locker-battlefield-ingenuity-and-a-weapons-longevity/', 'snippet': 'An army’s tools often long outlast an army’s creators, and can become instruments for forces those who handed out the weapons did not foresee.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This year, after Western journalists poured into Libya as the civilian population took up arms and lunged into war, a line of coverage on makeshift arms production and distribution found its way into many newspapers, magazines and Web sites. The New York Times was part of this, with coverage on the anti-Qaddafi forces’ hasty acquisition, production and fielding of all manner of repurposed arms and the vehicles to carry them. Often these weapons were poorly used. Sometimes they were fired just as indiscriminately as the weapons fired by Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi’s troops that NATO, in its United Nations-approved bid to protect civilians, claimed to marshal international will to stop. Some of these weapons were also instrumental in the anti-Qaddafi militias’ eventual success, as was visible in Misurata. (For a fuller view of many of these themes, read this report on the hidden plant in Misurata, or these explainers on the dangerous repurposing of 57-mm air-to-ground rockets or 122-millimeter Grad rockets or, in this case, the knuckleheaded firing of plainly labeled inert rockets toward Qaddafi forces in Libya’s mountainous west.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Afghan Gun Locker: Battlefield Ingenuity, and a Weapon’s Longevity', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Firearms', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Army', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T19:50:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By C.J. Chivers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Chivers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73763923-bf6a-592e-978a-ca85eaeac268', 'word_count': 1079, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73763923-bf6a-592e-978a-ca85eaeac268'}, {'abstract': 'After a $78 million restoration the Kiel Opera House reopened on Oct. 1 as the Peabody Opera House.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/opera-house-lights-shine-again-in-st-louis/', 'snippet': 'After a $78 million restoration the Kiel Opera House reopened on Oct. 1 as the Peabody Opera House.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The lights at the Kiel Opera House in St. Louis went dark after a philharmonic performance in 1991. Unused for years, the theater reopened on Oct. 1 as the Peabody Opera House, after undergoing a $78 million restoration that began in July 2010. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Opera House Lights Shine Again in St. Louis', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Radio City Music Hall', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'St Louis (Mo)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T19:52:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By MONICA FORRESTALL', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'FORRESTALL', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/135aeb2c-f2a3-5f5a-a72f-229fc3b4c911', 'word_count': 201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/135aeb2c-f2a3-5f5a-a72f-229fc3b4c911'}, {'abstract': 'Gary Lehman is said to be “physically exhausted.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/manager-cites-virus-as-reason-for-stars-withdrawal-from-mets-siegfried/', 'snippet': 'Gary Lehman is said to be “physically exhausted.”', 'lead_paragraph': ' The illness that has forced has forced the tenor Gary Lehman to withdraw from the title role in the Metropolitan Opera’s production of “Siegfried,” the third installment in the company’s new “Ring” cycle, is an energy-sapping virus, his manager said on Thursday. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Manager Cites Virus as Reason for Star’s Withdrawal from Met’s ‘Siegfried’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Heppner, Ben', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wagner, Richard', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'San Francisco Opera', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T20:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/733d664d-840c-5fef-9ae9-a6f69c708dea', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/733d664d-840c-5fef-9ae9-a6f69c708dea'}, {'abstract': 'In “Boss,” a new Starz series, Kelsey Grammer plays a Chicago mayor who controls everything but the melodrama.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/arts/television/boss-with-kelsey-grammer-on-starz-review.html', 'snippet': 'In “Boss,” a new Starz series, Kelsey Grammer plays a Chicago mayor who controls everything but the melodrama.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Boss,” a series on Starz about a crooked Chicago mayor, is almost good, and it falls short for the same reason that the George Clooney movie “Ides of March” isn’t good enough. Both are political thrillers that romanticize malfeasance, imbuing corruption with a sinister melodrama that defies common sense and cheapens the thrill of bad behavior.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/21/arts/21boss-span/21boss-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/21/arts/21boss-span/21boss-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 359}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'ca