10/21/business/global/criticism-of-spains-central-bank-grows-along-with-chance-of-recession.html', 'snippet': 'The Bank of Spain is facing accusations of mishandling problems in the banking sector and being too slow to address the collapse in the real estate market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MADRID — The Bank of Spain was seen as a beacon of rigor and caution at the start of the financial crisis. But now that the Spanish economy is facing the possibility of slipping back into recession, it is facing accusations of mishandling problems in the banking sector and being too slow to address the collapse in the real estate market. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As Spain Faces a Possible Recession, Criticism of Its Central Bank Is Growing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Spain Faces a Possible Recession, Criticism of Its Central Bank Is Growing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T20:17:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Raphael Minder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Raphael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Minder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9e7d030-a476-5f17-b223-1c7112507ca6', 'word_count': 1191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9e7d030-a476-5f17-b223-1c7112507ca6'}, {'abstract': 'A new exhibition in Boston explores the unstinting, even cruel naturalism Degas brought to the female form.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/arts/design/degas-and-the-nude-at-museum-of-fine-arts-boston-review.html', 'snippet': 'A new exhibition in Boston explores the unstinting, even cruel naturalism Degas brought to the female form.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — What’s the difference between “nude” and “naked”? “Degas and the Nude,” at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, answers that question once and for all.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/21/arts/21degas-span/21degas-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 343, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/21/arts/21degas-span/21degas-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 343}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/21/arts/21degas-span/DEGAS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 585, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/21/arts/21degas-span/21degas-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/21/arts/21degas-span/21degas-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Unblushing Career of Undressing Women', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Unblushing Career of Undressing Women', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Degas, Edgar', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Fine Arts (Boston)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T20:19:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e25bb920-6833-5ed5-8466-e31855e8f3c4', 'word_count': 1170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e25bb920-6833-5ed5-8466-e31855e8f3c4'}, {'abstract': 'The killing of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi resonates in the presidential race.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/romney-praises-qaddafis-demise/', 'snippet': 'The killing of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi resonates in the presidential race.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While campaigning in Iowa, Mitt Romney was asked Thursday in a radio interview with KSCJ in Sioux City about the news that Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi, the former Libyan leader, had been killed in his hometown of Surt, and he praised the development ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney Credits Libyan People for Qaddafi’s Demise', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T20:20:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7a47a7e-72b5-5463-bc74-34cc7d2ccc7c', 'word_count': 240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7a47a7e-72b5-5463-bc74-34cc7d2ccc7c'}, {'abstract': 'Over the past year, the city has added just 40,100 jobs in the private sector, for a slower rate of growth than the rest of the state and the nation since September 2010.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/citys-unemployment-rate-stays-at-8-7/', 'snippet': 'Over the past year, the city has added just 40,100 jobs in the private sector, for a slower rate of growth than the rest of the state and the nation since September 2010.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even with the return of more than 8,000 Verizon workers who had been striking, New York City did not add enough jobs last month to lower the unemployment rate, which remained at 8.7 percent, the State Department of Labor reported on Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'City’s Unemployment Rate Stays at 8.7%', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T20:24:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGeehan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGeehan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed5ed95c-2d69-54ac-bd5c-882818d75e88', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed5ed95c-2d69-54ac-bd5c-882818d75e88'}, {'abstract': 'All the cemetery’s a stage at Brooklyn’s historic Green-Wood, which will be the scene of a dance tour this weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/arts/at-green-wood-cemetery-dancers-celebrate-life.html', 'snippet': 'All the cemetery’s a stage at Brooklyn’s historic Green-Wood, which will be the scene of a dance tour this weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some might think a cemetery an odd setting for a celebration of life. That was certainly the reaction of HoSun Kang, assistant director and vice president of the Korean Traditional Music and Dance Institute, who said she hesitated when she was first approached to participate in “Dancing Through the Light: An Autumn Celebration,” a tour of Green-Wood Cemetery that opens this weekend. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/21/arts/21MISER/21MISER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 574, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/21/arts/21MISER/21MISER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/21/arts/21MISER/21MISER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Miser', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Weekend Miser', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Green-Wood Cemetery (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T20:25:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/791d9481-76bf-5b41-b2e5-8bb93e1f4b30', 'word_count': 474, 'uri': 'nyt://article/791d9481-76bf-5b41-b2e5-8bb93e1f4b30'}, {'abstract': 'Sanford Biggers mixes mediums including sound and video, sculpture and light, to express his messages.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/arts/design/sanford-biggerss-art-in-a-two-borough-exhibition.html', 'snippet': 'Sanford Biggers mixes mediums including sound and video, sculpture and light, to express his messages.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A squat, leafy tree, its trunk too broad to wrap your arms around, rises from a round patch of dirt at the center of the Brooklyn Museum’s fifth floor rotunda. Many years ago, apparently, it grew up through the body of a baby-grand piano, and now the tree is beginning to swallow the instrument the way farmland trees do the strands of wire fences. Miraculously the piano, tilted and, you would have thought, ruined, intermittently plays a mournful tune, its keys going up and down as if stroked by a poltergeist. The tune? “Strange Fruit,”  the elegiac ode to black victims of lynching made famous by Billie Holiday. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/21/arts/21biggers/BIGGERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/21/arts/21biggers/21biggers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/21/arts/21biggers/21biggers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Smile and Metaphor, Crossing Borough Lines', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Smile and Metaphor, Crossing Borough Lines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SculptureCenter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T20:25:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {