nergy efficiency seriously despite a weak economy and budget constraints. Massachusetts ranked first.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Massachusetts edges out California for first place in the annual rankings from the Council for an Energy-Efficient Economy. California ranks second, New York is third, and Oregon, Washington, Vermont and Rhode Island tie for fourth place. [The Los Angeles Times]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: Massachusetts Leads Efficiency Rankings', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T21:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b14aaac8-c6d4-52c5-9ab2-d1f326f4d5cd', 'word_count': 150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b14aaac8-c6d4-52c5-9ab2-d1f326f4d5cd'}, {'abstract': 'Ariel Schulman and Henry Joost talk about their techniques in directing the third installment of the ghosts-on-video franchise.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/ghosts-go-retro-in-paranormal-activity-3/', 'snippet': 'Ariel Schulman and Henry Joost talk about their techniques in directing the third installment of the ghosts-on-video franchise.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Spoiler Alert: In “Paranormal Activity 3,” cameras are set up inside a house, doors mysteriously slam shut, bed sheets mysteriously move and things mysteriously go bump in the night.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ghosts Go Retro in ‘Paranormal Activity 3’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T21:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7f9eb6c-d399-50ab-8bb1-7ea1eaeef56e', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7f9eb6c-d399-50ab-8bb1-7ea1eaeef56e'}, {'abstract': 'An oversight agency identified problems in Deloitte & Touche audits in 2007, but because of confidentiality requirements, the failings didn’t become public until 41 months later.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/business/deloittes-failings-revealed-but-only-after-3-years.html', 'snippet': 'An oversight agency identified problems in Deloitte & Touche audits in 2007, but because of confidentiality requirements, the failings didn’t become public until 41 months later.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With hindsight, we now know that auditors in 2007 should have been looking carefully at bank books.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/04/19/business/norris-75-standardinline/norris-75-standardinline-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2010/04/19/business/norris-75-standardinline/norris-75-standardinline-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Audit Flaws Revealed, at Long Last', 'kicker': 'High & Low Finance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Audit Flaws Revealed, At Long Last', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Company Accounting Oversight Board', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Deloitte & Touche', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sarbanes-Oxley Act', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACCOUNTING AND ACCOUNTANTS', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T21:01:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c531a31-c0cf-5e44-b77b-c4eed396f1fc', 'word_count': 1307, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c531a31-c0cf-5e44-b77b-c4eed396f1fc'}, {'abstract': 'David Henry Hwang’s new play, “Chinglish,” explores the language and cultural gaps between Chinese and Americans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/theater/david-henry-hwangs-chinglish.html', 'snippet': 'David Henry Hwang’s new play, “Chinglish,” explores the language and cultural gaps between Chinese and Americans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE playwright David Henry Hwang has been in high demand in China in recent years — not for works like his Tony Award-winning “M. Butterfly,” which is essentially banned there, but because of his standing as the only son of Asia to be represented regularly on Broadway. In 2005 he was invited to Shanghai to discuss Broadway-style shows that might fill the lavish cultural centers under construction across the country. Yet China’s appreciation for status symbols like Broadway and Mr. Hwang did not translate into understanding more mundane aspects of American life.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/arts/23HWANG1_SPAN/23HWANG1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 343, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/23/arts/23HWANG1_SPAN/23HWANG1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 343}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/arts/23HWANG1_SPAN/23HWANG1_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 605, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/arts/23HWANG1_SPAN/23HWANG1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/arts/23HWANG1_SPAN/23HWANG1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Do You Know What I Mean? Probably Not', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Do You Know What I Mean? Probably Not', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hwang, David Henry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chinese Language', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T21:01:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc300d1c-220e-544b-a8f5-5a09cc68cec0', 'word_count': 1446, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc300d1c-220e-544b-a8f5-5a09cc68cec0'}, {'abstract': 'A pupil of both Philip Crangi and Monique Péan starts her own jewelry line.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/a-new-jewel-in-town/', 'snippet': 'A pupil of both Philip Crangi and Monique Péan starts her own jewelry line.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eva Zuckerman, the designer of Eva Fehren, a new fine jewelry line, first started tinkering with jewelry while working for Mor Mor Rita, a high-end children’s brand. “Women actually bought my stuff!” exclaims Zuckerman, who put her Cooper Union fine arts background on hold for the opportunity to work for the jewelry designers Philip Crangi and, later, Monique Péan. “They’re such wonderful teachers,” she says, crediting both mentors with instilling in her the importance of sustainable gems (like petrified wood and lapis lazuli stones) and innovative craftsmanship (seen in her delicate gold stackable bands and geometric rings and bracelets). And from the looks of her debut effort, this promising pupil gets a gold star.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A New Jewel in Town', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T21:01:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bbf0978-5cd9-582c-b6cc-b66644b65839', 'word_count': 126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bbf0978-5cd9-582c-b6cc-b66644b65839'}, {'abstract': 'Two aspects of Andy Warhol’s art are on display in unalike but complementary shows in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/10/21/arts/20111021-WARHOL.html', 'snippet': 'Two aspects of Andy Warhol’s art are on display in unalike but complementary shows in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two aspects of Andy Warhol’s art are on display in unalike but complementary s