f BlackBerry phones, it did make one announcement at a software conference. The new operating system for those phones will be named BBX.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'RIM Faces a Naming Problem', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trademarks and Trade Names', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Research In Motion Ltd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T21:37:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Austen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c21620c5-e668-5ea4-9b9c-4dcdc1f27a27', 'word_count': 278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c21620c5-e668-5ea4-9b9c-4dcdc1f27a27'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers have found that how we use (or don’t use) Facebook may reflect actual differences in brain structure.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/20/does-facebook-change-your-brain/', 'snippet': 'Researchers have found that how we use (or don’t use) Facebook may reflect actual differences in brain structure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Those of you who responded to my female friendship post by diagnosing me with a whopper case of introversion were right on the mark: in fact, my neighbor, who specializes in such things, once told me that I was the most introverted person he had ever met.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Does Facebook Change Your Brain?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-20T21:38:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Warner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Warner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29a5e3e3-06fe-540f-b6b3-843cdc94470c', 'word_count': 412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29a5e3e3-06fe-540f-b6b3-843cdc94470c'}, {'abstract': 'When medical information is T.M.I., and New Yorkers in lines.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/fashion/of-course-shes-worried-social-qs.html', 'snippet': 'When medical information is T.M.I., and New Yorkers in lines.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My husband’s closest friends have three children, each with a separate behavioral diagnosis (A.D.H.D., Asperger’s, dyslexia). The mother spends a tremendous amount of time researching and navigating this world. Unfortunately, it’s all she talks about, in a stream of medical jargon. Worse, she’s constantly relating their diagnoses to my children’s behavior. “Are you worried he’s on the spectrum?” she’ll ask, when my 4-year-old spins in the backyard with a bubble wand. How should I deal with this? Stephanie, Boston', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923