mp which Mr. Edison regards as a crowning triumph is a model of simplicity and economy,” he said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oct. 21, 1879 | Thomas Edison Lights the Lamp', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T08:20:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd5ce29f-9fae-504c-ae7d-095f2621b375', 'word_count': 300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd5ce29f-9fae-504c-ae7d-095f2621b375'}, {'abstract': 'The Farm Security Administration sent Jack Delano on a side trip to Puerto Rico. A visit of “a few days” turned into a life’s work.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/a-masterwork-spanning-40-years-and-one-island/', 'snippet': 'The Farm Security Administration sent Jack Delano on a side trip to Puerto Rico. A visit of “a few days” turned into a life’s work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is the second of two pieces on the work of Jack Delano. Part One is here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Masterwork Spanning 40 Years and One Island', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T09:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Gonzalez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonzalez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ede1fcb-92de-55bf-94aa-bf14bc99d5b8', 'word_count': 978, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ede1fcb-92de-55bf-94aa-bf14bc99d5b8'}, {'abstract': 'During Sam Anderson’s trip to Japan for his interview with the novelist Haruki Murakami, he visited the places mentioned in Murakami’s fiction as well as sites significant to Murakami’s life and career.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/10/23/magazine/20Mag-Murakami-Tokyo.html', 'snippet': 'During Sam Anderson’s trip to Japan for his interview with the novelist Haruki Murakami, he visited the places mentioned in Murakami’s fiction as well as sites significant to Murakami’s life and career.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During Sam Anderson’s trip to Japan for his interview with the novelist Haruki Murakami, he visited the places mentioned in Murakami’s fiction as well as sites significant to Murakami’s life and career.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Murakami’s Tokyo', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murakami, Haruki', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tokyo (Japan)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T09:01:03+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/4560452c-0d0f-5844-81cf-e89d9c9c0972', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/4560452c-0d0f-5844-81cf-e89d9c9c0972'}, {'abstract': 'The novelist has become a global imaginative force by grappling with what’s contained below the surface — of Japan and of himself.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/magazine/the-fierce-imagination-of-haruki-murakami.html', 'snippet': 'The novelist has become a global imaginative force by grappling with what’s contained below the surface — of Japan and of himself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I prepared for my first-ever trip to Japan, this summer, almost entirely by immersing myself in the work of Haruki Murakami. This turned out to be a horrible idea. Under the influence of Murakami, I arrived in Tokyo expecting Barcelona or Paris or Berlin — a cosmopolitan world capital whose straight-talking citizens were fluent not only in English but also in all the nooks and crannies of Western culture: jazz, theater, literature, sitcoms, film noir, opera, rock ’n’ roll. But this, as really anyone else in the world could have told you, is not what Japan is like at all. Japan — real, actual, visitable Japan — turned out to be intensely, inflexibly, unapologetically Japanese.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23murakami1_span/23murakami1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 478, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23murakami1_span/23murakami1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 478}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23murakami1_span/23murakami1_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 811, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23murakami1_span/23murakami1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23murakami1_span/23murakami1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fierce Imagination of Haruki Murakami', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Underground Man', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japanese Language', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murakami, Haruki', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Translation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T09:02:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2c6cd03-2178-50b5-ad65-ead92913c217', 'word_count': 5855, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2c6cd03-2178-50b5-ad65-ead92913c217'}, {'abstract': 'Can you choose the words that best complete the sentences?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/test-yourself-english-oct-21-2011/', 'snippet': 'Can you choose the words that best complete the sentences?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The question was written by Shannon Doyne. It comes from the movie review, “A Splintering Psyche or Omens of Disaster?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | English, Oct. 21, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T09:05:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/332889f5-5400-517b-a86a-9f80787d13de', 'word_count': 47, 'uri': 'nyt://article/332889f5-5400-517b-a86a-9f80787d13de'}, {'abstract': '6 Q’s About the News | Why is Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi’s death significant?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/qaddafi-reported-dead-by-libyan-officials/', 'snippet': '6 Q’s About the News | Why is Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi’s death significant?', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHERE was Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi killed?WHERE did the rebellion against Colonel Qaddafi began last February?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Qaddafi Is Dead in Libya', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T09:16:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca Ruiz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ruiz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ed12b58-e1e9-59fe-8a6d-cb36a5742c2b', 'word_count': 98, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ed12b58-e1e9-59fe-8a6d-cb36a5742c2b'}, {'abstract': 'What kinds of activities do you tend to engage in on Facebook? How much time do you typically spend on the site?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/how-do-you-use-facebook/', 'snippet': 'What kinds of activities do you tend to engage in on Facebook? How much time do you typically spend on the site?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Research shows that for students, specific ways of using Facebook – not use of it in general – correlate with some real-life behaviors like participating in extracurricular activities and even with academic achievement. How do you tend to use Facebook, if at all? Do you think the ways you use it are reflected in other areas of your life?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Do You Use Facebook?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T09:20:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/84588d03-862e-5fb1-8d84-4962b29cbe41', 'word_count': 567, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84588d03-862e-5fb1-8d84-4962b29cbe41'}, {'abstract': 'In an interview at his home in Cambridge, Mass., Ezra F. Vogel discussed his book, “Deng Xiaoping and the Transformation of China.” Read edited excerpts from an interview with Mr. Vogel.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/revisiting-deng-xiaoping-a-word-with-ezra-f-vogel/', 'snippet': 'In an interview at his home in Cambridge, Mass., Ezra F. Vogel discussed his book, “Deng Xiaoping and the Transformation of China.” Read edited excerpts from an interview with Mr. Vogel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ezra F. Vogel, author of a new biography of Deng Xiaoping, retired from Harvard University in 2000, but still lives just a few hundred yards from the campus. In an interview at his home in Cambridge, Mass., he discussed his book, “Deng Xiaoping and the Transformation of China”; the differing views of Deng during his rule and now; and the differences between Chinese and American politics during the opening of China to the West. Mr. Vogel talked to The Times’s David Barboza. These are edited excerpts from their conversation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Revisiting Deng Xiaoping: A Word With Ezra F. Vogel', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chen Yi', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Deng Xiaoping', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hu Yaobang', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hua Guofeng', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jiang Zemin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mao Zedong', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zhang Hanzhi', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zhao Ziyang', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T10:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86ec45d7-7096-54a2-bbe8-b9cc0773da8d', 'word_count': 1231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86ec45d7-7096-54a2-bbe8-b9cc0773da8d'}, {'abstract': 'A new study assembled a sample of nearly 2,000 college students’ Facebook use and measured it against grades.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/fbook-grades/', 'snippet': 'A new study assembled a sample of nearly 2,000 college students’ Facebook use and measured it against grades.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How does Facebook activity affect a student’s grades? Reynol Junco, a professor at Lock Haven University in Pennsylvania, recently set out to determine exactly that.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Facebook’s Impact on Student Grades', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T10:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca R. Ruiz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Ruiz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f009220-08c7-5de7-ad04-3969e9f3263d', 'word_count': 394, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f009220-08c7-5de7-ad04-3969e9f3263d'}, {'abstract': 'The Tesla co-founder reflects on his portrayal in “Revenge of the Electric Car,” a documentary by the filmmaker Chris Paine opening in New York and Los Angeles on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/as-documentary-rolls-out-elon-musk-considers-his-revenge/', 'snippet': 'The Tesla co-founder reflects on his portrayal in “Revenge of the Electric Car,” a documentary by the filmmaker Chris Paine opening in New York and Los Angeles on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The director, Chris Paine, called me up one day and wanted to have lunch,” said Elon Musk, the Tesla Motors chief executive, in a recent telephone interview. He was discussing how he came to be featured prominently in Mr. Paine’s new film, “Revenge of the Electric Car,” which opens in New York and Los Angeles on Friday after having made the film festival rounds this year. A review of the film appeared in The Times on Friday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As Documentary Rolls Out, Elon Musk Considers His ‘Revenge’', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric and Hybrid Vehicles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ghosn, Carlos', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Musk, Elon', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chevrolet Division of General Motors Corp', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nissan Motor Co', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tesla Motors', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T10:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Motavalli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Motavalli', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9017d15-41e8-5a6c-a1c1-6f152189c348', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9017d15-41e8-5a6c-a1c1-6f152189c348'}, {'abstract': 'The Pitchfork Music Festival expands to Paris on Oct. 28 and 29.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/pitchfork-music-festival-comes-to-paris/', 'snippet': 'The Pitchfork Music Festival expands to Paris on Oct. 28 and 29.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since 2006, Pitchfork Media has supplemented its tastemaking music Web site with an annual music festival in Chicago. On Oct. 28 and 29, the festival will cross the Atlantic for its first full-fledged event outside the United States, importing North American, British and Scandinavian bands for French audiences, at the Pitchfork Music Festival Paris. (Though most tickets for the festival itself are sold out, they are still available for the opening and closing party events; organizers say additional tickets will be available for the nighttime, or “club,” sessions on the 28th.) ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pitchfork Music Festival Comes to Paris', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Festivals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pitchfork Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T10:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Moroz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moroz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9c01e3f-e7ed-528f-b538-860f01ff84ec', 'word_count': 299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9c01e3f-e7ed-528f-b538-860f01ff84ec'}, {'abstract': 'The best and most interesting teams have byes this weekend, but the games go on.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/week-7-matchups-when-the-middling-face-the-mediocre/', 'snippet': 'The best and most interesting teams have byes this weekend, but the games go on.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Colts (0-6) at Saints (4-2)Sunday, 8:20 p.m.Line: Saints by 14.The famous quote by the former N.F.L. commissioner Bert Bell — “On any given Sunday, any team in the N.F.L. can beat any other” — has been a source of reassurance for both fans and television programmers for decades. The league’s carefully maintained competitive balance almost guarantees that every week will feature several compelling games between high-quality, evenly matched opponents. Almost.Bell would never imagine a week like this, in which the best (the Patriots) and most fascinating (the Eagles, the 49ers) teams have byes, while the top contenders face pushovers and the mediocre slug it out with the middling. Matchups that looked good when the schedule was announced now merit double-digit point spreads. Case in point: the Colts vs. the Saints, a rematch of Super Bowl XLIV that promised to be a seesaw offensive battle, is now little more than a three-hour wake for Indianapolis and a chance to watch Sean Payton coach New Orleans from the booth instead of the sideline.Granted, Curtis Painter, like a frozen burrito under a filling station heat lamp, is slowly warming to the Colts’ starting quarterback job. His completion percentage has gone up every week, and the Colts offense no longer looks like an intramural team playing in a downpour. Also, Drew Brees offered hope that Payton might find a way to stand on, or slightly above, the field on the surgically repaired leg he broke in last week’s game. “I’m envisioning some kind of hovercraft on the sideline,” Brees said. Levitating coaches: another thing that Bert Bell could never imagine. Pick: Saints.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Week 7 Matchups: When the Middling Face the Mediocre', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T10:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Tanier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/531018b5-0981-5014-968b-c810a073af2a', 'word_count': 2121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/531018b5-0981-5014-968b-c810a073af2a'}, {'abstract': 'Investment in infrastructure construction and repair is an efficient way to create jobs, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/the-infrastructure-two-fer-jobs-now-and-future-growth/', 'snippet': 'Investment in infrastructure construction and repair is an efficient way to create jobs, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Laura D’Andrea Tyson is a professor at the Haas School of Business at the University of California, Berkeley, and served as chairwoman of the Council of Economic Advisers under President Clinton.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Infrastructure Twofer: Jobs Now and Future Growth', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infrastructure (Public Works)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T10:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': "By Laura D'Andrea Tyson", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': "D'andrea", 'lastname': 'Tyson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/264ed573-29fb-5d1c-942b-ec522d27d48a', 'word_count': 1272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/264ed573-29fb-5d1c-942b-ec522d27d48a'}, {'abstract': 'Three residents of 75 and 99 Ocean Avenue, adjacent buildings in Prospect-Lefferts Gardens, Brooklyn, talk about what it is like to live around fellow musicians.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2009/04/30/realestate/0503-cov-feature.html', 'snippet': 'Three residents of 75 and 99 Ocean Avenue, adjacent buildings in Prospect-Lefferts Gardens, Brooklyn, talk about what it is like to live around fellow musicians.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three residents of 75 and 99 Ocean Avenue, adjacent buildings in Prospect-Lefferts Gardens, Brooklyn, talk about what it is like to live around fellow musicians.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Musical Buildings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {