Why an epic collapse is the best thing that can happen to them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/magazine/the-thrill-of-defeat-for-sports-fans.html', 'snippet': 'Why an epic collapse is the best thing that can happen to them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It will provide little solace to wounded fans of the Boston Red Sox, who recently endured the most catastrophic late-season choke in baseball history, to know that their misery has been mathematically quantified: 278 million to 1. Those are the odds of the Red Sox’ blowing a nine-game lead (which they did) and missing the baseball playoffs entirely (which they did) in the precise fashion in which they did it, as calculated by Nate Silver in The Times.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23lede_span/23lede_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 339, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23lede_span/23lede_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 339}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23lede_span/mag-23Lede-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 744, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23lede_span/23lede_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23lede_span/23lede_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Thrill of Defeat for Sports Fans', 'kicker': 'First', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Thrill of Defeat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emotions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:07:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Sternbergh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sternbergh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/686f5b48-dfe8-549f-abf6-7db51aac1cbb', 'word_count': 1040, 'uri': 'nyt://article/686f5b48-dfe8-549f-abf6-7db51aac1cbb'}, {'abstract': 'What one middle-aged Clevelander understood about vinyl records that almost no one else did.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/magazine/you-are-here-building-a-house-of-wax-in-cleveland.html', 'snippet': 'What one middle-aged Clevelander understood about vinyl records that almost no one else did.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the heart of postindustrial Cleveland, in one of those cheery urban-reclamation areas with freshly painted brick buildings and almost unnaturally green lawn spaces, you’ll find Gotta Groove Records, one of around 20 plants in the United States that still press vinyl. On the day I visited, the guts of this converted elevator factory were packed with hissing, clanking vintage record presses, and the walls were lined with band fliers and colored LPs. A dry-erase board at the far end of the shop displayed the day’s jobs, Tom Waits and Tim Buckley among them.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23here_span/mag-23here-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg