 'Sneaking a peek; spiking the vote.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/magazine/in-flight-entertainment.html', 'snippet': 'Sneaking a peek; spiking the vote.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a recent flight, my colleague was seated next to an employee of our major competitor. My colleague realized this when the fellow began writing e-mails about possible problems with a significant new product. Is it ethical for us to capitalize on this fellow’s stupidity? B.C., NEW JERSEY', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In-Flight Entertainment', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In-Flight Entertainment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:08:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/32e05d5c-deab-5a25-8bb9-cdbdfda23ca6', 'word_count': 924, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32e05d5c-deab-5a25-8bb9-cdbdfda23ca6'}, {'abstract': 'After more than 30 years of war, Afghanistan’s special tragedy — its wonder — is that the obstinate violence no longer represents an assault on daily life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/10/23/magazine/mag-23Look.html', 'snippet': 'After more than 30 years of war, Afghanistan’s special tragedy — its wonder — is that the obstinate violence no longer represents an assault on daily life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After more than 30 years of war, Afghanistan’s special tragedy — its wonder — is that the obstinate violence no longer represents an assault on daily life.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Life During Wartime', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kabul (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:09:56+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/b1203995-c39a-5ec4-919c-20dfe7898db0', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/b1203995-c39a-5ec4-919c-20dfe7898db0'}, {'abstract': 'The only thing the new movie ‘Anonymous’ proves is that we’re living in a golden age of conspiratorial bunk.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/magazine/wouldnt-it-be-cool-if-shakespeare-wasnt-shakespeare.html', 'snippet': 'The only thing the new movie ‘Anonymous’ proves is that we’re living in a golden age of conspiratorial bunk.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Was Shakespeare a fraud?” That’s the question the promotional machinery for Roland Emmerich’s new film, “Anonymous,” wants to usher out of the tiny enclosure of fringe academic conferences into the wider pastures of a Hollywood audience. Shakespeare is finally getting the Oliver Stone/“Da Vinci Code” treatment, with a lurid conspiratorial melodrama involving incest in royal bedchambers, a vapidly simplistic version of court intrigue, nifty costumes and historically inaccurate nonsense. First they came for the Kennedy scholars, and I did not speak out, because I was not a Kennedy scholar. Then they came for Opus Dei, and I did not speak out, because I was not a Catholic scholar. Now they have come for me.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '60', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23riff-anonymous/23riff-anonymous-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23riff-anonymous/23riff-anonymous-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23riff-anonymous/23riff-anonymous-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Wouldn’t It Be Cool if Shakespeare Wasn’t Shakespeare?', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Wouldn’t It Be Cool If Shakespeare Wasn’t Shakespeare?’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:10:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Marche', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marche', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06dcacff-4fcf-5553-a099-301d465541e9', 'word_count': 1766, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06dcacff-4fcf-5553-a099-301d465541e9'}, {'abstract': 'What one summer on the Black Sea taught me about Vladimir Putin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/magazine/from-russia-with-lies.html', 'snippet': 'What one summer on the Black Sea taught me about Vladimir Putin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the summer of 1973, when I was 18, I camped on a Black Sea beach with a Ukrainian boy named Boris, whose blue eyes and sun-bleached hair made my knees wobble every time I looked in his direction. Had my mother discovered this transgression (I was supposed to be vacationing at my girlfriend’s family’s dacha), I would have felt a shame greater than what befell me six years later, when I betrayed my country by marrying an American and leaving Russia. But that summer, the Crimean sun, the turquoise sea and Boris’s cinnamon tan led me astray into the adult world of vranyo.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '66', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/mag-23Lives-t_CA0/mag-23Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/mag-23Lives-t_CA0/mag-23Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Russia With Lies', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Russia With Lies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putin, Vladimir V', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:10:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elena Gorokhova', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gorokhova', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ca6f60a-4e5f-5f3b-bc13-9bd164e49518', 'word_count': 785, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ca6f60a-4e5f-5f3b-bc13-9bd164e49518'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/magazine/reply-all-taken-by-pirates.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thanks to Jeffrey Gettleman, who continually sheds light on the tragedies and injustices in this corner of the world. I appreciate how he also highlights the good fight that many Somali people are undertaking as they make the best of an incredibly complicated situation. As Gettleman highlights in this article, the Somali situation is more about economics than about religious extremism. Economic desperation causes people to do crazy things. This is true in every country of the world.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Taken by Pirates', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taken By Pirates', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Somalia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Piracy at Sea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:11:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1570178-6642-5262-8969-80c47d31d7c2', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1570178-6642-5262-8969-80c47d31d7c2'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/magazine/reply-all-spalding-gray.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Dark Prince of Downtown,” in large part about Gray’s romantic and professional relationship with the theater director Elizabeth Le Compte, fails to mention that Le Compte is one of the foremost theater directors of our time. She changed and continues to change the form of theater, and all theater looks different because of her work. Gray ultimately found his storytelling voice through her directing — another bit of history that is incorrectly characterized. Readers of this article will think “Liz” serves primarily as Gray’s girlfriend, who directs theater on the side. If this were the story of the lover of a male director, we daresay he would not be depicted in this narrative as a foil, mother or handmaiden.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spalding Gray', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spalding Gray', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gray, Spalding', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:11:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/618816ac-0c7c-519d-bd0f-1f1c021fbbb1', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/618816ac-0c7c-519d-bd0f-1f1c021fbbb1'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/magazine/reply-all-can-cancer-ever-be-ignored.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The article “Can Cancer Ever Be Ignored?” reveals a nightmare of sloppy science. P.S.A. testing poses zero risk. It is not invasive, not radioactive, not even expensive. It is treatment that poses risk. Failing to distinguish between these two entirely different things is bad medicine, bad policy, simply bad thinking. Obviously, we have lots of work to do. We should be searching for better treatment options, better ways of distinguishing between aggressive and nonlethal cancers, better ways of identifying high-risk patients. But advising men not to be screened accomplishes nothing, except to relieve physicians of their responsibility to inform patients fully and treat them appropriately. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can Cancer Ever Be Ignored?', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Do I Have Cancer?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests (Medical)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostate Cancer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:11:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9df4526-ff28-5c43-b00c-2e6757ebc95a', 'word_count': 427, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9df4526-ff28-5c43-b00c-2e6757ebc95a'}, {'abstract': 'The patron saint of soulless capitalism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/magazine/bad-times-on-wall-street-boom-times-for-kevin-spacey.html', 'snippet': 'The patron saint of soulless capitalism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is not very likely that an economist will ever win a Nobel Prize for tracking the correlations between movie-star careers and the business cycle, but if anyone were to take up this area of research, Kevin Spacey would offer an especially challenging case study. His initial rise coincided with the ’90s boom, but for most of the Bush bubble years he was out of sight, doing theater in England. Now — call it happenstance or dialectics — his comeback has arrived more or less simultaneously with the global\xad crisis of capitalism.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23spacey/23spacey-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 856, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23spacey/23spacey-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/magazine/23spacey/23spacey-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bad Times on Wall Street, Boom Times for Kevin Spacey', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Patron Saint of Soulless Capitalism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spacey, Kevin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:11:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. SCOTT', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'SCOTT', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf04e7bb-6d93-58e3-a092-21d5b9b2042b', 'word_count': 815, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf04e7bb-6d93-58e3-a092-21d5b9b2042b'}, {'abstract': 'Officials said Kashmir is peaceful enough to warrant removing the Armed Forces Special Powers Act, which suspends basic rights and shields security forces.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/22/world/asia/kashmir-to-lift-reviled-security-law.html', 'snippet': 'Officials said Kashmir is peaceful enough to warrant removing the Armed Forces Special Powers Act, which suspends basic rights and shields security forces.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW DELHI — A much-despised law that suspends basic rights and shields security forces from prosecution in the disputed province of Kashmir will be lifted in some areas in the next few days, officials said Friday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/22/world/22KASHMIR/22KASHMIR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 734, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/22/world/22KASHMIR/22KASHMIR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/22/world/22KASHMIR/22KASHMIR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kashmir to Lift Reviled Security Law', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Indian Portion Of Kashmir Plans to Lift Security Law', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kashmir and Jammu (India)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:15:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lydia Polgreen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lydia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Polgreen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39e6b8dd-3592-549b-a9f8-6f533fecb604', 'word_count': 340, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39e6b8dd-3592-549b-a9f8-6f533fecb604'}, {'abstract': 'Occupy Boston is not the only protest site with its own library. In New York, Occupy Wall Street has one, as do encampments in Los Angeles; Portland, Ore and other cities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/22/us/tent-libraries-occupy-boston-and-beyond.html', 'snippet': 'Occupy Boston is not the only protest site with its own library. In New York, Occupy Wall Street has one, as do encampments in Los Angeles; Portland, Ore and other cities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — This city, home to the nation’s first major public library, has a new and somewhat grittier reading nook. Housed in a green military tent, the library at the Occupy Boston encampment in Dewey Square is overflowing with scholarly tomes that have no due dates or late fees.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/22/us/22libraries1/22libraries1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/22/us/22libraries1/22libraries1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/22/us/22libraries1/22libraries1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/22/us/22libraries1/22libraries1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/22/us/22libraries1/22libraries1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Occupying Boston and Beyond, With Tent Libraries for All', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Occupying Boston and Beyond, With Tent Libraries for All', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libraries and Librarians', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:16:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jess Bidgood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jess', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bidgood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a51c3d1-3c49-5e7a-9eed-57992fb27540', 'word_count': 758, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a51c3d1-3c49-5e7a-9eed-57992fb27540'}, {'abstract': 'Here’s the latest batch of readers’ responses to some of the shows collected through Storify.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/cmj-day-3-twitter-reviews-of-freshkills-araabmuzik-and-more/', 'snippet': 'Here’s the latest batch of readers’ responses to some of the shows collected through Storify.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s Day 4 of the CMJ Music Marathon and critics from The Times aren’t slowing down. Last night, @joncaramanica and @jonpareles covered the gamut of offerings in Brooklyn and the Lower East Side. But we can’t be everywhere. This year, we’ve invited readers to join us in appraising CMJ shows on Twitter.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CMJ Day 3: Twitter Reviews of Freshkills, AraabMuzik and More', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'CMJ Music Marathon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:18:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/403fa50d-fe67-5b7a-8306-31a428203a40', 'word_count': 108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/403fa50d-fe67-5b7a-8306-31a428203a40'}, {'abstract': 'Although some quarters of the fashion world continue to plead for a frivolousness that seems as naff as Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi’s blue shades, there are small signs of sanity.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/readjusting-our-eye-again/', 'snippet': 'Although some quarters of the fashion world continue to plead for a frivolousness that seems as naff as Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi’s blue shades, there are small signs of sanity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Revolutions with the sidearm of the Internet expose much, not least perfidy. The Telegraph in London has a fascinating obituary of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi — serious, dryly funny — that contains the observation that the Libyan leader looked like “a parody of Sixties radical chic.” It was those craggy features and retro blue-tinted shades. Colonel Qaddafi was no joke, but we once laughed (as the obituary noted) at his outlandishness. For a trip in 2002, to South Africa, his entourage consisted of a personal jet, two transport planes, a container ship filled with buses and goat meat, $6 million in petty cash and 400 security guards. Well, no more.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Readjusting Our Eye, Again', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:24:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0a697e4-e777-5948-8ec6-985f6230f8c8', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0a697e4-e777-5948-8ec6-985f6230f8c8'}, {'abstract': 'The Jewish concept of “pidyon shvuyim” — redeeming the captives, primarily by paying a ransom — helped validate the steep price of Gilad Shalit’s liberation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/22/us/pidyon-shvuyim-validated-the-price-of-shalits-release.html', 'snippet': 'The Jewish concept of “pidyon shvuyim” — redeeming the captives, primarily by paying a ransom — helped validate the steep price of Gilad Shalit’s liberation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the Sabbath morning of Nov. 5, less than three weeks after the release of Sgt. First Class Gilad Shalit in a prisoner exchange between Israel and Hamas, Jews in synagogues throughout the world will read a Torah portion concerning Abraham’s early journeys. The text recounts how invaders conquered the city of Sodom, taking Abraham’s nephew Lot as a captive, and the way Abraham raised an army to rescue him.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/22/us/RELIGION-1/RELIGION-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 593, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/22/us/RELIGION-1/RELIGION-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/22/us/RELIGION-1/RELIGION-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mitzvah Behind the Price of a Soldier’s Freedom', 'kicker': 'On Religion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Mitzvah Behind the Price of a Soldier’s Freedom', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shalit, Gilad', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hamas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:28:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samuel G. Freedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samuel', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Freedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f3281fc-37e4-5870-9f44-e2000e79794b', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f3281fc-37e4-5870-9f44-e2000e79794b'}, {'abstract': 'Fill in the blanks in the article “Builders of Corn Mazes Hope to Lose Visitors, and One Actually Did.” Use your own words and phrases, or choose from a scrambled list of the words or phrases that were removed.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/fill-in-corn-mazes/', 'snippet': 'Fill in the blanks in the article “Builders of Corn Mazes Hope to Lose Visitors, and One Actually Did.” Use your own words and phrases, or choose from a scrambled list of the words or phrases that were removed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Directions: Fill in the blanks in the article “Builders of Corn Mazes Hope to Lose Visitors, and One Actually Did.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fill-In | Corn Mazes', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:28:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95683f11-e631-5ab3-9c38-a14bad237749', 'word_count': 867, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95683f11-e631-5ab3-9c38-a14bad237749'}, {'abstract': 'Panelists at New York University said beef is too great an icon to lose its place in the American diet.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/beef-will-be-whats-for-dinner-for-a-long-time-panelists-say/', 'snippet': 'Panelists at New York University said beef is too great an icon to lose its place in the American diet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Have you been persuaded that beef is waning on the American plate because it’s too destructive to the ecosystem, too hormone-pumped and too corn-fed artery-clogging?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Beef Will Be What’s for Dinner for a Long Time, Panelists Say', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:43:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/496b4873-b3d2-518d-9e7e-62f9f2b11787', 'word_count': 451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/496b4873-b3d2-518d-9e7e-62f9f2b11787'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/what-were-reading-295/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Serious Eats: Those crazy kids find a Coke Freestyle machine and try all 127 possible flavor combinations. Fidgety behavior and tooth decay ensue. — Eric Asimov', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:43:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99095ed0-d2f4-5b38-8ee9-67f95c95d2f9', 'word_count': 671, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99095ed0-d2f4-5b38-8ee9-67f95c95d2f9'}, {'abstract': 'Take your pesto beyond pasta by adding it to quinoa, potatoes and vegetables, writes Martha Rose Shulman in this week’s Recipes for Health.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/pesto-dishes-without-the-pasta/', 'snippet': 'Take your pesto beyond pasta by adding it to quinoa, potatoes and vegetables, writes Martha Rose Shulman in this week’s Recipes for Health.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this week’s Recipes for Health, Martha Rose Shulman has pulled out the hand blender to whip up extra batches of puréed basil to be frozen and enjoyed in various pesto dishes all winter.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pesto Dishes Without the Pasta', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Herbs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vegetables', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:52:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/909ad2b0-ab21-5526-93b8-2c73e20a9f83', 'word_count': 273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/909ad2b0-ab21-5526-93b8-2c73e20a9f83'}, {'abstract': 'According to Chinese media reports, the mother of the toddler killed in a hit-and-run accident that shocked the country says a Twitter-like microblog in her name is a fake.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/mother-of-toddler-who-was-run-over-says-microblog-is-a-fake/', 'snippet': 'According to Chinese media reports, the mother of the toddler killed in a hit-and-run accident that shocked the country says a Twitter-like microblog in her name is a fake.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A 2-year-old girl who was hit by a van and then ignored as she lay writhing in the middle of a busy market street in China died on Friday of her injuries, as my colleague Michael Wines reports. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mother of Toddler Who Was Run Over Says Microblog Is a Fake', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hit-and-Run Drivers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:55:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. David Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8f1bf63-4d05-5d78-b1f2-ab1e31793ac3', 'word_count': 595, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8f1bf63-4d05-5d78-b1f2-ab1e31793ac3'}, {'abstract': 'On rooftops, balconies, sidewalks and streets, the traditional sukkah, recalling Israelites’ wandering in the desert, changes the face of New York neighborhoods.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/with-bamboo-roofs-and-plywood-walls-williamsburg-is-transformed-for-sukkot/', 'snippet': 'On rooftops, balconies, sidewalks and streets, the traditional sukkah, recalling Israelites’ wandering in the desert, changes the face of New York neighborhoods.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is possible, in many parts of New York, to pass through autumn unaware of the Jewish festival of Sukkot. It is, however, impossible to pass through Brooklyn neighborhoods like Williamsburg, Crown Heights and Borough Park without knowing that the eight-day harvest holiday is under way.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'With Bamboo Roofs and Plywood Walls, Williamsburg Is Transformed for Sukkot', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Williamsburg (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:55:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Dunlap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dunlap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01809bb2-400b-574f-8edb-9d9304ada70f', 'word_count': 172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01809bb2-400b-574f-8edb-9d9304ada70f'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Sevigny, the “Big Love” and “Boys Don’t Cry” star, will play a hired assassin and a pre-op transsexual who learns she has a son, on a new DirecTV series called “Hit and Miss.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/chloe-sevigny-has-even-more-to-hide-in-a-new-television-series/', 'snippet': 'Ms. Sevigny, the “Big Love” and “Boys Don’t Cry” star, will play a hired assassin and a pre-op transsexual who learns she has a son, on a new DirecTV series called “Hit and Miss.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'It seems you can’t have a Chloë Sevigny project without a character or characters trying to hide a shocking secret, whether it’s a lover who disguises her gender (in “Boys Don’t Cry”) or a polygamist family concealing its multiple marriages (on the HBO series “Big Love”). So try to sound surprised when you learn that there are a couple of unexpected twists to the character that Ms. Sevigny plays in her latest project, at least one of which is strongly hinted at in its title. In “Hit and Miss,” a new series that will be shown on the satellite service DirecTV, she portrays a character named Mia who is a hired assassin as well as a pre-op transsexual. When Mia learns she’s had a son by an ex-girlfriend who is dying of cancer, she is forced “to mix her killer instincts with her newly acquired maternal ones” (hey, that’s what it says in the press release).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chloë Sevigny Has, What Else, More Secrets in a New Television Series', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transgender and Transsexual', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABBOTT, PAUL', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sevigny, Chloe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'DirecTV', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:57:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f86eb47-1187-5118-9103-34cde6d025a2', 'word_count': 216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f86eb47-1187-5118-9103-34cde6d025a2'}, {'abstract': 'It turns out that today’s cycad plants originated only about 10 million years ago, not when dinosaurs roamed the earth, a new study says.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/on-our-radar-living-fossil-is-younger-than-thought/', 'snippet': 'It turns out that today’s cycad plants originated only about 10 million years ago, not when dinosaurs roamed the earth, a new study says.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Modern-day cycads that were assumed to have existed when dinosaurs roamed the earth over 200 million years ago are actually far younger, researchers report. [The Times of London]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: ‘Living Fossil’ Is Younger Than Thought', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coal', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Aid (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mines and Mining', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paleontology', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Finland', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Queensland (Australia)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T14:57:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71f5fbe0-74c1-53ed-9ce6-d0758a8cdd23', 'word_count': 135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71f5fbe0-74c1-53ed-9ce6-d0758a8cdd23'}, {'abstract': 'Euphoria in Libya has given way to friction and confusion over where and when to bury the former strongman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/22/world/africa/libyan-leaders-appear-to-wrangle-over-qaddafi-burial.html', 'snippet': 'Euphoria in Libya has given way to friction and confusion over where and when to bury the former strongman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MISURATA, Libya — International calls mounted Friday for Libya’s interim leaders to provide a fuller accounting of the final moments before Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi’s violent, messy death, as new videos circulated that showed him and his son Muatassim alive, apparently while in the custody of the former rebels. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/22/world/22LIBYA2_span/22LIBYA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/22/world/22LIBYA2_span/22LIBYA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/22/world/22LI