n', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T15:25:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef55360d-90a4-5b0f-b349-0650ce62ae30', 'word_count': 321, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef55360d-90a4-5b0f-b349-0650ce62ae30'}, {'abstract': 'Judy Battista, the Times’s N.F.L. reporter, answers readers’ questions.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/the-timess-battista-answers-questions/', 'snippet': 'Judy Battista, the Times’s N.F.L. reporter, answers readers’ questions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Judy Battista, the N.F.L. reporter for The New York Times, answers questions from readers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Times’s Battista Answers Questions', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Officiating (Sports)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haynesworth, Albert', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ochocinco, Chad', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palmer, Carson', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ponder, Christian', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tebow, Tim', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dallas Cowboys', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Denver Broncos', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Minnesota Vikings', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Patriots', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oakland Raiders', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Redskins', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T15:30:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6aa8428f-42f5-58ab-af55-5748c5e03a4a', 'word_count': 1474, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6aa8428f-42f5-58ab-af55-5748c5e03a4a'}, {'abstract': 'Never mind those pesky climate data.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/more-people-who-cant-handle-the-truth/', 'snippet': 'Never mind those pesky climate data.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you follow this blog regularly, you’ll know that whenever I present data — and I do present a lot of data — right-wingers will complain of “cherry-picking”. They never have a clear example of how I should do things differently — or if they do, it’s always obviously wrong. But what they really mean is that they won’t accept data that doesn’t tell them what they want to hear.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More People Who Can’t Handle The Truth', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T15:39:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3461b3d-0955-5067-84da-c8f4d56d0d25', 'word_count': 290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3461b3d-0955-5067-84da-c8f4d56d0d25'}, {'abstract': 'Liquidity, solvency, and all that.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/i-may-be-dumb-but-im-not-stupid/', 'snippet': 'Liquidity, solvency, and all that.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Or something like that. The usually very insightful Kantoos seems to have missed what people like me are actually saying about the need for emergency funding in Europe.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'I May Be Dumb, But I’m Not Stupid', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T15:40:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9485e6fa-b177-5a6a-a6c6-6d9dba730c6c', 'word_count': 212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9485e6fa-b177-5a6a-a6c6-6d9dba730c6c'}, {'abstract': 'Texas awakens the World Series with a ninth inning to remember, while St. Louis Manager Tony La Russa sets the head-scratching in motion.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/leading-off-rangers-go-bold-just-in-time/', 'snippet': 'Texas awakens the World Series with a ninth inning to remember, while St. Louis Manager Tony La Russa sets the head-scratching in motion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The top of the ninth inning of Game 2 of the World Series was such a tiny sliver in an otherwise unremarkable game. Sure, the starting pitchers performed admirably amid reports that their profession was as obsolete as the iPhone 2, but by the time Albert Pujols polished off the last of his fruitless four at-bats, well, hanging with the “action” was getting tougher with every pitch.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Leading Off: Rangers Go Bold, Just in Time', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Andrus, Elvis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Auriemma, Geno', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kinsler, Ian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'la Russa, Tony', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Luongo, Roberto', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palmer, Carson', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pujols, Albert', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Detroit Lions', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Rangers', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oakland Raiders', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Notre Dame', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T15:44:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/666e3249-c4e8-5c08-82e5-b25359bcc29c', 'word_count': 521, 'uri': 'nyt://article/666e3249-c4e8-5c08-82e5-b25359bcc29c'}, {'abstract': 'It’s nice that more women in positions of power feel safe talking about family commitments at work. What about those in less powerful jobs?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/can-powerful-women-change-the-workplace/', 'snippet': 'It’s nice that more women in positions of power feel safe talking about family commitments at work. What about those in less powerful jobs?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York Times’s otherwise distressing article  today on the very real possibility that, in 2012, women’s representation in the Senate may shrink for the first time in a generation did contain one real bright spot: Senator Patty Murray of Washington State, elected in the much-vaunted Year of the Woman in 1992, reflected on the contrast of then and now:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can Powerful Women Change the Workplace?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T15:45:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Warner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Warner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94a4ac62-1296-5731-8bab-8931cd3647a9', 'word_count': 338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94a4ac62-1296-5731-8bab-8931cd3647a9'}, {'abstract': 'The Dalai Lama explores the interface of contemplative and scientific approaches to environmental challenges.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/on-climate-ethics-cow-burps-and-the-dalai-lama/', 'snippet': 'The Dalai Lama explores the interface of contemplative and scientific approaches to environmental challenges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early this week, I heard through some e-mail traffic that 10 climate, behavioral and environmental scientists and scholars were meeting with the Dalai Lama to discuss “ecology, ethics and interdependence” — a mix at the core of this blog. The meeting is part of a continuing series of discussions on the interface between contemplative and scientific approaches to humanity’s challenges. Here’s one of a string of taped segments:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Climate, Ethics, Cow Burps and the Dalai Lama', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DALAI LAMA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'TIBET', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T15:47:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d569dc53-8aad-5a22-91cc-392befb70b7e', 'word_count': 620, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d569dc53-8aad-5a22-91cc-392befb70b7e'}, {'abstract': 'Autumn Unfolds: Many plants that offer high-quality fruits schedule foliar displays to coincide with the peak of bird migration.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/dry-leaves-dead-timber-and-enticements-for-migrating-birds/', 'snippet': 'Autumn Unfolds: Many plants that offer high-quality fruits schedule foliar displays to coincide with the peak of bird migration.', 'lead_paragraph': 'See a larger version of the forest from this week. Compare with the blow-up of last week. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dry Leaves, Dead Timber and Enticements for Migrating Birds', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seasons and Months', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trees and Shrubs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'INWOOD HILL PARK (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T15:52:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marielle Anzelone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marielle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anzelone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66717fa8-9f51-5437-9dab-4ffda03c1e0a', 'word_count': 572, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66717fa8-9f51-5437-9dab-4ffda03c1e0a'}, {'abstract': 'The Bosnian hero of Ismet Prcic’s first novel is shattered by war.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/shards-by-ismet-prcic-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Bosnian hero of Ismet Prcic’s first novel is shattered by war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a long and important history of memoirs and fictions, and hybrids of the two, that address the aftermath of war. “Shards,” the impressive first novel by Ismet Prcic, finds inventive ways to interrogate the anguish of enduring and then escaping Bosnia during the war of the 1990s. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/spiotta/spiotta-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 967, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/spiotta/spiotta-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/spiotta/spiotta-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Leaving Bosnia (and Not)', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Balkan Ghosts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bosnia and Herzegovina', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T15:55:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dana Spiotta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spiotta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/15a38d72-6a13-58aa-8ba8-35d7d416ab01', 'word_count': 1384, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15a38d72-6a13-58aa-8ba8-35d7d416ab01'}, {'abstract': 'Dana Spiotta asks the question: How does a writer describe a broken mind “without reducing it to something false or reassuringly conventional?”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/up-front-dana-spiotta.html', 'snippet': 'Dana Spiotta asks the question: How does a writer describe a broken mind “without reducing it to something false or reassuringly conventional?”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her most recent novel, “Stone Arabia,” Dana Spiotta used multiple voices and narrative devices to explore the slipperiness of identity and the capriciousness of memory. So too does the novel she reviews in this week’s Sunday Book Review: “Shards,” by Ismet Prcic, about a young man’s experiences of the Bosnian war and his mental breakdown in its aftermath. Spiotta said via e-mail that she was interested in Prcic’s attempt to find “an expressive form for a consciousness in crisis.” How, she asked, does a writer describe a broken mind “without reducing it to something false or reassuringly conventional? Memory is not particularly linear — it is associative, repetitive, subjective and porous. But the writer needs to convey disorder and dysfunction without making the novel itself disorderly or dysfunctional.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front: Dana Spiotta', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spiotta, Dana', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T15:55:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2e4e352-55a3-5c01-8755-f82f1a528709', 'word_count': 284, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2e4e352-55a3-5c01-8755-f82f1a528709'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to a recent review of Steven Pinker’s “Better Angels of Our Nature.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/the-better-angels-of-our-nature.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to a recent review of Steven Pinker’s “Better Angels of Our Nature.”',