review/evolution-of-theory.html', 'snippet': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Robert N. Bellah’s “Religion in Human Evolution.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Evolution of Theory', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Evolution Of Theory', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T15:56:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9ba16bb-495a-532b-b048-90189372dd26', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9ba16bb-495a-532b-b048-90189372dd26'}, {'abstract': 'The symbols found in airports, hospitals and government buildings are ubiquitous, but few people know where they had their origins.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/the-design-of-symbols.html', 'snippet': 'The symbols found in airports, hospitals and government buildings are ubiquitous, but few people know where they had their origins.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We see them in airports, hospitals and government buildings, in waiting rooms and bathrooms, on exits and entrances: schematic silhouettes of men, women and children. The graphic icons are characterized by a no-frills, geometric style that is immediately recognizable and decipherable wherever they’re found. But while the symbols are ubiquitous, few people know where they had their origins.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/Heller01/Heller01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 472, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/Heller01/Heller01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 472}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/Heller01/Heller01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 818, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/Heller01/Heller01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/Heller01/Heller01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Design of Symbols', 'kicker': 'Visuals', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vital Signs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bateman, Steven', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hopkinson, Martin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vossoughian, Nader', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hyland, Angus', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T15:56:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Heller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfb2c69c-3fed-5548-a324-e1e3bc5ebe45', 'word_count': 1338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfb2c69c-3fed-5548-a324-e1e3bc5ebe45'}, {'abstract': 'Fight crime, with fashion: a spring 2012 collection for superheroes, on view at the Ace Hotel on Monday.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/fashion-to-the-rescue/', 'snippet': 'Fight crime, with fashion: a spring 2012 collection for superheroes, on view at the Ace Hotel on Monday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thinking of fighting crime but don’t know what to wear? The solution may be found at the spring 2012 collection for superheroes presented by the Brooklyn Superhero Supply Co. The event, on Oct. 24 at 9 p.m. at the Ace Hotel, is a benefit for 826NYC, a nonprofit literacy organization. The contributing designers — Christian Joy, Opening Ceremony, United Bamboo, Matt Singer, Jack Spade, Renata Morales and Complex Geometries — were asked to come up with crime-fighting attire, which left the possibilities just about endless. “There’s a lot of flexibility as to what a superhero will wear,” says Joan Kim, the director of education at 826NYC. “The Incredible Hulk just wears shorts. Some wear different accessories, some wear capes.” The seven outfits will be sold at silent auction. Other designers, including Marc Jacobs and Stella McCartney, have donated pieces for the auction and raffle ($20 per ticket). Tickets are $75 for general admission, or $150, which includes an 8 p.m. cocktail party and five raffle tickets.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fashion to the Rescue', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T15:58:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f7511af-a47b-5e06-907d-8a7ef9a46a65', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f7511af-a47b-5e06-907d-8a7ef9a46a65'}, {'abstract': 'In Charles Frazier’s novel, a young woman becomes the guardian of her murdered sister’s twins.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/nightwoods-by-charles-frazier-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In Charles Frazier’s novel, a young woman becomes the guardian of her murdered sister’s twins.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among James Fenimore Cooper’s many literary offenses, Mark Twain charged, was “surplusage.” The word’s undue thickness perfectly matches its meaning. It also feels of a piece with Cooper’s own prose, and likewise Charles Frazier’s: elegantly archaic-sounding, rough-cut and contrived.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/BOYAGODA/BOYAGODA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 823, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/BOYAGODA/BOYAGODA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/BOYAGODA/BOYAGODA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Charles Frazier’s North Carolina Gothic', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Family Valuables', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Carolina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frazier, Charles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T15:59:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Boyagoda', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boyagoda', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7c4f528-b506-537a-beef-0f1dd0edf92c', 'word_count': 626, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7c4f528-b506-537a-beef-0f1dd0edf92c'}, {'abstract': 'Scholars and relatives wrestle with the legacy of a Rupert Brooke-like writer in Alan Hollinghurst’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/the-strangers-child-by-alan-hollinghurst-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Scholars and relatives wrestle with the legacy of a Rupert Brooke-like writer in Alan Hollinghurst’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alan Hollinghurst’s novels — a distinguished body of work, both daring and fastidious — have often set out to see Britain’s modern, decriminalized gay life against the dangers and excitements of earlier decades. “The Swimming Pool Library,” to take one example, showed its uninhibited young protagonist, William Beckwith, making up his mind whether or not to write the biography of the much older and necessarily more furtive Lord Nantwich. The decision-making allowed one era to resonate against the other, in a novel whose contemporary narrative voice allowed older gay literary sensibilities, like E. M. Forster’s and Ronald Firbank’s, to enter through echo and allusion.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/books/review/Mallon/Mallon-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 958, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/10/books/review/Mallon/Mallon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/10/books/review/Mallon/Mallon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Alan Hollinghurst Imagines a Fallen Poet’s Literary Afterlife', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Afterlife', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooke, Rupert', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hollinghurst, Alan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:02:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Mallon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mallon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/aba59feb-7b49-52e5-ade6-18336c1b31ee', 'word_count': 1225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aba59feb-7b49-52e5-ade6-18336c1b31ee'}, {'abstract': 'White whales abound in this book cover collection of Herman Melville’s masterwork.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/10/22/books/review/moby-dick-slideshow.html', 'snippet': 'White whales abound in this book cover collection of Herman Melville’s masterwork.', 'lead_paragraph': 'White whales abound in this book cover collection of Herman Melville’s masterwork.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/22/books/review/moby-dick-slideshow-slide-W2QW/moby-dick-slideshow-slide-W2QW-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 851, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/22/books/review/moby-dick-slideshow-slide-W2QW/moby-dick-slideshow-slide-W2QW-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 851}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/22/books/review/moby-dick-slideshow-slide-W2QW/moby-dick-slideshow-slide-W2QW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 722, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Moby-Dick: A Display of Book Covers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:03:40+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Books', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/1da8c281-00af-517f-9f9f-edb9db6bcba2', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/1da8c281-00af-517f-9f9f-edb9db6bcba2'}, {'abstract': 'Melville’s book about sin and redemption should be read by everyone, Nathaniel Philbrick says.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/why-read-moby-dick-by-nathaniel-philbrick-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Melville’s book about sin and redemption should be read by everyone, Nathaniel Philbrick says.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a hard sell Nathaniel Philbrick has undertaken in “Why Read Moby-Dick?” The novel’s plot has been recycled for decades, inspiring films, radio dramas, cartoons, comic books, a television mini-series, a couple of heavy metal albums, a music video and a rap rendition. How many potential readers approach the masterwork of Herman Melville without already knowing the story of Captain Ahab and the white whale? Any? And why would such an overly exposed audience embrace a work of such heft, especially as almost every edition carries the added weight of ponderous academic commentary? “Moby-Dick” would appear to be one of those unfortunate books that are taught rather than enjoyed.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/22/books/review/moby-dick-slideshow-slide-XIB1/moby-dick-slideshow-slide-XIB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 908, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/22/books/review/moby-dick-slideshow-slide-XIB1/moby-dick-slideshow-slide-XIB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 908}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/22/books/review/moby-dick-slideshow-slide-XIB1/moby-dick-slideshow-slide-XIB1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 677, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/22/books/review/moby-dick-slideshow-slide-XIB1/moby-dick-slideshow-slide-XIB1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/22/books/review/moby-dick-slideshow-slide-XIB1/moby-dick-slideshow-slide-XIB1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Read Moby-Dick', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Our American Bible', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whales and Whaling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Philbrick, Nathaniel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Melville, Herman', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:03:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Harrison', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harrison', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/78e47215-4d3a-52df-ac9f-09011c8a9ea0', 'word_count': 1214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78e47215-4d3a-52df-ac9f-09011c8a9ea0'}, {'abstract': 'The Republican political consultant accused of stealing $1.1 million from Mayor Bloomberg during the 2009 election was acquitted of the most serious charge, but the two charges of which he was convicted of are both felonies.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/haggerty-guilty-of-grand-larceny-and-money-laundering/', 'snippet': 'The Republican political consultant accused of stealing $1.1 million from Mayor Bloomberg during the 2009 election was acquitted of the most serious charge, but the two charges of which he was convicted of are both felonies.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Updated, 12:40 p.m. | John F. Haggerty Jr., a Republican political consultant accused of stealing $1.1 million from Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg during Mr. Bloomberg’s 2009 re-election campaign, was found guilty of second-degree grand larceny and second-degree money laundering by a jury in Manhattan on Friday morning. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Consultant Guilty of Grand Larceny and Money Laundering', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haggerty, John F Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:04:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Eligon, James Barron and Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eligon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3720ba0-9371-5520-b010-b13cb0342682', 'word_count': 592, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3720ba0-9371-5520-b010-b13cb0342682'}, {'abstract': 'In addition to a $3.2 million payment to the family of Milly Dowler, Mr. Murdoch will personally donate $1.6 million to charities selected by the family.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/murdoch-makes-payment-to-family-of-murdered-schoolgirl/', 'snippet': 'In addition to a $3.2 million payment to the family of Milly Dowler, Mr. Murdoch will personally donate $1.6 million to charities selected by the family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hours before Rupert Murdoch, chairman and chief executive of News Corporation, faces shareholders at the company’s annual meeting, the company announced Friday that it would pay $3.2 million to the family of Milly Dowler, the murdered British teenager whose phone was hacked by News Corporation’s News of the World tabloid.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Corp. to Pay $3.2 Million to Murdered Girl’s Family', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wiretapping and Other Eavesdropping Devices and Methods', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dowler, Milly', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEWS CORP', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News International Ltd', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of the World', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:04:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Chozick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4269abf7-eb91-53ec-b64f-4e4128f34e50', 'word_count': 343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4269abf7-eb91-53ec-b64f-4e4128f34e50'}, {'abstract': 'As a singer and actor, Harry Belafonte was both a matinee idol and a proud black man.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/my-song-by-harry-belafonte-with-michael-shnayerson-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'As a singer and actor, Harry Belafonte was both a matinee idol and a proud black man.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here is a gorgeous account of the large life of a Harlem boy, son of a Jamaican cleaning lady, Melvine Love, and a ship’s cook, Harold Bellan\xadfanti, who endured the grind of poverty under the watchful eye of his proud mother and waited for his chances, prepared to be lucky, and made himself into the international calypso star and popular folk singer, huge in Las Vegas, also Europe, and a mainstay of the civil rights movement of the ’60s, a confidant of Dr. King’s, who lived for years in a U-shaped 21-room apartment on West End Avenue, but never forgot what he ran so hard to escape from, the four or five families squeezed into a few rooms, the smell of Caribbean food cooking, the shared bathroom, his father drunk, yelling, blood on his hands, beating his mother, and “a terrible claustrophobic closet of fear.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/KEILLOR/KEILLOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 463, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/KEILLOR/KEILLOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 463}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/KEILLOR/KEILLOR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 833, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/KEILLOR/KEILLOR-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/KEILLOR/KEILLOR-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Radical Entertainment of Harry Belafonte', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Trailblazer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Martin Luther Jr', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shnayerson, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil Rights and Liberties', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Belafonte, Harry', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:05:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Garrison Keillor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Garrison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keillor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/563b98e8-03a1-55c3-b5ca-916f0a9014c9', 'word_count': 1812, 'uri': 'nyt://article/563b98e8-03a1-55c3-b5ca-916f0a9014c9'}, {'abstract': 'Alice Hoffman’s novel reimagines the first-century siege, through the eyes of women.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/the-dovekeepers-by-alice-hoffman-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Alice Hoffman’s novel reimagines the first-century siege, through the eyes of women.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You could call it a hoopla sandwich. On the back cover, a blurb from a famous, widely respected author describing the novel as “a major contribution to 21st-century literature.” On the jacket flap, a publisher’s summary proclaiming this book to be the writer’s “masterpiece.” Yet in between, instead of a gripping work of fiction that lives up to this praise, is a long novel full of middling descriptions, hackneyed characters and histrionic plot twists.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Women of Masada', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paths to Masada', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hoffman, Alice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Josephus, Flavius', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:06:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Fay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/14b2ee3f-d4b1-518d-8ad8-8be8e336fba6', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14b2ee3f-d4b1-518d-8ad8-8be8e336fba6'}, {'abstract': 'A look at the career of Deng Xiaoping, who changed China’s course.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/deng-xiaoping-and-the-transformation-of-china-by-ezra-f-vogel-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A look at the career of Deng Xiaoping, who changed China’s course.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two mighty rhetorical questions conclude this enormous biography of Deng Xiaoping (1904-97): “Did any other leader in the 20th century do more to improve the lives of so many? Did any other 20th-century leader have such a large and lasting influence on world history?” The answers emerge from this comprehensive, minutely documented book, but not as predictably as Ezra F. Vogel, a Harvard University emeritus professor of social sciences, assumes.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/MIRSKY/MIRSKY-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 430, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/MIRSKY/MIRSKY-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 430}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/MIRSKY/MIRSKY-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 735, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/MIRSKY/MIRSKY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1076, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/MIRSKY/MIRSKY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/MIRSKY/MIRSKY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/MIRSKY/MIRSKY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Deng Did It', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On the Capitalist Road', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Deng Xiaoping', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vogel, Ezra F', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:08:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Mirsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mirsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c74315e5-0adc-520d-a508-edc42f716fb2', 'word_count': 1195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c74315e5-0adc-520d-a508-edc42f716fb2'}, {'abstract': 'In José Saramago’s final novel, Cain is a flawed Everyman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/cain-by-jose-saramago-translated-by-margaret-jull-costa-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In José Saramago’s final novel, Cain is a flawed Everyman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'God is a petulant, small-minded tyrant in José Saramago’s final novel. Its protagonist, Cain, is sympathetic, a picaresque hero. But that description may give the wrong idea by implying ponderous qualities indicated by the phrase “village atheist.” Like that other largehearted, blasphemous comedian, Mark Twain, Saramago takes an alert interest in actual villages, and in the various villages of the mind. He perceives those literal and figurative human settlements as horrifying yet funny, a perspective that is the opposite of provincial.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/PINSKY/PINSKY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 745, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/PINSKY/PINSKY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/PINSKY/PINSKY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'José Saramago Goes Back to Genesis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Adam’s Son', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saramago, Jose', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:09:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Pinsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pinsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c51f696c-ff72-5d95-8a3d-dec173c0a052', 'word_count': 1295, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c51f696c-ff72-5d95-8a3d-dec173c0a052'}, {'abstract': 'A look at Hitler’s grip on Germany in the Third Reich’s last days.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/the-end-by-ian-kershaw-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A look at Hitler’s grip on Germany in the Third Reich’s last days.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By the morning of April 18, 1945, the war in Ansbach, a picturesque town in southern Germany, was just about over. With the Wehrmacht in retreat and the United States Army a few miles away, surrender seemed the only option. But Ansbach’s military commander, a middle-aged air force colonel named Ernst Meyer, was determined to fight to the bitter end. In an effort to avert a catastrophe, Robert Limpert, a 19-year-old theology student, destroyed what he thought were the phone lines between the town and local army headquarters. Turned in by two members of the Hitler Youth, Limpert was arrested, summarily tried and publicly executed while a crowd of his fellow citizens, some cheering, most silent, looked on. A few hours later, after Meyer had fled and Ansbach was captured without a fight, American troops cut down Limpert’s body from where it had been left hanging in the town hall square.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/20/books/review/sheehan/sheehan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 861, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/20/books/review/sheehan/sheehan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/20/books/review/sheehan/sheehan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hitler’s Last Gasp', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'State of Deception', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kershaw, Ian', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hitler, Adolf', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:10:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James J. Sheehan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Sheehan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd020380-542b-56d3-981a-d22b588d0ba9', 'word_count': 1220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd020380-542b-56d3-981a-d22b588d0ba9'}, {'abstract': 'Ha Jin’s novel recreates the horrors of the Japanese invasion of Nanjing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/nanjing-requiem-by-ha-jin-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Ha Jin’s novel recreates the horrors of the Japanese invasion of Nanjing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ha Jin has a talent for first lines. Consider these, from his latest novel, “Nanjing Requiem”: “Finally Ban began to talk. For a whole evening we sat in the dining room listening to the boy.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/HILTON/HILTON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/HILTON/HILTON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Recreating the Horrors of Nanjing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Harm’s Way', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:11:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isabel Hilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isabel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3649076d-62e4-50e1-adca-79366c0d76c8', 'word_count': 929, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3649076d-62e4-50e1-adca-79366c0d76c8'}, {'abstract': 'A look at the history behind the Egyptian uprising.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/the-struggle-for-egypt-by-steven-a-cook-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A look at the history behind the Egyptian uprising.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the morning of Feb. 11, 2011, hours before Hosni Mubarak submitted to the millions of his subjects clamoring for his resignation, a half-dozen retired generals sipped coffee poolside at the Gezira Club, kitted up for tennis and contemptuously dismissed the demonstrators in Tahrir Square. “Who do they represent?” scoffed a man who until recently had worked in state security. “They are loud, but don’t forget there are 79 million Egyptians who are not in Tahrir Square. They are the majority.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/CAMBANIS/CAMBANIS-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/CAMBANIS/CAMBANIS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/CAMBANIS/CAMBANIS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What the Generals Did to Egypt', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Since Nasser', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cook, Steven A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:12:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By THANASSIS CAMBANIS', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thanassis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'CAMBANIS', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e10f20ab-b427-5ce7-b7df-cbf775152f8a', 'word_count': 1327, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e10f20ab-b427-5ce7-b7df-cbf775152f8a'}, {'abstract': 'She was scheduled to perform next Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/second-time-no-charm-as-netrebko-cancels-carnegie-hall-recital/', 'snippet': 'She was scheduled to perform next Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The soprano Anna Netrebko has once again canceled her New York recital debut, which was scheduled for Carnegie Hall next Wednesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Second Time Is No Charm as Netrebko Cancels Carnegie Hall Recital', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bashkirova, Elena', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Netrebko, Anna', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnegie Hall', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:13:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8c8bc73-4403-5f00-a4da-c00185ff4c3b', 'word_count': 268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8c8bc73-4403-5f00-a4da-c00185ff4c3b'}, {'abstract': 'Thant Myint-U explains why Myanmar is destined to play an increasingly crucial role in the world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/where-china-meets-india-by-thant-myint-u-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Thant Myint-U explains why Myanmar is destined to play an increasingly crucial role in the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As economies and societies all around them have flourished, two countries have been strikingly left out of the East Asian boom of the past generation: North Korea and Myanmar. The strategic importance, internal miseries and governing oddities of North Korea are obvious and frequently in the news. Myanmar, by contrast, is rarely mentioned. When it does appear, it is usually as the object of some new calamity — like the 2008 cyclone that killed over 100,000 people, many of them perishing after Myanmar’s benighted military rulers refused outside aid — or as the setting for dramas involving Daw Aung San Suu Kyi, the Nobel Peace Prize winner whose party triumphed in the 1990 national elections but who has spent most of the years since then under house arrest.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/FALLOWS/FALLOWS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/FALLOWS/FALLOWS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/FALLOWS/FALLOWS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 690, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/FALLOWS/FALLOWS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/FALLOWS/FALLOWS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Improbably Bright Future of Myanmar', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Political Frontier', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:13:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Fallows', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fallows', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a51c5bfa-adbd-5c27-8fa8-8570b66064f8', 'word_count': 1201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a51c5bfa-adbd-5c27-8fa8-8570b66064f8'}, {'abstract': 'In her first novel, Kimberly Cutter explores the life and death of Joan of Arc.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/the-maid-by-kimberly-cutter-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In her first novel, Kimberly Cutter explores the life and death of Joan of Arc.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Dec. 22, 1905, an Onion-like headline ran in The New York Times: “Joan of Arc Appears to Startle Mark Twain.” The article described how the hosts at a dinner in Twain’s honor had arranged for a model to arrive dressed as Joan. (Twain had long been entranced by her, and had even written a novel called “Personal Recollections of Joan of Arc.”) When she entered the room, the author “had every appearance of a man who had seen a ghost.” When he recovered, Twain said to the assembled guests: “I studied that girl, Joan of Arc, for 12 years, and it never seemed to me that the artists and the writers gave us a true picture of her. They drew a picture of a peasant. . . . But they always missed the face — the divine soul, the pure character.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the Head of Joan of Arc', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Patron Saint', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cutter, Kimberly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:14:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Towers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Towers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2eca4d6-2faf-5619-aa47-3ad23f304046', 'word_count': 686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2eca4d6-2faf-5619-aa47-3ad23f304046'}, {'abstract': 'Jeffrey Eugenides’s new novel may have missed hitting No. 1, but at least his vest has a Twitter feed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Jeffrey Eugenides’s new novel may have missed hitting No. 1, but at least his vest has a Twitter feed.', 'lead_paragraph': ' VESTED INTERESTS: Last fall, Jonathan Franzen’s glasses were stolen off his face at a London party for his novel “Freedom.” Jeffrey Eugenides may be tempting similar literary vandalism if he doesn’t button up the vest he was recently seen wearing on a big billboard in Times Square advertising “The Marriage Plot,” which hits this week’s hardcover fiction list at No. 2. But at least the vest would be able to send news of its whereabouts, thanks to the Twitter feed @EugenidesVest, which started posting updates from its location “On Jeffrey’s Torso” on Oct. 13. In addition to grumbling about being stiffed by a certain prize committee (“Jeff tells me he’s been having some trouble with the National Book Awards. Something about fiction finalists and the number six.”), the vest seemed none too happy with a rather tart review of “The Marriage Plot” that appeared recently in these pages. “There’s no predicting where William Deresiewicz’s wardrobe will go,” the vest declared. “But I see bikini thongs in his future.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/30insidelist/30insidelist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 565, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/30insidelist/30insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/30insidelist/30insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eugenides, Jeffrey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:15:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa156a41-a4d1-5b89-820e-498ec9d9b7ce', 'word_count': 608, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa156a41-a4d1-5b89-820e-498ec9d9b7ce'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE MARRIAGE PLOT,  by Jeffrey Eugenides (Farrar, Straus & Giroux, $28.) Eugenides’s novel, which deftly renders the patter of college intellectuals and the sweet banter of courtship, is particularly astute about the uncertainties awaiting after graduation.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Editors' Choice: Recent Books Of Particular Interest", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:15:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b5ac33b-5c33-5ac1-8c63-e134ec10b2dc', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b5ac33b-5c33-5ac1-8c63-e134ec10b2dc'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE MIND’S EYE,  by Oliver Sacks (Vintage, $15.) In these graceful essays Sacks, a practicing neurologist, explores our potential to adapt in the face of disability — among his case studies is an accomplished musician who loses the ability to read music — and describes his own ordeal with ocular cancer.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:15:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/e76e045d-cf72-5ed1-a536-7f9862a72c60', 'word_count': 501, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e76e045d-cf72-5ed1-a536-7f9862a72c60'}, {'abstract': 'While the critic was on the receiving end of some of the best literary insults of the 20th century, he nearly always gave better than he got.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/books/review/dwight-macdonalds-war-on-mediocrity.html', 'snippet': 'While the critic was on the receiving end of some of the best literary insults of the 20th century, he nearly always gave better than he got.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The late, great literary and social critic Dwight Macdonald (1906-82) was on the receiving end of some of the best literary insults of the 20th century. Gore Vidal said to him, “You have nothing to say, only to add.” Leon Trotsky reportedly declared, “Every man has a right to be stupid, but comrade Macdonald abuses the privilege.” Paul Goodman cracked, “Dwight thinks with his typewriter.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/GArner/GArner-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 794, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/GArner/GArner-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/books/review/GArner/GArner-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dwight Macdonald’s War on Mediocrity', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bring It On', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Macdonald, Dwight', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:16:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dwight Garner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dwight', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1bc7261a-46b7-538d-bd12-7ffa346256f4', 'word_count': 1266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bc7261a-46b7-538d-bd12-7ffa346256f4'}, {'abstract': 'One of the best way to learn about the potential danger in an area of Afghanistan is to watch how children watch you.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/looking-to-the-children-for-guidance-in-afghanistan/', 'snippet': 'One of the best way to learn about the potential danger in an area of Afghanistan is to watch how children watch you.', 'lead_paragraph': ' It is a truism that children represent the future, but here in Afghanistan, that future is a very uncertain one. I have interacted with Afghan children as much as possible during the first half of my yearlong tour in eastern Afghanistan and am just as uncertain about the future of this country as when I arrived. While they have provided some of my best memories and most optimistic moments, they also have given rise to some of my deepest despair. While my experiences are by no means authoritative, I offer them as an imperfect window into Afghanistan’s future.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Looking to the Children for Guidance in Afghanistan', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Aid', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Army', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:21:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Capt. Brad Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Capt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bffede3c-e5cc-5282-96a0-7c93f66fe30c', 'word_count': 738, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bffede3c-e5cc-5282-96a0-7c93f66fe30c'}, {'abstract': 'The social media scholar Reynol Junco answers questions about his recent findings — some of which may surprise you — about the kinds of behaviors correlated with certain ways college students use Facebook.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/q-and-a-how-facebook-usecorrelates-with-student-outcomes/', 'snippet': 'The social media scholar Reynol Junco answers questions about his recent findings — some of which may surprise you — about the kinds of behaviors correlated with certain ways college students use Facebook.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is Facebook a worthless waste of time for students? Does it steal time and energy that they would otherwise spend on homework? Is it focusing their attention on trivia, facilitating unhealthy interactions with peers and teachers, and turning their minds to jelly?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q. and A. | How Facebook Use Correlates With Student Outcomes', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T16:22:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a84ff7d1-2158-58c2-8e19-6fcfd988cdef', 'word_count': 2457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a84ff7d1-2158-58c2-8e19-6fcfd988cdef'}, {'abstract': 'Admirers of Andy Warhol can see two shows this fall, but there are also new exhibits more in keeping with the themes of the capital.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/arts/artsspecial/in-washington-displays-of-ingenuity-gowns-and-mail.html', 'snippet': 'Admirers of Andy Warhol can see two shows this fall, but there are also new exhibits more in keeping with the themes of the capital.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Admirers of Andy Warhol can get a double dose of the art provocateur in Washington this fall. The National Gallery of Art will explore his take on sensational tabloid news, and the Hirshhorn Museum and Sculpture Garden will take in his vibrant, but enigmatic, interpretation of abstract impressionism.', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/arts/artsspecial/23DC1/23DC1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 676, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype