ates Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T18:32:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven V. Mazie', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Mazie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b5069e2-a05e-572b-9174-feadf905c6b9', 'word_count': 1112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b5069e2-a05e-572b-9174-feadf905c6b9'}, {'abstract': 'In the video, you can hear the disbelief and giddy joy in the voices of the fighters as they surround Qaddafi, who looks like a bloody rag doll but is still visibly alive. Some of them are shouting “God is great,” but mostly the sound registers as a roar, a wordless expression of triumph.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/21/qaddafis-faustian-bargain/', 'snippet': 'In the video, you can hear the disbelief and giddy joy in the voices of the fighters as they surround Qaddafi, who looks like a bloody rag doll but is still visibly alive. Some of them are shouting “God is great,” but mostly the sound registers as...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Editor’s Note: Since the start of the Libyan revolution, Robert F. Worth has written two stories for the magazine. The first, “On Libya’s Revolutionary Road,” was published in April; the second, “The Surreal Ruins of Qaddafi’s Never-Never Land,” appeared in our Sept. 25 issue. Since the news broke of Qaddafi’s death, Worth has been reporting for the paper, but took time out to put together these thoughts below:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Qaddafi’s Faustian Bargain', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'va