r, blazes in forests with large numbers of dead, needle-less trees turned into crown fires.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Justin Gillis explained at length in an Oct. 1 article, huge tracts of forest are dying across the West and around the world as a result of infestations and other phenomena that many link to climate change. A big question for those who manage forests is how these millions of acres of dead trees will respond to wildfire. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are Dead Trees More Combustible Than Live Ones?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fires and Firefighters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forest and Brush Fires', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Idaho', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Montana', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Western States (US)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-21T19:17:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Robbins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robbins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5262bd29-5753-5671-991f-f55abde379b4', 'word_count': 611, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5262bd29-5753-5671-991f-f55abde379b4'}, {'abstract': 'Brooklyn is not known to have a substantial Libyan population, but the nation’s flag is be