d to rise, perhaps significantly, with many people reported to be stuck beneath dozens of collapsed buildings.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/24/world/europe/24quake1/24quake1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 742, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/24/world/europe/24quake1/24quake1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/24/world/europe/24quake1/24quake1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Many Die as Eastern Turkey Is Jolted by Quake', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Many Die As Turkey Is Jolted By Quake', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Earthquakes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-23T14:03:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sebnem Arsu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sebnem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arsu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ddec59f-a42f-5fa2-8791-61f524e15f89', 'word_count': 653, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ddec59f-a42f-5fa2-8791-61f524e15f89'}, {'abstract': 'Banking problems are a symptom.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/23/deck-chairs-titanic/', 'snippet': 'Banking problems are a symptom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OK, yes, European banks do need more capital. But their problems are a symptom of the underlying sovereign debt problem, which can only be resolved, if at all, with ECB lending AND a commitment to reflate. Without that, the losses on sovereign debt will blow right through any amount of newly raised bank capital.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Deck Chairs, Titanic', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-23T14:05:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8bb1ede-92f5-527f-8a44-17b3ce4fb326', 'word_count': 128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8bb1ede-92f5-527f-8a44-17b3ce4fb326'}, {'abstract': 'The Afghan president appeared to be trying to reassure Pakistan after months of increasing tensions while also urging Islamabad to sever its ties to militant extremists.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/24/world/asia/karzai-says-afghanistan-would-back-pakistan-in-a-conflict-with-us.html', 'snippet': 'The Afghan president appeared to be trying to reassure Pakistan after months of increasing tensions while also urging Islamabad to sever its ties to militant extremists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — Days after he stood with Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton and accused Pakistan of harboring his country’s enemies, President Hamid Karzai of Afghanistan said this weekend that his country would support Pakistan if it ever went to war with the United States. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Afghan President Says His Country Would Back Pakistan in a Clash With the U.S.', 'kicker': None, '