y in Blue, or Death by Poker,” by Daniel Gallant at the Nuyorican Poets Cafe, focuses on five mob bosses and a suspicious dealer who hold a territory-carving card game.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/24/theater/reviews/a-felony-in-blue-or-death-by-poker-at-nuyorican-poets-cafe.html', 'snippet': '“A Felony in Blue, or Death by Poker,” by Daniel Gallant at the Nuyorican Poets Cafe, focuses on five mob bosses and a suspicious dealer who hold a territory-carving card game.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0 When you arrive at the Nuyorican Poets Cafe in the East Village, the usher hands you a program and a few extra photocopied sheets. One shows a familiar face: that of the usher himself, the playwright-director-actor Rome Neal, currently playing Thelonious Monk in a one-man show at the Moldy Fig, a jazz club a few blocks away. He’s also happy to point you toward the bathroom, which is plastered with posters for “Rome Neal’s Banana Puddin’,” a monthly jazz jam session and open-mike night at the poets cafe.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/felony/felony-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 237, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/felony/felony-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 237}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/felony/felony-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/felony/felony-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/felony/felony-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Card Game Played With High Stakes', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'A Felony in Blue, or Death by Poker'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Card Game Played With High Stakes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Neal, Rome', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poker (Card Game)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-23T21:19:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Grode', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grode', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/24278996-cffc-5252-a3c9-0fb1e31fcf68', 'word_count': 358, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24278996-cffc-5252-a3c9-0fb1e31fcf68'}, {'abstract': '“Monster In-Laws,” a new A&E reality show, takes families with in-law issues and pairs them with a “relationship expert” who is supposed to help them find a more workable way of interacting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/24/arts/television/monster-in-laws-reality-show-on-ae-review.html', 'snippet': '“Monster In-Laws,” a new A&E reality show, takes families with in-law issues and pairs them with a “relationship expert” who is supposed to help them find a more workable way of interacting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the people-behaving-dysfunctionally genre of reality television you’re likely to see almost anyone wander across the screen in addition to the loony stars: their relatives, their hairstylists, their therapists. But there’s one type of person you almost never see, because to put such a person on camera would undercut the fly-on-the-wall conceit behind these shows: a member of the crew doing the filming.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/monster/monster-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/monster/monster-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/monster/monster-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/monster/monster-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/monster/monster-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Family Dysfunctions and the Duct Tape Détente', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Family Dysfunctions and the Duct Tape Détente', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-23T21:28:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/061b3948-ff9b-5627-99df-0c13b25cc4db', 'word_count': 481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/061b3948-ff9b-5627-99df-0c13b25cc4db'}, {'abstract': 'Rick Perry has performed poorly in the G.O.P. debates and his ratings have fallen. Does it matter?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/roomfordebate/2011/10/23/do-good-debaters-make-good-presidents/', 'snippet': 'Rick Perry has performed poorly in the G.O.P. debates and his ratings have fallen. Does it matter?', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Do Good Debaters Make Good Presidents?', 'kicker': 'Room for Debate', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-23T21:30:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/44b5d699-3b09-5a62-8df3-85b8a9f68a53', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44b5d699-3b09-5a62-8df3-85b8a9f68a53'}, {'abstract': 'At the Chocolate Factory, the choreographer Lyndsey Karr and Gina Kohler performed “the One,” a dance production about love from a female perspective.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/24/arts/dance/lyndsey-karrs-one-at-chocolate-factory-review.html', 'snippet': 'At the Chocolate Factory, the choreographer Lyndsey Karr and Gina Kohler performed “the One,” a dance production about love from a female perspective.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Judging by her new work, which explores what it means to be a woman in love — its cheeky title is simply “the One” — it just wouldn’t do to describe Lyndsey Karr as the romantic type. As two women, more often naked than not, take the audience on a trip to three sites within the Chocolate Factory in Long Island City, Queens, Ms. Karr, with deadpan whimsy, both demonstrates and ridicules archetypes of the besotted. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/24one2/24one2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/24one2/24one2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/24one2/24one2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 671, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/24one2/24one2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/24one2/24one2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Women in Love Leap Into Elevator Shafts', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Women in Love Leap Into Elevator Shafts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chocolate Factory, The', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-23T21:34:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d78b2b65-771a-53ef-9401-39ac69106f77', 'word_count': 416, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d78b2b65-771a-53ef-9401-39ac69106f77'}, {'abstract': 'The guitarist George Benson performed covers, some of his original hits and selections from his new album, “Guitar Man,” at Town Hall on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/24/arts/music/george-benson-tours-with-guitar-man-at-town-hall-review.html', 'snippet': 'The guitarist George Benson performed covers, some of his original hits and selections from his new album, “Guitar Man,” at Town Hall on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'George Benson comes from the school of jazz in which you play frantic bebop guitar solos in long phrases over tight disco beats, sing love ballads, mug through exaggerated voices, provide your own canned laughter, tell stories about Miles Davis and James Brown and, like a ventriloquist, say “Shut up” to your instrument. It’s the school of jazz in which your keyboardist uses synthesizer settings to conjure the flutes and woodwinds that aren’t there — even playing an entire solo in fake-flute tones — and your showman drummer plays hammer-blow downbeats and moth-wing cymbal touches. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/benson/benson-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/benson/benson-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/benson/benson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/benson/benson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/24/arts/benson/benson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '’60s Soul-Jazz Filtered Through a Quiet Storm', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '’60s Soul-Jazz Filtered Through a Quiet Storm', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-23T21:40:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/07710585-ea6b-58ec-ae85-75ef85e2a7a2', 'word_count': 591, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07710585-ea6b-58ec-ae85-75ef85e2a7a2'}, {'abstract': 'At the world bridge championships in Veldhoven, the Netherlands, the six American teams are locked in close battles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/24/crosswords/bridge/us-teams-in-close-battles-at-bridge-championships.html', 'snippet': 'At the world bridge championships in Veldhoven, the Netherlands, the six American teams are locked in close battles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VELDHOVEN, the Netherlands — At the world championships here, after 48 boards of the 96-board quarterfinals, the six United States teams are locked in close battles.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/arts/bridge-inline/bridge-inline-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 617, 'width': 299, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Double Turns Double-Edged', 'kicker': 'Bridge', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Double Turns Double-Edged', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'ra