rticle', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1d7f9cf-8563-5ed9-b8bd-a68a61c4b611', 'word_count': 838, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1d7f9cf-8563-5ed9-b8bd-a68a61c4b611'}, {'abstract': 'The soprano Audra McDonald, who has been away in the realm of television, showed her singing chops at Carnegie Hall on Saturday evening.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/24/arts/music/audra-mcdonald-at-carnegie-hall-review.html', 'snippet': 'The soprano Audra McDonald, who has been away in the realm of television, showed her singing chops at Carnegie Hall on Saturday evening.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Audra McDonald’s Carnegie Hall concert on Saturday evening had the feel of a homecoming celebration for a prodigal daughter: in an unbroken wave of adoration, all was forgiven. Ms. McDonald’s crime, if there was one, was her temporary abandonment of musical theater for television. For four seasons, while playing Dr. Naomi Bennett in the ABC series “Private Practice,” she more or less disappeared from music. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/arts/music/audra/audra-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/23/arts/music/audra/audra-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/23/arts/music/audra/audra-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Prodigal Daughter Returns Home', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Prodigal Daughter Returns Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McDonald, Audra', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-24T00:22:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bed33f9-47b1-5856-99ad-4faab56a80e5', 'word_count': 565, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bed33f9-47b1-5856-99ad-4faab56a80e5'}, {'abstract': 'The leader of Libya’s transitional government declared to thousands of revelers in Benghazi that Libya’s revolution had ended, setting the country on the path to elections.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/10/24/world/africa/20111024_LIBYA.html', 'snippet': 'The leader of Libya’s transitional government declared to thousands of revelers in Benghazi that Libya’s revolution had ended, setting the country on the path to elections.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The leader of Libya’s transitional government declared to thousands of revelers in Benghazi that Libya’s revolution had ended, setting the country on the path to elections.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/24/world/africa/20111024_LIBYA-slide-FA8N/20111024_LIBYA-slide-FA8N-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 630, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/24/world/africa/20111024_LIBYA-slide-FA8N/20111024_LIBYA-slide-FA8N-t