llpens', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-24T10:30:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Keh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f19a5543-95bd-5a36-b4c4-7b380b661e0d', 'word_count': 262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f19a5543-95bd-5a36-b4c4-7b380b661e0d'}, {'abstract': 'Twitter serves many purposes — meme manufacturing, revolution aggregation, troll facilitation — but it also functions as a real-time discussion board for the things we see on television.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/24/the-new-rules-for-tv-spoilers/', 'snippet': 'Twitter serves many purposes — meme manufacturing, revolution aggregation, troll facilitation — but it also functions as a real-time discussion board for the things we see on television.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Editor’s Note: In the new issue of the magazine, Dan Kois — cultural critic and noted spoilerologist — wrote a Bonus Feature on “The New Rules for TV Spoilers,” in part inspired by the furor on Twitter over spoilers for the recent season finale for “Breaking Bad.” This is a special extended version of that piece, for The 6th Floor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The New Rules for TV Spoilers', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cable Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter'