' A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wnallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday Reading: Knowing When to Refinance', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-24T13:50:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e70d8782-8f4c-51c1-9388-4be3ca1e9e58', 'word_count': 165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e70d8782-8f4c-51c1-9388-4be3ca1e9e58'}, {'abstract': 'We are going to collect more information about ourselves and the world than we knew existed. Expect a bust, along with a benefit.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/24/big-data/', 'snippet': 'We are going to collect more information about ourselves and the world than we knew existed. Expect a bust, along with a benefit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is Big Data a Bubble?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Big Business of ‘Big Data’', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data Storage', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Enterprise Computing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-24T13:51:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Quentin Hardy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Quentin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hardy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd26a7f2-c82a-5484-b71d-45de9d0ebf97', 'word_count': 1006, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd26a7f2-c82a-5484-b71d-45de9d0ebf97'}, {'abstract': 'A postcard from a Colombian ranch managed to maintain a rich range of wildlife.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/24/a-ranch-with-room-for-cattle-and-capybara/', 'snippet': 'A postcard from a Colombian ranch managed to maintain a rich range of wildlife.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s a Dot Earth Postcard from Rhett Butler, the founder of the essential Mongabay.com blog tracking issues related to conserving forests and biological diversity worldwide. Butler took the images above while visiting a ranch in Colombia that is managed to accommodate both livestock and wildlife. Whether in South America, the Adirondacks, the Serengeti or Asia, there’s enormous logic in finding ways to mesh human livelihoods and wildlife that don’t involve fences. The card was prompted indirectly by my recent coverage of raids of Gibson Guitar facilities under environmental laws. His dispatch is below (I’ve added some contextual links): ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Ranch With Room for Cattle and Capybara', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMALS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biodiversity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Land Use Policies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ranches', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AMAZON JUNGLE', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colombia', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pantanal (Brazil)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South America', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-24T14:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46f6e6c8-6cf8-5406-b862-cf0afe78ba1f', 'word_count': 753, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46f6e6c8-6cf8-5406-b862-cf0afe78ba1f'}, {'abstract': 'The Los Angeles artist Liz Glynn will retrace Buckminster Fuller’s steps to build a life-size geodesic dome, pratfalls and all, at Performa.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/24/domebound/', 'snippet': 'The Los Angeles artist Liz Glynn will retrace Buckminster Fuller’s steps to build a life-size geodesic dome, pratfalls and all, at Performa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1948, the architect and inventor Buckminster Fuller gathered a group of his students at Black Mountain College to build a life-size geodesic dome. The dome was a flop. It succeeded, however, in planting the seed for the sculptor Kenneth Snelson, a student of Fuller’s, to develop the principle of tensegrity that would allow Fuller to build a dome for the United States Pavilion at Expo 67 in Montreal. For the Performa 11 biennial (11.performa-arts.org), the Los Angeles artist Liz Glynn will retrace Fuller’s steps, pratfalls and all. Part 1 of her performance — a loosely choreographed re-enactment of Fuller’s failed attempt — takes place on the first Sunday of the biennial in locations throughout New York City, including Times Square; for Part 2, 25 volunteers will assist in building a dome. Whether it will stand remains to be seen. But Glynn’s cast-concrete dome sculptures (above), on view at the Performa Hub through Nov. 21, are objects of perfection of which even Fuller might have approved. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Domebound', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-24T14:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marina Cashdan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cashdan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4490a986-8b60-5e82-b891-3fad6b9614b8', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4490a986-8b60-5e82-b891-3fad6b9614b8'}, {'abstract': 'How dare those Argentine voters reward a successful government?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/24/unacceptable-success/', 'snippet': 'How dare those Argentine voters reward a successful government?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dean Baker is once again on the warpath over Argentina, this time over news reports that treat Kirchner’s reelection as somehow mysterious. The economy is booming and the opposition is hopelessly divided; how on earth did she win?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Unacceptable Success', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-24T14:09:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a39adac-3328-50b4-8e36-23f3d76f98b2', 'word_count': 148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a39adac-3328-50b4-8e36-23f3d76f98b2'}, {'abstract': 'Realizing that its recycling rate has declined, the city studies garbage disposal and recycling practices in cities ranging from San Francisco to Seoul.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/24/new-york-recycling-reprobate-aims-to-do-better/', 'snippet': 'Realizing that its recycling rate has declined, the city studies garbage disposal and recycling practices in cities ranging from San Francisco to Seoul.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I reported in Sunday’s Times, New York City is exploring ways to reduce waste and improve its residential recycling rate, which is down from what it was a decade ago. One strategy practiced in many large cities is the “pay as you throw” system, which charges residents on the basis of weight for the trash they throw away.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York, Recycling Reprobate, Aims to Do Better', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recycling of Waste Materials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Seoul (South Korea)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-24T14:27:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mireya Navarro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mireya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Navarro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_o