er': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13326ea0-b211-57f1-b309-86434f9976b9', 'word_count': 1109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13326ea0-b211-57f1-b309-86434f9976b9'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Tom Corbett on Monday declared a fiscal emergency in Harrisburg, which allows him to assume financial control of the city and name the state’s first municipal receiver.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/us/pennsylvania-governor-declares-emergency-in-harrisburg.html', 'snippet': 'Gov. Tom Corbett on Monday declared a fiscal emergency in Harrisburg, which allows him to assume financial control of the city and name the state’s first municipal receiver.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Tom Corbett on Monday declared a fiscal emergency in Harrisburg, which allows him to assume financial control of the city and name the state’s first municipal receiver. Empowered by legislation he signed on Thursday, Mr. Corbett, left, said the capital city’s fiscal distress warranted such action. The Harrisburg City Council’s “failure to enact a recovery plan in order to deal with the city’s distressed finances has led me to declare a fiscal emergency,” Mr. Corbett, a Republican, said in a news release. “This action ensures that vital services will continue and public safety will be protected.” Mr. Corbett can now direct city and state officials to comply with his plan to ensure that debt obligations and payroll expenses are met. A receiver can be named in 30 days if officials fail to devise a full recovery plan that is acceptable to the state. This month, Harrisburg became the first state capital in at least 40 years to file for bankruptcy protection. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pennsylvania: Governor Declares Emergency', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Mid-Atlantic', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pennsylvania: Governor Declares Emergency', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Harrisburg (Pa)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disasters and Emergencies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Corbett, Thomas W', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T00:13:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bloomberg News', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bloomberg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'News', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd8c4d5d-0ac5-56b5-9fd8-fc4defc242c2', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd8c4d5d-0ac5-56b5-9fd8-fc4defc242c2'}, {'abstract': 'Under the temporary agreement, relatives of both Nazish Noorani and Kashif Parvaiz, who is charged with murdering her, will share their two children.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/nyregion/after-wife-killing-charge-2-families-share-custody.html', 'snippet': 'Under the temporary agreement, relatives of both Nazish Noorani and Kashif Parvaiz, who is charged with murdering her, will share their two children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The two families fighting for custody of two young boys whose mother was killed in New Jersey this summer will share the children for now, according to a temporary agreement approved on Monday by a Family Court judge in Brooklyn.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Custody Deal for 2 Families After Killing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Custody Deal For 2 Families After Killing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Katz, Michael', 'rank': 1, '