, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subways', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T01:23:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5dd0f77b-861e-5ad6-aa5f-4f3e9e631f87', 'word_count': 751, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5dd0f77b-861e-5ad6-aa5f-4f3e9e631f87'}, {'abstract': 'When the “roadless rule” was upheld in court last week, the environment and its allies got a big victory for a change.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/opinion/one-win-for-the-forests.html', 'snippet': 'When the “roadless rule” was upheld in court last week, the environment and its allies got a big victory for a change.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The environment and its allies have not had many victories recently, but there was a big one on Friday when the United States Court of Appeals for the 10th Circuit upheld the “roadless rule” — a Clinton administration regulation barring new road-building on 50 million acres of the national forests. The timber industry, off-road vehicle users and other commercial and political interests have long battled the rule, which will establish essential protections for the forests and the clean water, wildlife habitat and recreational opportunities they provide.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'One Win for the Forests', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'One Win for the Forests', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wilderness Areas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Land Use Policies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Homeland Security Department', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Forest Service', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCarthy, Kevin (1965- )', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T01:29:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c8a9627-9d55-5ddc-8d43-1c8817f9f785', 'word_count': 258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c8a9627-9d55-5ddc-8d43-1c8817f9f785'}, {'abstract': 'Two Civil War regiments, drawn from Boston’s elite, suffered some of the worst casualties of the war.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/24/the-purchase-by-blood/', 'snippet': 'Two Civil War regiments, drawn from Boston’s elite, suffered some of the worst casualties of the war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two cousins, on the field at battle’s start, catch each other’s eye, salute, and then — “when next I looked,” Oliver Wendell Holmes Jr. writes of his cousin, James Jackson Lowell, “he was gone.” At Cedar Mountain, Capt. Henry Sturgis Russell is captured because he has stayed to tend the wounds of his friend, Maj. James Savage. But once in Confederate hands the two are parted: Russell is exchanged while Savage begins the slow decline that will end in his lonely death at the hands of incompetent surgeons in a Confederate hospital. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Purchase by Blood', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War and Guerrilla Warfare', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Friendship', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T01:30:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Bundy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bundy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62721bdf-3301-54b0-8757-6bda621e4ed4', 'word_count': 1296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62721bdf-3301-54b0-8757-6bda621e4ed4'}, {'abstract': 'Networks of informants have helped capture or kill about two dozen high and midlevel drug traffickers, and sometimes given American agents access to the top cartel leaders.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/world/americas/united-states-infiltrating-criminal-groups-across-mexico.html', 'snippet': 'Networks of informants have helped capture or kill about two dozen high and midlevel drug traffickers, and sometimes given American agents access to the top cartel leaders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — American law enforcement agencies have significantly built up networks of Mexican informants that have allowed them to secretly infiltrate some of that country’s most powerful and dangerous criminal organizations, according to security officials on both sides of the border. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/25/world/JP-DEA-1/JP-DEA-1-articleLarg