 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fbdb085-e98b-5fb9-ae2d-d0ce7f606224', 'word_count': 999, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fbdb085-e98b-5fb9-ae2d-d0ce7f606224'}, {'abstract': 'A fifth-generation farmer in northern Maine shifts from corn rows, hay fields and dairy cows to growing organic vegetables and lots of garlic.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/from-a-farm-stand-new-and-old-insights/', 'snippet': 'A fifth-generation farmer in northern Maine shifts from corn rows, hay fields and dairy cows to growing organic vegetables and lots of garlic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Living in a cabin in the woods with three boys, two cats and a flexible schedule means every day is different. But there’s one constant to my weeks. On Thursday afternoons, I know I’ll be in a town 30 minutes away standing under a white awning and waiting in line.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From a Farm Stand, New and Old Insights', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organic Foods and Products', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sustainable Living', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maine', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T12:05:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Craig Leisher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Craig', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leisher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b79f6474-bbfe-584c-b296-a91382e287d8', 'word_count': 704, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b79f6474-bbfe-584c-b296-a91382e287d8'}, {'abstract': 'The Cleveland-Seattle offensive dud was merely an appetizer for “Monday Night Football.”', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/in-week-7-passing-defenses-continue-to-show-pop/', 'snippet': 'The Cleveland-Seattle offensive dud was merely an appetizer for “Monday Night Football.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, I wrote that the monster passing numbers from the first five weeks took a serious body blow in Week 6, evidenced by a huge decline in passing touchdowns. The knockout punch was delivered in Week 7. After teams averaged over 240 passing yards per game through the first six weeks of the season, the 26 teams playing in Week 7 combined for only 4,997 passing yards this past weekend, a paltry 191 yards per game. Nowhere was this more evident — on Sunday — than in Cleveland, as the Seahawks and Browns put on a pitiful display. The Browns and Seahawks combined for 435 yards and 9 points. It was the worst display of offense in a game not affected by severe weather in over a decade. The Colt McCoy pass attempt watch marches on: he threw 35 passes in a game in which the Browns allowed only 137 yards and 3 points.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Week 7, Passing Defenses Continue to Show Pop', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T12:05:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chase Stuart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chase', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stuart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0373b93-17e7-589d-b551-ca8f045992c6', 'word_count': 3049, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0373b93-17e7-589d-b551-ca8f045992c6'}, {'abstract': 'Reassessing the role of mammograms, a limited expansion of a mortgage program, how iPads affect hotel Internet systems and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/tuesday-reading-reassessing-the-role-of-mammograms/', 'snippet': 'Reassessing the role of mammograms, a limited expansion of a mortgage program, how iPads affect hotel Internet systems and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tuesday Reading: Reassessing the Role of Mammograms', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T12:09:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/260c03e6-e50b-5189-b44d-c23b9a04e23b', 'word_count': 128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/260c03e6-e50b-5189-b44d-c23b9a04e23b'}, {'abstract': 'Unusually heavy flooding and typhoons have killed 800 people and affected more than eight million in an arc that stretches across Thailand, Cambodia, Laos, Vietnam and the Philippines.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/26/world/asia/flood-waters-in-bangkok-shut-domestic-airport.html', 'sni