 Barron’s ran an article that was a virtual indictment of Amazon. It questioned the company’s ability ever to turn a profit, argued that Amazon was spending far too much money on marketing and suggested that the company was using misleading accounting metrics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T12:59:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77810237-0e35-5646-8f47-f0cfd865b2ae', 'word_count': 1948, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77810237-0e35-5646-8f47-f0cfd865b2ae'}, {'abstract': 'Amazon reveals more problems with a warehouse as it prepares to announce its third-quarter earnings.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/amazon-hot-and-cold/', 'snippet': 'Amazon reveals more problems with a warehouse as it prepares to announce its third-quarter earnings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After the close of the stock market Tuesday, Amazon.com will announce its third-quarter results. Analysts are predicting yet another revenue blowout, up 44 percent compared with last year. Amazon’s torrid growth more than 15 years after its founding makes it an object of envy and fear across the retail world. It is now a $100 billion company, about 12 times the value of Sears.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Amazon, Hot and Cold', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bezos, Jeffrey P', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Silicon Valley (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T13:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Streitfeld', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Streitfeld', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16f50108-20c2-504c-ae99-5fcca6ea02bc', 'word_count': 548, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16f50108-20c2-504c-ae99-5fcca6ea02bc'}, {'abstract': 'A new show illuminates this English dandy’s astonishingly productive engagement with the booming cultural metropolis that was Manhattan at midcentury.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/now-viewing-cecil-beatons-new-york/', 'snippet': 'A new show illuminates this English dandy’s astonishingly productive engagement with the booming cultural metropolis that was Manhattan at midcentury.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What was Manhattan for the wildly ambitious, talented and tirelessly self-promoting 24-year-old Cecil Beaton, but a candy box of opportunities, just waiting to be picked? And pick them he did, over the course of some five decades, in a peripatetic career that saw him cross-pollinating the social and cultural elites of the Old and New Worlds, and applying his protean gifts to everything from fashion photography and portraiture to illustration to stage and costume design. “Cecil Beaton: The New York Years,” curated by Donald Albrecht and opening today at the Museum of the City of New York, includes vintage fashion prints and a wide range of portraits, from Marilyn Monroe (cajoled into relaxation in Beaton’s Ambassador Hotel suite) to the very young Mick Jagger to Wallis Simpson and the aging Greta Garbo (one of the photographer’s rare heterosexual liaisons). Together with drawings, theatrical designs and other ephemera, they illuminate this English dandy’s astonishingly productive and long-lasting engagement with the booming cultural metropolis that was Manhattan at midcentury.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Viewing | Cecil Beaton’s New York', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T13:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leslie Camhi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leslie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Camhi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4c0dba1-5727-5d81-b828-91812b57ba65', 'word_count': 420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4c0dba1-5727-5d81-b828-91812b57ba65'}, {'abstract': 'ArcelorMittal, the world’s largest steel manufacturer, announced on Tuesday that it was pulling out of a $4.9 billion joint deal to acquire Macarthur Coal of Australia, leaving Peabody Energy as the sole buyer.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/arcelormittal-backs-out-of-bid-to-buy-macarthur-coal/', 'snippet': 'ArcelorMittal, the world’s largest steel manufacturer, announced on Tuesday that it was pulling out of a $4.9 billion joint deal to acquire Macarthur Coal of Australia, leaving Peabody Energy as the sole buyer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ArcelorMittal, the world’s largest steel manufacturer, announced on Tuesday that it was pulling out of a $4.9 billion joint bid with Peabody Energy to acquire Macarthur Coal of Australia.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'ArcelorMittal Backs Out of Bid for Macarthur Coal', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T13:01:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9902846b-f9a8-5e7b-b8d3-60393ab7693c', 'word_count': 236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9902846b-f9a8-5e7b-b8d3-60393ab7693c'}, {'abstract': 'Slash spending now, or we may be forced into austerity!', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/very-serious-americans/', 'snippet': 'Slash spending now, or we may be forced into austerity!', 'lead_paragraph': 'Digby finds Paul Ryan, winner of an award for fiscal responsibility, saying, well, something:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Very Serious Americans', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T13:02:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e27a982-4d29-5899-af68-4881f93d4071', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e27a982-4d29-5899-af68-4881f93d4071'}, {'abstract': 'Federal officials, citing health and safety issues, will not subsidize the high-rise after Nov. 1, and some tenants say they are too poor to move.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/26/nyregion/as-newark-tower-loses-us-aid-tenants-fear-eviction.html', 'snippet': 'Federal officials, citing health and safety issues, will not subsidize the high-rise after Nov. 1, and some tenants say they are too poor to move.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When it opened in 1971, Carmel Towers in Newark was a model of the sort of high-rise urban development that the federal government was encouraging through generous subsidies. Now, federal officials are cutting off financial aid to the building’s landlord, leaving hundreds of residents fearing that they could soon be evicted.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/nyregion/25Newark1/25Newark1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/26/nyregion/25Newark1/25Newark1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/nyregion/25Newark1/25Newark1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 654, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/nyregion/25Newark1/25Newark1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/26/nyregion/25Newark1/25Newark1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As Newark High-Rise Loses Aid, Fear Sweeps Through Its Tenants', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Newark High-Rise Loses Aid, Fear Sweeps Through Its Tenants', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evictions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public and Subsidized Housing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Newark Housing Authority', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Newark (NJ)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Housing and Urban Development Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vouchers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Aid (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T13:09:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGeehan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGeehan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bdf4983e-60dd-52d8-a5e8-8cb935b4bab6', 'word_count': 859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bdf4983e-60dd-52d8-a5e8-8cb935b4bab6'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Rick Perry of Texas discussed his new tax plan, his recent birther comments, Mitt Romney and other issues with John Harwood.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/10-questions-for-rick-perry/', 'snippet': 'Gov. Rick Perry of Texas discussed his new tax plan, his recent birther comments, Mitt Romney and other issues with John Harwood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Rick Perry of Texas is seeking to jump-start his Republican presidential campaign by proposing a new tax system: a 20 percent flat rate on individual and corporate income, down from the current 35 percent top rate, and elimination of existing taxes on estates, capital gains and dividends. He sat down with John Harwood of The Times and CNBC to discuss the plan, his challenge to a Republican rival, Mitt Romney, and other issues. What follows is a condensed, edited transcript of their conversation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '10 Questions for Rick Perry', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harwood, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T13:20:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Harwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/868d0082-dc30-5c48-95a5-3185ab80b194', 'word_count': 922, 'uri': 'nyt://article/868d0082-dc30-5c48-95a5-3185ab80b194'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Market is not impressed.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/irish-reality-check/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Market is not impressed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There have been a number of articles and blog posts lately claiming that Ireland is over the hump, that it’s all coming right; see for example this recent piece by Henry Kaspar. But I was checking to see if it was still true that Icelandic CDS prices — the cost of insuring against default — were much lower than Ireland’s. And given what we’ve been hearing, the Irish CDS story comes as something of a shock:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Irish Reality Check', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T13:28:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5e159b1-f8cd-537d-96fc-d3acfd0a1853', 'word_count': 194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5e159b1-f8cd-537d-96fc-d3acfd0a1853'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/what-were-reading-297/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Guardian: Jamie Oliver, a.k.a. Sisyphus, is growing concerned that the British government is backsliding on its commitment to better school lunches.— Jeff Gordinier', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T13:39:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad6c1644-654e-5326-87d1-1eedca6cc5d0', 'word_count': 379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad6c1644-654e-5326-87d1-1eedca6cc5d0'}, {'abstract': 'Concerns about the outcome of a meeting of European leaders on Wednesday weighed on financial stocks especially.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/26/business/daily-stock-market-activity.html', 'snippet': 'Concerns about the outcome of a meeting of European leaders on Wednesday weighed on financial stocks especially.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stocks in the United States and Europe sank Tuesday as the markets got another jolt from the European Union — this time the abrupt cancellation of a meeting of finance ministers that was meant to precede a leaders’ summit meeting on Wednesday. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stocks Close Sharply Lower on News From Europe', 'kicker': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stocks Close Sharply Lower on News From Europe', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T13:47:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a7dc798-5abd-55b7-ab05-17d1a826f582', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a7dc798-5abd-55b7-ab05-17d1a826f582'}, {'abstract': 'Some banks’ online application processes aren’t as seamless as they should be, according to a new Javelin research report.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/online-account-opening-isnt-as-easy-as-it-looks/', 'snippet': 'Some banks’ online application processes aren’t as seamless as they should be, according to a new Javelin research report.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’ve ever tried — and failed — to open and fund an online checking account, you’re not alone. A new report from Javelin Strategy & Research finds that only a little more than half of consumers who try to do so are successful.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Online Account Opening Isn’t as Easy as It Looks', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of America Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Citigroup Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ING Groep NV', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T14:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fff1f7fc-9fa9-5297-aa0f-b3fd195e134e', 'word_count': 418, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fff1f7fc-9fa9-5297-aa0f-b3fd195e134e'}, {'abstract': 'A British charity organization that promotes the study and life of Shakespeare, is protesting the release of “Anonymous,” a movie by Roland Emmerich that questions the authorship of his works, by covering over Shakespeare’s name on several signs.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/shakespeare-trust-hides-shakespeares-name-to-protest-shakespeare-film/', 'snippet': 'A British charity organization that promotes the study and life of Shakespeare, is protesting the release of “Anonymous,” a movie by Roland Emmerich that questions the authorship of his works, by covering over Shakespeare’s name on several signs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Shakespeare Birthplace Trust had no apparent problems with Roland Emmerich‘s previous movies about genocidal space aliens, apocalyptic solar flares and life in prehistoric times. But when Mr. Emmerich messed with Shakespeare, that’s where the trust drew the line – right through the name of the playwright it is set up to protect.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shakespeare Trust Hides Playwright’s Name to Protest ‘Anonymous’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emmerich, Roland', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Shakespeare Birthplace Trust', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T14:11:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f96f019-31f1-53a5-aae2-0bca91116a69', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f96f019-31f1-53a5-aae2-0bca91116a69'}, {'abstract': 'For those who love cats, a new high-tech way to frolic with felines.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/playtime-with-cats-at-any-distance/', 'snippet': 'For those who love cats, a new high-tech way to frolic with felines.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While visiting her mother in Pennsylvania this month, Lauren Pytel, a 47-year-old high school English teacher who lives in Tudor City, found herself pining for her cats, Tiggles ap Caduet and Munchie Effexorov, and their recently deceased siblings, Claritin and Zoloft. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Playtime With Cats, at Any Distance', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMALS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cats', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Remote Control Systems', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T14:17:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anna Jane Grossman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': 'Jane', 'lastname': 'Grossman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbb6bc28-a9c2-590e-a764-bdf82d3b3301', 'word_count': 935, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbb6bc28-a9c2-590e-a764-bdf82d3b3301'}, {'abstract': 'Game 5 put a crimp into Tony La Russa’s reputation as a genius micromanager, and Texas catcher Mike Napoli has his team one win from a World Series.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/leading-off-calling-all-geniuses/', 'snippet': 'Game 5 put a crimp into Tony La Russa’s reputation as a genius micromanager, and Texas catcher Mike Napoli has his team one win from a World Series.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If nothing else, Game 5 of the World Series makes us want to hit the pause button on baseball history. Because even if you know only a little about the game, you know Tony La Russa is supposed to be a genius. He will tell you that himself. Baseball announcers will tell you. Over and over and over. Isn’t it written in the rule book, right there with three strikes and you’re out? The old skit was “Who’s on first, what’s on second and Tony La Russa is a genius,” wasn’t it?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Leading Off: Calling All Geniuses', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emmert, Mark', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'la Russa, Tony', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Napoli, Mike', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Washington, Ron', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Texas Rangers', 'rank': 6, 'major':