sed by the current pastor of his old parish. But what did Mr. Carlin actually say about his old church and Catholic school?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before the dirty words came the classroom pranks. The petty theft of subway riders’ fedoras. The visions of sneaking into confessional and acting as de facto priest for a day.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Carlin Street’ Resisted by His Old Church. Was It Something He Said?', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Names, Geographical', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private and Sectarian Schools', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carlin, George', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Corpus Christi Church', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Morningside Heights (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T16:12:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Flegenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flegenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae17ba6a-1def-598f-a56f-effcc368f736', 'word_count': 1003, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae17ba6a-1def-598f-a56f-effcc368f736'}, {'abstract': 'The out-of-this-world looks from the two pop stars can be re-created this Halloween with items you might already own.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/embracing-the-pop-star-in-your-closet/', 'snippet': 'The out-of-this-world looks from the two pop stars can be re-created this Halloween with items you might already own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN New Yorkers want Halloween costumes with sartorial flair, they skip costume shops and drugstores and head instead to the Patricia Field boutique in the Bowery, where glitter wigs and bondage pants are the everyday garb of Ms. Field’s regular customers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Embracing the Pop Star in Your Closet', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T16:18:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Rosenbloom', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenbloom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58f60744-6a30-5a5c-b8b4-1ceb5519ff50', 'word_count': 932, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58f60744-6a30-5a5c-b8b4-1ceb5519ff50'}, {'abstract': 'Changers, a German-based start-up, will soon begin offering an ultra-simple solar charging device that can also track the amount of money and CO2 people save.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/charging-your-devices-through-solar-power/', 'snippet': 'Changers, a German-based start-up, will soon begin offering an ultra-simple solar charging device that can also track the amount of money and CO2 people save.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Companies have offered dozens of products that are supposed to let you charge a mobile device through solar power. But most have failed dismally. Some take too long to charge even a single battery, while others come with tedious connectors and wires.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Charging Your Devices Through Solar Power', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Solar Energy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T16:23:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c743a45b-db04-54d6-80c3-0dc0663ca257', 'word_count': 307, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c743a45b-db04-54d6-80c3-0dc0663ca257'}, {'abstract': 'Middle-class families are struggling to pay for long-term care.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/the-crisis-in-long-term-care/', 'snippet': 'Middle-class families are struggling to pay for long-term care.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The apparent demise of the Class Act leaves many middle-income Americans struggling to cope with rising expenses for long-term care for family members, The Times reported on Tuesday. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Crisis in Long-Term Care', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T16:26:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5194746-b7f9-5667-92bb-e6a34c1e2423', 'word_count': 368, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5194746-b7f9-5667-92bb-e6a34c1e2423'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Emmert, the N.C.A.A. president, backs a proposal to up grants to $2,000 for student-athletes. But a group of athletes, via petition, clearly wants more. What do you think?', 'web_url': 'https://thequad.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/n-c-a-a-s-grant-hike-proposal-fair-or-foul/', 'snippet': 'Mark Emmert, the N.C.A.A. president, backs a proposal to up grants to $2,000 for student-athletes. But a group of athletes, via petition, clearly wants more. What do you think?', 'lead_paragraph': 'With Mark Emmert, the N.C.A.A. president, coming out in favor of increasing athlete grants by $2,000, have we finally started working through the issue of student-athletes being compensated for playing college sports? Or is it the opening of Pandora’s box, deepening the competitive gulf between the larger, richer schools and the smaller, less cash-rich ones?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'N.C.A.A.’s Grant Hike Proposal: Fair or Foul?', 'kicker': 'The Quad', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emmert, Mark', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Collegiate Athletic Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T16:30:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne C. Gerstner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Gerstner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb0da696-f65a-5ab5-b8ee-003d5731c4ef', 'word_count': 587, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb0da696-f65a-5ab5-b8ee-003d5731c4ef'}, {'abstract': 'Two short films individually examine two seemingly disparate subjects and their respective influences on public space in 1960s and ’70s American suburbia.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/space-invaders-skateboarding-and-post-minimalism/', 'snippet': 'Two short films individually examine two seemingly disparate subjects and their respective influences on public space in 1960s and ’70s American suburbia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What do skateboarding and post-minimalist art have in common? More than you might guess, as will be revealed at the Emily Harvey Foundation in SoHo when Redmond Entwistle’s “Monuments” (2009) and Raphael Zarka’s “Species of Spaces in Skateboarding” (2008) screen together this week. These two short films individually examine two seemingly disparate subjects and their respective influences on public space in 1960s and ’70s American suburbia.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Space Invaders | Skateboarding and Post-Minimalism', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T16:30:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin McGarry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGarry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/690973d5-af1a-5dd3-bfd0-adc402dfe911', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/690973d5-af1a-5dd3-bfd0-adc402dfe911'}, {'abstract': 'The International League of Conservation Photographers has 102 members around the world addressing issues from poaching to global warming to deforestation.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/a-photographic-call-to-action/', 'snippet': 'The International League of Conservation Photographers has 102 members around the world addressing issues from poaching to global warming to deforestation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Clyde Butcher first began exploring the Everglades in 1984, “there was virtually nothing to photograph — it was one big ditch,” he says. A century of destructive intrusions like artificial canals, levees, pumps and spillways had left the swamps in a fragile and broken state.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Photographic Call to Action', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Everglades (Fla)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Madagascar', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T16:42:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Nuwer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nuwer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54bed033-fab7-574d-a4f1-237dfe481465', 'word_count': 525, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54bed033-fab7-574d-a4f1-237dfe481465'}, {'abstract': 'The Detroit automaker continued to be criticized for its MyFord Touch infotainment system, while Japanese manufacturers maintained their dominance.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/japanese-brands-dominate-consumer-reports-reliability-survey-while-ford-falls-10-spots/', 'snippet': 'The Detroit automaker continued to be criticized for its MyFord Touch infotainment system, while Japanese manufacturers maintained their dominance.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Japane