raditional Swedish specialties like herring dishes will now be served from late afternoon through evening in the bar and lounge.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A New Look for Aquavit', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T17:27:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/10d8ab7c-c7c0-5c13-8c1f-3b79a7e111a2', 'word_count': 105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10d8ab7c-c7c0-5c13-8c1f-3b79a7e111a2'}, {'abstract': 'Elementary schools shouldn’t be spending money on computers, a reader says, since play, not technology, helps young minds thrive. Readers are invited to respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/26/opinion/invitation-to-a-dialogue-computers-in-school.html', 'snippet': 'Elementary schools shouldn’t be spending money on computers, a reader says, since play, not technology, helps young minds thrive. Readers are invited to respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/opinion/26LETTERS/26LETTERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 806, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/opinion/26LETTERS/26LETTERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/26/opinion/26LETTERS/26LETTERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Invitation to a Dialogue: Computers in School?', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Invitation to a Dialogue: Computers in School?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T17:37:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/56d8567d-cb16-596c-a5c4-347dd5ba2efb', 'word_count': 321, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56d8567d-cb16-596c-a5c4-347dd5ba2efb'}, {'abstract': 'In this sleek coffee-table volume, Linda O’Keefe explores the subtleties of white — and traces its historic and cultural roots — in interiors, architecture, and art.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/design-bookshelf-brilliant/', 'snippet': 'In this sleek coffee-table volume, Linda O’Keefe explores the subtleties of white — and traces its historic and cultural roots — in interiors, architecture, and art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog480\',appendTo: \'fullScreenSlideShow\',showHeadLine: \'false\', renderstyle: "embeddedSlideShowTmag"}', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Design Bookshelf | ‘Brilliant’', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T17:52:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pilar Viladas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pilar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viladas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ecf5e62-2b97-541f-994b-b616aa008865', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ecf5e62-2b97-541f-994b-b616aa008865'}, {'abstract': 'The letter every chef should read.', 'web_url': 'https://bittman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/hey-chef-get-with-the-program/', 'snippet': 'The letter every chef should read.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve known George Faison for 25 years or more; he was a co-founder of D’Artagnan and is now a co-owner of Debragga and Spitler, a New York meat wholesaler that’s been doing business since 1924, and a main supplier to many of the city’s best restaurants. This is a letter George sent late last week to a well-known chef, and one he’ll be sending to others. (It’s worth noting, if for no other reason than to answer the inevitable question, which I asked myself, that George doesn’t only sell naturally-raised meats – he sells industrially-produced stuff as well. But he’s on a campaign to persuade the chefs who insist that’s what they want to change their minds, and I know he’d like to supply only the right stuff.) I’ve changed nothing except misspellings.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hey Chef! Get with the Program', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIBIOTICS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Livestock Diseases', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meat', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Faison, George', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pollan, Michael', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Centers for Disease Control and Prevention', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Journal of the American Medical Assn', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T17:53:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9715e2c3-5c25-577c-94f8-a1275bc515e1', 'word_count': 1292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9715e2c3-5c25-577c-94f8-a1275bc515e1'}, {'abstract': 'The top prosecutor says 67 people have been interrogated, 31 have been arrested and the main suspect, hiding in Canada, may be sought for extradition through an Interpol warrant.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/26/world/middleeast/new-arrests-and-interrogations-in-iran-fraud-case.html', 'snippet': 'The top prosecutor says 67 people have been interrogated, 31 have been arrested and the main suspect, hiding in Canada, may be sought for extradition through an Interpol warrant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Iranian authorities have widened their investigation into what they say is a record $2.6 billion embezzlement uncovered in a network of Iranian banks last month, interrogating at least 67 people and putting 31 of them under arrest, state media reported.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iran Makes New Arrests in Fraud Case', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iran Makes New Arrests In Fraud Case', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Khavari, Mahmoud-Reza', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank Melli Iran', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embezzlement', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Amir Khosravi, Mahafarid', 'r