ugel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/573b3da9-1257-5f11-8775-5753668ebe15', 'word_count': 1634, 'uri': 'nyt://article/573b3da9-1257-5f11-8775-5753668ebe15'}, {'abstract': 'Zoe Caldwell would like to invite you for tea and sandwiches.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/26/theater/zoe-caldwell-to-star-on-upper-east-side.html', 'snippet': 'Zoe Caldwell would like to invite you for tea and sandwiches.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Zoe Caldwell would like to invite you for tea and sandwiches. Figuratively and literally. The four-time Tony Award-winner is set to star in David Adjmi’s new play, “Elective Affinities,” directed by Soho Rep’s artistic director, Sarah Benson. The twist is that the site-specific production will take place in an Upper East Side town house that will hold a maximum of 30 people each evening, with Ms. Caldwell’s character, Alice Hauptmann, playing the role of host. Advance tickets sold out in less than 24 hours. (A limited number of tickets, however, may be made available on the day of each performance.) “I was sent from the Soho Rep a play, a one-woman play, and I said ‘Oh no no,’\xa0” Ms. Caldwell said in a telephone interview. “I did ‘Lillian’\xa0” — a 1986 play about Lillian Hellman — “and that almost did me in.” She added, “Then Charlie, my son, said, ‘You could at least read the script before you dismiss it.’ And I said, ‘Fair enough.’\xa0” Mr. Adjmi, best known as the author of “Stunning,” won a 2010 Whiting Award for playwriting. “I was so stunned by the writing which is marvelously exact,” Ms. Caldwell said of “Elective Affinities,” which was commissioned by the Royal Court Theater and produced by the Royal Shakespeare Company in 2005. The producers of the American premiere are Soho Rep, piece by piece productions and Rising Phoenix Repertory. It is scheduled to run Nov. 17 to Dec. 18.', 'print_section': 'C', 