ypes, people who pitch and spin even before the first coffee. When I found myself in San Francisco early this month along with Nancy Silverton, the esteemed founder of La Brea Bakery and the restaurant Campanile in Los Angeles, the only time we could get together was for breakfast. Ms. Silverton, who now owns two of Los Angeles’s most popular restaurants, Osteria Mozza and Pizzeria Mozza, with Mario Batali and Joe Bastianich, has just published her eighth book, “The Mozza Cookbook” (Alfred A. Knopf, $35), and she suggested Boulettes Larder, in the Ferry Building.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/dining/26FEEDME1_SPAN/FeedMe-jp-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/26/dining/26FEEDME1_SPAN/FeedMe-jp-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/dining/26FEEDME1_SPAN/FeedMe-jp-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/dining/26FEEDME1_SPAN/FeedMe-jp-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/26/dining/26FEEDME1_SPAN/FeedMe-jp-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mozzarella as Medium and Muse', 'kicker': 'Feed Me', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mozzarella as Medium and Muse', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Silverton, Nancy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T22:33:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Witchel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Witchel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17e363b5-9b28-5ae5-9245-a2cbb7647c91', 'word_count': 1165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17e363b5-9b28-5ae5-9245-a2cbb7647c91'}, {'abstract': 'How to convert your Wi-Fi tablet into something that can go online anywhere.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/qa-a-wi-fi-tablet-in-a-3g-world/', 'snippet': 'How to convert your Wi-Fi tablet into something that can go online anywhere.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q. I have an Android tablet that can connect to the Internet only through Wi-Fi. Can I get a 3G chip for it to use the phone data networks too? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: A Wi-Fi Tablet in a 3G World', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T22:33:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1e5607d-d6c3-5782-a394-a8b254daa229', 'word_count': 200, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1e5607d-d6c3-5782-a394-a8b254daa229'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to an Op-Ed article about sex education.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/26/opinion/talking-about-sex-in-class-and-at-home.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to an Op-Ed article about sex education.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Talking About Sex, in Class and at Home', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Talking About Sex, in Class and at Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Education', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T22:39:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/04e8f6ee-7120-51c2-8172-edeadfdc5d08', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04e8f6ee-7120-51c2-8172-edeadfdc5d08'}, {'abstract': 'Black and Blanco’s crumbly, shortbread-style cookies are inspired by North African treats called ghoribas.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/26/dining/moroccan-cookies-from-black-and-blanco.html', 'snippet': 'Black and Blanco’s crumbly, shortbread-style cookies are inspired by North African treats called ghoribas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steve Blanco, a jazz pianist whose mother is from Casablanca, Morocco, grew up eating crumbly, shortbread-style cookies that are called ghoribas in North Africa. About a year ago he and his girlfriend and partner, Heidi Karlsen, who has baked professionally, decided to try making them with notable tweaks, like replacing semolina with rye flour and butter or cooking oil with coconut oil. They call them Sandcastles. So far they have perfected two rich, nutty flavors: marzipan and black sesame. Next in line are maple-dusted cardamom and poppy-seed-lemon.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/dining/26STUFF_COOKIES/Stuff-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1007, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/dining/26STUFF_COOKIES/Stuff-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/26/dining/26STUFF_COOKIES/Stuff-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cookies From Casablanca, Now in New York', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Childhood Cookies, All Grown Up', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cookies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T22:40:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7ab5fe1-dac8-57d5-a171-5a8a6b56779e', 'word_count': 122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7ab5fe1-dac8-57d5-a171-5a8a6b56779e'}, {'abstract': 'Melt some nuggets of Spring Brook Farm’s raclette from Vermont in a saucepan, and you’re all set.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/26/dining/raclette-made-simple.html', 'snippet': 'Melt some nuggets of Spring Brook Farm’s raclette from Vermont in a saucepan, and you’re all set.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In its traditional Alpine home, raclette can involve rigmarole — lighting a fire (preferably in your ski lodge), holding a hunk of the cheese before the flames and scraping off what melts to serve with boiled potatoes and pickles. But you can simply melt some nuggets of Spring Brook Farm’s raclette from Vermont in a saucepan, and it’s just about as good. This mild yet complex cow’s-milk cheese has an earthy aroma and fruity flavor. It comes in big wheels with rust-colored rind enclosing a satiny, semisoft paste. As superb as it is for melting (burgers, pasta, soup, sandwiches), it is also excellent on a cheese tray. And it’s educational; Spring Brook is a host of the Farms for City Kids program, bringing fourth and fifth graders from urban schools to milk cows and do chores. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/dining/26STUFF_CHEESESPAN/Stuff-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/26/dining/26STUFF_CHEESESPAN/Stuff-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/dining/26STUFF_CHEESESPAN/Stuff-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/dining/26STUFF_CHEESESPAN/Stuff-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/26/dining/26STUFF_CHEESESPAN/Stuff-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Raclette Made Simple', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Raclette Made Simple', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheese', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T22:40:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f935e0de-1372-5dba-985e-ddd8d191a56f', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f935e0de-1372-5dba-985e-ddd8d191a56f'}, {'abstract': 'The Ervell shop is part of the continuing collaboration with the Boffo project.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/look-out-patrik-ervell-at-work/', 'snippet': 'The Ervell shop is part of the continuing collaboration with the Boffo project.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Patrik Ervell pop-up at Boffo Building Fashion at 57 Walker Street looks more like a construction site than a high-end designer shop. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Look Out: Patrik Ervell at Work', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-25T22:40:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanna Nikas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nikas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed56423a-