ated industry: supplying the world with desalinated water.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/26/world/asia/china-takes-loss-to-get-ahead-in-desalination-industry.html', 'snippet': 'The Chinese government hopes to become a force in yet another environment-related industry: supplying the world with desalinated water.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TIANJIN, China — Towering over the Bohai Sea shoreline on this city’s outskirts, the Beijiang Power and Desalination Plant is a 26-billion-renminbi technical marvel: an ultrahigh-temperature, coal-fired generator with state-of-the-art pollution controls, mated to advanced Israeli equipment that uses its leftover heat to distill seawater into fresh water. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/world/SALINE/SALINE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/26/world/SALINE/SALINE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/world/SALINE/SALINE-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/world/SALINE/SALINE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/26/world/SALINE/SALINE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'China Takes a Loss to Get Ahead in the Business of Fresh Water', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China Takes a Loss to Get Ahead in the Business of Fresh Water', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Investments', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservation of Resources', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Desalination', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infrastructure (Public Works)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-26T00:27:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Wines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01ce99f9-7d8a-5d7f-97ca-15e9aa366021', 'word_count': 1418, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01ce99f9-7d8a-5d7f-97ca-15e9aa366021'}, {'abstract': 'The costs and benefits of globalization have spread unevenly across New York State, with the city and its suburbs reaping most of the rewards, a report finds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/26/nyregion/report-details-globalization-effects-in-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'The costs and benefits of globalization have spread unevenly across New York State, with the city and its suburbs reaping most of the rewards, a report finds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As international trade has accelerated in the last four decades, no state has lost more of its ability to make things or gained more through exporting ideas and professional services than New York has, according to a report to be released on Wednesday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Report Lays Out State’s Great Gains and Losses From Globalization', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Report Lays Out State&#8217;s Great Gains and Losses From Globalization', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for an Urban Future', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-26T00:29:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGeehan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGeehan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/efa4bd44-e282-5c49-9adc-b4268ef3364f', 'word_count': 570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efa4bd44-e282-5c49-9adc-b4268ef3364f'}, {'abstract': 'A sustainable-food sensibility grows in New Mexico.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/25/food-blooms-in-the-desert/', 'snippet': 'A sustainable-food sensibility grows in New Mexico.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last month, I stood in the midday sun a few miles north of Anthony, N. M., and Anthony, Tex. — a town divided by the state line created in 1854 — staring out over a 15-acre plot of land that didn’t look like much. In this part of the world, it was once almost as easy to travel from the United States to Mexico and back as it is to go from one Anthony to another. Now the trip can drive you crazy, with long waits and inspections; if you’re undocumented and unlucky, it can kill you.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Food Blooms in the Desert', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deserts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Mexico', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-26T00:30:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/868ddba2-a6d3-5efd-a511-202edb2e0e4f', 'word_count': 922, 'uri': 'nyt://article/868ddba2-a6d3-5efd-a511-202edb2e0e4f'}, {'abstract': 'A new report from the Congressional Budget Office is likely to figure prominently in the escalating political fight over the economy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/26/us/politics/top-earners-doubled-share-of-nations-income-cbo-says.html', 'snippet': 'A new report from the Congressional Budget Office is likely to figure prominently in the escalating political fight over the economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The top 1 percent of earners more than doubled their share of the nation’s income over the last three decades, the Congressional Budget Office said Tuesday, in a new report likely to figure prominently in the escalating political fight over how to revive the economy, create jobs and lower the federal debt.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/us/SUB-CONGRESS/SUB-CONGRESS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 820, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/us/SUB-CONGRESS/SUB-CONGRESS-thumb