is', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soloski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cebabb79-9a8e-57f7-bc5f-c72ef3749fe3', 'word_count': 348, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cebabb79-9a8e-57f7-bc5f-c72ef3749fe3'}, {'abstract': 'Controversial animations from the Chinese blogger Pi San.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/10/30/magazine/26mag-chinese-animations.html', 'snippet': 'Controversial animations from the Chinese blogger Pi San.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Controversial animations from the Chinese blogger Pi San.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Controversial Cartoons of Pi San', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-26T20:05:26+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/d96cd06f-072a-592a-9036-f9b6ee44f4a3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/d96cd06f-072a-592a-9036-f9b6ee44f4a3'}, {'abstract': 'In China, it’s a form of defiance – and the government is not amused.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/magazine/the-dangerous-politics-of-internet-humor-in-china.html', 'snippet': 'In China, it’s a form of defiance – and the government is not amused.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cellphone vibrated softly, insistently, echoing off the whitewashed walls of the artist’s studio. It was a Sunday morning in early April, and Wang Bo — an Internet animator better known to his legions of online fans by his nickname, Pi San — ignored the call at first. He wanted no intrusions. A compact 40-year-old with short-cropped hair and arched eyebrows that give him a look of permanent bemusement, Pi San is most famous for creating a mischievous cartoon character named Kuang Kuang, but he earns money by making animations for corporations, and he was on a deadline. Pi San had bicycled to his studio in a defunct factory building on the outskirts of Beijing that morning, hoping to finish up some work in peace. But the buzzing of the phone didn’t stop.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/27/magazine/27mag-30Bloggers-t_3/27mag-30Bloggers-t_3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/27/magazine/27mag-30Bloggers-t_3/27mag-30Bloggers-t_3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/27/magazine/27mag-30Bloggers-t_3/27mag-30Bloggers-t_3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Where an Internet Joke Is Not Just a Joke', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In China, an Internet joke is not always just a joke. It’s a form of defiance — and the government is not amused.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ai Weiwei', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 4, 'major': '