d No Child Left Behind Act that passed out of the Senate education committee last week goes too far in relaxing state accountability and federal oversight of student achievement. The business community, civil rights groups and advocates of disabled children are rightly worried that the rewrite of the law would particularly hurt underprivileged children. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Wrong Fix for No Child Left Behind', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Wrong Fix for No Child Left Behind', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harkin, Tom', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Enzi, Mike', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'No Child Left Behind Act', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T01:25:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee88a932-fa30-5c9a-a53f-f04954934b76', 'word_count': 356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee88a932-fa30-5c9a-a53f-f04954934b76'}, {'abstract': 'After weeks of cautiously accepting the teeming, round-the-clock demonstrations spawned by Occupy Wall Street, several cities have come to the end of their patience.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/27/us/oakland-and-other-cities-crack-down-on-occupy-protests.html', 'snippet': 'After weeks of cautiously accepting the teeming, round-the-clock demonstrations spawned by Occupy Wall Street, several cities have come to the end of their patience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OAKLAND, Calif. — After weeks of cautiously accepting the teeming round-the-clock protests spawned by Occupy Wall Street, several cities have come to the end of their patience and others appear to be not far behind. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/27/us/27occupy_337/27occupy_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/27/us/27occupy_337/27occupy_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/27/us/27occupy_337/27occupy_337-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/27/us/27occupy_337/27occupy_337-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/27/us/27occupy_337/27occupy_337-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Some Cities Begin Cracking Down on ‘Occupy’ Protests', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Some Cities Begin Cracking Down on ‘Occupy’ Protests', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Atlanta (Ga)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oakland (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T01:26:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse McKinley and Abby Goodnough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Abby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodnough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d3e8131-ae46-5a44-8cd0-a00f3e4f7188', 'word_count': 1177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d3e8131-ae46-5a44-8cd0-a00f3e4f7188'}, {'abstract': 'A senator-turned-soldier and the infamous raid on Osceola, Mo.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/26/james-lanes-revenge/', 'snippet': 'A senator-turned-soldier and the infamous raid on Osceola, Mo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When James H. Lane led his brigade of Kansas troops into the town of Osceola, Mo., on Sept. 22, 1861, he seemed to answer a prophetic question posed by George Miller, a Missouri minister, earlier that year. When a young Missourian told the minister he was glad of the advent of national civil war, as Missourians “want a chance at Kansas,” Miller replied, “Does it not occur to you that it would also give Kansas a chance at Missouri?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': N