keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gupta, Rajat K', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rajaratnam, Raj', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Galleon Group', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'McKinsey & Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T01:36:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/91d9bcd4-c9a0-58a7-bae6-56d0f1879e83', 'word_count': 1214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91d9bcd4-c9a0-58a7-bae6-56d0f1879e83'}, {'abstract': 'Considering that only a small portion of women with breast cancer benefited from mammography in a new analysis, all women now face a difficult choice about the screening.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/27/opinion/more-questions-about-mammograms.html', 'snippet': 'Considering that only a small portion of women with breast cancer benefited from mammography in a new analysis, all women now face a difficult choice about the screening.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two years ago, a group of outside experts who advise the federal government raised a furor when it suggested that mammography screening to detect breast cancer early, when it is theoretically most treatable, should be scaled back. Now a new analysis published in the Archives of Internal Medicine, a journal of the American Medical Association, has found that while some women have their lives saved by mammograms, a vast majority found to have tumors do not benefit — probably because they have slow-growing cancers that would never have killed them or could have been treated at a later stage, or they have aggressive cancers that are deadly even if detected early. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Questions About Mammograms', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More Questions About Mammograms', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mammography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Breast Cancer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T01:39:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b0c83e5-c46b-5d39-9908-7f3b78acc2d3', 'word_count': 329, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b0c83e5-c46b-5d39-9908-7f3b78acc2d3'}, {'abstract': 'Ayatollah Ali Khamenei’s remarks about a parliamentary system in Iran were seen as the latest blow in his battle with President Mahmoud Ahmadinejad.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/27/world/middleeast/in-iran-rivalry-khamenei-takes-on-presidency-itself.html', 'snippet': 'Ayatollah Ali Khamenei’s remarks about a parliamentary system in Iran were seen as the latest blow in his battle with President Mahmoud Ahmadinejad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — An unusual proposal by Iran’s supreme leader to eliminate the position of president has highlighted an increasingly bitter struggle within the country’s political elite, as the leader and his allies continue to try to undercut the powers of Iran’s ambitious president, Mahmoud Ahmadinejad.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': N