on-occupy-protests.html', 'snippet': 'After weeks of cautiously accepting the teeming, round-the-clock demonstrations spawned by Occupy Wall Street, some stressed-out cities have run out of patience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OAKLAND, Calif. — After weeks of cautiously accepting the teeming round-the-clock protests spawned by Occupy Wall Street, several cities have come to the end of their patience and others appear to be not far behind. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/27/us/27occupy_337/27occupy_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/27/us/27occupy_337/27occupy_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/27/us/27occupy_337/27occupy_337-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/27/us/27occupy_337/27occupy_337-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/27/us/27occupy_337/27occupy_337-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cities Begin Cracking Down on ‘Occupy’ Protests', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Some Cities Begin Cracking Down on ‘Occupy’ Protests', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oakland (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Atlanta (Ga)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T06:06:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse McKinley and Abby Goodnough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Abby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodnough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbd6d58d-903a-5192-b5de-c57c27924075', 'word_count': 1201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbd6d58d-903a-5192-b5de-c57c27924075'}, {'abstract': 'A review of Bollywood’s most expensive movie.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/27/ra-one-dazzles-and-disappoints/', 'snippet': 'A review of Bollywood’s most expensive movie.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bollywood’s most expensive movie to date, “Ra.One,” is an “entertainment machine set to dazzle” Rachel Saltz writes in a New York Times review of the movie. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Ra.One’ Dazzles and Disappoints', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T06:44:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd02fdf5-6d69-5eed-805f-5dff47aa27ad', 'word_count': 106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd02fdf5-6d69-5eed-805f-5dff47aa27ad'}, {'abstract': 'On Oct. 27, 1904, the first rapid transit subway, the Interborough Rapid Transit, opened in New York City.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/27/oct-27-1904-new-york-city-subway-system-opens/', 'snippet': 'On Oct. 27, 1904, the first rapid transit subway, the Interborough Rapid Transit, opened in New York City.', 'lead_paragraph': ' On Oct. 27, 1904, the first rapid transit subway, the Interborough Rapid Transit (I.R.T.), opened in New York City. The line ran approximately nine miles from City Hall north to Grand Central Station, then west to Times Square and up the West Side to 145th Street.The New York Times described the excitement among city dwellers for the subway’s opening: “For the first time in his life Father Knickerbocker went underground yesterday; went underground, he and his children, to the number of 150,000, amid the tooting of whistles and the firing of salutes, for a first ride in a subway which for years had been scoffed at as an impossibility.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oct. 27, 1904 | New York City Subway System Opens', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T08:05:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0290751f-7993-5f57-a4e8-7a992f8484e0', 'word_count': 418, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0290751f-7993-5f57-a4e8-7a992f8484e0'}, {'abstract': 'Prime Minister George Papandreou of Greece hailed his country’s latest bailout as marking “a new day for Europe and for Greece.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/world/europe/greeks-applaud-eu-agreement-to-cut-debt.html', 'snippet': 'Prime Minister George Papandreou of Greece hailed his country’s latest bailout as marking “a new day for Europe and for Greece.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'ATHENS - George Papandreou, the Greek prime minister, on Thursday hailed his country’s latest bailout as marking “a new day for Europe and for Greece,” after European Union and International Monetary Fund officials reached a deal under which banks would write off 50 percent of the face value of their Greek debt, a major step toward working out an overall deal to safeguard the euro. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Greeks Applaud E.U. Agreement to Cut Debt', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Greeks Applaud E.U. Agreement to Cut Debt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Monetary Fund', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T08:27:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Niki Kitsantonis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Niki', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kitsantonis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/546238dd-d8e1-57ad-95e9-4c10f583191e', 'word_count': 688, 'uri': 'nyt://article/546238dd-d8e1-57ad-95e9-4c10f583191e'}, {'abstract': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/27/news-quiz-oct-27-2011/', 'snippet': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'See what you know about the news below. To prepare, you might scan the articles or summaries on today’s paper.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Quiz | Oct. 27, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T08:33:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tiffany Frasier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tiffany', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frasier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3dfe36af-a396-5042-93e8-8a6e4eba3c40', 'word_count': 20, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3dfe36af-a396-5042-93e8-8a6e4eba3c40'}, {'abstract': 'Kelsey Grammer is ready to show America his other side.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/magazine/questions-for-kelsey-grammer.html', 'snippet': 'Kelsey Grammer is ready to show America his other side.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a scene in your new Starz show, “Boss,” when, as Mayor Tom Kane of Chicago, you open a package containing the ears of an activist who has been causing you trouble. Then you nonchalantly grind them up in your garbage disposal. Something tells me you’re more like this guy than Frasier Crane. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/magazine/30talk/mag-30talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 392, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/magazine/30talk/mag-30talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/magazine/30talk/mag-30talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No More Mr. Nice Guy', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No More Mr. Nice Guy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}