': 'National Football League', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Major League Baseball', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T14:52:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Vecsey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vecsey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ead3eb86-280d-5c57-93a4-ad714b50aa64', 'word_count': 902, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ead3eb86-280d-5c57-93a4-ad714b50aa64'}, {'abstract': 'The postponement of Game 6 had its pluses and minuses, and made the Cardinals’ Chris Carpenter an option for Game 7, if the World Series gets that far.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/sports/world-series-who-benefits-from-extra-day-off-leading-off.html', 'snippet': 'The postponement of Game 6 had its pluses and minuses, and made the Cardinals’ Chris Carpenter an option for Game 7, if the World Series gets that far.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While the baseball world spends the day contemplating which team benefits from a washed out night in the World Series, there are many pluses in a day’s delay before Game 6. You can almost picture the Cardinals scrambling to install a new wireless bullpen phone system, with Tony La Russa wandering around Busch Stadium saying, “Can you hear me now?” The Rally Squirrel has another day to improve his first-to-third time. The Rangers can spend time being even pickier on what kind of Champagne they will stash in their clubhouse for a potential celebration. Is it better to spray oneself with ultra brut or demi-sec? Or perhaps a nice prosecco?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Benefits From Extra Day Off?', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Benefits From Extra Day Off?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Series', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miami Dolphins', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kansas State University', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stanford University', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Patriots', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carpenter, Chris', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'la Russa, Tony', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sparano, Tony', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, LeBron', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beasley, Michael', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Luck, Andrew', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T14:54:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cacb534f-cd08-5501-8639-165a2c5c9ef5', 'word_count': 739, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cacb534f-cd08-5501-8639-165a2c5c9ef5'}, {'abstract': 'Members of the four-person criminal ring, which since the 1990s sold dozens of fake paintings said to be made by artists like Heinrich Campendonk, Fernand Léger and Max Ernst, were sentenced to a total of 15 years in prison.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/27/art-forgers-whose-victims-included-steve-martin-are-sentenced/', 'snippet': 'Members of the four-person criminal ring, which since the 1990s sold dozens of fake paintings said to be made by artists like Heinrich Campendonk, Fernand Léger and Max Ernst, were sentenced to a total of 15 years in prison.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A group of art forgers who sold falsely attributed oil paintings to art collectors, and whose victims included the comedian Steve Martin, were sentenced on Thursday by a court in Cologne, Germany, Bloomberg News reported. Wolfgang Beltracchi, the leader of the ring, which since the 1990s sold dozens of fake paintings said to be made by artists like Heinrich Campendonk, Fernand Léger and Max Ernst, was given a six-year sentence; his wife, Helene, was sentenced to four years while her sister, Jeanette, received a 21-month suspended sentence. Otto Schulte-Kellinghaus, a fourth member of the ring, was sentenced to five years. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Art Forgers Whose Victims Included Steve Martin are Sentenced', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ernst, Max', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martin, Steve', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cologne (Germany)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T14:55:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b17adef-4054-58fc-802f-1c027fc5612d', 'word_count': 227, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b17adef-4054-58fc-802f-1c027fc5612d'}, {'abstract': 'One Police Plaza: An officer receives a new job after a pepper-spray encounter; and the proper behavior of an undercover officer is at issue in the Sean Bell police hearing.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/27/no-highway-therapy-for-pepper-spray-commander/', 'snippet': 'One Police Plaza: An officer receives a new job after a pepper-spray encounter; and the proper behavior of an undercover officer is at issue in the Sean Bell police hearing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When imperfect police officers are caught and punished, they sometimes receive a form of discipline known colloquially behind the Blue Wall of Silence as “highway therapy.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No ‘Highway Therapy’ for Pepper-Spray Commander', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bell, Sean', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bologna, Anthony', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Isnora, Gescard F', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T14:56:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Al Baker and Rob Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Al', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e9e0c9c-b458-5462-958d-ae21a3acd1a9', 'word_count': 922, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e9e0c9c-b458-5462-958d-ae21a3acd1a9'}, {'abstract': 'The mascot of the middle of nowhere, tumbleweeds are on the move once again. But the drought in southwest Kansas may mean fewer tumbleweeds than in years past.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/us/tumbleweeds-are-piling-up-across-the-plains.html', 'snippet': 'The mascot of the middle of nowhere, tumbleweeds are on the move once again. But the drought in southwest Kansas may mean fewer tumbleweeds than in years past.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CIMARRON, Kan. — The ubiquitous swerving and darting forms making their hopscotch journey across the landscape here in recent days are part of one of the most storied — and least celebrated — natural migrations on the Great Plains.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/us/TUMBLEWEEDS/TUMBLEWEEDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/28/us/TUMBLEWEEDS/TUMBLEWEEDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/us/TUMBLEWEEDS/TUMBLEWEEDS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 705, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/us/TUMBLEWEEDS/TUMBLEWEEDS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumb