//www.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/fashion/debi-mazar-plays-many-roles-what-i-wore.html', 'snippet': 'Debi Mazar favors vintage looks and sexy ensembles for work and travel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE actress Debi Mazar, 47, made her feature film debut in Martin Scorsese’s “Goodfellas” and played a publicist on the HBO series “Entourage.” She is now the host — with her husband, Gabriele Corcos — of “Extra Virgin,” a show on the Cooking Channel. A native of Queens, she lives in Los Angeles with Mr. Corcos and their daughters, Evelina and Giulia. BEE-SHYUAN CHANG', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/27/style/27whatiwore/27whatiwore-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 735, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/27/style/27whatiwore/27whatiwore-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/27/style/27whatiwore/27whatiwore-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Role-Playing Each Day', 'kicker': 'What I Wore | Debi Mazar', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Role-Playing Each Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hair', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mazar, Debi', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Buttons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Textiles', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shoes and Boots', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dresses', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T17:57:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bee-Shyuan Chang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bee-shyuan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/af3fc390-38b1-5962-b2d3-4f6dd3860871', 'word_count': 966, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af3fc390-38b1-5962-b2d3-4f6dd3860871'}, {'abstract': 'Rick Perry’s opponents in the Republican field appear not to be following suit in his plans to skip some debates.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/27/the-other-campaigns-take-on-perry-and-debates/', 'snippet': 'Rick Perry’s opponents in the Republican field appear not to be following suit in his plans to skip some debates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Rick Perry of Texas may be reserving the option of not participating in future debates. But his opponents in the Republican field appear not to be following suit. After all, the debates are good, low-cost exposure, particularly for candidates who are not at the top of the polls. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Other Campaigns Chime In on Perry and Debates', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debates (Political)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bachmann, Michele M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Ron', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T17:58:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Wheaton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wheaton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6055481-cfe2-50e7-aee3-3c449a6ca148', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6055481-cfe2-50e7-aee3-3c449a6ca148'}, {'abstract': 'When you shop at a big-box retailer are you fraternizing with the enemy? Or are you just trying to save money and stay in business?', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/27/coming-to-terms-with-wal-mart/', 'snippet': 'When you shop at a big-box retailer are you fraternizing with the enemy? Or are you just trying to save money and stay in business?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I remember the first time my husband and I visited the Wal-Mart Supercenter in Bentonville, Ark. It was July, 2003, just a few weeks after we had moved to Northwest Arkansas from Seattle, and it was the first time I had ever shopped at a Wal-Mart. As we turned the car off of Walton Boulevard — directly across from Wal-Mart’s corporate headquarters — it felt as if our Volkswagen Passat was being swallowed into the vast parking lot of pick-ups and S.U.V.’s the way the tiny rebel spaceship was sucked into Darth Vader’s star destroyer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Coming to Terms With Wal-Mart', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wal-Mart Stores Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARKANSAS', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T18:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Barbara Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Barbara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac0cb63a-ccc6-534c-9ce4-d4caf13c3c87', 'word_count': 998, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac0cb63a-ccc6-534c-9ce4-d4caf13c3c87'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers report that a combination of fatty acids expands the heart cells of a well-fed snake, a finding that holds promise for treating human heart disease and other ailments.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/11/01/health/python-digestion-study-holds-promise-for-human-heart-health.html', 'snippet': 'Researchers report that a combination of fatty acids expands the heart cells of a well-fed snake, a finding that holds promise for treating human heart disease and other ailments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOULDER, Colo. — Pythons are known for their enormous appetites. In a single meal they can devour animals at least as big as they are — deer, alligators, pigs, household pets. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/science/01pythona/01pythona-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/28/science/01pythona/01pythona-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/science/01pythona/01pythona-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/science/01pythona/01pythona-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/28/science/01pythona/01pythona-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Snakes’ Feat May Inspire Heart Drugs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Snakes’ Feat May Inspire Heart Drugs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Science (Journal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snakes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Heart', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T18:01:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lawrence K. Altman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lawrence', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Altman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/309aed1a-0b61-51b3-b8e0-dfa263f5cbf1', 'word_count': 1178, 'uri': 'nyt://article/309aed1a-0b61-51b3-b8e0-dfa263f5cbf1'}, {'abstract': 'The Phantom Limb Company’s “69°S.,” a sophisticated puppet show in which puppeteers are on stilts, opens at the Brooklyn Academy of Music’s Next Wave Festival this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/theater/69s-shackleton-tale-comes-to-bam-next-wave-festival.html', 'snippet': 'The Phantom Limb Company’s “69°S.,” a sophisticated puppet show in which puppeteers are on stilts, opens at the Brooklyn Academy of Music’s Next Wave Festival this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OF all the things we pay money to sit in a theater and watch, puppets are surely the strangest. By and large they are not particularly good at the thing we most often ask them to do — imitate us, that is — and yet people (some people anyway) love them precisely for their limitations. Puppetry may even be enjoying one of its periodic revivals right now. “War Horse” is such a hit in large part because of its marvelous lifelike horses, and in “Compulsion” last winter a marionette of Anne Frank was the only character with enough stage presence to stand up to Mandy Patinkin. In our enlightened era some fortunate puppets have been allowed privileges denied to the likes of Howdy, Kukla, Ollie and the rest. Basil Twist, arguably the master of avant-garde puppetry, has incorporated them into a drag show, while in “Avenue Q,” they get to talk filth and have sex. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30PHANTOM1/30PHANTOM1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 786, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30PHANTOM1/30PHANTOM1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30PHANTOM1/30PHANTOM1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Skimming the Ice, Strings Attached', 'kicker': 'Arts & Leisure', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Skimming the Ice, Strings Attached', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Puppets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': '69S. (Play)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Academy of Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Next Wave Festival', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Phantom Limb Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T18:08:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f93cca7-a5cf-5bd4-b420-a1b2e0dc48cd', 'word_count': 1748, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f93cca7-a5cf-5bd4-b420-a1b2e0dc48cd'}, {'abstract': 'One Afghan interpreter was killed, and an American civilian contractor and two Afghan security guards were wounded, in an attack at a small NATO base in Kandahar on Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/world/asia/attack-in-kandahar-breaks-rare-respite.html', 'snippet': 'One Afghan interpreter was killed, and an American civilian contractor and two Afghan security guards were wounded, in an attack at a small NATO base in Kandahar on Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KANDAHAR, Afghanistan — Insurgents armed with guns and rocket-propelled grenades attacked a small NATO base here on Thursday, breaking an unusual period of calm in this volatile city and setting off a standoff between the attackers and coalition and Afghan forces that was continuing late into the evening. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/world/28AFGHAN/28AFGHAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/28/world/28AFGHAN/28AFGHAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/world/28