e': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'Danielle Belopotosky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Danielle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belopotosky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/03f7e8f4-918a-5674-b698-728e98cf971d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/03f7e8f4-918a-5674-b698-728e98cf971d'}, {'abstract': 'Despite the grinding poverty on its outskirts, urban revival continues in the city center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/10/30/travel/20111030-hours-capetown.html', 'snippet': 'Despite the grinding poverty on its outskirts, urban revival continues in the city center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Despite the grinding poverty on its outskirts, urban revival continues in the city center.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/travel/30HOURS-slide-KCDT/30HOURS-slide-KCDT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 691, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/travel/30HOURS-slide-KCDT/30HOURS-slide-KCDT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/travel/30HOURS-slide-KCDT/30HOURS-slide-KCDT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '36 Hours in Cape Town', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cape Town (South Africa)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T19:10:28+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/447c5192-a0ee-5723-bee0-ccd1fe3ed2d1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/447c5192-a0ee-5723-bee0-ccd1fe3ed2d1'}, {'abstract': 'With urban revival continuing in parts of the city after the 2010 World Cup, Cape Town is still a place where nature dazzles, and history lingers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/travel/36-hours-in-cape-town.html', 'snippet': 'With urban revival continuing in parts of the city after the 2010 World Cup, Cape Town is still a place where nature dazzles, and history lingers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAPE TOWN overwhelms the senses. Its cultivated side, the bright lights and big buildings of the city center, collides with its geography — the dazzle and danger of the wind-whipped mountains and the two oceans that embrace it. The 2010 World Cup soccer tournament transformed parts of its infrastructure. A 55,000-seat stadium was built. High-end hotels, boutiques, restaurants, art galleries and B&Bs opened. But prices soared, and the tourist trade since then has disappointed. Some businesses have closed; several construction projects remain unfinished. Yet the urban revival continues in the City Bowl (the center), in the area around the University of Cape Town and in the old industrial neighborhood of Woodstock. Despite the grinding poverty in the townships on the city’s outskirts, this is one of the most naturally beautiful places in the world, where adventurers can sky-dive, kite-surf, rock-climb, hang-glide, shark-cage-dive; others can explore with less daring but just as much enthusiasm.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/travel/30HOURS_SPAN/30HOURS_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 225, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLar