ne, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T21:16:55+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'None None', 'person': [{'firstname': 'None', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'None', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/ea1b7a95-2093-5902-af05-87726e37c146', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/ea1b7a95-2093-5902-af05-87726e37c146'}, {'abstract': 'Employees staged a walkout last Thursday, the day of the planned reopening of the Impressionist galleries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/arts/design/musee-dorsay-reopens-after-weeklong-strike.html', 'snippet': 'Employees staged a walkout last Thursday, the day of the planned reopening of the Impressionist galleries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After a weeklong strike by museum workers, the Musée d’Orsay in Paris, right, reopened on Thursday, enabling visitors to discover the museum’s renovated galleries. Employees staged a walkout last Thursday, the day of the planned reopening of the Impressionist galleries, because no additional workers had been recruited despite the addition of more than 21,000 square feet of display space. They ended the strike Thursday morning as the French culture ministry agreed to start hiring more workers earlier than initially planned, according to a museum spokeswoman. The reopening had been widely anticipated after a $28 million project that took 18 months, during which nearly half the museum’s galleries were closed.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Musée d’Orsay Reopens After Weeklong Strike', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Musée d’Orsay Reopens After Weeklong Strike', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strikes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Musee d'Orsay", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T21:18:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elvire Camus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elvire', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Camus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/03809507-6d29-568d-9c27-fc069f090801', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03809507-6d29-568d-9c27-fc069f090801'}, {'abstract': 'When the price is set realistically for the market — even on the low side — savvy buyers will jump in.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/realestate/long-island-in-the-region-homes-priced-to-sell.html', 'snippet': 'When the price is set realistically for the market — even on the low side — savvy buyers will jump in.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE deal was done in a flash. Twenty minutes after Shari Benmen listed a three-bedroom two-bath 1927 brick home in Great Neck as a $799,000 exclusive with Coldwell Banker Residential, the phone rang.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/realestate/30LI-ZONE_SPAN/30LI-ZONE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/30/realestate/30LI-ZONE_SPAN/30LI-ZONE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/realestate/30LI-ZONE_SPAN/30LI-ZONE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 723, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy'