borrowers a credit toward closing costs if they accept a mortgage interest rate higher than they would ordinarily qualify for.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN you add everything up, closing costs can increase the price of a home by as much as $10,000, sometimes more. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/realestate/30mort-graphic/30mort-graphic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 310, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/30/realestate/30mort-graphic/30mort-graphic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 310}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/realestate/30mort-graphic/30mort-graphic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/realestate/30mort-graphic/30mort-graphic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Handling High Closing Costs', 'kicker': 'Mortgages', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Handling High Closing Costs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T21:21:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vickie Elmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vickie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76d52c10-6a8a-506f-95d1-dbeed4d5b942', 'word_count': 640, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76d52c10-6a8a-506f-95d1-dbeed4d5b942'}, {'abstract': 'The Museum of the City of New York is taking over the Seaport Museum, signaling yet another change for the maritime portal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/realestate/streetscapes-a-new-steward-on-south-street.html', 'snippet': 'The Museum of the City of New York is taking over the Seaport Museum, signaling yet another change for the maritime portal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE Museum of the City of New York is taking over operation of the troubled South Street Seaport Museum, which ran out of money earlier this year. The Seaport Museum has a network of structures in the area, but its central showpiece is the historic Schermerhorn Row, on the south side of Fulton Street from Front to South Streets. Over two centuries those sober merchant warehouses have faced the Fulton Market, on the north side of the street, in its four iterations dating from 1822, 1883, 1949 and 1983.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/realestate/30STREETS_SPAN/streets-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/30/realestate/30STREETS_SPAN/streets-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/realestate/30STREETS_SPAN/streets-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 622, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/realestate/30STREETS_SPAN/streets-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/realestate/30STREETS_SPAN/streets-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fish Were Just the Appetizer', 'kicker': 'Streetscapes | South Street', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fish Were Just the Appetizer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of the City of New York', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'FULTON FISH MARKET', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Street Seaport (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T21:22:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Gray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff42c5db-0411-5f3a-b2b2-75ec5ef533a0', 'word_count': 834, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff42c5db-0411-5f3a-b2b2-75ec5ef533a0'}, {'abstract': 'Compared with older apartments, new ones tend to have higher selling prices, hidden construction defects and more complicated financing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/realestate/getting-started-homework-for-condo-buyers.html', 'snippet': 'Compared with older apartments, new ones tend to have higher selling prices, hidden construction defects and more complicated financing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN a city like New York with an aging housing stock — much of it suffering from deferred maintenance — brand-new condominiums have an obvious appeal. Luxury amenities like pools, screening rooms, children’s play areas and high-end finishes can look pretty good when compared with a musty prewar building or a featureless postwar high-rise.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/realestate/30GETTINGSTARTED/getting-started-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 728, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/realestate/30GETTINGSTARTED/30GETTINGSTARTED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/realestate/30GETTINGSTARTED/30GETTINGSTARTED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Homework for Condo Buyers', 'kicker': 'Getting Started', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Homework for Condo Buyers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Condominiums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T21:22:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Rendon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rendon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92fb980e-a1d2-5299-8261-3422b26567cf', 'word_count': 1148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92fb980e-a1d2-5299-8261-3422b26567cf'}, {'abstract': 'After the birth of their second son, Vivek and Roshini Nasta knew they needed to move out of their loft, into a place with a more traditional layout.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/realestate/putting-down-roots-in-a-place-above-trees.html', 'snippet': 'After the birth of their second son, Vivek and Roshini Nasta knew they needed to move out of their loft, into a place with a more traditional layout.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BACK when they were newlyweds, a one-bedroom loft in the financial district was ideal for Vivek and Roshini Nasta. The couple — he works in the field of mobile communications and she is a gastroenterologist — bought the loft five years ago in a modern condominium at 15 Broad Street, in the building known as Downtown by Philippe Starck. “As a couple, I loved the loft,” Mr. Nasta said. “I would project movies in my cavernous space during parties.” ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/realestate/30-HUNT-4_395/30-HUNT-4_395-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 554, 'width': 539, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Putting Down Roots in a Place Above Trees', 'kicker': 'The Hunt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Putting Down Roots in a Place Above Trees', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'BATTERY PARK CITY (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T21:25:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/689e1d96-51ba-5d44-ba5f-ad9f181e8fca', 'word_count': 1107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/689e1d96-51ba-5d44-ba5f-ad9f181e8fca'}, {'abstract': 'Citing “financial challenges,” the Brooklyn Museum has raised its suggested admission price to $12.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/arts/design/brooklyn-museum-raises-suggested-admission-fee.html', 'snippet': 'Citing “financial challenges,” the Brooklyn Museum has raised its suggested admission price to $12.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Citing “financial challenges,” the Brooklyn Museum has raised its suggested admission price to $12, and $8 for students and those 62 and over. The change is a $2 hike in each category; the last increase was two and a half years ago. It’s still a bargain, considering the Museum of Modern Art charges adults $25 — and that’s not a suggestion — and in June the Metropolitan Museum of Art raised its suggested admission fee to $25, from $20. “We wanted to do this as modestly and cautiously as we could,” said Arnold L. Lehman, the Brooklyn Museum’s director.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brooklyn Museum Raises Suggested Admission Fee', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brooklyn Museum Raises Suggested Admission Fee', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Museum', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T21:26:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe9c8491-4f8d-5b85-be0b-45a30d413da5', 'word_count': 99, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe9c8491-4f8d-5b85-be0b-45a30d413da5'}, {'abstract': 'Occupy Wall Street prepares giant puppets for the 