ing so naïve. With him all is clever, harsh and self-conscious. Alienation, intellectual conceits and theater games abound. To enjoy him is to congratulate yourself on your own smartness.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28FORSYTHE/FORSYTHE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 854, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28FORSYTHE/28FORSYTHE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28FORSYTHE/28FORSYTHE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Plodding and Talking, All With Eyebrow Arched', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Plodding and Talking, All With Eyebrow Arched', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Academy of Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'FORSYTHE, WILLIAM', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T22:16:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7b0ec54-7e17-5af1-ab0a-909d94a1cc34', 'word_count': 740, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7b0ec54-7e17-5af1-ab0a-909d94a1cc34'}, {'abstract': 'In “Pulphead,” John Jeremiah Sullivan’s essays about American culture and his more personal reflections cohere into a memoirlike whole.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/books/pulphead-by-john-jeremiah-sullivan-review.html', 'snippet': 'In “Pulphead,” John Jeremiah Sullivan’s essays about American culture and his more personal reflections cohere into a memoirlike whole.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the best young nonfiction writers in English, the journalists and essayists whose bylines you avidly seek out in newspapers, magazines and online, John Jeremiah Sullivan probably isn’t (yet) in my personal Top 10 or 15. But I suspect that none of those other writers could have gathered together a book that’s as good as “Pulphead,” Mr. Sullivan’s brainy new collection of essays. It’s a big and sustaining pile of — as I’ve heard it put about certain people’s fried chicken — crunchy goodness. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28BOOK/SUBBOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 828, 'width': 649, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28BOOK/SUBBOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28BOOK/SUBBOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sizing Up Pop Culture’s Geniuses and Freaks', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sizing Up Pop Culture’s Geniuses and Freaks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, John Jeremiah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T22:17:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dwight Garner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dwight', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/568d124b-937d-5c3b-be75-151703a90844', 'word_count': 1070, 'uri': 'nyt://article/568d124b-937d-5c3b-be75-151703a90844'}, {'abstract': 'In “Crafting Modernism,” the Museum of Arts and Design is taking visitors on a tour of midcentury examples of American creativity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/arts/design/crafting-modernism-at-the-museum-of-arts-and-design.html', 'snippet': 'In “Crafting Modernism,” the Museum of Arts and Design is taking visitors on a tour of midcentury examples of American creativity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before its move from West 53rd Street to Columbus Circle, the Museum of Arts and Design called itself the American Craft Museum. And before that, it was the Museum of Contemporary Crafts. And before it was a museum at all, it was a series of programs run by the American Craftsman’s Council. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28CRAFTING1_SPAN/28CRAFTING1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28CRAFTING1_SPAN/28CRAFTING1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28CRAFTING1_SPAN/CRAFTING1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 