The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28HAUNTED2/28HAUNTED2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28HAUNTED2/28HAUNTED2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28HAUNTED2/28HAUNTED2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28HAUNTED2/28HAUNTED2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28HAUNTED2/28HAUNTED2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Go With Friends, or Go It Alone', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Go With Friends, or Go It Alone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Haunted Houses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Halloween', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-27T22:49:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven McElroy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McElroy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f1f27a8-cfa9-582a-b9be-b3a3211371c8', 'word_count': 719, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f1f27a8-cfa9-582a-b9be-b3a3211371c8'}, {'abstract': '“Real/Surreal,” an exhibition from the Whitney’s permanent collection, brings together works that touch both ends of the representational spectrum and often evoke despair or isolation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/arts/design/real-surreal-at-the-whitney-review.html', 'snippet': '“Real/Surreal,” an exhibition from the Whitney’s permanent collection, brings together works that touch both ends of the representational spectrum and often evoke despair or isolation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Seeing “Real/Surreal,” a marvelous exhibition at the Whitney Museum of American Art, is like visiting your grandmother’s attic and finding it loaded with forgotten treasures. Drawn from the museum’s permanent collection and beautifully installed by Carter Foster, a Whitney curator, the exhibition samples a trend in American art that was dominant from the 1920s to the early 1950s. It had painters and printmakers shrugging off the revolutionary formal innovations of Modernists like Picasso and Mondrian and sticking to centuries-old traditions of realistic representation.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/REAL1/REAL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 349, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/REAL1/REAL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 349}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/REAL1/REAL1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 598, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/REAL1/REAL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/REAL1/REAL1-thumbSt