'lead_paragraph': 'For 40 years, Bill Lavicka fought noisily to save old Chicago churches, houses and other neighborhood architecture from the wrecking ball. He worked quietly to restore buildings all over the city, rehabilitating dozens in his whimsical style. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/us/30CNCLAVICKA2/30CNCLAVICKA2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 941, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/us/30CNCLAVICKA2/30CNCLAVICKA2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/us/30CNCLAVICKA2/30CNCLAVICKA2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Preservationist Is Facing New Challenges', 'kicker': 'Chicago News Cooperative', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Preservationist Is Facing New Challenges', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colon and Colorectal Cancer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T01:57:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Murray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ec4f018-dba7-5a04-a5f5-34fa8ef68640', 'word_count': 557, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ec4f018-dba7-5a04-a5f5-34fa8ef68640'}, {'abstract': 'Closing remarks in the trial of Jason Arbeeny, a New York police detective accused of planting drugs on a woman and her boyfriend.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/nyregion/closing-arguments-in-police-drugs-corruption-case.html', 'snippet': 'Closing remarks in the trial of Jason Arbeeny, a New York police detective accused of planting drugs on a woman and her boyfriend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the corruption trial of a New York police detective wound down on Thursday afternoon, the detective’s lawyer portrayed him as anything but a dirty cop: the only blemishes on his 14-year career, the lawyer said, were a car accident on the job and having worked for a charity without permission from the department.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Drug-Planting Trial, Defense Says Officer Is Not Corrupt, but a Reformer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Drug-Planting Trial, Defense Says Officer Is Not Corrupt, but a Reformer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reichbach, Gustin L', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality and Misconduct', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T01:57:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Stelloh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelloh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/61c02ef3-0bbb-55e4-8d6f-ed8c1d05c0ba', 'word_count': 554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61c02ef3-0bbb-55e4-8d6f-ed8c1d05c0ba'}, {'abstract': 'An interesting looking grid from Tim Croce.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/27/crazy/', 'snippet': 'An interesting looking grid from Tim Croce.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRIDAY’S PUZZLE — Interesting looking grid, isn’t it? That was my first thought when I sat down to solve. All that wide, open space, and the black squares make the grid look like the number 8, if you squint a bit. I don’t believe that has anything to do with solving the puzzle, but it was my first impression.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday: Crazy Way to Go', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T02:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d1cfc8e-cffb-59da-b466-249762b8e50c', 'word_count': 216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d1cfc8e-cffb-59da-b466-249762b8e50c'}, {'abstract': 'In David Henry Hwang’s “Chinglish,” now on Broadway, an American businessman hoping to make his fortune in China goes through a maze of cultural confusion and linguistic blunders.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/theater/reviews/chinglish-by-david-henry-hwang-at-longacre-theater-review.html', 'snippet': 'In David Henry Hwang’s “Chinglish,” now on Broadway, an American businessman hoping to make his fortune in China goes through a maze of cultural confusion and linguistic blunders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even though much of the dialogue is in Mandarin, non-Chinese speakers should have no difficulty interpreting “Chinglish,” the sporadically funny new play by David Henry Hwang, which opened on Thursday night at the Longacre Theater. That’s not just because of the helpful supertitles — largely translations of mistranslations, in which English is merrily mutilated, and the principal source of this production’s mirth. Mr. Hwang’s comedy, about a bewildered American businessman hoping to make his fortune in capitalist China, is laid out with the frame-by-frame exactness of a comic strip.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28CHINGLISH_SPAN/28CHINGLISH_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28CHINGLISH_SPAN/28CHINGLISH_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28CHINGLISH_SPAN/JPCHINGLISH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 759, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28CHINGLISH_SPAN/28CHINGLISH_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28CHINGLISH_SPAN/28CHINGLISH_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Can’t Talk Very Good Your Language', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Chinglish'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Can’t Talk Very Good Your Language', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hwang, David Henry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T02:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/be6d6e20-a39d-560b-aa3a-ee578734b022', 'word_count': 1221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be6d6e20-a39d-560b-aa3a-ee578734b022'}, {'abstract': '“Asuncion,” a new play written by and starring Jesse Eisenberg, is about the delicate mysteries of male friendship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/theater/reviews/asuncion-at-the-cherry-lane-theater-review.html', 'snippet': '“Asuncion,” a new play written by and starring Jesse Eisenberg, is about the delicate mysteries of male friendship.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I want to be fun,” says Edgar, the confused and aimless character played by Jesse Eisenberg in “Asuncion,” a new play also written by Mr. Eisenberg that opened at the Cherry Lane Theater on Thursday night in a Rattlestick Playwrights Theater production. The pleading tone in which Edgar makes this pitiable statement says all you need to know about his past, present and future as a party animal.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28ASUNCION_SPAN/ASUNCION-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28ASUNCION_SPAN/ASUNCION-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28ASUNCION_SPAN/ASUNCION-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 702, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28ASUNCION_SPAN/ASUNCION-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/28/arts/28ASUNCION_SPAN/ASUNCION-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bromance Blossoms, but Angst Takes Root', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Asuncion'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bromance Blossoms, But Angst Takes Root', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rattlestick Playwrights Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eisenberg, Jesse', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cherry Lane Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T02:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/956c3509-27b5-562e-ae5a-281ccb532006', 'word_count': 907, 'uri': 'nyt://article/956c3509-27b5-562e-ae5a-281ccb532006'}, {'abstract': 'A modest increase in Medicare premiums will be welcome to beneficiaries and could pay political dividends to President Obama as he tries to win the votes of older Americans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/us/politics/medicare-premiums-rising-less-than-expected.html', 'snippet': 'A modest increase in Medicare premiums will be welcome to beneficiaries and could pay political dividends to President Obama as he tries to win the votes of older Americans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Monthly Medicare premiums for most beneficiaries will rise next year by $3.50, to $99.90, a much smaller increase than had been expected, the Obama administration said Thursday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rise in Medicare Premium Is Lower Than Predicted', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rise in Medicare Premium Is Lower Than Predicted', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Security (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T02:02:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Pear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pear', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa37eca9-9231-5c86-834c-3eed53de260d', 'word_count': 769, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa37eca9-9231-5c86-834c-3eed53de260d'}, {'abstract': 'After seven more hours of talks, officials for the league and the players union are hopeful the end to the lockout may be closer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/sports/basketball/for-the-nba-negotiations-are-taking-baby-steps.html', 'snippet': 'After seven more hours of talks, officials for the league and the players union are hopeful the end to the lockout may be closer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The N.B.A. lockout did not end Thursday night, but it edged ever closer to a finale, amid tense smiles, light-hearted quips and provocative word choices by the men who are negotiating a new labor deal.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cautious Optimism, but No Deal, as N.B.A. Talks Break for Night', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cautious Optimism, but No Deal, as N.B.A. Talks Break for Night', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stern, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fisher, Derek', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Silver, Adam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lockouts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hunter, Billy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T02:03:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard Beck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/865e2f37-a0f5-5815-85d9-558075d7b40b', 'word_count': 854, 'uri': 'nyt://article/865e2f37-a0f5-5815-85d9-558075d7b40b'}, {'abstract': 'State officials on Thursday prevented the widow of the man the police say set free dozens of exotic animals from the family’s wildlife preserve from retrieving the six surviving animals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/us/ohio-surviving-exotic-animals-are-to-stay-at-zoo.html', 'snippet': 'State officials on Thursday prevented the widow of the man the police say set free dozens of exotic animals from the family’s wildlife preserve from retrieving the six surviving animals.', 'lead_paragraph': ' State officials on Thursday prevented the widow of the man the police say set free dozens of exotic animals from the family’s wildlife preserve from retrieving the six surviving animals. At about the time the woman, Marian Thompson, was scheduled to pick up the animals, the State Department of Agriculture ordered them quarantined, directing that the animals remain at the Columbus Zoo and Aquarium, where they had been held since Oct. 18, when Terry Thompson uncaged 56 exotic animals before shooting himself. Forty-nine animals were hunted down and killed by law enforcement officers who said they feared the animals, which included lions and tigers, might attack people. A snow monkey was never found, and is believed to have been eaten by one of the big cats. State officials said the quarantine was ordered because the animals might be infected with a dangerous, contagious disease related to the squalid conditions under which they were kept at the Zanesville, Ohio, preserve. The three leopards, two Celebes macaques and a grizzly bear are to remain quarantined indefinitely. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ohio: Captured Animals Are to Stay at Columbus Zoo', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Midwest', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ohio: Captured Animals Are to Stay at Columbus Zoo', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMALS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thompson, Terry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thompson, Marian', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_da