'_id': 'nyt://article/7f6f70ac-4248-528c-9223-26008e8b04a9', 'word_count': 59, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f6f70ac-4248-528c-9223-26008e8b04a9'}, {'abstract': 'The factory-licensed body shell is said to feature stronger welds and higher-quality steel than that found on the original car.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/ahead-of-sema-ford-stamps-a-new-65-mustang-convertible/', 'snippet': 'The factory-licensed body shell is said to feature stronger welds and higher-quality steel than that found on the original car.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The details of a reproduction first-generation Mustang convertible body shell were announced Friday by the Ford Motor Company, a development that the automaker called in a media release a “classic car lover’s dream come true.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ahead of SEMA, Ford Stamps a New ’65 Mustang Convertible', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antique and Classic Cars', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Las Vegas (Nev)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T12:01:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Stenquist', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stenquist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5f127ea-da81-59d5-aebc-a10b645ce130', 'word_count': 307, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5f127ea-da81-59d5-aebc-a10b645ce130'}, {'abstract': 'Euro eh.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/here-we-go-again/', 'snippet': 'Euro eh.', 'lead_paragraph': 'European leaders reach an agreement; markets are enthusiastic. Then reality sets in. The agreement is at best inadequate, and possibly makes no sense at all.  Spreads stay high, and maybe even start widening again.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Here We Go Again', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T12:09:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbfee024-9356-5170-9a25-a7de9882486f', 'word_count': 39, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbfee024-9356-5170-9a25-a7de9882486f'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Lepage’s production of Wagner’s “Siegfried” opened Thursday night at the Metropolitan Opera with strong singing, remarkable playing and a creaky set.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/29/arts/music/metropolitan-operas-siegfried-is-led-by-impressive-singing.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Lepage’s production of Wagner’s “Siegfried” opened Thursday night at the Metropolitan Opera with strong singing, remarkable playing and a creaky set.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Those who saw the first two installments of Robert Lepage’s production of Wagner’s “Ring” cycle for the Metropolitan Opera last season pretty much knew what to expect when “Siegfried,” Part 3 of 4, opened on Thursday night. So let me momentarily put off reporting on the production and get to the best news: the impressive singing of the cast over all and the remarkable playing of the Met orchestra under the company’s principal conductor, Fabio Luisi.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/29/arts/29SIEGFRIED/29SIEGFRIED-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 790, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/29/arts/29SIEGFRIED/29SIEGFRIED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/29/arts/29SIEGFRIED/29SIEGFRIED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dragon, Dwarfs and Demigod: It Must Be Wagner', 'kicker': 'Opera Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dragon, Dwarfs And Demigod: It Must Be Wagner', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Luisi, Fabio', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lepage, Robert', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wagner, Richard', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Voigt, Deborah', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Terfel, Bryn', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T12:14:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4685f190-116d-58e3-ba8a-9572e9b88b8e', 'word_count': 911, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4685f190-116d-58e3-ba8a-9572e9b88b8e'}, {'abstract': 'Iceland: not unique in money inflows.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/dont-blame-the-krona/', 'snippet': 'Iceland: not unique in money inflows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One thing that was clear during yesterday’s Iceland conference was that many people here (I’m still in Reykjavik) believe that the floating krona was responsible for the huge capital inflows that set the stage of the crisis. The story they tell is that expectations of a rising krona, combined with relatively high interest rates, drew hot money in via the carry trade. And this story is used to argue that things would have been much better if Iceland had adopted the euro.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Blame The Krona', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T12:20:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a038a34a-1f84-5749-8dc3-7a2afddca7d8', 'word_count': 214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a038a34a-1f84-5749-8dc3-7a2afddca7d8'}, {'abstract': 'A rise in Medicare premiums is lower than predicted, the United States economy quickens and avoids a stall, Air France attendants plan to strike and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/friday-reading-rise-in-medicare-premiums-lower-than-predicted/', 'snippet': 'A rise in Medicare premiums is lower than predicted, the United States economy quickens and avoids a stall, Air France attendants plan to strike and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Reading: Rise in Medicare Premiums Lower Than Predicted', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T12:32:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6e9c2bd-a082-5177-a6a6-fd20f676ed28', 'word_count': 106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6e9c2bd-a082-5177-a6a6-fd20f676ed28'}, {'abstract': 'The main river in Thailand’s capital swelled to a record high on Friday, spilling over its banks and sending water rippling into the grounds of the Grand Palace.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/29/world/asia/flood-waters-menace-bangkoks-grand-palace.html', 'snippet': 'The main river in Thailand’s capital swelled to a record high on Friday, spilling over its banks and sending water rippling into the grounds of the Grand Palace.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BANGKOK — The main river in Thailand’s capital swelled to a record high on Friday, spilling over its banks and sending water rippling into the grounds of the Grand Palace, with its temples and gilded spires, the city’s primary historical monument.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/29/world/28thailand-image/28thailand-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/29/