'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oakland Raiders', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Redskins', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T14:16:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d22f959-0871-5706-886d-8486498cc1f2', 'word_count': 2039, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d22f959-0871-5706-886d-8486498cc1f2'}, {'abstract': 'The Cardinals’ Game 6 victory almost defied description, but that didn’t stop sportswriters.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/how-many-ways-to-say-wow/', 'snippet': 'The Cardinals’ Game 6 victory almost defied description, but that didn’t stop sportswriters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So Game 6 of the World Series was full of enough thrills for a year of amusement park visits, the crazy, sloppy humanness of the first six innings followed by the wild, whose-turn-is-it-to-be-a-superhero final innings that seemed able to go on forever until David Freese’s towering, preposterous home run dropped onto the lawn beyond Busch Stadium’s center-field fence. It left only one question remaining:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Leading Off: How Many Ways to Say Wow?', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Series', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cruz, Nelson', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freese, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hamilton, Josh', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Washington, Ron', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'St Louis Cardinals', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Texas Rangers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T14:17:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ce9cf35-e848-5421-9f10-c35a626b1d40', 'word_count': 532, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ce9cf35-e848-5421-9f10-c35a626b1d40'}, {'abstract': 'In her latest film, “Young Adult,” Charlize Theron plays another unlikable character who might even be less sympathetic than the serial killer Aileen Wuornos in “Monster.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/movies/charlize-theron-strong-enough-to-play-the-heavy.html', 'snippet': 'In her latest film, “Young Adult,” Charlize Theron plays another unlikable character who might even be less sympathetic than the serial killer Aileen Wuornos in “Monster.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'THERE may be women in Hollywood more beautiful than Charlize Theron, though it’s hard offhand to think who might qualify, and even harder to think of one who relies less on her looks. Ms. Theron, whose new film, “Young Adult,” directed by Jason Reitman, opens on Dec. 9, is a genuine star — with an almost Marilyn-like glamour — and yet she goes about her work as if she were a character actress. From one movie to the next she seldom looks the same, and not just in the ones where she has obviously transformed herself. To play the serial killer Aileen Wuornos in “Monster” (2003), for example, she put on 40 pounds, shaved her eyebrows and wore prosthetic teeth. ', 'print_section': 'MT', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credi