ing need apply.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/coalmines-and-military-keynesians/', 'snippet': 'Only useless spending need apply.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ah, so now we have a new principle of economics: government spending can’t create jobs, but cuts in government spending can destroy jobs — as long as the jobs are in the defense sector', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Coalmines And Military Keynesians', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:13:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec46f3a9-2d96-53f8-a043-50c824e4b8b9', 'word_count': 468, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec46f3a9-2d96-53f8-a043-50c824e4b8b9'}, {'abstract': 'In anticipation of Halloween: custom costumes from inspired designers.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/spooky-sketches-suno/', 'snippet': 'In anticipation of Halloween: custom costumes from inspired designers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'T challenges 10 designers to turn everyday objects in to killer Halloween costumes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spooky Sketches | Suno', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Costumes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Osterweis, Max', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:15:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09d014c5-8f40-5ca5-a1e4-7d04bea86d6b', 'word_count': 66, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09d014c5-8f40-5ca5-a1e4-7d04bea86d6b'}, {'abstract': 'A gunman fired shots at the United States Embassy in the Bosnian capital, Sarajevo, on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/shots-fired-at-u-s-embassy-in-bosnia/', 'snippet': 'A gunman fired shots at the United States Embassy in the Bosnian capital, Sarajevo, on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated | 5:22 p.m. A gunman fired shots at the United States Embassy in the Bosnian capital, Sarajevo, on Friday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shots Fired at U.S. Embassy in Bosnia', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'UNITED STATES FOREIGN SERVICE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bosnia and Herzegovina', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sarajevo (Bosnia and Herzegovina)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:15:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99c4f8d8-db89-596e-ada7-07c8c545a7b7', 'word_count': 904, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99c4f8d8-db89-596e-ada7-07c8c545a7b7'}, {'abstract': 'A Tibetan advocacy group said a bomb hit an unoccupied municipal building on Wednesday in eastern Tibet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/29/world/asia/bombing-reported-in-tibet.html', 'snippet': 'A Tibetan advocacy group said a bomb hit an unoccupied municipal building on Wednesday in eastern Tibet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — A bomb possibly set by Tibetan independence advocates damaged a municipal building in a town in eastern Tibet this week, according to a report on Friday by Free Tibet, an advocacy group based in London.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bombing Reported in Tibet', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:17:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d869a504-c179-58a5-a5f4-743d9a06dd41', 'word_count': 216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d869a504-c179-58a5-a5f4-743d9a06dd41'}, {'abstract': 'A new collection features letters from the years when Samuel Beckett wrote “Waiting for Godot.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/books/review/samuel-becketts-midgame.html', 'snippet': 'A new collection features letters from the years when Samuel Beckett wrote “Waiting for Godot.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Samuel Barclay Beckett was born in Foxrock, south of Dublin, in the spring of 1906 — there’s a dispute about the precise date; he died in Paris on Dec. 22, 1989. Over a period of 60 years, he wrote more than 15,000 letters. The editors of “The Letters of Samuel Beckett” plan to publish, in four volumes, their choice of about 2,500 letters in full, and to cite a further 5,000 in their copious annotations. The letters in French are given in the original, then translated into English. The first volume, the letters of 1929-40, was published in 2009.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/DONOGHUE/DONOGHUE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 744, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/DONOGHUE/DONOGHUE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/DONOGHUE/DONOGHUE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Samuel Beckett’s Midgame', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Midgame', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Letters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beckett, Samuel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:18:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Denis Donoghue', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Denis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Donoghue', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc90b612-5078-5d73-b7de-4da2d5c66a17', 'word_count': 1680, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc90b612-5078-5d73-b7de-4da2d5c66a17'}, {'abstract': 'After four years without a word-count limit, the Common Application has imposed one. But students say they don’t know what it will mean — longer essays will make it through, but might be judged harshly.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/29/education/college-application-essay-as-haiku-for-some-500-words-isnt-enough.html', 'snippet': 'After four years without a word-count limit, the Common Application has imposed one. But students say they don’t know what it will mean — longer essays will make it through, but might be judged harshly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Penn Weinberger had grown attached to his college admissions essay. The topic — coping with a brother’s attention deficit disorder — was important to him. The anecdotes clicked. The characters had dimension. The meaning, as his teachers at Hunter College High School had long advised him, was shown, not told.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'College Application Essay as Haiku? For Some, 500 Words Aren’t Enough', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dear U., Admit Me. Thanks.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Common Application, The', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADMISSIONS STANDARDS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:20:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Flegenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flegenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/baacc4c9-e07f-583a-84ee-f5e9d1899cd2', 'word_count': 504, 'uri': 'nyt://article/baacc4c9-e07f-583a-84ee-f5e9d1899cd2'}, {'abstract': 'Spalding Gray’s journals reveal how hard he worked at appearing spontaneous.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/books/review/the-journals-of-spalding-gray-edited-by-nell-casey-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Spalding Gray’s journals reveal how hard he worked at appearing spontaneous.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Swimming to Cambodia” — the monologue that made Spalding Gray (relatively) famous, about the time he spent in Thailand playing a small role in “The Killing Fields,” the film about the Cambodian genocide — Gray tells a strange, disconcerting story about the death of Thomas Merton. Merton, the Trappist monk celebrated for his devotion to an eloquently spiritualized silence, was “a hero of mine because he knew how to shut up,” Gray, the compulsive verbalizer, tells us.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/ROSENBAUM/ROSENBAUM-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 416, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/ROSENBAUM/ROSENBAUM-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 416}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/ROSENBAUM/ROSENBAUM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 738, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/ROSENBAUM/ROSENBAUM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/ROSENBAUM/ROSENBAUM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Spalding Gray Left Us', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Art of Artlessness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Casey, Nell', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gray, Spalding', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:21:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Rosenbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e844f162-b1d1-55ba-a848-730202dd92d9', 'word_count': 1963, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e844f162-b1d1-55ba-a848-730202dd92d9'}, {'abstract': 'The Common Applications has reinstated a 500-word limit on personal statements, but The Choice would love to see both your longer and shorter versions.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/your-common-application-essay-uncut/', 'snippet': 'The Common Applications has reinstated a 500-word limit on personal statements, but The Choice would love to see both your longer and shorter versions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As early-admissions deadlines bear down next week, many high school seniors and their college admissions advisers are grappling with the 500-word limit the Common Application reinstated on personal statements this spring. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Your Common Application Essay, Uncut', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADMISSIONS STANDARDS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:22:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a85844bb-38ad-57ae-b121-877842ff8819', 'word_count': 201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a85844bb-38ad-57ae-b121-877842ff8819'}, {'abstract': 'In Trevor Cole’s novel, a ceramist wants to make sure her friends feel a moment of joy before they die.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/books/review/practical-jean-by-trevor-cole-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In Trevor Cole’s novel, a ceramist wants to make sure her friends feel a moment of joy before they die.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some of the more memorable literary perpetrators of homicide are driven not by psychotic fixations on pentagrams or by what happened to them in a foster home in 1972 but by what they perceive as simple practicality. This is true of a character as conscience-free as Patricia Highsmith’s Tom Ripley and as conscientious as Jean Vale Horemarsh, the mild, seemingly ordinary ceramist at the center of the Canadian writer Trevor Cole’s “Practical Jean,” a tidy social comedy with some very dark dimensions.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/Wolitzer/Wolitzer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 737, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/Wolitzer/Wolitzer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/Wolitzer/Wolitzer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Murder at the Least Expected Moment', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pain Killer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cole, Trevor', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:24:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Meg Wolitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wolitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b80b6a99-a03b-5df2-92b2-d9f3ff5778f3', 'word_count': 591, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b80b6a99-a03b-5df2-92b2-d9f3ff5778f3'}, {'abstract': 'In a series of vignettes, Helen Oyeyemi constructs a love triangle involving a writer, his wife and his most inspired creation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/books/review/mr-fox-by-helen-oyeyemi-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In a series of vignettes, Helen Oyeyemi constructs a love triangle involving a writer, his wife and his most inspired creation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Helen Oyeyemi’s captivating new novel, “Mr. Fox,” begins with a jaunty spirit and a sense of play. We meet Mr. Fox; he is a writer of slasher books, and he has an assistant, a woman named Mary whom he conjured in a trench during his days fighting in World War I. He also has a wife, Daphne. At some point or other, all three of them write.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/bender/bender-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 724, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/bender/bender-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/bender/bender-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Writer of Slasher Books Finds More Than a Muse', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Leaping Off the Page', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oyeyemi, Helen', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:24:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aimee Bender', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aimee', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bender', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4ace69d-0264-5a06-8289-17f2570c4717', 'word_count': 1166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4ace69d-0264-5a06-8289-17f2570c4717'}, {'abstract': 'For John Brown, violence was necessary to defeat slavery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/books/review/midnight-rising-john-brown-and-the-raid-that-sparked-the-civil-war-by-tony-horwitz-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'For John Brown, violence was necessary to defeat slavery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early one morning in the mid-1990s a squad of Civil War re-enactors wandered into Tony Horwitz’s front yard in rural Virginia. He grabbed some mugs of coffee and went out to meet them. They were ordinary guys — a salesman, a forklift operator, a waiter — who were so devoted to reliving the war that one re-enactor had learned how to bloat himself to look like one of the corpses that litter Mathew Brady’s famous battlefield photos. Horwitz had found his muse.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/27/books/review/boyleSUB/boyleSUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 579, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/27/books/review/boyleSUB/boyleSUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/27/books/review/boyleSUB/boyleSUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Road to Harpers Ferry', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Terrible Swift Sword', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Horwitz, Tony', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:25:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Boyle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boyle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b13eaac1-f6b2-5485-9b86-92f142b93b66', 'word_count': 1152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b13eaac1-f6b2-5485-9b86-92f142b93b66'}, {'abstract': 'Richard Brookhiser looks beyond Madison’s personal frailties in this biography.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/books/review/james-madison-by-richard-brookhiser-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Richard Brookhiser looks beyond Madison’s personal frailties in this biography.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No one would ever have mistaken James Madison for George Washington. Short, scrawny and sickly, he suffered from a hypochondria that convinced him he would lead neither a long nor a healthy life. He was a miserable public speaker who tended to lapse into inaudible mumbling, and well into his career as a politician, he continued to shrink back in horror at the idea of going out on the stump and putting on “an electioneering appearance.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/BEEMAN/BEEMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 767, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/BEEMAN/BEEMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/BEEMAN/BEEMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'James Madison, the ‘Father of Politics’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nation Builder', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brookhiser, Richard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Madison, James', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:25:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Beeman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beeman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a52b7c1-4920-508d-8319-1ecd9e6f84d7', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a52b7c1-4920-508d-8319-1ecd9e6f84d7'}, {'abstract': 'Where ghost hunters track the spirits of miners and mistresses, and other metaphysical outliers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/travel/death-valley-three-million-acres-of-weird.html', 'snippet': 'Where ghost hunters track the spirits of miners and mistresses, and other metaphysical outliers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT’S just before midnight on the edge of Death Valley and I’m standing in a dark room in the Amargosa Opera House and Hotel with five people who are certain that we’re talking to ghosts.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/travel/30JOURNEYS1_SPAN/30JOURNEYS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/30/travel/30JOURNEYS1_SPAN/30JOURNEYS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/travel/30JOURNEYS1_SPAN/30JOURNEYS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'capti