icker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'W. Virginia Set to Exit; Big East Won’t Let Go', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football (College)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Big East Conference', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'West Virginia University', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Big 12 Conference', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:26:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pete Thamel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pete', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thamel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e824fe28-37f1-5b12-a20c-2e5914fe97fd', 'word_count': 685, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e824fe28-37f1-5b12-a20c-2e5914fe97fd'}, {'abstract': 'In Colson Whitehead’s novel, humans spared the effects of a plague battle zombies in Lower Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/books/review/zone-one-by-colson-whitehead-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In Colson Whitehead’s novel, humans spared the effects of a plague battle zombies in Lower Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A literary novelist writing a genre novel is like an intellectual dating a porn star. It invites forgivable prurience: What is that relationship like? Granted the intellectual’s hit hanky-panky pay dirt, but what’s in it for the porn star? Conversation? Ideas? Deconstruction?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/Duncan/Duncan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1015, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/Duncan/Duncan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/Duncan/Duncan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Plague of Urban Undead in Lower Manhattan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Urban Undead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Whitehead, Colson', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:29:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glen Duncan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Duncan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/81d6619d-75e4-500c-bf96-89f8b37a758d', 'word_count': 1187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81d6619d-75e4-500c-bf96-89f8b37a758d'}, {'abstract': 'David Bellos offers a new approach to translation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/books/review/is-that-a-fish-in-your-ear-translation-and-the-meaning-of-everything-by-david-bellos-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'David Bellos offers a new approach to translation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The theory of translation is very rarely — how to put this? — comical. Its mode is elegy, and severe admonishment. In the 20th century, its great figures were Vladimir Nabokov, in exile from Soviet Russia, attacking libertines like Robert Lowell for their infidelities to the literal sense; or Walter Benjamin, Jewish in a proto-Nazi Berlin, describing the Task of the Translator as an impossible ideal of exegesis. You can never, so runs the elegiac argument, precisely reproduce a line of poetry in another language. Poetry! You can hardly even translate “maman.” . . . And this elegiac argument has its elegiac myth: the Tower of Babel, where the world’s multiplicity of languages is seen as mankind’s punishment — condemned to the howlers, the faux amis, the foreign menu apps. Whereas the ideal linguistic state would be the lost universal language of Eden.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/27/books/review/thirlwell/thirlwell-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1014, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/27/books/review/thirlwell/thirlwell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/27/books/review/thirlwell/thirlwell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Joyful Side of Translation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Making Sense of It', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Translation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Language and Languages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bellos, David', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:29:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By ADAM THIRLWELL', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'THIRLWELL', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f875af05-404a-5912-8f50-953cf5c16e4f', 'word_count': 1314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f875af05-404a-5912-8f50-953cf5c16e4f'}, {'abstract': 'Simon Sebag Montefiore tells Jerusalem’s long history, from King David to the 1967 war.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/books/review/jerusalem-by-simon-sebag-montefiore-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Simon Sebag Montefiore tells Jerusalem’s long history, from King David to the 1967 war.', 'lead_paragraph': '“If I forget thee, O Jerusalem,” declares the Psalmist, “may my right hand forget its cunning.” He neglects to mention that remembering Jerusalem is no picnic either. In “Jerusalem: The Biography,” Simon Sebag Montefiore unleashes so many kings, killers, prophets, pretenders, caliphs and crusaders, all surfing an ocean of blood, that the reader may begin to long for redemption, not from the book, which is impossible to put down, but from history itself.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/30SUBROSEN/30SUBROSEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/30SUBROSEN/30SUBROSEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 425}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/30SUBROSEN/30SUBROSEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 788, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/30SUBROSEN/30SUBROSEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/30SUBROSEN/30SUBROSEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Caliphs, Crusaders, and the Bloody History of Jerusalem', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Name Ever Dear', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Montefiore, Simon Sebag', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:29:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Rosen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2cf5819b-2699-5706-a991-208d9e5413ae', 'word_count': 1199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2cf5819b-2699-5706-a991-208d9e5413ae'}, {'abstract': 'Two films directed by Steven Spielberg, “War Horse” and “The Adventures of Tintin,” will be released this holiday season, even as he works on a third, “Lincoln.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/movies/the-season-of-spielberg.html', 'snippet': 'Two films directed by Steven Spielberg, “War Horse” and “The Adventures of Tintin,” will be released this holiday season, even as he works on a third, “Lincoln.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES', 'print_section': 'MT', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30CIEPLY3_SPAN/30CIEPLY3_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30CIEPLY3_SPAN/30CIEPLY3_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30CIEPLY3_SPAN/30CIEPLY3_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30CIEPLY3_SPAN/30CIEPLY3_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30CIEPLY3_SPAN/30CIEPLY3_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Makes Spielberg Jump?', 'kicker': 'Holiday Movies', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Makes Spielberg Jump?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spielberg, Steven', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:30:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2dafaf77-e3fa-5b41-8fc8-f31c30d8759e', 'word_count': 1909, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2dafaf77-e3fa-5b41-8fc8-f31c30d8759e'}, {'abstract': 'John Jeremiah Sullivan’s essays balance an emphasis on pop heroism with a search for his own roots.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/books/review/pulphead-by-john-jeremiah-sullivan-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'John Jeremiah Sullivan’s essays balance an emphasis on pop heroism with a search for his own roots.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is something of a surprise that one of the best magazine profiles of the last decade is about Axl Rose. But such is the work of John Jeremiah Sullivan, who can take pretty much anything you never thought you’d want to read about respectably (Axl Rose, “Real World”) or anything you never thought you’d want to read about at all (a Christian-rock festival, long-forgotten naturalist loons), and make of it the sort of essay-world you just want to dwell inside. Sullivan seems able to do almost anything, to work in any register, and not just within a single piece but often in the span of a single paragraph. Here, for example, is his first sketch of Rose in person, which can only be quoted at length:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/LEVIKRAUSS/LEVIKRAUSS-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/LEVIKRAUSS/LEVIKRAUSS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/LEVIKRAUSS/LEVIKRAUSS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Examining Pop Culture’s Heroes, and Himself', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sweet World of Mine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallace, David Foster', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {