"Clip: 'Immortals'", 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:32:08+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'None None', 'person': [{'firstname': 'None', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'None', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/97e92995-edeb-52ce-8b46-5e0dc50dacb1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/97e92995-edeb-52ce-8b46-5e0dc50dacb1'}, {'abstract': 'Esther Freud’s novel charts the diverging fortunes of a group of actors who meet as students at drama school.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/books/review/lucky-break-by-esther-freud-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Esther Freud’s novel charts the diverging fortunes of a group of actors who meet as students at drama school.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Esther Freud was an actress before she became a novelist, and as we can guess from the title of a droll essay about her acting career’s high point, “I Was an Alien in ‘Doctor Who,’ ” the change was an excellent move. She’s a superbly gifted writer, with a touch so light she’s often undervalued. Freud’s seventh novel, “Lucky Break,” which follows a group of actors for nearly 15 years — from their first day at drama school until they’re ready for Botox — may not be her most ambitious, but it’s her most breezily charming and typically shrewd, and comes with a new satirical edge.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/james/james-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/james/james-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/books/review/james/james-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Novel of Competing Actors Following the Call of the Stage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In and Out of Character', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freud, Esther', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:32:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caryn James', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'James', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/61d42fd9-3016-53b7-b828-e72c6cb353ed', 'word_count': 586, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61d42fd9-3016-53b7-b828-e72c6cb353ed'}, {'abstract': 'In 1980, the publishing industry’s awards ceremony went Hollywood — with results disastrous enough to ensure it would never happen again.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/books/review/the-short-unsuccessful-life-of-the-american-book-awards.html', 'snippet': 'In 1980, the publishing industry’s awards ceremony went Hollywood — with results disastrous enough to ensure it would never happen again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Nov. 16, literary types will put on their evening wear, gather in a Manhattan ballroom and clap politely for the presentation of this year’s National Book Awards. It’s the publishing industry’s biggest awards ceremony — and its best excuse to party — but it remains a subdued affair. Last year’s jolt of celebrity came from a jeans-wearing Patti Smith, whose “Just Kids” won the nonfiction award. Far more typical was the fiction prize, which went to the little-known “Lord of Misrule,” by Jaimy Gordon. It would have been a shocking choice if the National Book Awards weren’t known for this sort of thing. They’re awards for insiders.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/books/review/essaypic/essaypic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 791, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/26/books/review/essaypic/essaypic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/26/books/review/essaypic/essaypic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Short, Unsuccessful Life of the American Book Awards', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Guilty Parties', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Book Awards', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:33:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Craig Fehrman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Craig', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fehrman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69ce3c1f-d014-5087-8bb9-fc5af3be6087', 'word_count': 1304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69ce3c1f-d014-5087-8bb9-fc5af3be6087'}, {'abstract': '“The Devil’s Dictionary, Tales and Memoirs” is a new collection of the most spine-chilling of the powerful American horror writer’s work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/books/review/ambrose-bierce-the-man-and-his-demons.html', 'snippet': '“The Devil’s Dictionary, Tales and Memoirs” is a new collection of the most spine-chilling of the powerful American horror writer’s work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The definition of “ghost, n” in Ambrose Bierce’s puckish “Devil’s Dictionary” is “the outward and visible sign of an inward fear,” and although Bierce was not an entirely reliable lexicographer, his authority on this subject is beyond question. He knew more than any man should about both inward fears and their outward and visible signs. And he was at least arguably the most powerful American writer of horror fiction between Poe and Lovecraft. (Henry James’s supernatural tales are in a class by themselves.) In his career Bierce wrote more than 40 spooky stories, most of them about ghosts, along with a fair number of short narratives — some true, some fanciful — about the horrors of war. All his tales, both the ones about soldiers and the ones about the haunted and the haunting, are steeped in loneliness and dread, which he evokes with the precision of someone familiar with their every nuance. A fat new Library of America volume, Ambrose Bierce: The Devil’s Dictionary, Tales, and Memoirs ($35), collects the best of his work, and almost every page has something to give you the chills, though not always, perhaps, in the way the author intended.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'capti