od and became a family-friendly symbol of cold war gunslinging.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Editors' Choice: Recent Books of Popular Interest", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:33:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1b99763-b784-5bc1-9d51-0c7e12792613', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1b99763-b784-5bc1-9d51-0c7e12792613'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MARY ANN IN AUTUMN,  by Armistead Maupin (Harper Perennial, $14.99.) The characters from Maupin’s “Tales of the City” series — which began in 1974 as a serial about a young Midwesterner in San Francisco named Mary Ann Singleton — are reunited in this tenderhearted novel. Twenty years have passed since Mary Ann left family and friends in San Francisco to pursue a television career in New York, but personal calamities have driven her back.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:33:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan 