 novel “Zone One” hits this week’s fiction list, have against Connecticut?', 'lead_paragraph': 'ZOMBIES CROSSING: A week ago, all Colson Whitehead had to brag about was a bunch of well-reviewed books, a spot on various unofficial best-young-writer lists and 93,589 Twitter followers. Now, with “Zone One,” he has his first bona fide best seller. Whitehead has written books about an elevator inspector (“The Intuitionist”), a terminally ironic P.R. junketeer (“John Henry Days”) and, in 2009’s “Sag Harbor,” someone who seemed an awful lot like a young Colson Whitehead. So why a novel about zombies, or rather — the Z word never appears in the book — about a depressed young man named Mark Spitz charged with clearing generally pathetic “skels” out of some of the most valuable real estate in postapocalyptic New York?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/06/books/review/06insidelist/06insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/11/06/books/review/06insidelist/06insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Whitehead, Colson', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T16:53:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': ''