lic bread, an “Italian Thanksgiving” turkey dinner and sandwiches or platters of eggplant parm and meatball parm, refined in the hands of the chef-partners, Mario Carbone and Rich Torrisi. It will have a small takeout counter in the front, seats at the bar (a full bar this time), and tables for 30 in back. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Parm, the Torrisi Spinoff, Opens in Days', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T19:34:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/784f150b-ce1b-5355-9d77-b812fdb2b8e0', 'word_count': 218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/784f150b-ce1b-5355-9d77-b812fdb2b8e0'}, {'abstract': 'In addition to having mites and lice, swiftlets sharing caves with bats on Gawa Island, Papua New Guinea, potentially carry interesting internal parasites too.', 'web_url': 'https://scientistatwork.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/on-gawa-swiftlets-parasites-and-limestone/', 'snippet': 'In addition to having mites and lice, swiftlets sharing caves with bats on Gawa Island, Papua New Guinea, potentially carry interesting internal parasites too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jack Dumbacher, curator of ornithology and mammalogy at the California Academy of Sciences, writes from Milne Bay, Papua New Guinea, where he is studying island birds and their diseases. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Gawa, Swiftlets, Parasites and Limestone', 'kicker': 'Scientist at Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Caves and Caverns', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Islands', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parasites', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Swiftlet (Bird)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Papua New Guinea', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T19:36:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Dumbacher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dumbacher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d120bfee-a3bf-58e2-9f9a-d216cba76615', 'word_count': 615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d120bfee-a3bf-58e2-9f9a-d216cba76615'}, {'abstract': 'Energy analysts find that even a wartime-style push on known technologies and policies won’t meet California’s climate goal.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/a-reality-check-on-ambitious-climate-targets/', 'snippet': 'Energy analysts find that even a wartime-style push on known technologies and policies won’t meet California’s climate goal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An interesting commentary published in Nature this week explores what it would take to achieve California’s ambitious plan to reduce emissions of greenhouse gases 80 percent below 1990 levels by 2050. It is worth exploring here because it holds lessons that apply far beyond that state.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Reality Check on Ambitious Climate Targets', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T19:37:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d58819ee-93da-518b-b433-b4090ad6a9e6', 'word_count': 3152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d58819ee-93da-518b-b433-b4090ad6a9e6'}, {'abstract': 'The perfect book for this trick-or-treating season is “Batman: The Long Halloween,” entering the graphic book paperback list at No. 9.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/graphic-books-best-sellers-happy-halloween/', 'snippet': 'The perfect book for this trick-or-treating season is “Batman: The Long Halloween,” entering the graphic book paperback list at No. 9.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our paperback list has the perfect book for this trick-or-treating season. “Batman: The Long Halloween,” at No. 9, is a new edition of one of Batman’s greatest adventures, with extra pages previously available only in a hardcover version. The story, written by Jeph Loeb and illustrated by Tim Sale, is set in the early days of Batman’s career. Gotham City is overrun with mobsters, and the colorful freaks that make up the hero’s rogues gallery are just starting to emerge. The mystery, originally told over 13 issues, centers around a murderer named Holiday, who strikes on a special day each month – whether it’s Christmas, Mother’s Day or Independence Day. He does not get a lot of panel time, but one of the creepiest moments comes from Calendar Man, who is housed in Arkham Asylum and does not enjoy the fact that another villain is stealing his motif of date-based crimes. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Graphic Books Best Sellers: Happy Halloween', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graphic Novels', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T19:48:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d78fc6af-1b4d-54b0-adb1-a88f8e3992fa', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d78fc6af-1b4d-54b0-adb1-a88f8e3992fa'}, {'abstract': 'The composer-to-composer dedication honors relationships and also may serve as a subtle marketing tool.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/arts/music/chamber-music-society-festival-centers-on-dedications.html', 'snippet': 'The composer-to-composer dedication honors relationships and also may serve as a subtle marketing tool.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN 1801 a composer and cellist named Andreas Romberg decided to dedicate a set of string quartets to Haydn. “This dedication will surely not be unappreciated by you,” Romberg wrote excitedly to his publisher, “as it will doubtless promote the sale of the work. Now tell me if we don’t understand our public — or rather, the world!” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30DEDICATIONS_SPAN/30DEDICATIONS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30DEDICATIONS_SPAN/30DEDICATIONS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30DEDICATIONS_SPAN/30DEDICATIONS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 597, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30DEDICATIONS_SPAN/30DEDICATIONS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30DEDICATIONS_SPAN/30DEDICATIONS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Before Twitter, Name-Dropping Self-Promotion', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Before Twitter, Name-Dropping Self-Promotion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Orion String Quartet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Escher String Quartet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T19:48:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily H. Green', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': 'H.', 'lastname': 'Green', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ef526b9-b5d7-56d3-ac81-f0dc4e22a8fd', 'word_count': 1579, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ef526b9-b5d7-56d3-ac81-f0dc4e22a8fd'}, {'abstract': 'A new CD of Leon Kirchner works includes his Piano Concerto No. 1; “Lily,” for soprano and chamber orchestra; and “Music for Orchestra.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/arts/music/leon-kirchner-cd-with-piano-concerto-no-1-and-lily.html', 'snippet': 'A new CD of Leon Kirchner works includes his Piano Concerto No. 1; “Lily,” for soprano and chamber orchestra; and “Music for Orchestra.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'LEON KIRCHNER, who died at 90 in 2009, was best known as a searching, formidable and vividly intuitive composer. But he was as complete a musician as America produced in the mid-20th-century: an excellent pianist, a probing conductor and a revered (if intimidating) teacher at Harvard. Students like Yo-Yo Ma and Alan Gilbert passed through his legendary seminar in the analysis and performance of chamber music.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30LEON1_SPAN/30LEON1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30LEON1_SPAN/30LEON1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30LEON1_SPAN/30LEON1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30LEON1_SPAN/30LEON1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/arts/30LEON1_SPAN/30LEON1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Many Points of Entry to Music', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Many Points Of Entry To Music', 'name'