Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bucks Editors', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bucks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Editors', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c32e973e-ee59-51ec-af7a-3b23585d0a46', 'word_count': 311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c32e973e-ee59-51ec-af7a-3b23585d0a46'}, {'abstract': 'At the Books in Browsers conference in San Francisco, participants wrestled with the basics: What is a book?', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/28/books-unbound/', 'snippet': 'At the Books in Browsers conference in San Francisco, participants wrestled with the basics: What is a book?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is any form of traditional media under more assault than the book? For half a millennium, it was happily contained between covers: easily understood by a child, disposable, nearly indestructible, demanding minimal energy, a triumph of economy and form. The Internet has upended all that, of course. Yet the strongest impression from several of the sessions at the Books in Browsers conference in San Francisco over the last two days is that while the off-line book might be dying, the online book has yet to be born.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Books Unbound', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-28T20:27:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Streitfeld', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Streitfeld', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role'