he fruits of the fall harvest at local farms. At this time of year that means apples (and apple cider), along with pumpkins, squashes, gourds — and pies. These three orchards are representative of what is available at many farms around the state. ', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/nyregion/30DINECT-1/30DINECT-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/30/nyregion/30DINECT-1/30DINECT-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/nyregion/30DINECT-1/30DINECT-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/nyregion/30DINECT-1/30DINECT-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/nyregion/30DINECT-1/30DINECT-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Final Delights Before the Frost', 'kicker': 'Connecticut Dining | Farms and Orchards', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Final Delights Before the Frost', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Autumn (Season)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'APPLES', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-29T05:15:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abefa9db-f09e-5d11-8e40-37269db5cf62', 'word_count': 907, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abefa9db-f09e-5d11-8e40-37269db5cf62'}, {'abstract': 'Richard Dresser’s comedy “Rounding Third,” playing in Stony Point, follows a Little League team coached by a mismatched pair: a jock and a preppy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/nyregion/review-rounding-third-at-penguin-rep-is-a-hit.html', 'snippet': 'Richard Dresser’s comedy “Rounding Third,” playing in Stony Point, follows a Little League team coached by a mismatched pair: a jock and a preppy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometimes a costume says it all. In Richard Dresser’s “Rounding Third,” the final offering of the Penguin Rep Theater season, the opening scene depicts two men meeting face to face for the first time. One, wearing horn-rimmed glasses and a tweed sport jacket over a V-neck sweater, looks natty. The other, in well-worn jeans and a T-shirt emblazoned with “Local Sports Legend,” could not be scruffier. Charlotte Palmer-Lane’s textbook-clear outfits tell you right off the bat that Mr. Dresser’s comic meditation on Little League baseball is going to center on an odd couple.', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/nyregion/30ARTSWE-1_SPAN/30ARTSWE-1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/30/nyregion/30ARTSWE-1_SPAN/30ARTSWE-1_SPAN-articleLarg