le/58e67947-e632-5f3a-9fb8-101c4f57c514', 'word_count': 799, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58e67947-e632-5f3a-9fb8-101c4f57c514'}, {'abstract': 'The United States can save billions of dollars on its nuclear weapons program without compromising security.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/opinion/sunday/the-bloated-nuclear-weapons-budget.html', 'snippet': 'The United States can save billions of dollars on its nuclear weapons program without compromising security.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Twenty years after the end of the cold war, the United States still has about 2,500 nuclear weapons deployed and 2,600 more as backup. The Obama administration, in an attempt to mollify Congressional Republicans, has also committed to modernizing an already hugely expensive complex of nuclear labs and production facilities. Altogether, these and other nuclear-related programs could cost $600 billion or more over the next decade. The country does not need to maintain this large an arsenal. It should not be spending so much to do it, especially when Congress is considering deep cuts in vital domestic programs.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Bloated Nuclear Weapons Budget', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Bloated Nuclear Weapons Budget', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Global Zero', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strategic Arms Reduction Treaty', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coburn, Tom', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Joint Select Committee on Deficit Reduction', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARMS CONTROL AND LIMITATION AND DISARMAMENT', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Alamos National Laboratory', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-29T21:27:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3cf2994-2df9-5101-86c7-cb8503458f33', 'word_count': 803, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3cf2994-2df9-5101-86c7-cb8503458f33'}, {'abstract': 'Europe must face the human consequences of the war it waged in Libya and accept stranded and fleeing refugees.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/opinion/sunday/libyas-forgotten-refugees.html', 'snippet': 'Europe must face the human consequences of the war it waged in Libya and accept stranded and fleeing refugees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tripoli, Libya', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/sunday-review/30COVER/30COVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/30/sunday-review/30COVER/30COVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/sunday-review/30COVER/30COVER-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/sunday-review/30COVER/30COVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/sunday-review/30COVER/30COVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After Liberation, Nowhere to Run', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Liberation, Nowhere to Run', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Migrant and Foreign Workers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Amnesties, Commutations and Pardons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asylum, Right of', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMNESTY INTERNATIONAL', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Human Rights Watch', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-29T21:27:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By WILLIAM WHEELER and AYMAN OGHANNA', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'WHEELER', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ayman', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'OGHANNA', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/31d9bf79-90c1-5ac1-a16b-1c79d7e3a8d1', 'word_count': 2550, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31d9bf79-90c1-5ac1-a16b-1c79d7e3a8d1'}, {'abstract': 'What is the difference between intelligence and genius? Steve Jobs’s biographer has an idea.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/opinion/sunday/steve-jobss-genius.html', 'snippet': 'What is the difference between intelligence and genius? Steve Jobs’s biographer has an idea.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONE of the questions I wrestled with when writing about Steve Jobs was how smart he was. On the surface, this should not have been much of an issue. You’d assume the obvious answer was: he was really, really smart. Maybe even worth three or four reallys. After all, he was the most innovative and successful business leader of our era and embodied the Silicon Valley dream writ large: he created a start-up in his parents’ garage and built it into the world’s most valuable company. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/sunday-review/30JOBS/30JOBS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 540, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/sunday-review/30JOBS/30JOBS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/sunday-review/30JOBS/30JOBS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Genius of Jobs', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Genius of Jobs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jobs, Steven P', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Intelligence (IQ)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Franklin, Benjamin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Einstein, Albert', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Creativity', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gates, Bill', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-29T21:28:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Walter Isaacson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Walter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isaacson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a874953-04be-5a44-a144-c37fc1c7beea', 'word_count': 1260, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a874953-04be-5a44-a144-c37fc1c7beea'}, {'abstract': 'The Arab Spring has not yielded any intellectual standard-bearers of the kind who shaped almost every modern revolution from 1776 onward.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/sunday-review/the-arab-intellectuals-who-didnt-roar.html', 'snippet': 'The Arab Spring has not yielded any intellectual standard-bearers of the kind who shaped almost every modern revolution from 1776 onward.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN mid-June, the Syrian poet known as Adonis, one of the Arab world’s most renowned literary figures, addressed an open letter to the Syrian president, Bashar al-Assad. The stage was set for one of those moments, familiar from revolutions past, in which an intellectual hero confronts an oppressive ruler and eloquently voices the grievances of a nation. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/opinion/30worth-img/30worth-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/30/opinion/30worth-img/30worth-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/opinion/30worth-img/30worth-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 758, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/opinion/30worth-img/30worth-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/30/opinion/30worth-img/30worth-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Arab Intellectuals Who Didn’t Roar', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Arab Intellectuals Who Didn’t Roar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adonis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Coups D'Etat and Attempted Coups D'Etat", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aswany, Alaa Al', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-29T21:28:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert F. Worth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Worth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/89a1e30b-4851-5c76-888e-bc3490ca1374', 'word_count': 1683, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89a1e30b-4851-5c76-888e-bc3490ca1374'}, {'abstract': 'Republican senators have sabotaged the National Criminal Justice Commission Act, which would be an essential step toward major criminal justice reform.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/opinion/sunday/falling-crime-teeming-prisons.html', 'snippet': 'Republican senators have sabotaged the National Criminal Justice Commission Act, which would be an essential step toward major criminal justice reform.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Senator Jim Webb, Democrat of Virginia, has a smart proposal to create a bipartisan commission to review the nation’s troubled criminal justice system and offer recommendations for reform. The National Criminal Justice Commission Act would be a valuable first step toward reducing crime as well as punishment. Unfortunately, Senate Republicans derailed the bill recently, with some falsely claiming that it would encroach on states’ rights. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Falling Crime, Teeming Prisons', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Falling Crime, Teeming Prisons', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Webb, James H Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-29T21:28:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f2b7f51-354e-53d4-b394-30837dbb0d3e', 'word_count': 351, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f2b7f51-354e-53d4-b394-30837dbb0d3e'}, {'abstract': 'A Bangladeshi-born artist and academic is mistakenly detained at the Detroit airport. He doesn’t get mad. He gets even.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/opinion/sunday/giving-the-fbi-what-it-wants.html', 'snippet': 'A Bangladeshi-born artist and academic is mistakenly detained at the Detroit airport. He doesn’t get mad. He gets even.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON June 19, 2002, I ran into a bit of a problem that turned my life upside down. It happened at the Detroit airport as I was entering the country. I realized something wasn’t right when the immigration agent at United States Customs slid my passport through the reader, then froze. “Is there something wrong?” I asked. He was still frozen. After a few moments, he said, “Follow me, please,” and I ended up at the Immigration and Naturalization Service’s airport office. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/30/opinion/30elahi_image/30elahi_image-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 325, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/30/opinion/30elahi_image/30elahi_image-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 325}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit':