r': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/05fe4ae6-a516-5650-8e3b-02bdcf60bcbf', 'word_count': 872, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05fe4ae6-a516-5650-8e3b-02bdcf60bcbf'}, {'abstract': 'The Hungarian-born pianist Andras Schiff, presents Bartok and Schubert, with the Budapest Festival Orchestra, conducted by Ivan Fischer, as part of his series of Perspectives concerts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/arts/music/budapest-festival-orchestra-at-carnegie-hall-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Hungarian-born pianist Andras Schiff, presents Bartok and Schubert, with the Budapest Festival Orchestra, conducted by Ivan Fischer, as part of his series of Perspectives concerts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many of the artists chosen by Carnegie Hall to present a series of Perspectives concerts have brought in colleagues from diverse international cultures to share the programs and expand, well, the perspective. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/31/arts/Festival/Festival-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bartok at Home, With a Touch of the Mischievous', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bartok at Home, With a Touch of the Mischievous', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schiff, Andras', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Budapest Festival Orchestra', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fischer, Ivan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-30T22:45:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/99b034e7-be35-5994-ada4-dc06f009a8fe', 'word_count': 809, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99b034e7-be35-5994-ada4-dc06f009a8fe'}, {'abstract': '“And They’re Off ...” is a horse-racing mockumentary about a losing trainer who gets a big chance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/movies/and-theyre-off-horse-racing-mocumentary-review.html', 'snippet': '“And They’re Off ...” is a horse-racing mockumentary about a losing trainer who gets a big chance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A low-key mockumentary set in the world of horse racing, “And They’re Off ...” has its moments but not quite enough to carry it across the finish line. (Racing puns stop now.) Though some of the sport’s stars — like the trainers Doug O’Neill and Bob Baffert, he of the white moptop and dark shades — make cameo appearances and acquit themselves decently, the movie is more interested in down-and-out dreamers than in Kentucky Derby or Breeders’ Cup winners.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/31/arts/And-Theyre-Off/And-Theyre-Off-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 721, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/31/arts/And-Theyre-Off/And-Theyre-Off-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/31/arts/And-Theyre-Off/And-Theyre-Off-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Someday His Horse Will Come (and Win)', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'And They’re Off'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Someday His Horse Will Come (And Win)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oteri, Cheri', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Astin, Sean', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-30T22:49:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Saltz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saltz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/55699d85-2b8c-51e4-85bb-26c758f9c3de', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55699d85-2b8c-51e4-85bb-26c758f9c3de'}, {'abstract': 'Ecstatic trance music from Pakistan and Morocco came to the Met on Saturday night with Qawal Najmuddin Saifuddin & Brothers and Hassan Hakmoun and Friends, to mark the opening of Islamic galleries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/arts/music/qawal-najmuddin-saifuddin-brothers-at-met-museum-review.html', 'snippet': 'Ecstatic trance music from Pakistan and Morocco came to the Met on Saturday night with Qawal Najmuddin Saifuddin & Brothers and Hassan Hakmoun and Friends, to mark the opening of Islamic galleries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A trans-Islamic jam session, blending ecstatic trance music from Pakistan and Morocco, was the finale of the concert Saturday night at the Metropolitan Museum of Art’s Grace Rainey Rogers Auditorium. The Pakistani group Qawal Najmuddin Saifuddin & Brothers — making its New York City debut — and the New York group of Moroccan Gnawa musicians, Hassan Hakmoun and Friends, shared a double bill, and then performed together. The concert marked the opening of the museum’s dazzling New Galleries for the Art of the Arab Lands, Turkey, Iran, Central Asia and Later South Asia.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/31/arts/MET/MET-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 345, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/10/31/arts/MET/MET-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 345}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/31/arts/MET/MET-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/31/arts/MET/MET-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/31/arts/MET/MET-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tides of Trance Meld Divine Echoes of South Asia and Africa', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tides of Trance Meld Divine Echoes of South Asia and Africa', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-30T22:51:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b59f572-aa81-55bd-b3db-fca0b9449aa1', 'word_count': 583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b59f572-aa81-55bd-b3db-fca0b9449aa1'}, {'abstract': 'The Finnish music director Osmo Vanska led the Minnesota Orchestra in Tchaikovsky and Nielsen at Carnegie Hall on Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/arts/music/minnesota-orchestra-at-carnegie-hall-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Finnish music director Osmo Vanska led the Minnesota Orchestra in Tchaikovsky and Nielsen at Carnegie Hall on Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was high time the Minnesota Orchestra and its Finnish music director, Osmo Vanska, brought something else to Carnegie Hall. For five concerts in a row in previous visits an ensemble known to be adventurous in Minneapolis played only Beethoven and Sibelius: great composers, to be sure, and in sterling performances, Sibelius in particular being a Vanska specialty. But still.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/31/arts/MINN/MINN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 697, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Not Business as Usual for Visiting Minnesotans', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Not Business as Usual For Visiting Minnesotans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Minnesota Orchestra', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tchaikovsky, Peter Ilyich', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nielsen, Carl', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vanska, Osmo', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-30T22:52:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8476f7f-a841-5e8d-a7e0-173ec14fbfdb', 'word_count': 544, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8476f7f-a841-5e8d-a7e0-173ec14fbfdb'}, {'abstract': '“The Dumb Waiter,” Harold Pinter’s one-act play, follows two hit men killing time before targeting something more sentient.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/theater/reviews/the-dumb-waiter-at-the-duo-theater-review.html', 'snippet': '“The Dumb Waiter,” Harold Pinter’s one-act play, follows two hit men killing time before targeting something more sentient.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Torturous drips of water, screeching metal doors, muffled offstage voices, gurgling toilets. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/31/arts/DUMB-1320001650074/DUMB-1320001650074-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 877, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/31/arts/DUMB-1320001650074/DUMB-1320001650074-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/31/arts/DUMB-1320001650074/DUMB-1320001650074-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Waiting, Waiting and Waiting Some More for a Kill Order', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'The Dumb Waiter' ", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Waiting, Waiting and Waiting Some More for a Kill Order', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Asian-American Theater Co', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pinter, Harold', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-30T22:54:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/94322087-d28a-5815-beca-0d51e7dc1792', 'word_count': 308, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94322087-d28a-5815-beca-0d51e7dc1792'}, {'abstract': 'Reggie Workman, the principal bassist on the original John Coltrane recording of “Africa/Brass,” performed all the music from that album with his African-American Legacy Project.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/arts/music/reggie-workman-and-eric-reed-at-rose-theater-review.html', 'snippet': 'Reggie Workman, the principal bassist on the original John Coltrane recording of “Africa/Brass,” performed all the music from that album with his African-American Legacy Project.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In remaking the music from John Coltrane’s 1961 record “Africa/Brass,” as Reggie Workman did on Friday at Rose Theater, the first step is filling its dimensions. It’s not just that you have to fill it with loudness, though you do: the album has a huge cumulative sound. You have to fill it with people, presence and purpose, and you have to justify its repetitiveness. You have to honor pieces of music that seem to want to go on forever.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/31/arts/Reggie-1/Reggie-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 660, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Remembering Sounds That Coltrane Built', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Remembering Sounds That Coltrane Built', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Workman, Reggie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reed, Eric', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Impulse Records', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coltrane, John', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-30T22:55:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e744694-77bf-5ed8-a289-4ee0d23156bb', 'word_count': 717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e744694-77bf-5ed8-a289-4ee0d23156bb'}, {'abstract': 'For 25 years, the Dover Bookshop in London has offered copyright-free images to graphic artists, educators, even tattoo artists. But online competition now threatens the business.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/business/media/a-london-bookstore-sells-clip-art-in-an-online-world.html', 'snippet': 'For 25 years, the Dover Bookshop in London has offered copyright-free images to graphic artists, educators, even tattoo artists. But online competition now threatens the business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/31/business/LINK--1/LINK--1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/31/business/LINK--1/LINK--1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/31/business/LINK--1/LINK--1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Store of Images, From a Time When ‘Cut and Paste’ Meant Just That', 'kicker': 'Link by Link', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Store of Images, From a Time When ‘Cut and Paste’ Meant Just That', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copyrights and Copyright Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dover Publications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tattoos', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-30T22:57:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab4177ae-951d-5d69-b854-04c433ae8ff6', 'word_count': 1066, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab4177ae-951d-5d69-b854-04c433ae8ff6'}, {'abstract': 'Readers had mixed to positive reactions to David Henry Hwang’s comedy about Chinese-American relations (and relationships) that opened on Broadway last week.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/30/curtain-down-heads-up-readers-review-chinglish/', 'snippet': 'Readers had mixed to positive reactions to David Henry Hwang’s comedy about Chinese-American relations (and relationships) that opened on Broadway last week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NYTimes.com readers had mixed to positive reactions to “Chinglish,” David Henry Hwang‘s new comedy about Chinese-American relations (and relationships) that opened on Broadway last week. Some found the show’s approach to linguistic and cultural confusion humorous; others thought that it had a one-note plot and that the jokes failed to land. (In his review for The New York Times, Ben Brantley called “Chinglish” a “sporadically funny” play “laid out with the frame-by-frame exactness of a comic strip.”)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Curtain Down, Heads Up: Readers Review ‘Chinglish’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hwang, David Henry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-30T23:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b71342ed-2645-5775-9dd5-fcec1722ac76', 'word_count': 459, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b71342ed-2645-5775-9dd5-fcec1722ac76'}, {'abstract': 'Andrea Jung, the chief executive of Avon, is under pressure after a dreadful third-quarter report and the disclosure of a federal inquiry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/business/calling-for-avon-to-make-a-change.html', 'snippet': 'Andrea Jung, the chief executive of Avon, is under pressure after a dreadful third-quarter report and the disclosure of a federal inquiry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A good look in the mirror might help Avon’s management. Andrea Jung, the chief executive of the $8 billion cosmetics company, has twice failed to turn it around. Never mind the federal investigation into possible wrongdoing that was disclosed last week; Avon’s performance even before then had been dismal.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/31/business/31-views/31-views-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 533, 'width': 496, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/31/business/31-views/31-views-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/31/business/31-views/31-views-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Calling for Avon to Make a Change', 'kicker': 'Reuters Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Calling for Avon to Make a Change', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'PMI Group', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corp (Freddie Mac)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insurance', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jung, Andrea', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal National Mortgage Assn (Fannie Mae)', 'ran