ears ago, is being resurrected for Fox by one of the brothers. The network said on Friday that Keenen Ivory Wayans, left, was set to host and produce two half-hour specials in the mold of “In Living Color,” which was shown between 1990 and 1994. The shows will appear on Fox next spring and could serve as test episodes for a full series. The programs will have new cast members and musical performances. Mr. Wayans and one of his brothers, Damon, were standouts on the original series, which also featured performers who were not well known at the time, including David Alan Grier, Jennifer Lopez, Jim Carrey and Jamie Foxx.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘In Living Color’ to Return on Fox', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘In Living Color’ To Return on Fox', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wayans, Keenen Ivory', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-30T23:55:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/333508b8-18b9-5244-a3cf-c3df9d02dcdb', 'word_count': 125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/333508b8-18b9-5244-a3cf-c3df9d02dcdb'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to a Sunday Review essay about dropouts, entrepreneurs and the value of a college education.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/opinion/studied-the-dropout-vs-the-graduate.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to a Sunday Review essay about dropouts, entrepreneurs and the value of a college education.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor: ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/31/opinion/31letters-art/31letters-art-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/10/31/opinion/31letters-art/31letters-art-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/10/31/opinion/31letters-art/31letters-art-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Studied: The Dropout vs. the Graduate', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Studied: The Dropout vs. the Graduate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dropouts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-30T23:55:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/2142e014-1247-50a7-80bd-744fa4b5f4e5', 'word_count': 634, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2142e014-1247-50a7-80bd-744fa4b5f4e5'}, {'abstract': 'Artists in America constitute a tiny portion of the nation’s work force, but are better paid than the average worker, according to a survey released by the National Endowment for the Arts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/arts/study-says-artists-have-higher-salaries.html', 'snippet': 'Artists in America constitute a tiny portion of the nation’s work force, but are better paid than the average worker, according to a survey released by the National Endowment for the Arts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Artists in America constitute a tiny portion of the nation’s work force, but they tend to be more entrepreneurial, better educated and, with a median salary of $43,230, better paid than the average worker by nearly $4,000, according to a survey released Friday by the National Endowment for the Arts. Nearly 2.1 million Americans — 1.4 percent of the total work force — fit into the 11 occupations that the government counts under the artist heading. That includes comics and movie stars, set designers and floral designers. Six in 10 have college degrees, compared with 1 in 3 over all, and 1 out of 3 are self-employed (3.5 times more likely than the average worker). But can you make a living? Mom and Dad want to know. No problem if you become a movie star, concert master or architect (whose median annual salary is $63,000). But the median income drops to $30,000 or less in acting, photography, music or dance. When was the last time your waiter was a part-time actor?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Study Says Artists Have Higher Salaries', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Study Says Artists Have Higher Salaries', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Endowment for the Arts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-30T23:55:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname':