'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/260c7107-77c8-554e-8b5f-a82bc47607f3', 'word_count': 306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/260c7107-77c8-554e-8b5f-a82bc47607f3'}, {'abstract': 'Lin-Manuel Miranda, a Tony Award-winning composer and lyricist, is set to play the role of Charley Kringas in the upcoming Encores! production of Stephen Sondheim’s “Merrily We Roll Along.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/heres-charley-lin-manuel-miranda-signs-on-to-merrily-we-roll-along/', 'snippet': 'Lin-Manuel Miranda, a Tony Award-winning composer and lyricist, is set to play the role of Charley Kringas in the upcoming Encores! production of Stephen Sondheim’s “Merrily We Roll Along.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aside from playing Bernardo in a sixth-grade production of “West Side Story” and singing “Someone in a Tree” from “Pacific Overtures” in college, Lin-Manuel Miranda hasn’t had much of an opportunity to perform the work of Stephen Sondheim. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Here’s Charley: Lin-Manuel Miranda Signs on to ‘Merrily We Roll Along’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Encores! Great American Musicals in Concert (Series)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tony Awards (Theater Awards)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miranda, Lin-Manuel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sondheim, Stephen', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-31T16:16:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac46a5ee-b27c-5e55-ae3d-06884c195969', 'word_count': 352, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac46a5ee-b27c-5e55-ae3d-06884c195969'}, {'abstract': 'One writer posits that this generation is trying to do divorce differently — with better results for the children.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/can-you-have-a-good-divorce/', 'snippet': 'One writer posits that this generation is trying to do divorce differently — with better results for the children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was utterly taken with Susan Gregory Thomas’s article on “The Good Divorce” in the Sunday Styles section. My parents are rounding the bend on 61 years of marriage, but I well remember the nuclear effect that divorce had on my best friend’s life. Her parents split when she was 11. They went on to poison every holiday, every birthday, every milestone event for decades. The first time I can recall them ever being in a (large) room together without tearing it down was at my friend’s wedding, when we were 30.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can You Have a Good Divorce?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-31T16:21:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peggy Orenstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peggy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Orenstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/431f1211-0ca1-58fe-b856-e9956ec06abc', 'word_count': 507, 'uri': 'nyt://article/431f1211-0ca1-58fe-b856-e9956ec06abc'}, {'abstract': 'The ideal physician surely possesses both competence and compassion. Will our quest to eradicate the coldhearted doctor be another fad with consequences we may regret?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/11/01/health/views/the-downside-of-doctors-who-feel-your-pain.html', 'snippet': 'The ideal physician surely possesses both competence and compassion. Will our quest to eradicate the coldhearted doctor be another fad with consequences we may regret?', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I started my medical internship, my father the doctor told me that when he was an intern, the competence of his colleagues was inversely proportional to how much their patients liked them. My heart sank. I had the likability market covered. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Downside of Doctors Who Feel Your Pain', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Downside Of Doctors Who Feel Your Pain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-31T16:22:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Views', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Rosenbaum, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenbaum', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88be022a-727e-5d50-a1ec-0a41a462ccdf', 'word_count': 615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88be022a-727e-5d50-a1ec-0a41a462ccdf'}, {'abstract': 'The institution named for the first chief justice, where a new building is about to be dedicated, was originally the College of Police Science, but students considered the acronym (COPS) undignified.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/how-john-jay-got-its-name-but-not-who-suggested-it/', 'snippet': 'The institution named for the first chief justice, where a new building is about to be dedicated, was originally the College of Police Science, but students considered the acronym (COPS) undignified.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was originally dubbed the College of Police Science in 1965, but students considered the acronym (COPS) undignified. A contest was held to find a better name (the prize, half a gallon of Scotch). Theodore Roosevelt was rejected because he was already memorialized in other institutions. Objections were mustered to Felix Frankfurter — the name, some said, invited ridicule.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How John Jay Got Its Name (but Not Who Suggested It)', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jay, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'John Jay College of Criminal Justice', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-31T16:26:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95ce90a3-0954-5db5-aae3-fa2f993b2a5c', 'word_count': 635, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95ce90a3-0954-5db5-aae3-fa2f993b2a5c'}, {'abstract': 'Detroit Lions defensive end Ndamukong Suh will meet with N.F.L. Commissioner Roger Goodell about his recent fines and penalties for illegal hits.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/suh-goodell-to-discuss-rules-in-meeting/', 'snippet': 'Detroit Lions defensive end Ndamukong Suh will meet with N.F.L. Commissioner Roger Goodell about his recent fines and penalties for illegal hits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If image is everything, then Detroit Lions defensive end Ndamukong Suh realizes he could have a problem.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Suh and Goodell to Discuss Rules in Meeting', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schwartz, Jim', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Suh, Ndamukong', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Detroit Lions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-31T16:28:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne C. Gerstner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Gerstner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bcdf7859-9485-5f30-a685-9523c6fbbb50', 'word_count': 561, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bcdf7859-9485-5f30-a685-9523c6fbbb50'}, {'abstract': 'The rapper, actress and “Chicago” star hosted a previous talk show, “The Queen Latifah Show,” which ran from 1999 to 2001.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/queen-latifah-will-host-new-talk-show-planned-for-2013/', 'snippet': 'The rapper, actress and “Chicago” star hosted a previous talk show, “The Queen Latifah Show,” which ran from 1999 to 2001.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Having dispensed the indispensable advice that when you’re good to mama, mama’s good to you, Queen Latifah, the rapper, actress and star of the 2002 film version of “Chicago,” will become a full-time purveyor of life advice and inspiration on a new syndicated daytime talk show. Sony Pictures Television said on Monday that it will produce the as-yet-untitled show with Flavor Unit Entertainment, the production company of Queen Latifah and Shakim Compere, and Overbrook Entertainment, the company of James Lassiter and Will Smith (who in 1991 gave Queen Latifah one of her earliest screen appearances on his sitcom “The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air”). ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Queen Latifah Will Host New Talk Show Planned for 2013', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-31T16:30:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4acbf3df-fed1-5271-9885-f1459e29fa71', 'word_count': 132, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4acbf3df-fed1-5271-9885-f1459e29fa71'}, {'abstract': 'The failure of Beacon Power, which received a federal loan guarantee, could add to criticism of the Obama administration’s green energy financing.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/on-our-radar-another-energy-bankuptcy/', 'snippet': 'The failure of Beacon Power, which received a federal loan guarantee, could add to criticism of the Obama administration’s green energy financing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Beacon Power, a Massachusetts company that received a $43 million federal loan guarantee, has filed for bankruptcy. The failure of the company, which develops energy storage systems, is likely to add to criticism of the federal government’s green energy initiatives. [The Hill]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: Another Energy Bankruptcy', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_