terested in enrolling out-of-state students, The Chronicle of Higher Education is reporting.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/resorting/', 'snippet': 'Selective public universities are increasingly interested in enrolling out-of-state students, The Chronicle of Higher Education is reporting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an article published on Sunday in The Chronicle of Higher Education, Eric Hoover and Josh Keller reported that public universities are escalating their recruitment of out-of-state students – students who not only bring geographic diversity to campus, but also pay higher tuition rates and improve a school’s bottom line. That finding echoes the results of a recent Inside Higher Ed survey that concluded that more universities are seeking students based on their ability to pay.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Public Universities Seeking Out-of-State Students', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-10-31T17:08:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca R. Ruiz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Ruiz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2730cb93-d221-5c57-ab38-f2a4f5c69d8d', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2730cb93-d221-5c57-ab38-f2a4f5c69d8d'}, {'abstract': 'From now till December, a place to get spooked on the High Line.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/creep-show-sue-de-beers-haunt-room/', 'snippet': 'From now till December, a place to get spooked on the High Line.', 'lead_paragraph': "The New York artist Sue de Beer has long established herself as an aesthetic interlocutor of teens and the occult, so Halloween provides an apt occasion for her to unveil a new work. De Beer’s practice first took root through domestically appointed video installations that channeled deep, dark, suburban, subcultural angst. She has steadily drifted toward explorations of the paranormal, conjuring Puritan New England for her 2007 horror film, “The Quickening,” and more recently — and more minimally — evoking the inherent eeriness of spaces through structures inspired by Brion Gysin’s “Dream Machine” or corporate entities like the Seagram Building. “Haunt Room,” which opened over the weekend on the 14th Street passageway of the High Line, is a 15-foot-wide frosted plexiglass tetradecagon outfitted with powerful infrasound speakers that pump low-frequency tones outside the range of human hearing. According to experiments conducted by Goldsmiths College, these vibrations are capable, in theory, of producing a “haunted” feeling — or at the very least the perception of strange smells and physiological hallucinations like dizziness. Visitors may enter and judge for themselves how easily their sensory perceptions are smeared. “This is a New York ‘haunted house'” de Beer says. “New York City architecture doesn’t have gabled roofs or spooky parlors. It has office buildings, apartment buildings. And the only empty buildings here are either condemned or in the financial district.”", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Creep Show | Sue de Beer’s ‘Haunt Room’', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Halloween', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High Line (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Beer, Sue', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Friends of the High Line', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-31T17:10:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin McGarry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGarry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/968ec704-aee2-53db-97b1-7d8a60806402', 'word_count': 245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/968ec704-aee2-53db-97b1-7d8a60806402'}, {'abstract': 'Some ask whether it is cruel and unusual punishment for a death row inmate to wait years, or even decades, before the sentence is carried out.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/11/01/us/death-row-inmates-wait-years-before-execution.html', 'snippet': 'Some ask whether it is cruel and unusual punishment for a death row inmate to wait years, or even decades, before the sentence is carried out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON —In 1978, when he was 27, Manuel Valle killed a police officer in Coral Gables, Fla. In September, when he was 61, Mr. Valle was put to death for his crime.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/01/us/BAR/BAR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 725, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lifelong Death Sentences', 'kicker': 'Sidebar', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lifelong Death Sentences', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Breyer, Stephen G', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 5, 'major':