l Population Reaches 7 Billion, or Doesn’t', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Population', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Third World and Developing Countries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations Population Fund', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Philippines', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sarajevo (Bosnia and Herzegovina)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-31T20:48:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d4b01de-7ef0-5414-8197-34dedb3169b5', 'word_count': 728, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d4b01de-7ef0-5414-8197-34dedb3169b5'}, {'abstract': 'The Occupy London movement does not yet have concrete aims, but it does appear to have deep, and eclectic, philosophical underpinning.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/the-ideas-behind-a-move-to-occupy-london/', 'snippet': 'The Occupy London movement does not yet have concrete aims, but it does appear to have deep, and eclectic, philosophical underpinning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Occupy London movement does not yet have concrete aims, but it does appear to have some deep, and eclectic, philosophical underpinnings. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Ideas Behind a Move to ‘Occupy London’', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Anonymous (Internet Group)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'WikiLeaks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-31T20:49:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ravi Somaiya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ravi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Somaiya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8071d7eb-d12a-5b7a-b509-b06802c8be2e', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8071d7eb-d12a-5b7a-b509-b06802c8be2e'}, {'abstract': 'Introducing The Loyal Opposition, Andrew Rosenthal’s new political blog.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/10/31/welcome/', 'snippet': 'Introducing The Loyal Opposition, Andrew Rosenthal’s new political blog.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you don’t know my name, you probably know my work. I’m the editorial page editor of The New York Times, which means I’m in charge of the paper’s opinion sections in print and online — editorials, Op-Eds, letters to the editor and Sunday Review. I head the editorial board, the 19 people who produce the unsigned editorials you can read on our site and in our paper every day. Those articles represent the institutional voice of the board, the editor and the publisher — not, and I can’t say this often enough even if Republican politicians refuse to believe it, the Times newsroom.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Welcome', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-31T20:53:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d3afdd5-8bb8-5256-a47c-0cf263c712a8', 'word_count': 477, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d3afdd5-8bb8-5256-a47c-0cf263c712a8'}, {'abstract': 'Animal cannibalism used to be considered accidental or pathological, but scientists now realize that it can sometimes make good evolutionary sense.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/11/01/science/animal-cannibalism-may-make-good-evolutionary-sense.html', 'snippet': 'Animal cannibalism used to be considered accidental or pathological, but scientists now realize that it can sometimes make good evolutionary sense.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Richard Shine, a biologist at the University of Sydney in Australia, first heard the mystery of the missing eggs, he feared it was another case of what might be called invasive toadkill. He and his colleagues were studying the cane toad, Rhinella marina, a big, warty, sludge-colored Latin American amphibian that was brought to the continent years ago in an ill-fated effort at beetle control. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/01/science/01CANNIBAL_SPAN/01CANNIBAL_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/01/science/01CANNIBAL_SPAN/01CANNIBAL_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/01/science/01CANNIBAL_SPAN/01CANNIBAL_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 597, 'width': 1