/www.nytimes.com/2011/11/01/science/earth/bringing-up-the-issue-of-population-growth.html', 'snippet': 'A campaign by the Center for Biological Diversity is intended to start a debate about how overpopulation crowds out species and hastens climate change.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Major American environmental groups have dodged the subject of population control for decades, wary of getting caught up in the bruising politics of reproductive health.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/01/us/population1/population1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 377, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/01/us/population1/population1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 377}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/01/us/population1/population1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 642, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/01/us/population1/population1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/11/01/us/population1/population1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Breaking a Long Silence on Population Control', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Breaking a Long Silence On Population Control', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for Biological Diversity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations Population Fund', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Population', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'BIRTHS', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birth Control and Family Planning', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Population Council', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-10-31T22:21:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mireya Navarro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mireya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Navarro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3445825c-7050-535c-9487-43280f585878', 'word_count': 1209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3445825c-7050-535c-9487-43280f585878'}, {'abstract': 'The jazz virtuoso Chick Corea commandeers the Blue Note for most of November, performing in a retrospective of the many styles he has mastered.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/11/01/arts/music/chick-corea-jazz-chameleon-in-all-his-colors.html', 'snippet': 'The jazz virtuoso Chick Corea commandeers the Blue Note for most of November, performing in a retrospective of the many styles he has mastered.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chick Corea, the ebullient and eternally youthful pianist, bandleader and composer, turned 70 this year. Being a jazz musician, he’s bound by custom to celebrate this mile marker on the bandstand, aglow in retrospection. Had there been a corporate-sponsored jazz festival in New York this summer, he probably would have headlined Carnegie Hall sometime around his birthday, on June 12. Instead he’s taking over the Blue Note for most of November, beginning on Tuesday: two sets a night, in 10 different configurations, with collaborators old and new. It’s the more fitting option by far.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [